## Facility Operations Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Facility Operations** element defines how Palestine House manages, maintains, and controls its physical environment to ensure safe, functional, and high-quality daily operations.

Its purpose is to ensure that the facility consistently supports programming, guest experience, and operational efficiency without unnecessary disruption.

This element establishes a disciplined approach to managing:

- Space readiness
- Building maintenance
- Safety compliance
- Infrastructure
- Equipment
- Operational functionality

The facility is not treated as a passive asset but as an active operational system that requires continuous planning, monitoring, and maintenance.

Without structured Facility Operations:

- Programming becomes vulnerable to disruption.
- Guest experience becomes inconsistent.
- Safety risks increase.
- Operational efficiency declines.
- Infrastructure failures become more frequent.

Ultimately, this element provides the operational foundation that enables every Palestine House to function safely, reliably, and consistently.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Facility Operations element is applied continuously across every physical area of Palestine House.

### Areas Covered

This element applies to all operational spaces, including:

- Event spaces
- Exhibition areas
- Community spaces
- Reception and front-of-house
- Meeting rooms
- Offices
- Back-of-house operations
- Storage areas
- Technical rooms
- Building infrastructure
- External areas where applicable

### Daily Operations

Facility Operations forms part of the daily operating routine through:

- Opening procedures
- Facility readiness checks
- Safety inspections
- Equipment inspections
- Cleaning verification
- Closing procedures
- End-of-day facility reset

These activities ensure the House is fully prepared before guests arrive and properly secured after operations conclude.

### Event Operations

Facility Operations supports every stage of event delivery.

#### Before Events

Facility teams complete:

- Venue setup
- Equipment preparation
- Safety inspections
- Operational readiness checks
- Accessibility verification
- Environmental preparation

#### During Events

Teams continuously monitor:

- Facility conditions
- Safety
- Equipment performance
- Guest comfort
- Operational issues
- Infrastructure functionality

Any issues are addressed immediately to minimise disruption.

#### After Events

Facility Operations includes:

- Venue reset
- Cleaning
- Equipment storage
- Damage inspections
- Maintenance reporting
- Readiness for future operations

### Ongoing Facility Management

This element also supports long-term facility performance through:

- Preventive maintenance
- Scheduled inspections
- Safety compliance reviews
- Infrastructure monitoring
- Equipment servicing
- Asset condition assessments

Facility Operations must be embedded into everyday operations rather than treated as an occasional maintenance activity.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Facility Operations is executed by **LOCAL** teams while operating within standards established by **GLOBAL**.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing and maintaining the network-wide Facility Operations framework.

Responsibilities include:

- Facility standards
- Safety requirements
- Operational guidelines
- Infrastructure standards
- Compliance requirements
- Network-wide consistency

GLOBAL also:

- Reviews facility compliance
- Monitors critical operational risks
- Supports continuous improvement
- Oversees escalation of significant facility issues

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is accountable for ensuring Facility Operations are consistently executed.

Responsibilities include:

- Operational oversight
- Facility readiness
- Compliance with standards
- Maintenance planning
- Resource allocation
- Performance monitoring

### Operational Team Responsibilities

Facility staff and operational teams are responsible for daily execution, including:

- Routine inspections
- Opening and closing procedures
- Maintenance coordination
- Equipment monitoring
- Issue identification
- Incident reporting
- Facility upkeep

Event teams also support Facility Operations by ensuring venue readiness and identifying issues during live events.

### Area Ownership

Every operational area within Palestine House should have clearly assigned ownership to ensure:

- Accountability
- Consistent monitoring
- Timely issue resolution
- Clear reporting responsibilities
- No operational gaps

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Facility Operations element delivers clear and measurable operational outcomes.

### Facility Outcomes

The facility remains:

- Safe
- Clean
- Functional
- Reliable
- Operationally ready

Guests and staff experience a consistently high-quality physical environment.

### Event Outcomes

Effective Facility Operations ensure that:

- Events proceed without unnecessary disruption.
- Infrastructure supports programming effectively.
- Equipment performs reliably.
- Operational interruptions are minimized.

### Guest Experience Outcomes

Guests benefit from:

- Comfortable environments
- Accessible facilities
- Reliable infrastructure
- Clean and welcoming spaces
- Consistent operational quality

### Operational Outcomes

A well-managed facility enables:

- Higher operational efficiency
- Reduced downtime
- Better staff productivity
- Improved coordination
- Faster issue resolution

### Risk Management Outcomes

Proactive facility management reduces risks associated with:

- Safety
- Compliance
- Infrastructure failure
- Equipment breakdown
- Operational disruption

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, Facility Operations create a stable operational foundation that:

- Supports organizational growth
- Protects the Palestine House brand
- Extends the life of physical assets
- Improves operational resilience
- Ensures the physical environment consistently reflects the quality and professionalism of Palestine House

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Facility Operations element governs the management and operational readiness of all Palestine House physical facilities.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Building maintenance
- Facility readiness
- Infrastructure management
- Equipment management
- Safety management
- Preventive maintenance
- Operational inspections
- Compliance monitoring
- Issue reporting
- Operational continuity

It establishes how the facility should be managed on an ongoing basis to support safe and reliable operations.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Technical maintenance plans
- Engineering specifications
- Vendor maintenance contracts
- Specialist building services
- Equipment manufacturer requirements
- Professional engineering advice

These supporting systems must be developed and managed separately where required.

### Operational Boundaries

Facility issues must never be managed informally.

All maintenance, inspections, repairs, and operational responses must follow approved procedures and reporting processes.

Effective Facility Operations depend upon:

- Disciplined execution
- Timely maintenance
- Preventive planning
- Accurate reporting
- Clear accountability

### Risk and Escalation

Facility Operations cannot compensate for:

- Poor building design
- Inadequate infrastructure
- Insufficient resources
- Deferred maintenance
- Systemic operational deficiencies

Any issue affecting:

- Safety
- Regulatory compliance
- Infrastructure integrity
- Business continuity
- Operational readiness

must be treated as **high risk** and escalated immediately through the appropriate operational and governance channels to ensure timely resolution and protect Palestine House.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Facility Operations

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Audience:** House Managers and Operations Teams  
> **Framework:** 10-Step Facility Operations Lifecycle

---

# Purpose of This Guide

Facility Operations covers everything required to operate a Palestine House venue safely, cleanly, consistently, and professionally.

It governs the complete lifecycle of the venue, including:

- Daily opening and closing
- Preventive maintenance
- Supplier management
- Safety and compliance
- Incident response
- Asset management
- Annual planning

A Palestine House is more than a building.

It is the physical expression of Palestinian culture, dignity, and community in every city where it operates.

Every clean surface, functioning light, secure entrance, and well-maintained facility reflects the standards of Palestine House.

---

# Facility Operations Lifecycle

| Stage | Focus |
|------:|-------|
| **1** | Daily Operations |
| **2** | Weekly Maintenance |
| **3** | Supplier Management |
| **4** | Safety & Compliance |
| **5** | Incident Response |
| **6** | Asset Stewardship |
| **7** | Annual Review & Planning |

---

# Stage 1 — Daily Operations

## Step 1 — Complete the Daily Opening Checklist

No Palestine House venue may open until the Daily Opening Checklist has been completed in full.

This checklist confirms the venue is:

- Safe
- Legally compliant
- Clean
- Operationally ready
- Properly staffed

---

## Opening Checklist

### Entrance & Exterior

Verify:

- Exterior cleanliness
- Signage visibility
- Door numbering
- Accessible entrance
- No obstructions
- Overnight damage or graffiti documented and resolved

---

### Safety Systems

Confirm:

- Fire exit signs illuminated
- Fire extinguishers present
- Alarm system operational
- Emergency exits unobstructed

> **Critical Rule**
>
> If any life-safety system is not operational, the venue must not open.

---

### Lighting & Climate

Confirm:

- Public lighting functioning
- HVAC correctly set
- AV systems tested (where required)

---

### Cleanliness

Inspect:

- Reception
- Gallery
- Event spaces
- Toilets
- Café / Kitchen
- Public circulation areas

---

### Staffing

Confirm:

- Required staff present
- Cover arranged for absences
- Daily briefing completed
- Deliveries and maintenance communicated

---

## Decision Point

**Has any critical safety system failed?**

### Yes

Do **not** open the venue.

- Notify the House Manager.
- Contact the relevant supplier.
- Open only after the issue has been resolved or an approved temporary control is in place.

### No

Open the venue.

Log any minor maintenance items.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Opening checklist completed
- [ ] Safety systems confirmed
- [ ] Maintenance issues logged
- [ ] Team briefed
- [ ] Venue approved for opening

---

## Step 2 — Complete the Daily Closing Checklist

Closing procedures are as important as opening procedures.

Every day should finish with the venue secure, clean, and ready for tomorrow.

---

### Closing Checklist

#### Event Reset

- Restore room layouts
- Secure hired equipment
- Inventory checked
- Lost property recorded

---

#### Cleaning & Waste

- Public areas cleaned
- Waste removed
- Food stored or disposed correctly

---

#### Equipment Shutdown

- AV systems switched off
- HVAC set to overnight mode
- Computers secured
- POS systems closed

---

#### Security

Confirm:

- Doors locked
- Windows secured
- Alarm activated
- Keys returned
- Temporary access codes removed

---

#### Maintenance

Record:

- Leaks
- Flickering lights
- Sticky locks
- Minor faults

Every issue must be logged before leaving.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Closing checklist completed
- [ ] Venue secured
- [ ] Equipment stored
- [ ] Maintenance tracker updated
- [ ] Daily Operations Log completed

---

# Stage 2 — Weekly Maintenance

## Step 3 — Conduct the Weekly Facility Walkthrough

Daily inspections identify immediate issues.

Weekly inspections identify gradual deterioration.

The walkthrough must be completed by the House Manager.

---

## Walkthrough Process

### Review Every Area

Inspect:

- Exterior
- Reception
- Gallery
- Event Hall
- Meeting Rooms
- Toilets
- Kitchen / Café
- Storage
- Plant Room
- Outdoor Areas

---

### Building Condition

Inspect:

- Walls
- Floors
- Ceilings
- Damp
- Cracks
- Pest activity

---

### Fixtures & Furniture

Check:

- Furniture stability
- Lighting
- Electrical outlets
- Plumbing
- Accessibility equipment

---

### Maintenance Review

Review every outstanding maintenance item.

Escalate overdue work.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the inspection identified:**

- Structural concerns?
- Safety hazards?
- Maintenance items overdue by more than 14 days?

### Yes

Escalate immediately.

Structural issues require qualified specialists.

Safety hazards may require partial or full closure.

### No

Continue normal operations.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Walkthrough completed
- [ ] Photographs taken
- [ ] Maintenance Tracker updated
- [ ] Overdue items escalated
- [ ] Weekly report submitted

---

# Stage 3 — Supplier Management

## Step 4 — Maintain the Supplier Register

Reliable suppliers are essential for consistent facility operations.

Maintain relationships—not just contact lists.

---

## Mandatory Supplier Categories

Maintain approved suppliers for:

- Cleaning
- General Maintenance
- Electrical
- Plumbing
- Fire Safety
- HVAC
- Locksmith & Security
- Waste Management
- Pest Control

---

## Supplier Record Must Include

- Company
- Contact Person
- Emergency Contact
- Contract Dates
- Scope
- Response Times
- Service History
- Certification Expiry
- Insurance Details

---

## Supplier Standards

Every supplier must have:

- Public Liability Insurance
- Relevant trade certification
- Reference checks completed

Never use uncertified contractors for regulated work.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Supplier Register complete
- [ ] Certifications verified
- [ ] Annual service schedule populated
- [ ] Quarterly review completed

---

# Stage 4 — Safety & Compliance

## Step 5 — Manage Compliance

Safety compliance is:

- A legal obligation
- An insurance requirement
- A moral responsibility

---

## Compliance Pillars

### Fire Safety

Maintain:

- Fire Risk Assessment
- Fire Alarm Testing
- Fire Extinguisher Servicing
- Emergency Lighting
- Staff Evacuation Training

---

### Accessibility

Audit:

- Step-free access
- Accessible toilets
- Hearing support
- Signage
- Staff awareness

---

### Statutory Compliance

Maintain current certificates for:

- Electrical Safety
- Gas Safety *(if applicable)*
- Food Hygiene *(if applicable)*
- Occupancy licences

Local legal advice is mandatory.

---

## Decision Point

**Is any compliance certificate overdue or expired?**

### Yes

Resolve immediately.

Operations may need to stop.

Notify HQ within 24 hours.

### No

Maintain renewal reminders.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Fire Risk Assessment current
- [ ] Safety systems serviced
- [ ] Accessibility audit completed
- [ ] Certificates current
- [ ] Staff trained

---

# Stage 5 — Incident Response

## Step 6 — Respond to Facility Incidents

Every incident must be:

- Managed safely
- Documented completely
- Reported appropriately

---

## Five-Step Incident Protocol

### Step 1

Protect people first.

Call emergency services if required.

---

### Step 2

Preserve the scene.

Take photographs.

Record:

- Time
- Conditions
- Witnesses

---

### Step 3

Complete the Incident Report.

Include:

- Date
- Time
- Location
- Description
- People involved
- Witnesses
- Response
- Injuries
- Photographs

---

### Step 4

Notify:

- HQ
- Insurers (where required)

---

### Step 5

Conduct a Root Cause Review.

Determine:

- Cause
- Preventive action
- Responsible owner

---

## Decision Point

**Did the incident involve personal injury?**

### Yes

Call emergency services where appropriate.

Notify HQ immediately.

Insurance notification required.

### No

Complete the Incident Report and follow-up process.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Incident managed safely
- [ ] Incident Report completed
- [ ] HQ notified
- [ ] Insurers notified where required
- [ ] Preventive action documented

---

# Stage 6 — Asset Stewardship

## Step 7 — Maintain the Fixed Asset Register

Protect every significant Palestine House asset.

---

## Fixed Assets Include

- AV Equipment
- Furniture
- Café Equipment
- Security Systems
- Computers
- Art Installations
- Custom Fit-Out Items

---

## Asset Register Fields

- Asset Name
- Description
- Serial Number
- Purchase Date
- Purchase Cost
- Replacement Value
- Location
- Custodian
- Condition
- Inspection Date
- Warranty

---

### Asset Disposal

No significant asset may be:

- Sold
- Donated
- Removed

without written authorisation.

HQ approval is required above defined thresholds.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Asset Register complete
- [ ] Insurance updated
- [ ] Quarterly reviews completed
- [ ] Disposal records maintained
- [ ] Annual submission completed

---

## Step 8 — Manage the Landlord Relationship

The lease creates ongoing obligations.

Manage them proactively.

---

## Lease Management System

### Understand

- Repair responsibilities
- Permitted use
- Alteration permissions
- Reinstatement obligations

---

### Calendar

Maintain:

- Rent dates
- Service charge dates
- Inspections
- Break clauses
- Lease expiry

---

### Communication

Maintain a written record of:

- Emails
- Letters
- Calls
- Agreements

---

## Decision Point

**Has the landlord raised a significant concern?**

### Yes

Notify HQ.

Seek legal advice before responding.

### No

Respond within five working days.

Resolve and confirm in writing.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Lease obligations summarised
- [ ] Calendar maintained
- [ ] Communication log complete
- [ ] Landlord issues resolved

---

# Stage 7 — Annual Review

## Step 9 — Complete the Annual Facility Review

Conduct a strategic review every year.

---

### Review Includes

- Building condition
- Compliance documents
- Maintenance performance
- Supplier performance
- Budget review
- Capital planning

---

### Annual HQ Submission

Include:

- Compliance status
- Maintenance analysis
- Asset Register
- Budget
- Condition Assessment

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Condition Assessment completed
- [ ] Compliance reviewed
- [ ] Maintenance analysed
- [ ] Budget prepared
- [ ] HQ submission completed

---

## Step 10 — Prepare the Annual Facility Calendar

Plan maintenance before the year begins.

---

### Calendar Must Include

#### Compliance

- Fire inspections
- Electrical inspections
- Gas inspections
- Food Safety
- Emergency lighting

---

#### Supplier Services

- HVAC
- Pest Control
- Deep Cleaning
- Security Systems

---

#### Capital Projects

- Renovations
- Accessibility improvements
- Equipment replacement
- Fit-out upgrades

---

#### Lease Milestones

- Rent Reviews
- Break Clauses
- Expiry Dates
- Inspections

---

#### Budget Reviews

Quarterly facility budget monitoring.

---

## Decision Point

**Does every scheduled activity have both a budget and a confirmed supplier?**

### Yes

Share the calendar with:

- Deputy
- Programme Team
- HQ

### No

Do not finalise the calendar until every gap has been resolved or formally escalated.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] 12-month calendar completed
- [ ] Compliance events booked
- [ ] Capital projects coordinated
- [ ] Calendar shared with HQ
- [ ] Monthly review scheduled

