## Food & Beverage Operations Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Food & Beverage Operations** element defines how Palestine House manages all food and beverage activities to ensure quality, safety, consistency, and alignment with the overall cultural experience.

Its purpose extends beyond simply providing refreshments. Food and beverage are treated as an integral part of the guest journey and a meaningful expression of Palestinian culture and hospitality.

This element establishes a structured approach to managing:

- Food quality
- Beverage quality
- Food safety
- Hygiene
- Service consistency
- Operational reliability
- Cultural presentation

It ensures that all food and beverage offerings are delivered in a controlled, hygienic, and dependable manner while reinforcing the identity and values of Palestine House.

Without structured Food & Beverage Operations:

- Food safety risks increase.
- Service becomes inconsistent.
- Supplier variability affects quality.
- Guest satisfaction declines.
- Operational failures create reputational risk.

Ultimately, this element ensures that food and beverage enhance the Palestine House experience rather than becoming a point of operational failure.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Food & Beverage Operations element applies wherever food or beverages are provided within Palestine House.

### Operational Scenarios

This element is used during:

- Daily operations
- Public events
- Private functions
- Community gatherings
- Workshops
- Cultural programs
- Partner-led activities
- Special events

### Planning Phase

During planning, Food & Beverage Operations support:

- Menu development
- Supplier selection
- Product sourcing
- Service model planning
- Resource planning
- Operational coordination

Planning ensures that food and beverage services align with the event, audience, operational capacity, and Palestine House standards.

### Setup Phase

Before service begins, this element governs:

- Equipment preparation
- Food preparation readiness
- Beverage preparation readiness
- Stock verification
- Service area setup
- Hygiene inspections
- Operational readiness checks

### Live Operations

During service, the element governs:

- Food preparation
- Beverage preparation
- Safe food handling
- Service flow
- Guest interactions
- Hygiene compliance
- Operational monitoring
- Quality control

### Post-Service Operations

After service concludes, Food & Beverage Operations include:

- Cleaning
- Equipment shutdown
- Food storage
- Inventory updates
- Waste management
- Hygiene verification
- Operational reset

Food & Beverage Operations should be embedded into every event plan and daily operational routine rather than treated as an optional or secondary service.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Food & Beverage Operations are executed by **LOCAL** teams while operating within standards established by **GLOBAL**.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing and maintaining the Food & Beverage operational framework across the Palestine House network.

Responsibilities include:

- Food safety standards
- Service guidelines
- Approved operational models
- Brand consistency
- Network-wide quality standards
- Operational controls

GLOBAL also reviews and approves high-risk areas, including:

- External food vendors
- Catering partnerships
- Significant operational changes
- Compliance requirements
- High-risk food service activities

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is accountable for ensuring Food & Beverage Operations:

- Meet GLOBAL standards
- Comply with local regulations
- Support operational objectives
- Deliver consistent quality
- Maintain safe operating practices

### Food & Beverage Team Responsibilities

Designated Food & Beverage staff, kitchen teams, or approved operating partners are responsible for daily execution, including:

- Food preparation
- Beverage preparation
- Food handling
- Service delivery
- Hygiene compliance
- Cleaning
- Stock management
- Operational reporting

### Operational Ownership

Clear responsibility must be assigned for every stage of the Food & Beverage operation, including:

- Sourcing
- Receiving
- Storage
- Preparation
- Service
- Cleaning
- Waste management

Defined ownership ensures accountability and prevents operational gaps.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Food & Beverage Operations element delivers clear and measurable operational outcomes.

### Guest Experience Outcomes

Guests receive:

- Safe food and beverages
- Consistent product quality
- Professional service
- Reliable availability
- Hygienic service environments

Food and beverage should enhance the overall Palestine House experience while reinforcing hospitality and cultural identity.

### Operational Outcomes

Structured Food & Beverage Operations result in:

- Consistent service delivery
- Efficient workflows
- Reduced operational disruption
- Better coordination between teams
- Reliable event execution

### Risk Management Outcomes

Operational risks are significantly reduced through structured processes that minimise:

- Food contamination
- Hygiene failures
- Service delays
- Supplier inconsistency
- Operational breakdowns

### Partnership Outcomes

Professional Food & Beverage Operations strengthen confidence among:

- Event partners
- Catering partners
- Suppliers
- Community organizations
- Sponsors

Events involving food and beverage are delivered with greater reliability and professionalism.

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, Food & Beverage Operations contribute to a more complete and cohesive Palestine House experience where food and hospitality become an integral part of the cultural and social environment rather than operating as an independent service.

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Food & Beverage Operations element governs the complete management of food and beverage activities across Palestine House.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Supplier sourcing
- Food procurement
- Beverage procurement
- Food preparation
- Beverage preparation
- Food handling
- Service delivery
- Hygiene management
- Food storage
- Inventory management
- Waste management
- Operational monitoring

It establishes how food and beverage operations should be managed to ensure safe, reliable, and consistent service.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Food safety certifications
- Local health regulations
- Food licensing requirements
- Professional kitchen standards
- Specialist culinary operations
- Regulatory compliance obligations

These requirements must be managed separately in accordance with applicable local laws and regulations.

### Operational Boundaries

Informal or unapproved food handling practices are not permitted.

All Food & Beverage Operations must follow approved procedures relating to:

- Food safety
- Hygiene
- Quality control
- Operational consistency
- Brand standards

Safe and effective operations depend upon:

- Trained personnel
- Reliable suppliers
- Disciplined execution
- Proper food handling
- Strict hygiene practices

### Risk and Escalation

Food & Beverage Operations cannot compensate for:

- Poor supplier selection
- Inadequate staff training
- Unsafe food handling
- Regulatory non-compliance
- Weak operational controls

Any activity that introduces:

- Health risks
- Food safety concerns
- Regulatory compliance issues
- Significant operational disruption
- Reputational risk

must be treated as **high risk** and escalated immediately through the appropriate operational and governance channels to ensure full control, compliance, and protection of Palestine House.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Food & Beverage Operations

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Framework:** 10-Step Food & Beverage Operations Lifecycle

---

# What This Guide Covers

Food and Beverage (F&B) can be one of Palestine House’s strongest culture-and-revenue engines—or one of its greatest operational challenges.

When operated well, a Palestine House café:

- Deepens the guest experience
- Generates sustainable income
- Expresses Palestinian culture
- Strengthens hospitality and belonging
- Supports the wider programming mission

When operated poorly, it can create:

- Hygiene and food safety risks
- Financial leakage
- Slow and confusing service
- Excessive waste
- Reputational damage
- Distraction from the programming mission

This guide explains how to select and operate the right F&B model for each House, including:

- Model selection
- Menu design
- Hygiene compliance
- Supplier procurement
- Inventory control
- Staffing and training
- Event-night surge management
- Cleaning and reset
- Performance review and renewal

F&B is not mandatory for every Palestine House location. Where it exists, however, it must meet the Palestine House standard.

---

# Food & Beverage Operations Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Area |
|------:|------------------|
| **1** | Model Selection |
| **2** | Menu Design |
| **3** | Safety & Compliance |
| **4** | Service Setup |
| **5** | Procurement |
| **6** | Inventory Control |
| **7** | Staffing & Training |
| **8** | Event Surge |
| **9** | Cleaning & Reset |
| **10** | Review & Renewal |

---

# Stage 1 — Model Selection

> **Timing:** Before any F&B operation begins

## Step 1 — Choose the Right F&B Model for Your House

The first and most important F&B decision is not what to serve. It is how complex the operation should be.

Palestine House provides three F&B model options, ranging from a light café to a curated chef or vendor format.

Choosing a model that exceeds the House’s operational capacity or current footfall creates unnecessary risk and can undermine both the guest experience and the cultural mission.

> **Operating Principle**
>
> Do not overbuild.
>
> Begin with a simpler model and scale only when operational readiness has been demonstrated.

---

## The Three F&B Models

| Model | Name | Description | Best Suited For |
|-------|------|-------------|-----------------|
| **A** | Light Café | Drinks and a small range of ready-to-serve items, including coffee, tea, juices, pastries, and packaged partner products. Low complexity, low risk, and good margins. | Small and medium Houses and Houses in the launch phase |
| **B** | Limited Kitchen | A small menu containing a limited number of hot items prepared on-site. Provides a stronger cultural experience but requires more staff, higher compliance, and tighter waste management. | Houses with strong operational capacity and steady footfall |
| **C** | Curated Pop-Ups | Rotating chefs or vendors operate under Palestine House standards. Creates cultural variety with less permanent kitchen overhead but requires stronger partner management. | Houses with mature community partnerships and an established programming rhythm |

---

> **Decision Rule**
>
> If the House cannot deliver flawless hygiene and calm service flow, choose the simpler model.
>
> A quiet, clean, and excellent Light Café is always preferable to a chaotic kitchen.

---

## Decision Point

**Can the team operate daily hygiene checks, temperature logs, and event-surge service without creating additional management pressure?**

### Yes

Proceed with the selected model.

Complete the **F&B Model Decision Worksheet** before publicly announcing the café.

### No

Select **Model A — Light Café**.

Build operational confidence before upgrading. Do not launch a more complex model until the team can confidently meet all operational requirements.

---

## Expected Output — F&B Model Selected

- [ ] F&B Model Decision Worksheet completed
- [ ] House Manager approval obtained
- [ ] Selected model confirmed in writing
- [ ] Model shared with HQ through the launch or operations update
- [ ] Kitchen or service area configured for the selected model
- [ ] Opening timeline agreed
- [ ] Compliance and staffing confirmed before service begins

---

# Stage 2 — Menu Design

> **Timing:** Four to six weeks before F&B service opens

## Step 2 — Design a Menu That Is Simple, Cultural, and Financially Sound

The Palestine House menu is more than a café menu. It is a cultural expression.

Every item should help guests feel that they are inside a Palestinian cultural space rather than a generic coffee shop.

Cultural authenticity must, however, be balanced with operational discipline.

A short menu delivered consistently is more powerful than a large menu delivered poorly.

---

## The Small Menu Rule

The opening menu should contain:

- A maximum of **6–10 drinks**
- A maximum of **6–10 food items**
- Fewer items where appropriate during launch
- **2–3 signature Palestine House items**
- A completed margin check for every menu item

No item may be added to the menu before its margin has been assessed.

---

## Signature Item Principles

Every Palestine House signature item must meet four requirements:

| Requirement | Standard |
|-------------|----------|
| **Cultural Story** | Staff can explain the item’s origin, ingredient, or cultural connection in approximately 30 seconds. |
| **Reliable Supply** | Ingredients or products can be sourced consistently. |
| **Healthy Margin** | The item contributes positively to the F&B operation. |
| **Simple Preparation** | The item can be prepared consistently without specialist chef skills. |

Do not add an item that fails any one of these tests.

---

## Early-Stage Traps to Avoid

Avoid:

- Too many fresh products that spoil during low-footfall periods
- Excessive customisation options that slow service
- Complex kitchen recipes without sufficient staffing capacity
- Seasonal items without a confirmed replacement plan

---

## Expected Output — Menu Designed and Ready

- [ ] Starter Menu Template completed
- [ ] Item costs documented
- [ ] Margins calculated
- [ ] Cultural story tags documented
- [ ] Signature items confirmed
- [ ] Supply chain confirmed
- [ ] Preparation method documented
- [ ] Allergen information prepared
- [ ] Arabic and English menu board installed
- [ ] Menu board is legible and brand-aligned
- [ ] Staff briefed on every menu item
- [ ] Staff trained on each signature item’s cultural story

---

# Stage 3 — Safety & Compliance

> **Timing:** Before the first day of F&B service

## Step 3 — Establish Hygiene, Food Safety, and Allergen Standards

Hygiene and food safety are legal obligations and brand-critical standards.

A single food safety incident can:

- Harm a guest
- Damage the Palestine House reputation
- Expose the Local Partner to legal liability
- Create insurance and compliance consequences

Before F&B service begins:

- All hygiene systems must be active
- All staff must be trained
- All permits must be obtained
- All local food safety laws must be confirmed

This is a non-negotiable operational gate.

---

## Core Hygiene Rules

These rules apply to every F&B model:

- Handwashing stations must remain stocked and accessible
- Gloves must be used where required by local law
- Hair restraints must be worn where required
- Preparation surfaces must be sanitised before, during, and after service
- Raw and ready-to-eat products must be separated where applicable
- Refrigeration temperatures must be maintained and logged daily
- Allergen information must be visible and available at the point of order
- Unwell staff must not handle food

> **No exceptions apply to the sick-day rule.**

---

## Allergen and Dietary Management

Allergen mistakes may have life-threatening consequences.

Every menu item must have documented allergen information.

Staff must:

- Understand the allergens present in each menu item
- Answer only questions they are trained and authorised to answer
- Escalate uncertain questions to a supervisor
- Use separate utensils or preparation areas where required
- Follow cross-contamination controls

> **Rule**
>
> Never guess.
>
> When uncertain, stop and escalate.

---

> **Important**
>
> Food safety and allergen laws differ by country and city.
>
> The House Manager must confirm all required permits, certifications, inspections, and staff training with the relevant local authority before service begins.
>
> HQ does not define local food law, but it requires full compliance with it.

---

## Decision Point

**Are all local permits confirmed, all staff hygiene-trained, and all allergen records complete?**

### Yes

Proceed with opening.

Activate the:

- Daily Hygiene Checklist
- Temperature Log

from the first day of service.

### No

Do not open F&B service.

Resolve every outstanding compliance requirement before serving guests.

---

## Expected Output — Safety & Compliance Confirmed

- [ ] Required local permits obtained
- [ ] Permits filed and accessible
- [ ] All F&B staff hygiene-trained
- [ ] Allergen training completed
- [ ] Daily Hygiene Checklist activated
- [ ] Temperature Log activated
- [ ] Allergen information documented for every item
- [ ] Allergen information available at the point of order
- [ ] Emergency response protocol established
- [ ] Escalation contact confirmed for allergic reactions or temperature breaches

---

# Stage 4 — Service Setup

> **Timing:** Before opening day

## Step 4 — Configure the Service Flow for Speed and Calm

Long queues damage the atmosphere.

Confusion at the counter creates frustration that even strong programming may not fully recover.

The physical service flow must be designed before the first guest is served.

It must not be improvised during a busy period.

A well-designed flow makes the café feel calm and effortless, including on high-footfall event nights.

---

## Standard Café Service Flow

1. The guest orders at the counter from a clearly displayed menu.
2. The guest pays at the counter by POS or cash.
3. Payment is never delayed until collection.
4. The guest receives a number or name confirmation.
5. The guest moves to the marked pickup point.
6. Staff call the guest calmly using the number or name.
7. The guest collects the order and moves into the wider space.

---

## Physical Setup Standards

The service area must include:

- A clear and legible menu board at the ordering point
- A separate and clearly marked pickup point
- A defined queue route
- Additional queue controls for event nights
- A self-service water station
- A tested POS system

The POS system must be tested and reconciled before every service period.

---

## Expected Output — Service Flow Confirmed

- [ ] Ordering point defined
- [ ] Pickup point defined
- [ ] Queue route established
- [ ] Physical layout tested
- [ ] POS system configured
- [ ] POS system tested
- [ ] Daily reconciliation procedure active
- [ ] Staff trained on the full service flow
- [ ] Water station installed
- [ ] Water station stocked
- [ ] Dry run completed
- [ ] Normal order-to-pickup time demonstrated at under three minutes

---

# Stage 5 — Procurement & Suppliers

> **Timing:** Before opening and quarterly thereafter

## Step 5 — Build an Approved Supplier List

F&B procurement is not based on price alone.

Every supplier providing ingredients, products, or packaging represents the Palestine House brand.

Suppliers must:

- Meet quality standards
- Deliver reliably
- Comply with food safety requirements
- Support viable margins
- Present no reputational risk

The Approved Supplier List is a system for protecting quality, margins, compliance, and the brand.

---

## Supplier Approval Process

Before approving a supplier:

1. Test product samples for taste, quality, and consistency.
2. Review pricing and confirm viable margins.
3. Assess at least two deliveries for reliability.
4. Confirm hygiene and food safety compliance.
5. Complete an ethics and brand-alignment check.
6. Add the supplier to the Approved Supplier List.
7. Set a quarterly review date.

---

## Non-Negotiable Supplier Standards

Every supplier must provide:

- Consistent quality
- Reliable delivery
- Transparent pricing
- Agreed payment terms
- Relevant hygiene compliance
- No ethical or reputational risk

A backup option must exist for every critical product category.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the supplier completed the full approval process?**

This includes:

- Sample testing
- Margin assessment
- Delivery trials
- Compliance confirmation
- Brand-alignment review

### Yes

Add the supplier to the Approved Supplier List.

Set the quarterly review date.

### No

Do not purchase from the supplier.

There are no exceptions based on price, urgency, or convenience.

---

## Expected Output — Approved Supplier List Active

- [ ] Approved Supplier List completed
- [ ] All current suppliers recorded
- [ ] Product categories documented
- [ ] Approval status recorded
- [ ] Review dates established
- [ ] No unapproved suppliers in use
- [ ] Backup suppliers confirmed for critical categories
- [ ] Quarterly supplier review scheduled
- [ ] Underperforming suppliers flagged and replaced

---

# Stage 6 — Inventory Control

> **Timing:** Weekly and ongoing

## Step 6 — Control Inventory, Portioning, and Waste

F&B operations commonly lose money through:

1. Waste
2. Uncontrolled portioning

Both can be reduced through a simple and consistent inventory system.

If waste is not measured, the House is paying for losses without understanding where they occur.

The minimum Palestine House inventory system includes:

- Weekly stocktakes
- Daily waste logs
- Reorder points
- Portion controls
- Monthly variance reviews

---

## Minimum Inventory System

### Weekly Stocktake

Complete a stocktake every week.

It should take approximately 10–20 minutes and cover every critical item.

Compare the results with the previous week.

---

### Reorder Points

Set a reorder point for every high-use item.

The House should know when to reorder before the item runs out.

---

### Portion Control

Document preparation and serving quantities for every prepared item.

Do not rely on visual estimation.

---

### Daily Waste Log

Record waste under four categories:

| Waste Category | Examples |
|----------------|----------|
| **Spoilage** | Expired or incorrectly stored products |
| **Overproduction** | Excess batches or unsold prepared items |
| **Mistakes & Returns** | Incorrect orders, preparation errors, guest returns |
| **Freebies & Comps** | Complimentary products or unauthorised giveaways |

---

## Reading the Waste Log

| Waste Pattern | Likely Cause |
|---------------|--------------|
| High spoilage | Over-ordering or poor storage |
| High overproduction | Incorrect batch sizes |
| High mistakes and returns | Training or service-flow failures |
| High freebies and comps | Weak approval controls |

Review the waste log weekly—not monthly.

---

## Expected Output — Inventory System Active

- [ ] Weekly Stocktake Sheet completed
- [ ] Stocktake completed every week without exception
- [ ] Reorder points established
- [ ] Critical products monitored
- [ ] Daily Waste Log active
- [ ] Waste reviewed weekly by the F&B Lead
- [ ] Portion Guide prepared
- [ ] Portion Guide displayed in the preparation area
- [ ] Monthly variance review completed
- [ ] Unexplained variance escalated to the House Manager

---

# Stage 7 — Staffing & Training

> **Timing:** Before opening and whenever a new team member joins

## Step 7 — Assign Clear Ownership and Train Every Team Member

F&B operations without clear ownership become inconsistent.

Every café, including a small Light Café, requires one person to be accountable for:

- Hygiene
- Compliance
- Stock
- Service quality
- Reporting
- Team performance

This person is the **F&B Lead**.

The position may be part-time in a smaller House, but it must exist.

Without an F&B Lead, standards become dependent on individual judgement and drift over time.

---

## Minimum Staffing Roles

| Role | Responsibility | Required For |
|------|----------------|--------------|
| **F&B Lead** | Overall standards, compliance, inventory, reporting, and service quality | All models |
| **Barista / Server** | Guest service, ordering, payment, and preparation | All models |
| **Runner / Support** | Restocking, queue support, collection flow, and surge assistance | All models on event nights |
| **Kitchen Operator** | On-site preparation of food | Models B and C |

---

## Minimum Training Before the First Shift

Every team member must be trained in:

### Hygiene

- Handwashing
- Glove use
- Surface sanitation
- Sick-day rules

### Allergens

- Allergens in every menu item
- Escalation procedures
- The “never guess” rule

### Service Flow

- Ordering
- Payment
- Preparation
- Pickup
- Queue management

### Menu Knowledge

- Every menu item
- Ingredients
- Preparation method
- Cultural story of signature items

### Operational Logs

- Temperature logging
- Waste logging
- Hygiene checklists

---

> **Cultural Storytelling Standard**
>
> Cultural storytelling is a mandatory part of F&B training.
>
> Every person serving guests should be able to answer “What is this?” with a brief and genuine cultural explanation—not only a price or ingredient list.

---

## Expected Output — Staffed and Trained

- [ ] F&B Lead appointed
- [ ] Responsibilities documented
- [ ] Reporting line confirmed
- [ ] All serving staff trained
- [ ] No untrained person handling food
- [ ] No untrained person serving guests
- [ ] Training completion recorded
- [ ] House Manager sign-off obtained
- [ ] Launch-week rota confirmed
- [ ] Event-night coverage confirmed

---

# Stage 8 — Event Surge Management

> **Application:** Every event with more than 50 expected attendees

## Step 8 — Activate the Event Surge Plan

Events create sudden footfall increases that standard café staffing and menus may not absorb.

A programming night may send between 50 and 200 guests to the café:

- Before the programme
- During an interval
- Immediately after the programme

Without preparation, this may create:

- Long queues
- Noise
- Service delays
- Confusion
- Stock shortages
- Damage to the event atmosphere

The Event Surge Plan must be activated for every event with more than 50 expected attendees.

---

## Event Surge Activation Steps

1. Activate the reduced Event Menu 60–90 minutes before the event.
2. Limit the menu to faster-preparation items.
3. Pre-batch appropriate high-demand products where operationally and legally suitable.
4. Confirm at least one additional team member.
5. Establish a clear and visible queue.
6. Mark the pickup point.
7. Confirm café cut-off times during talks or screenings.
8. Prevent service noise from competing with programming.
9. Complete the post-peak reset.
10. Log waste, clean surfaces, check stock, and reset the area.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Event Surge Plan been activated at least 90 minutes before the event begins?**

### Yes

Proceed with normal surge preparation.

The F&B Lead must confirm readiness to the House Manager before doors open.

### No

Activate the plan immediately.

An unprepared café during a high-footfall event will damage the guest experience and programme atmosphere.

---

## Expected Output — Event Surge Managed

- [ ] Event Surge Readiness Checklist completed
- [ ] Checklist completed for every event above 50 attendees
- [ ] Reduced Event Menu activated
- [ ] Menu activated 60–90 minutes before the event
- [ ] Additional staff confirmed
- [ ] Staff briefed before doors open
- [ ] Queue route prepared
- [ ] Pickup point marked
- [ ] Service cut-off time confirmed
- [ ] Post-event reset completed
- [ ] Waste logged
- [ ] Surfaces cleaned
- [ ] Stock checked

---

# Stage 9 — Cleaning & Reset

> **Timing:** After every shift and through a scheduled weekly deep clean

## Step 9 — Complete the Cleaning SOP

Cleanliness is not only a compliance requirement. It is a source of guest trust.

A dirty counter, disorganised fridge, or sticky floor affects how guests judge the entire Palestine House operation.

The Cleaning SOP applies regardless of:

- How busy the shift was
- Staffing pressure
- Programming demands
- How late the event ended

Post-shift cleaning and weekly deep cleaning are mandatory.

---

## Post-Shift Cleaning SOP

Complete after every shift:

- Sanitise all preparation surfaces
- Clean all equipment used
- Sweep the F&B floor
- Mop the F&B floor
- Empty all bins
- Do not leave waste overnight
- Restock cups
- Restock napkins
- Restock consumables
- Check the fridge
- Confirm the fridge is closed
- Confirm the fridge is clean
- Confirm the fridge is organised
- Complete the Waste Log
- Obtain supervisor sign-off on the Daily Hygiene Checklist

---

## Weekly Deep Clean

Complete every week on a scheduled day:

- Remove and clean all refrigerator shelves
- Clean behind equipment
- Clean underneath equipment
- Clear and clean drains
- Clean filters where applicable
- Reorganise storage
- Check expiry dates
- Rotate stock using first-in, first-out
- Check cleaning supplies
- Replenish cleaning supplies

---

## Expected Output — Cleaning Standards Maintained

- [ ] Post-shift cleaning completed after every shift
- [ ] Supervisor sign-off recorded
- [ ] Weekly deep clean scheduled
- [ ] Weekly deep clean completed
- [ ] Daily Hygiene Checklist filed
- [ ] Records accessible for inspection
- [ ] Cleaning supply shortages reported immediately
- [ ] Equipment shortages reported to the F&B Lead

---

# Stage 10 — Review & Renewal

> **Timing:** Quarterly and at year-end

## Step 10 — Review Performance and Renew the Model

F&B operations that are not reviewed will gradually drift.

Common symptoms include:

- Stale menus
- Declining margins
- Supplier underperformance
- Training gaps
- Reduced cultural storytelling
- Hygiene failures
- Uncontrolled waste

The required review rhythm is:

- **Quarterly performance review**
- **Annual strategic renewal**

Both are mandatory while F&B service is operating.

---

## Quarterly F&B Review Agenda

### Financial Performance

Review:

- Revenue
- Margin by item
- Total waste cost
- Variance against target

### Menu Performance

Review:

- Best-performing items
- Underperforming items
- Items to retire
- Items to replace

### Hygiene Compliance

Review:

- Checklist failures
- Temperature breaches
- Near-misses
- Corrective actions

### Supplier Performance

Review:

- Product quality
- Delivery reliability
- Pricing
- Suppliers requiring replacement

### Team Performance

Review:

- F&B Lead performance
- Staff consistency
- Training gaps
- Rota adequacy

### Guest Feedback

Review:

- Complaints
- Compliments
- Suggestions
- Recurring service issues

---

## Annual F&B Strategic Renewal

Once each year, review whether the selected F&B model remains suitable.

Consider:

- Has footfall increased?
- Has team capacity improved?
- Have community preferences changed?
- Is Model A still appropriate?
- Is the House ready to move to Model B?
- Are curated pop-ups generating cultural value?
- Does the menu continue to express Palestinian food culture authentically?
- Are signature items still relevant and financially viable?

Before the new operating year:

- Confirm or change the model
- Renew the menu
- Reassess suppliers
- Reset performance targets
- Update staff training

---

## Decision Point

**Has the quarterly review identified any of the following?**

- A compliance failure
- Margin variance above 10%
- A serious supplier-quality issue

### Yes

Resolve the issue within seven days.

Document the action taken.

Notify HQ immediately where the issue involves compliance.

### No

Continue normal operations.

Document and file the quarterly review.

---

## Expected Output — F&B Review Complete

- [ ] Quarterly review completed
- [ ] Review documented
- [ ] Outcomes shared with the House Manager
- [ ] Compliance issues resolved
- [ ] Margin issues resolved
- [ ] Supplier issues resolved
- [ ] Corrective actions logged
- [ ] Annual model review completed
- [ ] Model confirmed or updated
- [ ] Menu renewed
- [ ] Underperforming items retired
- [ ] New signature items assessed
- [ ] Cultural stories documented
- [ ] Margin checks completed
- [ ] Approved Supplier List updated
- [ ] Suppliers reassessed
- [ ] Underperforming suppliers replaced
- [ ] F&B performance included in the monthly House Pack submitted to HQ

