## Inventory & Procurement Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Inventory & Procurement** element defines how Palestine House sources, manages, and controls all materials, equipment, supplies, and operational resources required to support daily operations and programming.

Its purpose is to ensure that operational needs are consistently met in a timely, cost-effective, and controlled manner while maintaining high standards of quality, compliance, and supplier reliability.

This element establishes a disciplined system for:

- Resource planning
- Supplier management
- Procurement
- Inventory control
- Stock management
- Financial accountability

It prevents:

- Stock shortages
- Overstocking
- Unapproved purchasing
- Supplier inconsistency
- Operational disruption

Procurement decisions are not treated as reactive or informal activities but as structured operational processes aligned with organizational priorities, budgets, and governance requirements.

Ultimately, this element ensures that Palestine House has the right resources, in the right quantities, at the right time, while maintaining financial discipline and operational readiness.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Inventory & Procurement element is applied continuously across every operational function within Palestine House.

### Operational Areas

This element supports all areas requiring operational resources, including:

- Programming
- Facility Operations
- Food & Beverage Operations
- Administration
- Events
- Marketing
- Community engagement
- Maintenance activities

### Planning Phase

During planning, Inventory & Procurement supports:

- Demand forecasting
- Event planning
- Operational resource planning
- Maintenance planning
- Budget forecasting
- Procurement scheduling

Forecasting ensures resources are available before they are required.

### Procurement Phase

During procurement, this element governs:

- Supplier selection
- Approved supplier usage
- Supplier evaluation
- Price negotiation
- Purchase approvals
- Purchase order management
- Order placement

All procurement activities should follow approved financial and operational controls.

### Operational Phase

During day-to-day operations, Inventory & Procurement governs:

- Inventory tracking
- Stock control
- Storage management
- Material usage
- Equipment availability
- Consumption monitoring
- Waste reduction

These activities ensure that operational teams have reliable access to required resources while maintaining inventory accuracy.

### Post-Usage Activities

Following operational use, this element includes:

- Inventory reconciliation
- Stock replenishment
- Restocking decisions
- Supplier performance evaluation
- Procurement performance review
- Inventory reporting

Inventory & Procurement should be integrated into both daily operational workflows and event planning cycles to ensure continuous readiness and operational control.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Inventory & Procurement is executed by **LOCAL** teams while operating within standards established by **GLOBAL**.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing the procurement and inventory management framework across the Palestine House network.

Responsibilities include:

- Procurement policies
- Financial controls
- Approved supplier frameworks
- Governance standards
- Procurement compliance
- Network-wide operational consistency

GLOBAL also reviews and approves:

- High-value purchases
- High-risk procurement activities
- Strategic supplier decisions
- Procurement exceptions
- Governance compliance

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is accountable for ensuring Inventory & Procurement activities:

- Follow approved procedures
- Remain within budget
- Support operational priorities
- Meet organizational requirements
- Comply with procurement policies

### Operational Team Responsibilities

Designated operational staff are responsible for daily inventory and procurement activities, including:

- Inventory management
- Stock monitoring
- Purchase requests
- Order placement
- Inventory reconciliation
- Storage management
- Record maintenance
- Supplier coordination

### Inventory Ownership

Clear ownership should be assigned for every inventory category to ensure:

- Accountability
- Inventory accuracy
- Timely replenishment
- Proper stock control
- Consistent reporting
- Effective operational oversight

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Inventory & Procurement element delivers clear and measurable operational outcomes.

### Operational Outcomes

Operational teams benefit from:

- Reliable access to required resources
- Reduced operational interruptions
- Improved planning
- Greater operational readiness
- Consistent resource availability

### Inventory Outcomes

Effective inventory management results in:

- Optimized stock levels
- Reduced waste
- Fewer stock shortages
- Better inventory visibility
- Improved storage efficiency

### Procurement Outcomes

Structured procurement processes provide:

- Better cost control
- Improved purchasing efficiency
- Reliable supplier performance
- Consistent purchasing decisions
- Stronger procurement governance

### Financial Outcomes

Disciplined Inventory & Procurement contribute to:

- Better budget management
- Reduced unnecessary expenditure
- Greater financial accountability
- Improved purchasing transparency
- Lower operational risk

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, this element strengthens Palestine House by:

- Building reliable supplier relationships
- Improving operational resilience
- Supporting sustainable growth
- Reducing procurement risk
- Creating a stable operational foundation through disciplined resource management

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Inventory & Procurement element governs the complete lifecycle of acquiring, managing, and controlling operational resources across Palestine House.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Resource planning
- Supplier sourcing
- Supplier management
- Procurement
- Purchase approvals
- Ordering
- Inventory control
- Stock tracking
- Storage management
- Inventory reconciliation
- Restocking
- Procurement performance monitoring

It establishes how operational resources are acquired, managed, and controlled throughout their lifecycle.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Financial management systems
- Contract management
- Legal procurement processes
- Enterprise purchasing systems
- Specialized procurement procedures
- Complex tendering processes

These supporting systems must be managed separately where required.

### Operational Boundaries

Informal purchasing outside approved procedures is not permitted.

All procurement and inventory activities must follow established controls relating to:

- Financial approvals
- Procurement governance
- Supplier approval
- Inventory accountability
- Operational compliance

Effective Inventory & Procurement depend upon:

- Accurate forecasting
- Disciplined inventory tracking
- Reliable recordkeeping
- Budget control
- Compliance with approval processes

### Risk and Escalation

Inventory & Procurement cannot compensate for:

- Poor operational planning
- Inaccurate forecasting
- Weak budget management
- Supplier underperformance
- Failure to follow procurement procedures

Any procurement activity that:

- Exceeds approved financial thresholds
- Involves new or unapproved suppliers
- Introduces operational risk
- Creates financial risk
- Presents compliance concerns
- Has potential reputational impact

must be escalated through the appropriate **GLOBAL** governance channels for review and approval before proceeding.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Inventory & Procurement

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Framework:** 10-Step Inventory & Procurement Lifecycle

---

# What This Guide Covers

Inventory and Procurement is the operational backbone of Palestine House.

Every event delivered, every cup of coffee served, every cultural installation created, and every piece of merchandise distributed depends on goods being:

- Sourced
- Ordered
- Delivered
- Received
- Stored
- Controlled
- Reported

Poor procurement leads to:

- Budget overruns
- Supplier problems
- Operational disruption
- Stock shortages
- Governance failures

Strong procurement creates:

- Reliability
- Cost control
- Operational resilience
- Ethical purchasing
- Consistent service delivery

This guide explains the complete procurement lifecycle, from identifying operational needs through supplier management, purchasing, inventory control, reporting, and annual optimisation.

---

# Inventory & Procurement Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Area |
|------:|------------------|
| **1** | Needs Assessment |
| **2** | Supplier Sourcing |
| **3** | Purchase & Ordering |
| **4** | Delivery & Receiving |
| **5** | Stock Management |
| **6** | Controls & Reporting |
| **7** | Review & Optimisation |

---

# Stage 1 — Needs Assessment

> **Timing:** Before any procurement activity begins

## Step 1 — Understand the Six Procurement Categories

Procurement is not one activity.

It consists of six operational categories, each with:

- Different suppliers
- Different approval requirements
- Different stock behaviour
- Different operational priorities
- Different ownership

Every category must have a clearly assigned owner.

If ownership is unclear, procurement responsibility automatically defaults to the House Manager.

Failure to assign ownership results in:

- Duplicate purchasing
- Missed purchasing
- Budget errors
- Stock shortages
- Accountability gaps

---

## The Six Procurement Categories

| Category | Typical Items | Primary Owner |
|-----------|---------------|---------------|
| **1. Café & F&B Supplies** | Food ingredients, beverages, packaging, disposables, cleaning materials | Café Manager / Operations Lead |
| **2. Events & Programming Materials** | Exhibition materials, workshop consumables, printing, display items, audience stationery | Programming Lead / Events Coordinator |
| **3. Facilities & Maintenance** | Cleaning equipment, maintenance materials, safety supplies, replacement parts | House Manager / Facilities Lead |
| **4. Brand & Merchandise** | Publications, tote bags, apparel, ceramics, prints and branded merchandise | House Manager *(HQ Approval Required)* |
| **5. Technology, AV & Equipment** | Audio-visual equipment, IT hardware, production equipment and peripherals | Technical Lead / House Manager |
| **6. Office & Administration** | Office stationery, administrative consumables and printing supplies | Operations Lead / Finance Lead |

---

## Procurement Principles by Category

### Café & F&B Supplies

- Highest purchasing frequency
- Daily or weekly ordering
- Prioritise Palestinian and Middle Eastern products where quality and commercial viability permit

---

### Events & Programming Materials

Purchasing is driven by the programming calendar.

Order sufficiently in advance to avoid event disruption.

---

### Facilities & Maintenance

Maintain minimum stock at all times.

A venue that cannot maintain itself cannot safely host guests.

---

### Brand & Merchandise

Every branded item requires HQ approval before production.

Never order branded merchandise without approved artwork on file.

---

### Technology & Equipment

Higher-value purchases require formal quotation and comparison before approval.

---

### Office & Administration

Lowest procurement priority.

Do not compromise café or programme budgets to maintain office supplies.

Purchase in quarterly bulk orders where practical.

---

> **Important — Procurement Ethics**
>
> Palestine House will not establish supplier relationships with organisations associated with:
>
> - Political violence
> - Human rights violations
> - Sanctioned entities
> - Significant reputational risk
>
> Any supplier presenting political or reputational concerns must be referred to HQ before engagement.

---

## Expected Output — Procurement Categories Assigned

- [ ] All six procurement categories assigned to named owners
- [ ] Responsibilities documented within the House Operational Plan
- [ ] Category owners understand their procurement scope
- [ ] Typical ordering frequency understood
- [ ] Key supplier types identified
- [ ] HQ approval requirement understood for Brand & Merchandise
- [ ] Procurement ethics standard communicated to all relevant staff

---

# Stage 2 — Supplier Sourcing

> **Timing:** Before opening and reviewed annually

## Step 2 — Build the Approved Supplier Register

Operations should never depend upon supplier contact details stored on one individual's mobile phone.

The Approved Supplier Register provides operational continuity even if staff change.

Every significant procurement category should maintain:

- One primary supplier
- At least one approved backup supplier

---

## Supplier Register Requirements

### Supplier Identity

Record:

- Business name
- Trading name
- Registered address
- Ordering contact
- Account manager
- Emergency contact

---

### Category Coverage

Document:

- Procurement category
- Products or services supplied
- Primary or backup status

---

### Commercial Terms

Maintain:

- Payment terms
- Minimum order quantities
- Delivery lead times
- Delivery charges
- Currency
- VAT treatment
- Discounts
- Volume pricing

---

### Ethics & Vetting

Record:

- Ethics review completed
- Political or reputational concerns
- Last ethics review date
- Palestinian-owned or diaspora-owned status

Where quality and commercial terms permit, Palestinian and diaspora suppliers should be prioritised.

---

### Supplier Performance

Monitor:

- Delivery reliability
- Product quality
- Communication
- Responsiveness
- Disputes

Update supplier performance during every quarterly review.

Suppliers demonstrating repeated performance issues should be downgraded or removed.

---

## Decision Point

**Does every primary supplier have an approved backup supplier?**

### Yes

Maintain quarterly performance reviews.

### No

Identify the missing backup suppliers before opening.

Single-supplier dependency represents operational risk.

---

## Expected Output — Approved Supplier Register Established

- [ ] Approved Supplier Register completed
- [ ] Primary suppliers identified
- [ ] Backup suppliers identified
- [ ] Commercial terms documented
- [ ] Ethics reviews completed
- [ ] Palestinian and diaspora suppliers identified where appropriate

---

## Step 3 — Establish Procurement Thresholds and Purchase Approval

Not every purchase requires the same level of approval.

A minor consumable purchase and a large equipment purchase require different governance.

Palestine House must maintain documented procurement thresholds.

---

## Procurement Approval Framework

| Tier | Typical Value | Approval Requirement |
|------|---------------|----------------------|
| **Tier 1** | Petty Cash / Small Purchases | Category Owner |
| **Tier 2** | Standard Purchase Orders | House Manager |
| **Tier 3** | Significant Purchases | House Manager + Finance Lead / Board Member + HQ Notification |
| **Tier 4** | Major Purchases | Board Approval + HQ Written Approval |

> **Special Rule**
>
> Brand & Merchandise purchases require HQ approval regardless of value.

---

## Standard Purchase Order Process

1. Procurement need identified.
2. Approved supplier selected.
3. Purchase Order prepared.
4. Purchase Order submitted for approval.
5. Approved Purchase Order sent to supplier.
6. Delivery checked against Purchase Order.
7. Invoice matched before payment.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the procurement approval framework been documented and approved?**

### Yes

Continue with procurement planning.

### No

Document the approval framework before major procurement begins.

---

## Expected Output — Procurement Approval Structure Established

- [ ] Procurement threshold framework approved
- [ ] Purchase Order template available
- [ ] Category owners trained
- [ ] HQ approval embedded into Brand & Merchandise purchasing

---

# Stage 3 — Purchase & Ordering

> **Timing:** Throughout the operational year

## Step 4 — Conduct Planned Procurement

Reactive purchasing is expensive.

Procurement should follow a planned operational rhythm linked to inventory levels and programming.

---

## Procurement Planning Rhythm

### Weekly

Café & F&B:

- Complete stock review
- Compare against minimum stock levels
- Review upcoming events
- Raise Purchase Orders

---

### Event-Based

Programming:

- Review each event three to four weeks before delivery.
- Order event materials no later than five working days before the event.

---

### Monthly

Facilities and Administration:

- Review inventory
- Replenish below-minimum items

Technology needs should be assessed monthly but purchased only through approved procurement tiers.

---

### Quarterly

Brand & Merchandise:

- Review stock
- Submit HQ approval requests
- Order after approval

---

## Purchase Order Standards

Every Purchase Order must include:

- Item description
- Brand or specification
- Quantity
- Unit price
- Total value
- Delivery date
- Delivery address
- Budget code
- Approver

Incomplete Purchase Orders should be returned before approval.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the supplier already on the Approved Supplier Register?**

### Yes

Raise the Purchase Order.

### No

Complete supplier vetting before purchasing.

---

## Expected Output — Procurement Planning Active

- [ ] Weekly café procurement completed
- [ ] Event purchasing completed on schedule
- [ ] Monthly facilities review completed
- [ ] Standard Purchase Orders used for all purchases

---

## Step 5 — Apply Ethics and Brand Compliance

Every procurement decision reflects Palestine House values.

Two mandatory compliance checks apply.

---

## Supplier Ethics Review

Confirm:

- Legitimate business registration
- No political violence association
- No extremist associations
- No sanctioned entities
- Transparent ownership
- No reputational concerns

Any uncertainty must be referred to HQ.

---

## Brand Compliance Review

Applies to all products carrying:

- Palestine House name
- Logo
- Wordmark
- Brand identity

HQ approval must be received before production.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the supplier passed both ethics and brand compliance?**

### Yes

Proceed with procurement.

### No

Pause purchasing.

Refer to HQ.

---

## Expected Output — Ethics & Brand Compliance Applied

- [ ] Ethics review completed
- [ ] HQ referrals completed where required
- [ ] HQ brand approvals filed
- [ ] No unapproved branded merchandise produced

---

# Stage 4 — Delivery & Receiving

> **Timing:** Every delivery

## Step 6 — Manage Goods Receiving

Receiving goods is a critical operational control.

Never accept a delivery without verification.

---

## Goods Receiving Process

### On Arrival

- Retrieve the Purchase Order
- Verify supplier
- Reject unauthorised deliveries unless approved

---

### Count & Inspect

Verify:

- Quantities
- Product descriptions
- Expiry dates
- Product condition
- Packaging
- Model numbers
- Brand quality

---

### Record Discrepancies

If anything is:

- Missing
- Damaged
- Incorrect
- Expired

record it before signing.

Photograph damaged items.

---

### Complete the Goods Received Record

Record:

- Purchase Order
- Supplier
- Date
- Items received
- Discrepancies
- Receiver

---

### Notify Supplier

Report discrepancies within 24 hours.

Request:

- Replacement
- Credit
- Refund

Copy the Finance Lead.

---

### Update Inventory

Only update inventory after receiving has been completed.

Never update stock from Purchase Orders alone.

---

## Decision Point

**Does branded merchandise exactly match HQ-approved artwork?**

### Yes

Accept the delivery.

### No

Reject the delivery.

Notify both HQ and the supplier immediately.

---

## Expected Output — Goods Receiving Active

- [ ] Purchase Orders verified
- [ ] Deliveries inspected
- [ ] Goods Received Record completed
- [ ] Discrepancies documented
- [ ] Suppliers notified within 24 hours
- [ ] Inventory updated from verified records only

---

# Stage 5 — Stock Management

> **Timing:** Throughout the operational year

## Step 7 — Operate the Inventory System

An inventory system does not need to be complex.

It must simply be accurate and consistently maintained.

---

## Inventory System Requirements

The system must:

- Track stock levels
- Record storage locations
- Log stock movements
- Flag minimum stock alerts
- Support monthly reconciliation

---

> **Important**
>
> Physical stock counts remain mandatory.
>
> Conduct:
>
> - Monthly counts for Café and Events
> - Quarterly counts for all other categories

---

## Expected Output — Inventory System Active

- [ ] Inventory system established
- [ ] All six procurement categories included
- [ ] Stock movements recorded
- [ ] Monthly physical counts completed
- [ ] Reorder alerts active

---

## Step 8 — Establish Minimum Stock Thresholds

Minimum stock thresholds determine when purchasing begins—not when stock runs out.

---

## Threshold Calculation

Determine:

1. Average weekly usage
2. Supplier lead time
3. Safety buffer
4. Reorder quantity

---

## Critical Items

Maintain elevated minimum stock for:

- Coffee
- Sugar
- Core café items
- First aid supplies
- Safety equipment
- Core event materials

Critical items should never fall below one week's supply.

---

## Decision Point

**Has every significant inventory item been assigned a minimum threshold?**

### Yes

Activate reorder alerts.

### No

Prioritise:

1. Café
2. Events
3. Facilities
4. Administration

---

## Expected Output — Minimum Stock Thresholds Active

- [ ] Thresholds established
- [ ] Reorder quantities calculated
- [ ] Critical items protected
- [ ] Alerts activated
- [ ] Thresholds reviewed after three months

---

# Stage 6 — Controls & Reporting

> **Timing:** Monthly and quarterly

## Step 9 — Conduct Procurement Controls

Procurement controls connect:

Purchase Orders → Goods Received → Invoices → Budgets

Every transaction must complete this chain.

---

## Monthly Reconciliation

Complete:

1. Purchase Order review
2. Goods Received matching
3. Invoice matching
4. Budget reconciliation
5. Monthly Procurement Report

---

## Decision Point

**Can every invoice be matched to both a Purchase Order and a Goods Received Record?**

### Yes

Approve payment.

### No

Do not pay.

Investigate before authorisation.

Escalate unresolved issues.

---

## Expected Output — Monthly Procurement Reconciliation

- [ ] Purchase Orders reviewed
- [ ] Goods Received matched
- [ ] Invoices verified
- [ ] Budget reviewed
- [ ] Monthly Procurement Report completed

---

# Stage 7 — Review & Optimisation

> **Timing:** Annual review with quarterly supplier check-ins

## Step 10 — Conduct the Annual Procurement Review

Every supplier relationship must be reviewed annually.

Review:

- Performance
- Ethics
- Pricing
- Community impact
- Inventory thresholds
- Procurement processes

---

## Annual Review Agenda

### Supplier Performance

Rate each supplier:

- Retain
- Watch
- Replace

---

### Ethics Review

Repeat supplier ethics assessments annually.

Refer any new concerns to HQ.

---

### Value for Money

Review pricing.

Obtain comparison quotes where annual price increases exceed 10%.

---

### Community Procurement

Identify opportunities to support:

- Palestinian-owned businesses
- Diaspora-owned businesses

where quality and commercial terms remain appropriate.

---

### Inventory Threshold Review

Adjust reorder points using actual annual usage.

---

### Process Improvement

Review:

- Stockouts
- Delivery failures
- Supplier disputes
- Procurement delays
- Reporting failures

Implement improvements before the next operating year.

---

## Expected Output — Annual Procurement Review Complete

- [ ] Suppliers assessed
- [ ] Ethics reviews completed
- [ ] Pricing compared
- [ ] Community supplier opportunities assessed
- [ ] Inventory thresholds recalibrated
- [ ] Annual Procurement Report submitted to the Board
- [ ] Annual Procurement Report submitted to HQ
- [ ] Improvement actions agreed

---

# The Procurement Standard

Every procurement decision reflects Palestine House values.

The suppliers selected demonstrate who Palestine House trusts.

The ethics checks demonstrate what Palestine House stands for.

The Palestinian and diaspora businesses supported strengthen the community.

The controls enforced demonstrate accountability to:

- Guests
- Funders
- The Board
- HQ

The inventory maintained demonstrates the operational discipline required to deliver every Palestine House experience with reliability, dignity, and consistency.

Procurement is not a back-office activity.

It is a direct expression of Palestine House stewardship.

