# Membership, Community & Service

## Customer Service & Recovery Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Customer Service & Recovery** element defines how Palestine House consistently delivers a high-quality guest experience and responds effectively when service breakdowns occur.

Its purpose is to protect trust, maintain dignity, and ensure that every interaction reinforces the Palestine House cultural embassy promise.

Customer service is not treated as a support function but as a core operational system that directly influences:

- Reputation
- Guest retention
- Community growth
- Long-term trust

This element ensures that service delivery is:

- Structured
- Consistent
- Repeatable
- Aligned with Palestine House brand standards

It also establishes a disciplined service recovery approach that transforms moments of failure into opportunities to strengthen guest relationships.

Without this system, service delivery becomes inconsistent, and small operational issues can quickly escalate into significant reputational risk.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Customer Service & Recovery element is used continuously across all guest-facing operations at every Palestine House.

### Daily Operations

This element applies throughout normal day-to-day operations, including:

- Guest visits
- Events
- Membership interactions
- Post-event engagement
- Community activities

### Guest Touchpoints

It is applied at every stage of the guest journey, including:

- Welcome and entry
- Ticketing
- Seating
- Programming participation
- General enquiries
- Member services
- Guest departures
- Follow-up communications

### Service Recovery

This element becomes particularly important whenever service issues occur, including:

- Delays
- Miscommunication
- Guest dissatisfaction
- Operational disruptions
- Booking issues
- Service failures
- Unexpected incidents

### Training and Development

Customer Service & Recovery is embedded into:

- Staff onboarding
- Customer service training
- Escalation protocol training
- Service recovery training
- Ongoing coaching

This ensures every team member understands:

- Service expectations
- Escalation procedures
- Recovery methods
- Guest communication standards

### Operational Improvement

The element is also integrated into:

- Weekly operational reviews
- Incident tracking
- Complaint analysis
- Service performance reviews
- Continuous improvement activities

Recurring issues should be identified, analysed, and addressed systematically.

Customer Service & Recovery is an active operational system that must be consistently applied every day—not only during visible service failures.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Responsibility for Customer Service & Recovery is distributed across **LOCAL** operations with clearly defined ownership, authority, and escalation pathways.

### Front-of-House Responsibilities

Front-of-house staff are the primary operators of the service system and are responsible for:

- Delivering excellent guest service
- Identifying issues early
- Resolving routine service concerns in real time
- Maintaining professionalism
- Protecting the guest experience

### Supervisor and Shift Lead Responsibilities

Supervisors and Shift Leads are responsible for:

- Managing escalated situations
- Supporting front-of-house staff
- Resolving complex service issues
- Making decisions within delegated authority
- Ensuring consistent service recovery

### General Manager / Head of Operations Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is accountable for the overall Customer Service & Recovery system, including:

- Staff training
- Performance monitoring
- Service quality
- Operational consistency
- Continuous improvement
- System oversight

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing and protecting the overall service framework by defining:

- Minimum customer service standards
- Brand expectations
- Service recovery principles
- Escalation frameworks
- Network-wide operational consistency

GLOBAL also assumes responsibility for high-risk incidents involving:

- Safety
- Ethics
- Reputation
- Network-wide brand protection

Any incident exceeding LOCAL authority must be escalated to GLOBAL immediately.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Customer Service & Recovery element delivers clear and measurable operational outcomes.

### Guest Experience Outcomes

Guests consistently feel:

- Welcomed
- Respected
- Supported
- Listened to
- Valued

This standard should be maintained regardless of the circumstances.

### Service Recovery Outcomes

Effective recovery should:

- Resolve issues quickly
- Reduce unnecessary escalation
- Protect the overall guest experience
- Restore guest confidence
- Reinforce trust

Recovery should strengthen relationships rather than weaken them.

### Community Outcomes

Strong customer service should contribute to:

- Higher guest retention
- Increased repeat visits
- Stronger member loyalty
- Positive word-of-mouth
- Greater community trust

### Operational Outcomes

The system should create a structured feedback loop where:

- Complaints are captured
- Incidents are logged
- Root causes are identified
- Improvements are implemented
- Lessons are shared across the team

Rather than treating complaints as isolated events, they become valuable inputs for operational improvement.

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, the Customer Service & Recovery system should produce:

- More resilient operations
- Better-prepared teams
- Greater confidence under pressure
- Consistent service quality
- Stronger operational discipline

Ultimately, excellent service reinforces Palestine House as a trusted, reliable, and professionally operated cultural institution.

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Customer Service & Recovery element covers the complete lifecycle of guest interaction, from the initial welcome through post-visit follow-up, including all service recovery activities required when issues occur.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Guest service standards
- Guest interactions
- Complaint handling
- Escalation rules
- Service recovery actions
- Incident logging
- Post-incident follow-up
- Continuous service improvement

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Detailed Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)
- Customer service scripts
- Authority matrices
- Incident response procedures
- Operational work instructions

These supporting documents must be developed separately to operationalize specific scenarios.

### Recovery Boundaries

Service recovery must operate within clearly defined:

- Financial limits
- Operational authority
- Brand standards
- Ethical expectations

Unlimited discretion is not permitted, as inconsistent recovery actions create operational risk and unfair guest experiences.

### Governance and Compliance

This element is designed to resolve **service-related issues only**.

It does **not** override:

- Governance requirements
- Legal obligations
- Safety procedures
- Compliance standards
- Ethical policies

Any issue involving:

- Compliance
- Misconduct
- Safety
- Legal matters
- Significant reputational risk

must follow formal escalation procedures and cannot be resolved informally through standard customer service recovery.

### Local Adaptation

LOCAL teams are encouraged to adapt their communication style, hospitality, and delivery to suit their local community.

However, all service delivery and recovery actions must remain fully aligned with:

- GLOBAL service standards
- Palestine House brand expectations
- Network-wide non-negotiables
- The cultural embassy mission of Palestine House$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Customer Service & Recovery

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Audience:** All Guest-Facing Teams  
> **Standard:** Level 1 (Mandatory)

---

# Purpose of This Guide

This guide explains exactly how to respond when a guest, member, or community partner experiences a problem or expresses dissatisfaction with Palestine House.

Every interaction reflects our values.

How we recover from mistakes is just as important as how we prevent them.

Follow every step in sequence.

> **Recovery done well transforms disappointed guests into loyal supporters.**

---

# Who This Guide Is For

This guide applies to all staff who interact with:

- Guests
- Members
- Event attendees
- Community partners

Including:

- Front-of-House Team
- Event Coordinators
- Café Staff
- Membership Administrators
- House Managers

> **House Managers are responsible for ensuring every team member is trained on this guide before opening day.**

---

# Step 1 — Acknowledge the Issue Immediately

When someone raises a concern:

- In person
- By phone
- By email
- By message
- In writing

Stop what you are doing and give them your full attention.

## Do

- Listen actively.
- Stay calm.
- Show genuine concern.

## Do Not

- Argue
- Explain
- Justify
- Become defensive

Your first responsibility is to ensure the guest feels heard.

### Suggested Responses

> "Thank you for telling us. I want to make sure we get this right."

> "I'm going to look into this for you personally."

If the discussion is taking place publicly, invite the guest to continue in a quieter private space.

> **Protecting a guest's dignity is non-negotiable.**

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Guest acknowledged within **2 minutes**
- [ ] Conversation moved to a private space if appropriate
- [ ] Guest feels heard
- [ ] No defensive behaviour from staff

---

# Step 2 — Listen and Understand the Full Problem

Begin with one clear question.

> **"Can you tell me exactly what happened and when?"**

Allow the guest to speak without interruption.

Take concise notes.

When they finish, summarise your understanding.

Example:

> "So if I understand correctly, [summary]. Is that right?"

Do not assume the desired outcome.

Ask directly:

> **"What would a good outcome look like for you?"**

> **Note**
>
> If the guest is distressed:
>
> - Give them time.
> - Offer water.
> - Never rush the conversation.

Patience is part of the Palestine House service standard.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Complete understanding of the issue
- [ ] Desired outcome identified
- [ ] Notes include:
  - Date
  - Time
  - Issue
  - Requested resolution

---

# Step 3 — Categorise the Complaint

Identify the complaint category before taking action.

| Category | Examples | Primary Owner |
|-----------|----------|---------------|
| Service Quality | Poor service, rude staff, delays | House Manager |
| Facility Issue | Cleanliness, maintenance, accessibility | House Manager + Operations |
| Membership / Billing | Incorrect charges, missing benefits | Membership Administrator |
| Programming / Events | Wrong information, booking issues, cancellations | Events Lead |
| Safety / Safeguarding | Unsafe behaviour, safeguarding concerns | **Immediate HQ Escalation** |
| Brand / Reputation | Political issues, media, public complaints | **Immediate HQ Escalation** |

---

## Decision Point

**Is this a Safety, Safeguarding, or Brand/Reputation issue?**

### Yes

Stop immediately.

Escalate to:

- House Manager
- HQ

Proceed directly to **Step 9**.

### No

Continue to Step 4.

---

# Step 4 — Resolve What You Can Immediately

If the issue is within your authority:

Resolve it immediately.

Do not:

- Delay unnecessarily
- Pass the guest to another staff member if you can solve the issue yourself

## Examples

| Situation | Immediate Resolution |
|-----------|----------------------|
| Wrong seating | Move the guest immediately |
| Incorrect café order | Replace without charge |
| Incorrect event information | Correct the information immediately |
| Missing membership benefit | Apply the benefit and confirm in writing |

Always conclude with a sincere apology.

Example:

> "I'm sorry this happened. Here's what I've done to fix it."

---

## Decision Point

**Can you resolve this issue with your current authority?**

### Yes

- Resolve immediately.
- Apologise.
- Log the issue.
- Proceed to Step 7.

### No

Continue to Step 5.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Resolution completed or next steps communicated
- [ ] Guest receives a sincere apology
- [ ] Guest is never left waiting without an update

---

# Step 5 — Escalate to the House Manager

If the issue exceeds your authority:

Escalate immediately.

Tell the guest:

> "I want to make sure this is handled properly. I'm going to involve our House Manager right away."

Before introducing the House Manager:

- Brief them privately.
- Provide your notes.

The guest should **never** have to repeat the entire story.

---

## Required Response Times

| Complaint Type | Response Time |
|---------------|---------------|
| In-person | Within **15 minutes** |
| Written / Digital | Within **24 hours** |
| Membership / Billing | Within **48 hours** |

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Guest introduced to House Manager
- [ ] Notes handed over
- [ ] Clear timeframe communicated

---

# Step 6 — Offer a Recovery Gesture

Once the issue has been resolved—or is actively being resolved—consider a recovery gesture.

A recovery gesture demonstrates care.

It is **not** compensation for silence.

## Recovery Matrix

| Situation | Recovery Gesture | Approval |
|-----------|-----------------|----------|
| Minor service issue | Complimentary beverage or discount | Front-of-House |
| Event inconvenience | Complimentary future ticket or upgrade | House Manager |
| Significant experience failure | One-month membership extension or refund | House Manager |
| Serious long-term impact | Membership upgrade or Director apology | HQ Approval |

> **Important**
>
> Recovery gestures come **after** acknowledgement and resolution—not instead of them.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Appropriate gesture offered
- [ ] Approval obtained
- [ ] Recovery logged

---

# Step 7 — Log the Complaint

Every complaint must be logged before the end of the working day.

No exceptions.

## Required Information

- Date
- Time
- Guest name *(if provided)*
- Complaint category
- Issue summary
- Resolution
- Recovery gesture
- Status
- Staff member

Complaint logs are reviewed monthly and reported to HQ.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Complaint logged the same day
- [ ] All required fields completed
- [ ] Available for House Manager review

---

# Step 8 — Follow Up With the Guest

For escalated complaints:

House Manager follows up within **48 hours**.

Confirm:

- Resolution satisfaction
- Recovery received
- No outstanding concerns

Example:

> Dear [Name],
>
> I'm following up regarding the concern you raised on [Date]. I wanted to ensure everything has been resolved to your satisfaction. Please contact me directly if there is anything further we can assist with.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the guest satisfied after follow-up?**

### Yes

Mark complaint:

**Resolved**

### No

Escalate immediately to HQ.

Proceed to Step 9.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Follow-up completed within 48 hours
- [ ] Satisfaction confirmed or escalation initiated
- [ ] Complaint record updated

---

# Step 9 — Escalate to HQ

Certain situations require immediate HQ involvement.

Do not manage these locally.

---

## Immediate HQ Escalation Triggers

- Safety incident
- Safeguarding issue
- Minor involved
- Political content
- Discrimination
- Harassment
- Legal threat
- Formal written complaint
- Media enquiry
- Public social media complaint
- Guest remains dissatisfied after two local resolution attempts

---

## HQ Email Must Include

- Complaint log
- Incident summary
- Actions taken
- Current status
- Required HQ decision
- Any urgent deadlines

---

> **HQ Standards**
>
> - Acknowledge within **4 hours**
> - Provide direction within **24 hours**
>
> If no acknowledgement is received:
>
> Send a second email marked **URGENT**.

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] HQ notified
- [ ] Complaint marked **Escalated to HQ**
- [ ] HQ acknowledgement received

---

# Step 10 — Review and Prevent Recurrence

At the end of every month, the House Manager reviews all complaints.

For every recurring issue ask:

1. What caused it?
2. What change prevents it?
3. Who owns the corrective action?

---

## Pattern Rule

If the same issue occurs **three or more times within one month**:

- Escalate to HQ
- Submit a corrective action plan within **14 days**

Include complaint analysis in the monthly HQ report under:

**Customer Service & Recovery**

Include:

- Total complaints
- Complaint categories
- Resolution rate
- Open cases
- Trends
- Corrective actions

---

### Expected Output

- [ ] Monthly review completed before the 5th
- [ ] Recurring issues identified
- [ ] Corrective actions assigned
- [ ] HQ report submitted

---

# Quick Reference — Customer Service & Recovery Flow

| Step | Action | Owner | Timeframe |
|------:|--------|-------|-----------|
| 1 | Acknowledge issue | Any Staff | Within 2 minutes |
| 2 | Listen and understand | Any Staff | Immediately |
| 3 | Categorise complaint | Any Staff | Immediately |
| 4 | Resolve if possible | Any Staff | Immediately |
| 5 | Escalate to House Manager | House Manager | 15 min (in-person) / 24 hrs (written) |
| 6 | Offer recovery gesture | House Manager | Same interaction |
| 7 | Log complaint | Staff / House Manager | End of working day |
| 8 | Follow up | House Manager | Within 48 hours |
| 9 | Escalate to HQ | House Manager | Immediately when triggered |
| 10 | Monthly review | House Manager | By the 5th of the following month |

---

# When to Contact HQ

## Contact HQ Immediately

- Safety or safeguarding incidents
- Political content complaints
- Legal threats
- Media enquiries
- Public complaints
- Guest unresolved after two attempts

---

## Include in the Monthly HQ Report

- Total complaints
- Complaint categories
- Resolution rate
- Average resolution time
- Open complaints
- Recovery gestures
- Recurring issues
- Corrective actions

---

# A Note on Standards

Palestine House exists to serve the Palestinian community and all cultural guests with dignity, care, and excellence.

Every guest deserves to feel welcome.

Every guest who experiences a problem deserves to leave knowing that their concern was taken seriously.

Customer service recovery is more than resolving complaints.

It is how Palestine House demonstrates its values when they matter most.

---

**Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.**$md$, $md$# What to Watch Out For

## Customer Service & Recovery

**Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
*15 Risk Cards*

---

## Purpose

This document identifies the most common operational failures in **Customer Service & Recovery**, helping House Managers and staff recognise risks before they become larger operational, reputational, or compliance issues.

Each risk card describes:

- What the issue looks like
- Why it matters
- What action to take
- When escalation is mandatory

> **Important**
>
> Read each card in full—not just the title.
>
> The **"Escalate if"** section defines when the issue moves beyond local authority and must be referred to the House Manager or HQ.

---

# Risk Card Categories

- **Common Mistakes**
- **Risks**
- **Red Flags**
- **Compliance Issues**
- **Escalation Triggers**

---

# Section 1 — Common Mistakes

Operational errors that gradually damage guest trust and satisfaction.

---

## 01. Arguing or Defending Before Listening

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

A staff member:

- Interrupts the guest
- Explains why the issue wasn't their fault
- Quotes policy before fully hearing the concern

The interaction feels defensive instead of supportive.

### Why it matters

Guests who feel dismissed are far more likely to escalate complaints.

Within Palestine House, where community trust is fundamental, a single poor interaction can damage the House's reputation well beyond the original complaint.

### What to do

Train every staff member to follow the acknowledgement sequence:

1. Stop
2. Listen completely
3. Summarise the concern
4. Respond appropriately

Role-play this process during every customer service training session.

House Managers should immediately coach any defensive behaviour observed.

### Escalate if

A guest:

- Walks away during the conversation
- Posts publicly on social media
- Contacts HQ directly regarding staff behaviour

---

## 02. Resolving a Complaint Without Logging It

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Staff resolve an issue immediately—for example:

- Replacing an incorrect order
- Offering an apology
- Moving a guest

—but never record the incident because it appeared minor.

### Why it matters

Unlogged complaints:

- Hide recurring problems
- Prevent trend analysis
- Distort monthly reporting
- Leave no record if the complaint resurfaces

### What to do

Complaint logging must be mandatory regardless of severity.

Operational requirements:

- Include complaint logging in the daily closing checklist.
- House Manager reviews the complaint log every day.
- Cross-check recorded incidents against shift observations.

### Escalate if

- A guest raises the same complaint multiple times with no previous record.
- HQ receives a complaint that has no corresponding local log entry.

---

## 03. Offering a Recovery Gesture Before Giving a Genuine Apology

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Staff immediately offer:

- Discounts
- Free items
- Upgrades

before acknowledging the guest's experience.

### Why it matters

Recovery gestures offered too early often appear transactional and may be interpreted as an attempt to buy silence rather than genuinely resolve the issue.

### What to do

Train staff to always follow this sequence:

1. Acknowledge
2. Listen
3. Understand
4. Apologise sincerely
5. Resolve or escalate
6. Offer an appropriate recovery gesture

> Recovery gestures are the **final step**, never the first.

### Escalate if

- The guest rejects the recovery gesture.
- The guest accepts the gesture but continues sharing negative experiences publicly.

---

## 04. Passing a Guest Between Staff Without a Proper Handover

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

A guest is transferred between staff members without:

- A proper introduction
- Complaint notes
- A verbal briefing

The guest must repeat the same story multiple times.

### Why it matters

Repeated explanations create frustration and suggest poor internal organisation.

This directly contradicts Palestine House's commitment to dignity and hospitality.

### What to do

Whenever transferring responsibility:

- Personally introduce the next staff member.
- Brief them before the transfer.
- Ensure the guest never has to repeat their complaint.

House Managers should remain available within **15 minutes** for in-person escalations.

### Escalate if

- The guest has already spoken to more than two staff members.
- The guest says:

> "I've already explained this twice."

---

# Section 2 — Risks

Structural or operational weaknesses that reduce service quality over time.

---

## 05. Untrained Staff Handling Complex or Sensitive Complaints

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

A junior staff member attempts to resolve complaints involving:

- Discrimination
- Safety concerns
- Political content

without escalation.

### Why it matters

Untrained responses may:

- Create legal exposure
- Damage Palestine House's reputation
- Misrepresent official policy
- Affect the wider network

### What to do

Ensure every staff member:

- Completes training before guest-facing duties.
- Receives quarterly refresher training.

Provide a one-page reference card covering:

- What staff can resolve
- When to involve the House Manager
- When HQ involvement is mandatory

Display the reference in the staff area.

### Escalate if

An untrained staff member:

- Responds to political complaints
- Makes public statements
- Represents Palestine House policy without approval

---

## 06. Recovery Gestures Used Inconsistently

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

Different staff members offer different compensation for similar situations.

Examples include:

- Free coffee
- Event tickets
- No gesture at all

Some gestures are offered without required approval.

### Why it matters

Inconsistency:

- Creates unfairness
- Increases complaints
- Creates financial risk
- Establishes unrealistic expectations

### What to do

Implement the Recovery Gesture Authority Matrix.

House Managers should:

- Review it during training.
- Audit recovery gestures monthly.
- Investigate any unauthorised gestures.

### Escalate if

- Guests compare inconsistent recovery gestures.
- Monthly recovery costs exceed approved limits.

---

## 07. Delayed Responses to Written or Digital Complaints

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

Complaints submitted through:

- Email
- Social media
- Contact forms

remain unanswered for multiple days.

### Why it matters

Guests expect acknowledgement within **24 hours**.

Delayed responses often become public complaints.

### What to do

Assign daily responsibility for monitoring:

- Email
- Social media
- Contact forms

House Managers should verify each day that every complaint has received an initial response.

### Escalate if

- Guests publicly complain about being ignored.
- Guests forward unanswered complaints directly to HQ.

---

# Section 3 — Red Flags

Serious situations requiring immediate escalation.

---

## 08. Guest Mentions Legal Action or the Media

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

The guest mentions:

- Lawyers
- Consumer protection agencies
- Journalists
- Media coverage
- Formal legal action

### Why it matters

Legal and media situations require HQ oversight.

Local staff are not authorised to respond independently.

### What to do

Politely pause the discussion and say:

> "I want to make sure this is handled at the right level. I am going to involve our senior leadership immediately."

Then:

- Notify the House Manager.
- Escalate to HQ the same day.

### Escalate if

Any written legal correspondence is received.

HQ must review before any written response is issued.

---

## 09. Complaint Involves a Minor or Safeguarding Concern

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

The complaint involves:

- Anyone under 18
- Vulnerable adults
- Safety concerns
- Welfare concerns

### Why it matters

Safeguarding concerns carry immediate legal and ethical obligations.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Notify the House Manager.
- Notify HQ on the same day.
- Do not attempt local resolution.

Treat every safeguarding concern as a **Priority 1 Incident**.

### Escalate if

Any complaint involves:

- A minor
- A vulnerable adult
- Potentially inappropriate conduct

There is **no minimum threshold** for safeguarding escalation.

---

## 10. Complaints About Political Content

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Guests describe events, speakers, displays, or staff comments as:

- Political
- Partisan
- Biased
- One-sided

### Why it matters

Palestine House's non-political positioning is a core licensing principle.

Any perceived breach affects the entire network.

### What to do

The House Manager should **not** attempt to determine whether the complaint is valid.

Immediately:

- Gather full details.
- Escalate everything to HQ.
- Await instructions.

### Escalate if

The complaint includes terms such as:

- Political
- Partisan
- Biased
- One-sided

HQ leads all communications.

---

# Section 4 — Compliance Issues

Mandatory operational requirements.

---

## 11. No Complaint Log or Incomplete Logging

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Complaint records are:

- Missing
- Incomplete
- Stored informally
- Unavailable for review

### Why it matters

Complaint logging is a core Palestine House compliance requirement.

Without it:

- HQ cannot monitor quality.
- Reporting obligations cannot be met.
- Accountability cannot be demonstrated.

### What to do

House Managers should:

- Review logs daily.
- Audit logs monthly.
- Reconstruct missing entries where possible, clearly identifying reconstructed information.

### Escalate if

- HQ requests complaint records that cannot be produced.
- Monthly reporting cannot be completed because records are missing.

---

## 12. HQ-Level Complaints Managed Locally

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Complaints involving:

- Safety
- Political content
- Legal threats
- Media
- Repeated unresolved issues

are handled locally without HQ involvement.

### Why it matters

Failure to escalate qualifying complaints breaches Palestine House operating standards and licensing requirements.

### What to do

Review escalation triggers during every quarterly training.

Display the escalation checklist within the staff office.

If escalation is missed, notify HQ immediately—even after the fact.

### Escalate if

HQ learns about a qualifying complaint from:

- Guests
- Media
- Community members

before hearing from the House.

This constitutes a serious compliance failure.

---

# Section 5 — Escalation Triggers

Situations where local authority is no longer sufficient.

---

## 13. Guest Remains Unsatisfied After Two Resolution Attempts

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

The House Manager has made two documented attempts to resolve the issue, but the guest remains dissatisfied.

### Why it matters

Further local attempts usually worsen the situation.

### What to do

Compile:

- Complaint history
- Resolution attempts
- Supporting documentation

Submit everything to HQ.

Do **not** attempt a third independent resolution.

### Escalate if

- The guest explicitly rejects the proposed resolution.
- The guest later contacts HQ or makes a public statement.

---

## 14. Complaint Appears Publicly Online

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

A guest publishes a complaint on:

- Social media
- Review platforms
- Community forums

before the issue has been resolved.

### Why it matters

Public complaints affect:

- Community trust
- Membership growth
- Donor confidence
- Media perception

### What to do

Immediately:

- Capture screenshots.
- Preserve the URL.
- Notify HQ within **2 hours**.

While awaiting instructions, send only this private message:

> "We are aware of your concern and are addressing it. Our team will be in touch shortly."

Do **not** comment publicly.

### Escalate if

The post:

- Receives more than 10 engagements
- Is shared by community groups
- Is picked up by journalists

HQ must lead all communications.

---

## 15. Multiple Complaints of the Same Type

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

Three or more complaints of the same category occur within one month.

Examples:

- Service quality
- Membership billing
- Event information

### Why it matters

Repeated complaints indicate systemic operational failure rather than isolated incidents.

### What to do

Include the issue in the monthly HQ report.

Complete:

- Root cause analysis
- Corrective action plan
- Responsible owner
- Completion date

Submit the plan to HQ within **14 days**.

### Escalate if

The same complaint category appears in **three consecutive monthly reports** without an approved corrective action plan.

HQ will initiate a formal operational review.

---

# A Note for House Managers

This document is designed to **prevent problems before they occur**, not simply respond after they happen.

Use it:

- During onboarding
- During customer service training
- During monthly complaint reviews
- As part of continuous operational improvement

The risks described here represent the most common failure points observed across cultural venues worldwide.

> **When in doubt, escalate.**
>
> Palestine House HQ would rather receive an unnecessary escalation than first learn about a qualifying incident through a public complaint or third party.

---

