## Business Model & Revenue Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Business Model & Revenue** element defines how Palestine House sustains its operations financially while maintaining full alignment with its cultural mission, values, and governance standards.

Its purpose is to ensure that all revenue-generating activities are structured, diversified, and carefully managed to support long-term financial sustainability while reducing dependency on any single source of income.

This element establishes a disciplined framework for:

- Revenue strategy
- Revenue diversification
- Pricing frameworks
- Financial sustainability
- Commercial governance
- Resource reinvestment

Revenue is never pursued at the expense of the Palestine House mission or brand integrity. Instead, every income-generating activity must strengthen the organization while supporting its cultural purpose.

This element ensures that financial success contributes directly to:

- Sustainable operations
- High-quality programming
- Community impact
- Organizational growth
- Long-term resilience

Ultimately, it provides the financial foundation required for Palestine House to grow responsibly while remaining true to its mission.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Business Model & Revenue element is applied continuously across all strategic and operational activities within Palestine House.

### Revenue Activities

This element supports all approved revenue-generating activities, including:

- Membership programs
- Ticketed events
- Retail operations
- Food & Beverage services
- Sponsorships
- Partnerships
- Venue hire (where applicable)
- Approved commercial initiatives

Each revenue stream must operate within approved financial and governance frameworks.

### Strategic Planning

During planning, this element governs:

- Revenue strategy
- Business model development
- Revenue diversification
- Pricing strategies
- Financial forecasting
- Revenue targets
- Growth planning

Financial planning should support both operational sustainability and long-term organizational objectives.

### Daily Operations

During day-to-day operations, this element governs:

- Revenue collection
- Membership income
- Ticket sales
- Retail revenue
- Food & Beverage income
- Partnership revenue
- Financial monitoring

Revenue generation should remain fully integrated into operational decision-making.

### Partnership Development

During partnership negotiations, this element ensures:

- Financial agreements align with organizational objectives
- Revenue opportunities support operational capacity
- Commercial relationships protect the Palestine House brand
- Partnerships remain consistent with governance standards

### Financial Monitoring

Ongoing financial management includes:

- Revenue tracking
- Performance reporting
- Target monitoring
- Financial analysis
- Revenue forecasting
- Strategy refinement

Regular performance reviews ensure financial sustainability remains aligned with operational priorities.

---

## Who Is Responsible

The Business Model & Revenue element is governed by **GLOBAL** and executed by **LOCAL** teams with clearly defined accountability.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing the overall financial framework for the Palestine House network.

Responsibilities include:

- Business model design
- Approved revenue streams
- Pricing frameworks
- Financial policies
- Commercial governance
- Network-wide financial standards

GLOBAL also reviews and approves:

- High-value partnerships
- Strategic commercial agreements
- Major revenue initiatives
- Financial performance across locations
- Significant pricing changes

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is responsible for implementing approved revenue strategies within their location.

Responsibilities include:

- Revenue planning
- Income generation
- Budget management
- Financial performance
- Operational alignment
- Achievement of financial targets

Revenue activities should always support both operational priorities and the Palestine House mission.

### Finance and Operational Team Responsibilities

Finance and operational staff are responsible for:

- Revenue tracking
- Transaction management
- Financial reporting
- Performance monitoring
- Revenue reconciliation
- Record maintenance

Accurate financial data supports informed operational and strategic decision-making.

### Governance Requirements

Any deviation from approved:

- Revenue models
- Pricing frameworks
- Commercial policies
- Financial agreements

must be escalated to **GLOBAL** for review and approval before implementation.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Business Model & Revenue element delivers clear and measurable organizational outcomes.

### Financial Outcomes

Palestine House benefits from:

- Stable revenue
- Predictable cash flow
- Diversified income sources
- Improved financial resilience
- Stronger long-term sustainability

A diversified business model reduces reliance on any single revenue stream.

### Operational Outcomes

Structured financial management enables:

- Reliable operational funding
- Better resource allocation
- Improved planning
- Consistent program delivery
- Reduced operational disruption

### Strategic Outcomes

Effective revenue management supports:

- Sustainable organizational growth
- Better financial decision-making
- Increased investment capacity
- Stronger long-term planning
- Greater organizational resilience

### Partnership Outcomes

Clear financial frameworks strengthen:

- Partner confidence
- Commercial transparency
- Sponsorship opportunities
- Long-term collaborations
- Financial accountability

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, the Business Model & Revenue framework enables Palestine House to:

- Expand sustainably
- Maintain operational excellence
- Strengthen financial independence
- Increase community impact
- Grow without compromising quality, governance, or mission alignment

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Business Model & Revenue element governs the design, implementation, and management of all approved revenue-generating activities across Palestine House.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Membership revenue
- Event revenue
- Retail income
- Food & Beverage revenue
- Sponsorship revenue
- Partnership income
- Pricing strategies
- Revenue planning
- Financial performance monitoring
- Revenue reporting

It establishes how income should be generated, managed, and reinvested to support the long-term sustainability of Palestine House.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Financial management systems
- Accounting practices
- Legal agreements
- Financial auditing
- Tax compliance
- Corporate finance processes

These supporting systems must be managed separately in accordance with applicable legal and financial requirements.

### Operational Boundaries

Revenue activities that conflict with the Palestine House mission, brand values, or governance standards are not permitted.

All revenue-generating activities must comply with approved:

- Business models
- Pricing frameworks
- Financial policies
- Governance requirements
- Commercial standards

Effective Business Model & Revenue management depends upon:

- Accurate financial tracking
- Disciplined execution
- Revenue diversification
- Operational alignment
- Strong financial governance

### Risk and Escalation

The Business Model & Revenue element cannot compensate for:

- Weak programming
- Poor audience engagement
- Ineffective commercial strategy
- Weak financial controls
- Operational capacity limitations

Any revenue activity that introduces:

- Financial risk
- Legal risk
- Reputational risk
- Governance concerns
- Significant commercial risk

must be escalated through the appropriate **GLOBAL** governance channels for review and approval before implementation.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Business Model & Revenue

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Framework:** Business Model & Revenue Lifecycle

---

# Business Model & Revenue Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Area |
|------:|------------------|
| **1** | Business Model Design |
| **2** | Revenue Stream Activation |
| **3** | Pricing & Positioning |
| **4** | Financial Planning |
| **5** | Performance Monitoring |
| **6** | Review & Optimisation |

---

# Purpose of This Guide

This guide explains how to build, activate, and manage a financially sustainable business model for your Palestine House location.

A successful business model is not simply about generating income.

Its purpose is to ensure that Palestine House can:

- Sustain its mission
- Serve its community
- Meet its financial obligations
- Remain resilient without relying on a single source of income

Use this guide:

- During initial setup
- When introducing new revenue streams
- During pricing reviews
- When preparing the annual financial plan
- When responding to financial underperformance

---

# Who This Guide Is For

This guide is intended for:

- House Manager
- Finance / Operations Lead
- Board members responsible for financial sustainability

Revenue decisions must never be made independently at the local level.

The following always require **written HQ approval** before implementation:

- New revenue streams
- Pricing structures
- Commercial arrangements
- Sponsorship agreements

HQ defines the Business Model Framework.

Each Palestine House location adapts the approved framework to its local context while reporting performance transparently.

---

# Step 1 — Understand the Palestine House Business Model Framework

Palestine House is a mission-led cultural institution—not a conventional commercial business.

Income exists to support the mission rather than replace it.

---

## Core Business Model Principles

### Mission First

Every revenue activity must support:

- Cultural objectives
- Community benefit
- Non-political positioning

---

### Diversification

No individual revenue stream should contribute more than **60%** of total annual income.

---

### Transparency

All financial activity must be reported to HQ according to the approved reporting schedule.

---

### HQ Alignment

The following require written HQ approval before implementation:

- Revenue streams
- Pricing changes
- Commercial agreements

---

## Approved Revenue Stream Categories

There are five approved revenue stream categories.

Every House Manager should understand each before activating any of them.

---

> **Important**
>
> Any commercial activity outside the approved Business Model Framework—including sponsorships, partnerships or revenue activities not expressly approved—constitutes a breach of the Palestine House License Agreement.
>
> New revenue proposals must be submitted to HQ before implementation.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] House Manager understands all five revenue streams
- [ ] Finance Lead understands the Business Model Framework
- [ ] Framework document reviewed by the team
- [ ] HQ business model contact documented

---

# Step 2 — Map Revenue Streams to Your Local Context

Not every Palestine House location should activate every revenue stream immediately.

Local conditions determine the appropriate rollout.

Consider:

- Community size
- Venue capacity
- Staffing
- Licensing
- Regulatory requirements
- Local partnerships

---

## Approved Revenue Streams

### Event & Programme Income

Income generated through:

- Ticket sales
- Workshops
- Screenings
- Registration fees
- Event partnerships

---

### Membership & Community Income

Recurring income through membership programmes.

Examples include:

- Community Membership
- Supporter Membership
- Patron Membership

Membership structures require HQ approval.

---

### Café & Hospitality Income

Revenue generated through:

- Café operations
- Catering
- Food service
- Hospitality
- Venue hire with catering

Requires:

- Licensing
- Food safety compliance
- HQ operational approval

---

### Brand & Merchandise Income

Revenue from:

- Palestine House merchandise
- Cultural products
- Publications

All merchandise requires HQ approval.

---

### Grants & Philanthropic Income

Funding from:

- Foundations
- Arts councils
- Cultural institutions
- Individual donors

Every application requires HQ review.

Funding from politically affiliated organisations is prohibited.

---

## Decision Point

**Have appropriate revenue streams been identified for the first 12 months?**

### Yes

Prepare the Revenue Stream Activation Plan.

Submit to HQ.

### No

Complete the local context assessment before activation.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Revenue mapping completed
- [ ] Local capacity assessed
- [ ] Activation plan documented
- [ ] Initial rollout limited to appropriate streams
- [ ] HQ acknowledgement received

---

# Step 3 — Set Pricing Within the HQ Pricing Framework

Pricing is both:

- A commercial decision
- A community access decision

Prices should balance:

- Accessibility
- Sustainability
- Brand positioning

---

## Pricing Principles

### Community Access

Provide:

- Concessions
- Tiered pricing
- Community access allocations

---

### Quality Signal

Pricing should reinforce the Cultural Embassy standard.

Flagship events should never be priced below the approved minimum threshold.

---

### Cost Recovery

Pricing should recover direct costs.

Loss-making programmes require HQ approval.

---

### Consistency

Similar activities should maintain consistent pricing.

Avoid ad hoc pricing decisions.

---

Before publishing pricing:

1. Complete the Pricing Schedule.
2. Submit to HQ.
3. Receive written approval.

---

## Decision Point

**Has HQ approved the Pricing Schedule?**

### Yes

Pricing may be published.

### No

Do not publish or sell until written approval is received.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Pricing Schedule completed
- [ ] Community access rates established
- [ ] HQ approval received
- [ ] Annual pricing review scheduled

---

# Step 4 — Build the Annual Financial Plan

Every Palestine House location operates against an annual financial plan approved by HQ.

The plan should represent realistic projections—not aspirations.

---

## Required Components

### Income Projections

Forecast revenue by stream.

Include assumptions such as:

- Event numbers
- Attendance
- Membership growth
- Café revenue

---

### Expenditure Budget

Include:

- Fixed costs
- Variable costs
- Operating surplus or deficit explanation

---

### Diversification Review

Demonstrate that no revenue stream exceeds **60%** of total projected income.

Include mitigation plans if required.

---

### HQ Financial Obligations

Budget for:

- Licensing fees
- HQ contributions
- Shared costs

---

### Cash Reserves

Maintain a minimum reserve equal to **three months** of fixed operating costs.

---

## Decision Point

**Has HQ approved the Annual Financial Plan?**

### Yes

Operate according to the approved plan.

### No

Do not begin the financial year without approval.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Financial plan submitted
- [ ] Revenue assumptions documented
- [ ] Diversification confirmed
- [ ] HQ obligations budgeted
- [ ] Minimum cash reserve included

---

# Step 5 — Activate Revenue Streams

Planning alone does not create income.

Every revenue stream requires operational readiness.

---

## Event & Programme Income

Confirm:

- Ticketing platform
- Payment gateway
- Refund policy
- Event reconciliation
- Tax receipt capability

---

## Membership Income

Confirm:

- Membership system
- Recurring payments
- Member onboarding
- Benefits
- Member register

---

## Café & Hospitality

Confirm:

- Food licences
- Food safety
- POS system
- Supplier agreements
- Waste management

---

## Merchandise

Confirm:

- HQ approval
- Inventory system
- Sales platform
- Payment processing
- Returns policy

---

## Grants

Confirm:

- Grants register
- Ethics review
- HQ review
- Reporting calendar

---

> **Important**
>
> Never accept any payment until payment processing and financial recording systems are fully operational.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Operational readiness completed
- [ ] Payment systems tested
- [ ] Required licences confirmed
- [ ] HQ notified of activation

---

# Step 6 — Monitor Revenue Performance Monthly

Revenue management continues after activation.

Every month review:

- Actual revenue
- Budget performance
- Cash reserves
- Diversification
- Unexpected income
- Revenue shortfalls

---

> **Important**
>
> Two consecutive months of revenue performance more than **20% below projection** must be reported to HQ together with a recovery plan.

---

## Decision Point

**Has any revenue stream underperformed by more than 20% for two consecutive months?**

### No

Continue monitoring.

### Yes

Prepare a Revenue Recovery Note.

Submit to HQ within five working days.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Monthly review completed
- [ ] Year-to-date performance tracked
- [ ] Cash reserves confirmed
- [ ] Recovery plans submitted where required

---

# Step 7 — Manage Grants Within Ethics Guidelines

Grants require additional governance.

---

## Non-Negotiable Requirements

### Ethics Review

Every funder must pass HQ ethics review.

---

### HQ Co-Review

Every application requires HQ review before submission.

---

### Restricted Funds

Restricted grants must be:

- Separately tracked
- Managed according to grant conditions
- Reported appropriately

---

Funding from politically affiliated organisations is prohibited.

---

## Decision Point

**Have all funders passed HQ ethics review?**

### Yes

Continue the grants programme.

### No

Pause all applications until compliance is complete.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Ethics reviews completed
- [ ] HQ reviews completed
- [ ] Grants register maintained
- [ ] Restricted funds tracked
- [ ] Reporting delivered

---

# Step 8 — Manage Sponsorships

Commercial sponsorship may supplement revenue but carries reputational risk.

---

## Sponsorship Requirements

Every sponsorship requires:

- HQ approval
- Ethics review
- Brand review

---

### Sponsorship Restrictions

Sponsors must not:

- Have political affiliations
- Be government-controlled
- Conflict with Palestine House values

---

### Branding Rules

Palestine House branding must always remain the primary identity.

---

### Exit Rights

Every agreement should include termination rights if reputational issues arise.

---

## Decision Point

**Has HQ approved the sponsorship?**

### Yes

Proceed.

### No

Do not confirm the arrangement.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Sponsor reviewed
- [ ] HQ approval received
- [ ] Branding hierarchy confirmed
- [ ] Exit clause included
- [ ] Sponsor register maintained

---

# Step 9 — Report Financial Performance

Financial reporting is mandatory.

---

## Minimum Reporting Schedule

| Report | Deadline |
|---------|----------|
| Monthly Financial Summary | Within 10 working days of month end |
| Quarterly Business Model Review | Within 15 working days of quarter end |
| Annual Financial Plan | 30 days before financial year |
| Unplanned Financial Events | Within 5 working days |

---

## Decision Point

**Are all reporting obligations current?**

### Yes

Continue reporting.

### No

Submit outstanding reports immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Monthly reports submitted
- [ ] Quarterly reviews completed
- [ ] Annual plan submitted
- [ ] Unplanned events reported
- [ ] No outstanding submissions

---

# Step 10 — Review and Improve the Business Model

The business model should evolve every year.

---

## Annual Review Topics

Review:

### Performance

Which revenue streams:

- Met expectations
- Exceeded expectations
- Underperformed

---

### Diversification

Assess revenue concentration.

---

### Community Feedback

Evaluate:

- Pricing
- Membership value
- Accessibility

---

### New Opportunities

Consider new approved revenue streams.

---

### Risk Assessment

Identify:

- Revenue dependency
- Contingency plans
- Emerging risks

---

### HQ Alignment

Review any updates issued by HQ.

---

Any proposed changes should be included within the Annual Financial Plan submission.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Annual Business Model Review been completed and submitted to HQ?**

### Yes

Begin the new financial year.

### No

Complete the review before continuing.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Annual review completed
- [ ] Community feedback incorporated
- [ ] Diversification confirmed
- [ ] Proposed changes documented
- [ ] HQ approval received

