## Business Model & Revenue

**Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**

---

## Business Model & Revenue Lifecycle Stages

- **Stage 2:** Venue & Space Setup
- **Stage 3:** Finance & Legal Setup
- **Stage 4:** Hiring & Team Setup
- **Stage 5:** Partnerships & Fundraising
- **Stage 6:** Marketing & Pre-Launch
- **Stage 7:** Launch & Post-Launch Review

---

## Overview

**10 Risk Items** across **5 Categories**:

- Common Mistakes
- Risks
- Red Flags
- Compliance Issues
- Escalation Triggers

This document identifies the most common, high-impact, and compliance-critical issues in Business Model & Revenue operations at Palestine House—from weak financial planning and revenue concentration to funding ethics, governance failures, donor dependency, financial reporting issues, and escalation situations that must reach HQ without exception.

---

# Common Mistakes

These are the most frequent Business Model & Revenue planning and implementation failures, typically occurring during **Lifecycle Stages 2–4**.

---

## 01. Launching Without an HQ-Reviewed Business Model or Financial Projection

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

The venue is nearly complete and recruitment begins.

Operations move forward based on general assumptions about:

- Events
- Memberships
- Café income
- Venue hire

However:

- No documented business model exists.
- No 12-month financial projection has been prepared.
- No break-even analysis has been completed.
- HQ has not reviewed the financial plan.

The House begins spending before understanding whether the model is financially sustainable.

### Why it matters

The business model is the financial foundation of the House.

Without it:

- Spending lacks strategic direction.
- Revenue shortfalls become surprises.
- Financial decisions are made without context.
- HQ cannot provide meaningful governance support.

Instead of managing expected variances, the House discovers problems only when cash becomes critically low.

### What to do

Complete the Business Model before committing significant expenditure.

Prepare:

- Business Model
- 12-month financial projection
- Revenue assumptions
- Break-even month
- Monthly reporting framework

Submit the complete package to HQ before signing the licence agreement.

### Escalate if

Operations begin—or significant capital commitments are made—without an HQ-reviewed business model.

Immediately:

- Notify HQ.
- Pause further financial commitments until review is complete.

---

## 02. Building the Revenue Model Around One Dominant Income Source

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

The financial model depends almost entirely upon one revenue stream.

Common examples include:

- Events
- Venue hire
- Memberships

Secondary revenue streams exist only as future ideas rather than operational plans.

When the primary revenue source underperforms, the entire financial model begins to fail.

### Why it matters

Revenue concentration creates predictable financial vulnerability.

Diversified income allows Palestine House to absorb fluctuations in any one activity.

Single-stream dependency exposes the organisation to immediate cash-flow pressure.

### What to do

Build the Year 1 model around **at least three active revenue streams**, each with:

- Activation plan
- Pricing assumptions
- Performance targets
- Responsible owner

Stress test the model.

Ask:

> **If the largest revenue stream falls by 30%, can the House still achieve break-even?**

Share the stress-tested model with HQ.

### Escalate if

A major revenue shortfall cannot be absorbed by other income streams and cash reserves are projected to fall below the agreed minimum within **60 days**.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

# Risks

These structural risks are most active during **Lifecycle Stages 3–6**.

---

## 03. Accepting Funding That Influences Programming or Organisational Positioning

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

A donor offers significant funding.

However, the funding includes expectations such as:

- Programming influence
- Speaker approval
- Political positioning
- Brand placement requirements

The offer is accepted because the House needs funding.

### Why it matters

Funding conditions compromise:

- Curatorial independence
- Governance
- Non-Political positioning

Once one exception is made, enforcing funding ethics becomes significantly more difficult.

### What to do

Implement an HQ-approved **Funding Ethics Policy**.

Automatically reject funding that requires:

- Programming influence
- Speaker approval rights
- Political positioning
- Brand arrangements inconsistent with Palestine House standards

Require HQ approval for funding above the agreed threshold.

### Escalate if

Funding with political, programming, or positioning conditions is under consideration—or has already been accepted.

Pause negotiations.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

## 04. Over-Reliance on One Major Donor

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

One donor provides the majority of annual operating income.

Programming and staffing become dependent upon that relationship.

When the donor withdraws—or changes expectations—the House faces immediate financial pressure.

### Why it matters

Single-donor dependency creates:

- Financial vulnerability
- Governance risk
- Reduced organisational independence

Major donor concentration also increases the risk of funding influence.

### What to do

Apply a donor concentration policy.

Suggested limits:

- Maximum **40%** of annual operating budget in Year 1
- Reduce to **25%** by Year 3

Maintain contingency plans for every major donor.

Review concentration quarterly with HQ.

### Escalate if

A donor providing more than **40%** of annual operating funding indicates possible withdrawal or reduction.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

# Red Flags

These indicate deeper financial integrity or governance concerns requiring investigation.

---

## 05. Monthly Revenue Reports Are Late or Incomplete

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Monthly revenue reports are:

- Late
- Missing
- Incomplete

Reporting delays become routine.

### Why it matters

Late reporting prevents HQ from identifying problems early.

Persistent reporting failures often indicate deeper financial difficulties.

Timely reporting is a governance obligation—not an administrative task.

### What to do

Treat monthly reporting as non-negotiable.

Submit complete reports by the **10th of each month**.

If delays are unavoidable:

Notify HQ before the deadline.

Suggested escalation process:

- First late report → Reminder
- Two consecutive late reports → HQ discussion
- Three consecutive late reports → Formal governance review

### Escalate if

Two or more consecutive reports are missing or incomplete.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

## 06. Pricing That Excludes the Core Community

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Financial pressure gradually increases:

- Ticket prices
- Membership fees
- Workshop pricing

Attendance shifts toward wealthier audiences.

Palestinian community participation begins to decline.

### Why it matters

Pricing that excludes the intended community represents a mission failure.

Financial sustainability should come through diversified revenue—not by pricing out the community.

### What to do

Develop a **Community Access Policy**.

Consider:

- Community pricing
- Student discounts
- Pay-what-you-can options
- Cross-subsidy through commercial venue hire

Review pricing quarterly alongside attendance data.

### Escalate if

Community members consistently identify affordability as a barrier, or attendance data shows declining Palestinian community participation.

Consult HQ before making structural pricing changes.

---

# Compliance Issues

These compliance requirements apply throughout **Lifecycle Stages 3–7**.

---

## 07. Accepting Grants or Donations Without Completing Funding Vetting

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Funding is accepted before completing:

- Disclosure
- Due diligence
- Funding vetting

Later, donor concerns emerge.

### Why it matters

Funding sources directly affect Palestine House's:

- Independence
- Reputation
- Non-Political positioning

Retrospective discovery creates far greater reputational risk than declining funding beforehand.

### What to do

Apply the funding vetting checklist before accepting funding.

Review:

- Political affiliations
- Sanctions concerns
- Ethics concerns
- Funding conditions
- Source of funds

Require HQ approval above the agreed threshold.

File completed documentation with every grant or donation.

### Escalate if

Funding has been accepted before vetting—or vetting identifies unresolved concerns.

Immediately:

- Notify HQ.
- Do not spend the funds until HQ guidance is received.

---

## 08. Operating a Membership Programme Without Proper Agreements or Privacy Compliance

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Memberships are sold while collecting personal data.

However:

- No written membership agreement exists.
- Privacy obligations are unclear.
- Data protection procedures are undocumented.

### Why it matters

This creates simultaneous:

- Legal risk
- Financial risk
- Reputational risk

Memberships represent long-term trust—not simply financial transactions.

### What to do

Before launching memberships:

Prepare a legally reviewed Membership Agreement.

Include:

- Benefits
- Fees
- Renewals
- Cancellations
- Refunds
- Data protection
- Member rights

Ensure systems comply with local privacy legislation.

### Escalate if

A member complaint, payment dispute, or privacy request occurs without documented membership or privacy procedures.

Notify HQ before responding.

---

# Escalation Triggers

These situations require immediate HQ involvement.

> **All escalation triggers below are mandatory.**
>
> Local teams must not manage these situations independently.

---

## E1. Funding Is Later Found to Be Politically Affiliated, Sanctions-Listed, or Ethics-Compromised

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

After accepting funding, it becomes apparent that the donor has:

- Political affiliations
- Sanctions concerns
- Serious ethical issues

The issue emerges through:

- Audit
- Community complaint
- Media enquiry

### Why it matters

This represents:

- Governance failure
- Financial control failure
- Significant reputational risk

Improper local handling may worsen the situation.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Quarantine the funds.
- Stop all expenditure.
- Document:
  - Source
  - Vetting completed
  - Timeline
- Notify HQ within **24 hours**.

Do not:

- Contact the donor
- Issue statements
- Respond to media

without HQ approval.

### Escalate if

Any accepted funding is found to have political, sanctions, or ethics concerns.

HQ notification within **24 hours** is mandatory.

---

## E2. Cash Reserves Are Forecast to Fall Below the Agreed Minimum Within 60 Days

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

Forward cash-flow projections indicate the House will fall below its minimum reserve.

Causes may include:

- Weak event attendance
- Slow memberships
- Lost sponsorship
- Revenue underperformance

No credible recovery plan exists.

### Why it matters

Early escalation provides time to:

- Increase revenue
- Reduce costs
- Activate fundraising
- Receive HQ support

Late escalation significantly reduces available options.

### What to do

Review a rolling **60-day cash forecast** every month.

When forecasts fall below the agreed reserve:

- Notify the House Manager immediately.
- Notify HQ before the end of the reporting week.

Do not wait until month-end.

### Escalate if

The 60-day forecast falls below the agreed reserve threshold at any time.

Notify HQ within **five working days**.

Do not attempt to manage significant cash shortages without HQ involvement.

---

# The Revenue Standard

A Palestine House that cannot sustain itself financially cannot sustain its cultural mission.

Financial discipline protects:

- Independence
- Community trust
- Long-term sustainability
- Organisational integrity

Every Business Model & Revenue safeguard exists to ensure Palestine House remains:

- Financially resilient
- Ethically funded
- Operationally accountable
- Faithful to its mission

These protections include:

- Business model review
- Financial projections
- Funding ethics
- Revenue diversification
- Monthly reporting
- Cash forecasting
- Mandatory escalation

Financial discipline is not separate from the cultural mission.

It is what makes the mission possible.

> **Never shortcut financial governance because of launch excitement or short-term pressure. Sustainable cultural impact depends on sustainable financial stewardship.**

