# Financial Operations & Controls Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Area |
|------:|------------------|
| **1** | Controls Foundation |
| **2** | Banking & Cash Setup |
| **3** | Budget & Approvals |
| **4** | Procurement & Payments |
| **5** | Income Reconciliation |
| **6** | Month-End Close |
| **7** | Audit & Compliance |
| **8** | Annual Review |

---

# Purpose of This Guide

This guide explains how to establish, operate, and maintain the financial controls required for every Palestine House location.

Financial controls are not administrative paperwork—they are the governance framework that:

- Protects community trust
- Safeguards organisational assets
- Ensures funds are used appropriately
- Supports transparency
- Gives HQ confidence in every location across the network

Follow this guide during initial setup and revisit it whenever financial controls are under pressure, including:

- High-volume event periods
- Staff transitions
- Audit preparation
- Financial incidents
- Control failures

The controls described here represent the **minimum required operating standard** for every Palestine House location.

---

# Who This Guide Is For

This guide is intended for:

- House Manager
- Finance / Operations Lead
- Board members responsible for financial oversight

Financial controls are a shared responsibility.

- The **Finance/Ops Lead** operates the financial systems.
- The **House Manager** is accountable for the control environment.
- The **Board** approves and reviews the controls framework.
- **HQ** establishes the minimum standards for the network.

---

# Step 1 — Establish the Financial Controls Framework

Financial controls must exist **before the first transaction occurs**.

This includes:

- Ticket sales
- Supplier payments
- Membership income
- Grants
- Donations
- Merchandise sales

Beginning operations without controls creates unnecessary financial risk and significant reconciliation problems later.

---

## The Four Pillars of Financial Control

### Separation of Duties

No individual should both:

- Request and approve expenditure
- Approve and record the same payment

---

### Approval Authority

Every expenditure category must have defined approval limits.

Typical approval flow:

- Finance/Ops Lead
- House Manager
- Board Chair / Finance Committee

according to the Approval Authority Matrix.

---

### Documentation First

No funds should move without supporting documentation.

Examples include:

- Purchase Requests
- Purchase Orders
- Supplier invoices
- Expense claims

Every receipt must be allocated to the correct account before month-end.

---

### Regular Reconciliation

Reconcile every:

- Bank account
- Cash float
- Payment card

at least monthly.

Reconciliations should always be reviewed by someone who did not prepare them.

---

> **Important**
>
> Every Palestine House location must maintain a Board-approved **Approval Authority Matrix**.
>
> Operating without one is considered a financial control failure.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Financial Controls Framework approved before first transaction
- [ ] Approval Authority Matrix signed by the Board
- [ ] Matrix displayed within the finance workspace
- [ ] Separation of duties documented
- [ ] HQ copy submitted
- [ ] House Manager and Finance Lead understand the framework

---

# Step 2 — Set Up Banking, Payment Processing & Cash Handling

All Palestine House finances must operate through bank accounts held in the legal entity's name.

Personal accounts must never be used.

---

## Operating Bank Account

Before the first transaction:

- Dedicated operating account established
- Minimum two authorised signatories
- Internet banking configured
- Account details shared with HQ

---

## Reserve Account

Maintain a separate reserve account holding the equivalent of:

**Three months of fixed operating costs**

Reserve funds require Board approval before use.

---

## Payment Processing

Before accepting:

- Ticket payments
- Memberships
- Merchandise sales
- Online payments

confirm:

- Payment gateway tested
- Real transaction completed
- Settlement process verified

---

## Cash Handling

Where cash is accepted:

Maintain documented procedures covering:

- Cash collection
- Counting
- Safe storage
- Deposits
- Till reconciliation
- Two-person cash counts

Cash discrepancies must be investigated immediately.

---

## Petty Cash

Only establish petty cash where operationally necessary.

It should:

- Have a maximum approved balance
- Be reconciled at every top-up
- Never replace standard procurement processes

---

## Decision Point

**Are all banking and payment systems operational before the first transaction?**

### Yes

Notify HQ of:

- Operating account
- Signatories

### No

Delay all financial activity until systems are operational.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Operating account established
- [ ] Reserve account established
- [ ] Payment gateway tested
- [ ] Cash handling documented
- [ ] Petty cash approved
- [ ] HQ notified

---

# Step 3 — Operate Procurement & Payment Approval

Every payment must follow the same controlled process.

---

## Stage 1 — Purchase Request

Before contacting suppliers:

Prepare a Purchase Request including:

- Business need
- Budget line
- Estimated cost

Finance/Ops Lead reviews the request.

---

## Stage 2 — Supplier Selection

Above the competitive quote threshold:

- Obtain at least two quotations
- Document supplier selection
- Record selection rationale

Approved suppliers may not require new quotations for repeat purchases within approved scope.

---

## Stage 3 — Purchase Order

Before suppliers begin work:

Issue a signed Purchase Order confirming:

- Scope
- Cost
- Payment terms

No work should begin without an approved Purchase Order.

---

## Stage 4 — Three-Way Match & Payment

Before payment:

Match:

- Purchase Order
- Supplier Invoice
- Delivery Confirmation

Only when all three agree should payment proceed.

---

## Decision Point

**Has every payment followed the four-stage procurement process?**

### Yes

Continue operating.

### No

Document every exception immediately.

Investigate payments made outside approved procedures.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Purchase Requests completed
- [ ] Competitive quotations obtained
- [ ] Purchase Orders approved
- [ ] Three-way matching completed
- [ ] Payments recorded
- [ ] No unsupported invoices paid

---

# Step 4 — Reconcile Income Streams

Income reconciliation ensures every payment received has been correctly recorded.

Failure to reconcile creates:

- Financial leakage
- Fraud risk
- Audit issues

---

## Event Income

Reconcile within **48 hours**.

Match:

- Ticketing reports
- Bank receipts
- Complimentary tickets
- Variances

---

## Membership Income

Reconcile weekly.

Confirm:

- Membership register
- Payments
- Renewals
- Benefits activation

---

## Café Income

Reconcile daily.

Match:

- POS totals
- Cash
- Card settlements

Investigate every significant variance.

---

## Space Hire

Match:

- Booking
- Invoice
- Payment

Treat deposits as deferred income until hire completion.

---

## Grants & Donations

Reconcile immediately.

Allocate restricted income to the correct restricted fund on receipt.

Acknowledge donations within **five working days**.

---

> **Important**
>
> Never accept income unless payment processing and financial recording systems are fully operational.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Reconciliations completed on schedule
- [ ] No unresolved variances older than seven days
- [ ] Restricted income correctly allocated
- [ ] Cash discrepancies investigated
- [ ] Monthly reconciliation reviewed

---

# Step 5 — Manage Restricted & Designated Funds

Restricted funds must never be mixed with general operating income.

---

## Fund Categories

### Restricted Grants

Funds limited to specific approved purposes.

---

### Restricted Donations

Purpose-specific donations.

If the purpose cannot be fulfilled, funds should be returned—not redirected.

---

### Designated Reserves

Board-designated funds for future projects.

These require Board approval before release.

---

## Maintain for Every Restricted Fund

- Separate account code
- Spending tracker
- Reporting calendar
- Signed grant agreement

---

## Decision Point

**Is every restricted fund separately tracked?**

### Yes

Continue.

### No

Immediately establish separate tracking and notify HQ if funds have been mixed.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Separate account codes
- [ ] Spending trackers maintained
- [ ] Reporting calendars maintained
- [ ] Agreements filed
- [ ] Separate reporting to HQ
- [ ] No ineligible expenditure

---

# Step 6 — Complete Month-End Close

Complete month-end within **10 working days**.

---

## Month-End Sequence

### 1. Complete Reconciliations

Resolve all outstanding items.

---

### 2. Post Accruals & Prepayments

Record:

- Outstanding liabilities
- Future period expenses

---

### 3. Reconcile Bank Accounts

Prepared by Finance/Ops Lead.

Reviewed by someone independent.

---

### 4. Produce Monthly Accounts

Include:

- Income vs Budget
- Expenditure vs Budget
- Year-to-date performance
- Cash reserves

---

### 5. Submit Monthly Finance Report

Use HQ Template T05.

House Manager approval required.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Monthly Finance Report been submitted within ten working days?**

### Yes

Continue.

### No

Notify HQ before the deadline if delays are unavoidable.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Reconciliations completed
- [ ] Accruals posted
- [ ] Bank reconciliations reviewed
- [ ] Monthly accounts produced
- [ ] Finance report submitted
- [ ] No overdue submissions

---

# Step 7 — Prepare for Audit

Audit preparation begins in **Month 10**.

Do not leave preparation until year-end.

---

## Audit Checklist

Maintain throughout the year:

- Bank reconciliations
- Purchase Orders
- Invoices
- Income reconciliations
- Restricted fund records
- Payroll records
- Grant documentation
- Sponsorship approvals
- Approval Authority Matrix
- Board minutes
- Trial balance

---

The Finance/Ops Lead maintains the audit file continuously.

---

> **Important**
>
> Auditor reports and management letters must be submitted to HQ within **10 working days** of receipt.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the audit file maintained continuously throughout the year?**

### Yes

Proceed with annual audit.

### No

Conduct an immediate gap assessment.

Notify HQ if records cannot be reconstructed.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Audit file maintained
- [ ] Month 10 review completed
- [ ] Auditor appointed
- [ ] Audit findings shared with HQ
- [ ] Corrective actions assigned
- [ ] Board approves audited accounts

---

# Step 8 — Respond to Financial Control Failures

Financial control failures should be handled immediately.

---

## Response Sequence

### 1. Stop & Contain

Prevent further activity.

---

### 2. Document

Record:

- What happened
- When
- Who
- Financial impact

---

### 3. Notify the House Manager

Report on the same business day.

---

### 4. Notify HQ

Report within **five working days** when required.

Examples include:

- Missing documentation
- Significant variances
- Restricted fund misuse
- Suspected fraud

---

### 5. Remediate

Improve the control process.

Assign:

- Owner
- Completion date
- Review date

---

### 6. Review

Confirm the issue has not recurred.

---

## Decision Point

**Are financial control failures documented and escalated immediately?**

### Yes

Continue reviewing controls monthly.

### No

Conduct a review of all unresolved control failures from the previous three months.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Failure documented immediately
- [ ] House Manager notified
- [ ] HQ notified where required
- [ ] Remediation implemented
- [ ] Monthly review completed
- [ ] Audit file updated

---

