## Financial Operations & Controls

**Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**

---

## Financial Operations & Controls Lifecycle Stages

1. Controls Foundation
2. Banking & Cash Setup
3. Budget & Approvals
4. Procurement & Payments
5. Income Reconciliation
6. Month-End Close
7. Audit & Compliance
8. Annual Review

---

## Overview

**15 Risk Cards** across **5 Categories**:

- Common Mistakes
- Risks
- Red Flags
- Compliance Issues
- Escalation Triggers

This document identifies the highest-impact operational, governance, and compliance risks in Financial Operations & Controls across Palestine House.

Each risk card explains:

- What the issue looks like
- Why it matters
- Required corrective action
- Mandatory escalation point

> **When in doubt, escalate.**
>
> Palestine House would rather receive an unnecessary escalation than discover a financial control failure through an audit finding, funder enquiry, or Board complaint.

---

# Common Mistakes

Operational errors that weaken financial controls.

---

## 01. Paying Invoices Without a Matching Purchase Order

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Suppliers deliver goods or services.

Invoices are paid because:

- Delivery is confirmed.
- The supplier is trusted.
- The agreement was made verbally or via email.

However:

- No Purchase Order (PO) exists.

Over time this becomes standard practice.

### Why it matters

A Purchase Order establishes:

- Approved scope
- Agreed pricing
- Authorised expenditure

Without a PO:

- There is no approval trail.
- Three-way matching cannot occur.
- Fraudulent or erroneous payments become harder to detect.

### What to do

Always issue a Purchase Order **before** committing to a supplier.

Implement:

- Mandatory PO requirement
- Monthly invoice review
- Three-way matching:
  - Purchase Order
  - Delivery confirmation
  - Invoice

Treat every invoice without a PO as an exception requiring investigation before payment.

### Escalate if

Either of the following occurs:

- More than **three payments** in one month are processed without Purchase Orders.
- Any payment above the House Manager's Approval Authority Matrix (AAM) threshold lacks a Purchase Order.

Notify:

- House Manager
- Board Finance Committee (if the pattern is systematic)

---

## 02. Using Petty Cash as an Informal Expense Account

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Petty cash gradually becomes the default payment method for:

- Event supplies
- Staff refreshments
- Programme expenses
- Recurring purchases

Receipts become incomplete.

Top-ups occur without reconciliation.

### Why it matters

Petty cash is one of the highest-risk financial controls.

Poor management creates:

- Missing funds
- Weak accountability
- Audit concerns
- Opportunity for misuse

### What to do

Follow the Cash Handling Procedure.

Requirements:

- Signed receipt for every payment
- Reconciliation at every top-up
- Fixed float maximum
- No recurring expenditure
- Separate custodian and reviewer

Review petty cash monthly.

### Escalate if

Petty cash cannot be reconciled.

If the unreconciled amount exceeds the AAM threshold:

Notify:

- House Manager
- Board Finance Committee

---

## 03. Accepting Restricted Grant Income Before Establishing Tracking

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Grant income is deposited into general operating funds.

Separate:

- Account codes
- Spending trackers
- Reporting schedules

are created later.

### Why it matters

Restricted grants have contractual conditions from the moment funds are received.

Poor segregation may lead to:

- Ineligible expenditure
- Grant repayment
- Compliance breaches
- Audit findings

### What to do

Before accepting restricted funding:

Create:

- Dedicated accounting code
- Spending tracker
- Reporting calendar

Only approve grant acceptance once these systems exist.

### Escalate if

Restricted funds:

- Are mixed with unrestricted income, or
- Have been spent on ineligible costs.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

# Risks

Structural risks that weaken financial controls over time.

---

## 04. One Person Controls the Entire Financial Process

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

One individual performs all major financial functions:

- Purchase requests
- Approvals
- Accounting
- Bank reconciliation

No independent review exists.

### Why it matters

Separation of duties is the foundation of financial control.

Without it:

- Errors remain undetected.
- Fraud becomes harder to identify.
- Operations depend entirely on one person.

### What to do

Review separation of duties quarterly.

Ensure every financial process includes at least:

- One preparer
- One reviewer

Where staffing is limited:

Use Board members or approved volunteers for secondary review.

Submit updated responsibilities to HQ annually.

### Escalate if

One individual has:

- Approved and recorded their own payments for more than one month, or
- Completed bank reconciliations without independent review.

Notify the Board Finance Committee immediately.

---

## 05. Revenue Concentration

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

One source generates more than **60%** of total annual income.

Examples:

- One grant
- One sponsor
- One programme
- One donor

No diversification strategy exists.

### Why it matters

Revenue concentration creates long-term financial vulnerability.

Loss of the dominant income source may make operations financially unsustainable within months.

### What to do

Include diversification targets within the Annual Financial Plan.

Define:

- Alternative revenue streams
- Growth targets
- Milestones
- Responsible owners

Report progress quarterly.

### Escalate if

One revenue stream exceeds **60%** of total income without an active diversification plan.

Submit:

- Board-approved response plan
- Quarterly Business Model Review

to HQ within **15 working days**.

---

## 06. Financial Controls Break Down During Busy Event Periods

**Category:** Risk

### What it looks like

During peak activity:

- Purchase Orders are delayed.
- Cash controls weaken.
- Income reconciliation falls behind.
- Month-end close is postponed.

The team plans to catch up later.

### Why it matters

Busy periods create the highest financial risk.

Controls that only work during quiet periods are ineffective.

Delayed reconciliation reduces financial accuracy.

### What to do

Plan additional finance capacity before busy periods.

Identify critical controls requiring support, including:

- Cash reconciliation
- Event reconciliation
- Supplier payments
- Month-end close

Discuss anticipated pressures with HQ where necessary.

### Escalate if

Either occurs:

- Income reconciliation falls more than **7 days** behind.
- Month-end close exceeds **10 working days** due to backlog.

Produce a formal recovery plan.

---

# Red Flags

These situations require immediate investigation.

---

## 07. Cash Variances Are Recorded but Never Investigated

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Daily reconciliations regularly identify:

- Small shortages
- Small overages

Variances are recorded but ignored.

### Why it matters

Repeated unexplained variances often indicate:

- System weaknesses
- Technical errors
- Financial misconduct

Auditors treat recurring unexplained differences as serious control failures.

### What to do

Investigate every variance above the approved threshold immediately.

Review cumulative trends monthly.

Document every investigation.

### Escalate if

A recurring variance pattern continues for more than **two weeks** without explanation.

Notify:

- House Manager
- Board Finance Committee

before continuing normal cash operations.

---

## 08. One Person Controls an Entire Supplier Relationship

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

One employee manages:

- Supplier selection
- Purchase Orders
- Delivery approval
- Payment approval

No independent review exists.

### Why it matters

Exclusive supplier relationships increase procurement risk.

Potential issues include:

- Inflated pricing
- Poor performance
- Undetected misconduct

### What to do

Review supplier ownership regularly.

For above-threshold suppliers:

- Require second-person approval.
- Obtain competitive quotations at least quarterly.

Ensure the House Manager has visibility of all supplier records.

### Escalate if

A supplier relationship has operated without independent oversight for more than one quarter.

Review all payments made to that supplier over the previous **12 months**.

---

## 09. Reserve Balance Falls Below the Minimum Without a Recovery Plan

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Financial reports show reserves below the required minimum.

No formal recovery plan accompanies the report.

### Why it matters

Reserve balances protect Palestine House from unexpected financial shocks.

Without recovery planning, financial resilience steadily weakens.

### What to do

Whenever reserves fall below the minimum:

Prepare a written recovery plan including:

- Current reserve
- Target reserve
- Timeline
- Recovery actions
- Responsible owners

Present the plan to the Board Finance Committee.

### Escalate if

Either occurs:

- Reserve falls below **two months** of fixed operating costs.
- Reserve remains below the **three-month minimum** for more than three consecutive months.

Notify HQ immediately.

---

# Compliance Issues

Formal breaches of Palestine House financial control standards.

---

## 10. Approval Authority Matrix Not Updated After Staff Changes

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Finance staff change.

However:

- Approval Authority Matrix (AAM) remains outdated.
- Former staff remain listed.
- New staff approve payments without documented authority.

### Why it matters

The AAM is a live governance document.

Outdated approval authorities invalidate the documented approval structure.

### What to do

Whenever financial roles change:

- Update the AAM.
- Obtain Board Chair approval.
- Share with HQ within **10 working days**.

No person may approve payments unless named in the current AAM.

### Escalate if

Either occurs:

- AAM remains outdated beyond **30 days**.
- Payments have been approved by unauthorised individuals.

Notify HQ immediately.

Provide an updated AAM within **5 working days**.

---

## 11. Monthly Finance Reports Are Persistently Late

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Monthly Finance Reports repeatedly arrive:

- Late
- Incomplete
- Not at all

HQ receives no advance notice.

### Why it matters

Timely reporting is a requirement of the Local Partner Agreement.

Persistent delays reduce HQ's governance visibility.

### What to do

Build reporting deadlines into the annual finance calendar.

If delays become unavoidable:

Notify HQ before the deadline.

### Escalate if

Either occurs:

- Three consecutive reports arrive more than **5 working days** late.
- One report arrives more than **15 working days** late.

Provide HQ with:

- Overdue report
- Formal remediation plan

---

## 12. Sponsorship or Grant Income Accepted Without HQ Ethics Review

**Category:** Compliance Issue

### What it looks like

Funding is accepted before:

- HQ ethics review
- HQ grant co-review

Co-branded materials may already exist.

### Why it matters

HQ review protects Palestine House from:

- Political funding
- Reputational risk
- Unapproved commitments

These controls cannot be bypassed because of time pressure.

### What to do

Build HQ review into every sponsorship and grant timeline.

Never:

- Accept funding
- Produce co-branded materials
- Submit grant applications

without HQ approval where required.

### Escalate if

Funding has already been accepted—or grant applications submitted—without HQ review.

Notify HQ immediately.

Do not spend received funds until guidance is received.

---

# Escalation Triggers

Situations where HQ must immediately assume oversight.

---

## 13. Suspected Fraud or Deliberate Misappropriation

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

Transactions cannot be explained.

Examples include:

- Unknown suppliers
- Duplicate payments
- Missing income
- Unexplained cash withdrawals

### Why it matters

Fraud investigations require:

- Evidence preservation
- Legal consistency
- Independent oversight

Improper local handling may compromise investigations.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Pause affected activities.
- Preserve evidence.
- Secure financial systems.
- Document findings.

Do not confront individuals.

Do not contact banks or authorities before HQ guidance.

### Escalate if

Any unexplained transaction exceeds the Approval Authority Matrix threshold.

Notify HQ:

- By phone on the same business day.
- In writing within **24 hours**.

---

## 14. Qualified Audit Opinion or Significant Audit Finding

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

External auditors issue:

- Qualified opinion
- Adverse opinion
- Disclaimer of opinion

or identify major control failures.

### Why it matters

Audit findings affect governance across the Palestine House network.

HQ must coordinate the organisational response.

### What to do

Within **10 working days**:

Submit to HQ:

- Auditor's report
- Management letter
- Remediation plan

Include:

- Named owners
- Completion dates

### Escalate if

Any qualified, adverse, or disclaimer audit opinion is issued.

Contact HQ by phone on the day the report is received.

---

## 15. Funder Requests Repayment or Begins a Formal Compliance Investigation

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

A funder:

- Requests repayment
- Questions grant expenditure
- Schedules a compliance review

### Why it matters

Grant compliance issues carry:

- Legal implications
- Financial consequences
- Reputational risk

HQ must coordinate responses.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Acknowledge receipt only.
- Gather:
  - Grant agreement
  - Spending tracker
  - Receipts
  - Bank records
  - Submitted reports
- Forward everything to HQ.

Do not discuss the matter substantively with the funder before HQ guidance.

### Escalate if

Any funder:

- Requests repayment,
- Initiates a compliance investigation, or
- Identifies ineligible expenditure.

Notify HQ within **24 hours**.

---

# A Note for House Managers

Financial controls matter most when maintaining them is least convenient.

Strong governance is demonstrated:

- When suppliers demand urgent payment.
- When grant deadlines are close.
- When events create operational pressure.
- When reconciliations feel easy to postpone.

Those are precisely the moments when Palestine House standards must be maintained.

> **HQ is your partner—not your auditor.**
>
> Maintain controls under pressure.
> Escalate early.
> Protect financial integrity before problems become crises.

