---

## Expected Output

- [ ] KPI Framework understood
- [ ] HQ targets confirmed
- [ ] KPI owners assigned
- [ ] Data sources identified
- [ ] Team briefed

---

# Step 2 — Set Up KPI Data Collection Systems

Every KPI requires a reliable data source.

Before reporting begins, confirm that every required system exists and has been tested.

---

## Ticketing & Event Registration

Required before the first public event.

Capture:

- Attendance
- Event type
- Cultural pillar
- Community participation

Test report exports before launch.

---

## CRM / Membership System

Required before the first membership sale.

Must report:

- Active members
- New members
- Renewals
- Membership status

---

## Financial Reporting System

Required before the first financial transaction.

Configure:

- Revenue streams
- Cost centres
- Restricted funds
- Annual Financial Plan account codes

The Finance/Ops Lead should be able to generate:

- Trial Balance
- Income & Expenditure Report
- Restricted Fund Schedule

without manual reconstruction.

---

## Guest Feedback & Complaints

Required before the first public event.

Maintain:

- Standardised feedback survey
- Complaint register
- Resolution tracking

Use the same questions consistently.

---

## Compliance & Incident Log

Required from day one.

Track:

- Financial control failures
- Brand deviations
- Non-political compliance incidents
- Governance events

Review monthly.

---

## Decision Point

**Are all five data collection systems operational and tested?**

### Yes

Run a full test reporting cycle.

### No

Complete setup before reporting begins.

Estimated KPI data should never be submitted.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Systems operational
- [ ] Test reports generated
- [ ] Owners assigned
- [ ] KPI definitions documented

---

# Step 3 — Produce the Monthly Performance Report

The Monthly Performance Report is the primary accountability document submitted to HQ.

---

## Stage 1 — Data Collection

**By the 5th working day**

Collect:

- Guest data
- Membership data
- Financial reports
- Incident logs

Use actual system data—not estimates.

---

## Stage 2 — Finance Report

**By the 8th working day**

Complete:

- Month-end close
- Bank reconciliation
- Monthly Finance Report

Provide explanations for variances greater than **10%**.

---

## Stage 3 — KPI Summary

**By the 9th working day**

Prepare:

- KPI results
- Target comparison
- RAG status
- Narrative commentary

### RAG Definitions

| Status | Definition |
|---------|------------|
| 🟢 Green | On target |
| 🟠 Amber | Within 10% of target |
| 🔴 Red | More than 10% below target or zero-tolerance breach |

Every Amber and Red KPI must include:

- Cause
- Recovery action
- Owner
- Target completion date

---

## Stage 4 — Submission

**By the 10th working day**

The House Manager:

- Reviews
- Signs
- Submits

Follow up if HQ acknowledgement is not received within **three working days**.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Monthly Performance Report been submitted within ten working days?**

### Yes

Continue monitoring KPI recovery actions.

### No

Submit immediately with explanatory notes.

Persistent late reporting is a compliance breach.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Monthly report submitted
- [ ] KPI RAG ratings completed
- [ ] Recovery plans documented
- [ ] House Manager sign-off obtained
- [ ] HQ acknowledgement received

---

# Step 4 — Conduct Quarterly Performance Reviews

Monthly reports explain current performance.

Quarterly reviews identify trends and strategic actions.

---

## Review Areas

### KPI Trends

Review:

- Improvement
- Stability
- Decline

across all KPI domains.

---

### Revenue Diversification

Assess:

- Income mix
- Diversification
- Financial sustainability

---

### Programme Quality

Review:

- Guest feedback
- Community participation
- Cultural impact

---

### Grants & Sponsorship

Evaluate:

- Active applications
- Funding pipeline
- Future opportunities

---

### Operational Compliance

Review:

- KPI breaches
- Financial control failures
- Brand deviations
- Non-political incidents

---

### Quarterly Actions

Define:

- Improvement priorities
- Named owners
- Completion dates

---

> **Important**
>
> The Quarterly Performance Review must be formally received by the Board and recorded in Board minutes.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Quarterly Review been completed and submitted within fifteen working days?**

### Yes

Continue monitoring quarterly actions.

### No

Complete immediately and notify HQ.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Quarterly review completed
- [ ] Board review documented
- [ ] KPI trends analysed
- [ ] Actions assigned
- [ ] HQ acknowledgement received

---

# Step 5 — Measure Community Impact

Attendance measures participation.

Community impact measures meaningful outcomes.

---

## Post-Event Feedback

After every programme:

Collect a standardised survey covering:

- Cultural relevance
- Community connection
- Suggested improvements

Use identical questions every time.

---

## Community Stories

Collect at least:

**Two qualitative stories per quarter**

Obtain informed consent before use.

---

## Community Partner Feedback

Each quarter:

Seek structured feedback from:

- Community organisations
- Cultural groups
- Palestinian associations

---

## Audience Composition

Measure:

- Palestinian community participation
- Broader public participation

Use an HQ-approved methodology.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Event surveys completed
- [ ] Community stories collected
- [ ] Partner feedback documented
- [ ] Audience composition tracked
- [ ] Impact summary prepared

---

# Step 6 — Maintain HQ Audit Readiness

Audit readiness should exist continuously—not only before an audit.

---

## Maintain Records

### Reporting

- Monthly reports
- HQ acknowledgements
- Recovery plans

---

### Financial

- Audit files
- Purchase Orders
- Bank reconciliations
- Three-way matches

---

### Governance

- Board minutes
- Approval Authority Matrix
- Annual Financial Plan

---

### KPI Data

Every KPI should be traceable back to its original source.

---

### Compliance

Maintain:

- Controls Failure Log
- Brand Deviation Log
- Non-Political Incident Log

---

### People

Maintain:

- Training records
- Employment records

---

> **Important**
>
> Documents that exist but cannot be located during an audit are effectively missing.

---

## Decision Point

**Can all required audit records be produced within one working day?**

### Yes

Continue maintaining audit readiness.

### No

Conduct an immediate records gap assessment.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Reports accessible
- [ ] Financial records organised
- [ ] Governance records current
- [ ] KPI evidence traceable
- [ ] Compliance logs current
- [ ] Audit self-assessment completed

---

# Step 7 — Prepare the Annual Performance Submission

The Annual Submission demonstrates the location's performance over the full year.

It also supports:

- HQ review
- Partner renewal
- Future planning

---

## Section 1 — KPI Performance

Include:

- Annual KPI table
- Target comparisons
- Annual RAG ratings
- Performance analysis

---

## Section 2 — Community Impact

Include:

- Feedback trends
- Community stories
- Partner feedback
- Audience composition

---

## Section 3 — Financial Assessment

Include:

- Revenue
- Expenditure
- Diversification
- Reserve position
- Audit summary
- Sustainability assessment

Attach auditor findings where applicable.

---

## Section 4 — Governance

Summarise:

- Board meetings
- Compliance incidents
- HQ notifications
- Local Partner Agreement issues
- Approval Authority Matrix
- Training records

---

## Section 5 — Strategy

Present proposals for:

- Revenue development
- Programme expansion
- Partnerships
- Staffing
- Investment

Clearly identify proposals requiring HQ approval.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the Annual Performance Submission been completed and submitted at least 30 days before the new operating year?**

### Yes

Await HQ approval before commencing the new year.

### No

Complete outstanding sections immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Annual submission completed
- [ ] Five mandatory sections included
- [ ] House Manager signed
- [ ] Board Chair signed
- [ ] Audited accounts attached
- [ ] HQ confirmation received

---

# Step 8 — Use Reporting to Improve Performance

Reporting should drive decision-making—not simply satisfy compliance requirements.

---

## Monthly Learning Review

After each report:

Discuss:

- What the data revealed
- What actions should change
- What lessons were learned

Document outcomes.

---

## KPI Review

Before each Annual Submission:

Review whether KPI targets remain appropriate.

Support any proposed changes with evidence.

---

## Benchmarking

Request network benchmarks from HQ.

Compare local performance with the wider Palestine House network.

---

## Community Feedback Loop

Show guests how their feedback produced improvements.

Communities that see change are more likely to continue providing valuable feedback.

---

## Decision Point

**Is reporting actively influencing operational decisions?**

### Yes

Document improvements and include them in the Annual Submission.

### No

Introduce monthly learning reviews immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Monthly learning reviews completed
- [ ] Decisions linked to KPI evidence
- [ ] Annual KPI review completed
- [ ] Network benchmarking utilised
- [ ] Community feedback loop closed

