## Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program** element defines how Palestine House operates its beverage service as both a cultural ritual and a craft discipline.

Its purpose is to ensure that every cup served under the Palestine House name expresses authentic Palestinian hospitality while consistently meeting approved quality, preparation, and operational standards.

For many guests, **qahwa** and tea service represent their most frequent interaction with Palestine House. This element ensures those moments remain meaningful cultural experiences rather than routine transactions.

While the **Menu & Palestinian Culinary Identity** element governs **what** beverages are offered and their cultural identity, this element governs **how** those beverages are prepared, served, maintained, and continuously improved behind the bar.

This element establishes a structured framework for:

- Beverage program identity
- Drink build standards
- Qahwa and tea service rituals
- Barista training and certification
- Daily calibration
- Equipment maintenance
- Water quality management
- Bean and leaf sourcing
- Modifier management
- Beverage quality assurance

It protects Palestine House from three common beverage-specific risks:

- **Ritual erosion** — the gradual loss of authentic hospitality and cultural service traditions.
- **Calibration drift** — inconsistent drink quality caused by poor calibration and preparation discipline.
- **Equipment and water degradation** — declining beverage quality resulting from neglected maintenance and water management.

Ultimately, this element ensures that every beverage served becomes a repeatable expression of Palestinian hospitality, craftsmanship, and cultural identity.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program element is applied every day across every location where beverages are prepared and served under the Palestine House name.

### Beverage Service Areas

This element governs beverage preparation within:

- Café counters
- Main beverage bars
- Event beverage service
- Catering operations
- Pop-up formats
- Mobile beverage stations

Every beverage service point must operate according to the same approved standards.

### Daily Operations

The program begins before opening and concludes after closing.

Daily activities include:

- Morning equipment preparation
- Daily calibration (dial-in)
- Water quality verification
- Beverage quality checks
- Build standard verification
- Closing equipment care
- Cleaning and maintenance

Consistency begins before the first guest is served.

### Beverage Program Development

This element is also applied during:

- Beverage program design
- New drink development
- Seasonal beverage planning
- Beverage testing
- Program optimization
- Quarterly program reviews

Every beverage introduced into the program should complete the approved development and review process.

### Operational Changes

This element is applied whenever changes affect beverage preparation, including:

- Bean changes
- Tea or leaf changes
- Milk substitutions
- Water filter replacement
- Equipment upgrades
- Seasonal menu launches

Any operational change that may affect beverage quality should trigger review and recalibration.

### Staff Development

The Beverage Program also governs:

- Barista onboarding
- Beverage training
- Certification
- Practical assessments
- Refresher training
- Continuous skill development

Training ensures every team member can consistently deliver the approved Palestine House beverage experience.

### Relationship to Other Systems

Every beverage governed by this element must also comply with the **Menu & Palestinian Culinary Identity** framework.

That framework governs:

- Master recipe cards
- Cultural storytelling
- Costing
- Dietary documentation
- Halal records
- Final approval

The Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program governs operational execution behind the bar and never replaces or bypasses that approval process.

---

## Who Is Responsible

The Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program is executed at the **LOCAL** level while operating within standards established by **GLOBAL**.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL establishes and maintains the beverage program framework across the Palestine House network.

Responsibilities include:

- Beverage program standards
- Qahwa ritual standards
- Tea service standards
- Cultural hospitality guidelines
- Beverage documentation standards
- Program governance

GLOBAL also:

- Reviews quarterly beverage summaries
- Protects beverage consistency across locations
- Maintains cultural integrity
- Supports long-term program development

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **House Manager** is accountable for ensuring the beverage program reflects approved Palestine House standards.

Responsibilities include:

- Program oversight
- Cultural integrity
- Operational compliance
- Beverage quality assurance
- Approval of ritual content changes
- Team accountability

### Food & Beverage Lead Responsibilities

The **F&B Lead** manages the Beverage Program on a day-to-day basis.

Responsibilities include:

- Beverage build standards
- Barista training
- Certification management
- Daily calibration systems
- Equipment maintenance
- Water quality management
- Bean and tea sourcing specifications
- Modifier control
- Beverage performance monitoring

### Barista and Service Team Responsibilities

Baristas and beverage service staff are responsible for:

- Following approved build cards
- Performing daily calibration
- Preparing drinks consistently
- Recording calibration activities
- Completing equipment care routines
- Reporting beverage quality issues
- Delivering approved hospitality rituals

Every beverage should be prepared according to documented standards without unauthorized variation.

### ASWĀTNA Responsibilities

Where appropriate, **ASWĀTNA** may support:

- Cultural verification
- Hospitality rituals
- Story development
- Educational programming
- Cultural authenticity

ASWĀTNA helps ensure that beverage rituals remain authentic expressions of Palestinian culture.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program element delivers clear and measurable organizational outcomes.

### Beverage Quality Outcomes

Every beverage achieves:

- Consistent taste
- Repeatable preparation
- Reliable quality
- Standardized presentation
- Predictable guest experience

Guests receive the same beverage regardless of the day or the individual preparing it.

### Cultural Outcomes

The beverage experience becomes:

- Authentically Palestinian
- Culturally meaningful
- Ritual-driven
- Educational
- Memorable

Qahwa and tea service remain living traditions rather than decorative additions to a conventional café.

### Operational Outcomes

Structured beverage management enables:

- Daily calibration
- Better beverage consistency
- Reduced operational variation
- Improved equipment reliability
- Higher preparation standards

### Financial Outcomes

Disciplined beverage management supports:

- Accurate cup costing
- Better modifier control
- Protected beverage margins
- Controlled ingredient usage
- Improved inventory predictability

Variations in beans, milk, and other ingredients become measurable and explainable.

### Equipment Outcomes

Routine care results in:

- Longer equipment lifespan
- Fewer equipment failures
- Improved beverage consistency
- Lower maintenance costs
- Greater operational reliability

Preventive care replaces reactive repairs.

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, this element enables Palestine House to:

- Preserve authentic hospitality rituals
- Build a distinctive beverage identity
- Maintain consistent beverage quality
- Protect operational profitability
- Deliver one of the most frequent and meaningful expressions of the Palestine House mission through every cup served

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program element governs how Palestine House develops, operates, maintains, and continuously improves its beverage program.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Beverage program identity
- Beverage architecture
- Bean specifications
- Tea and leaf specifications
- Water quality standards
- Drink build standards
- Qahwa service rituals
- Tea service rituals
- Barista training
- Certification
- Daily calibration
- Equipment maintenance
- Equipment fault management
- Modifier definition
- Modifier costing
- Seasonal beverage launches
- Quarterly beverage program reviews

It establishes the operational standards required to consistently deliver high-quality beverages while preserving Palestine House's cultural hospitality traditions.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Master beverage recipe governance
- Cultural story verification
- Full costing documentation
- Dietary documentation
- Halal documentation
- Beverage approval processes
- Food hygiene procedures
- Supplier approval controls
- Stock management
- POS reconciliation
- Manufacturer maintenance requirements
- Local water quality regulations

These responsibilities remain governed by:

- **Menu & Palestinian Culinary Identity**
- **Food & Beverage Operations**
- Applicable manufacturer guidance
- Relevant local regulatory requirements

### Operational Boundaries

The following are not permitted:

- Unauthorized drink modifications
- Preparation outside approved build standards
- Skipping daily calibration
- Neglecting equipment maintenance
- Ignoring water quality procedures
- Unapproved changes to hospitality rituals
- Failure to complete required maintenance records

All beverage activities must comply with approved:

- Beverage program standards
- Build cards
- Calibration procedures
- Equipment care protocols
- Water quality standards
- Hospitality ritual guidelines

Effective Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program management depends upon:

- Daily operational discipline
- Accurate calibration
- Preventive equipment maintenance
- Reliable water quality
- Consistent staff training
- Strict adherence to approved beverage standards

### Risk and Escalation

The Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program element cannot compensate for:

- Missed daily calibration
- Poor equipment maintenance
- Inadequate water quality management
- Weak beverage preparation discipline
- Failure to follow approved operating procedures

Any:

- Cultural concern regarding hospitality rituals
- Equipment malfunction affecting beverage quality or safety
- Milk handling incident
- Significant calibration failure
- Water quality issue
- Material deviation from approved beverage standards

must be treated as a high-risk issue and escalated immediately through the appropriate **GLOBAL** governance channels for review, corrective action, and formal approval to protect the quality, authenticity, and reputation of the Palestine House beverage program.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Guide:** Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program • 10 Steps

