# What This Guide Covers

The bar is where Palestine House performs hospitality most often.

While many guests may attend only occasional events, almost every visitor experiences the House through a cup of qahwa, tea, or coffee. Every beverage therefore becomes an expression of Palestinian culture, hospitality, and operational excellence.

This guide explains how to:

- Define the beverage identity
- Build the beverage architecture
- Specify sourcing and water standards
- Standardise every drink
- Protect traditional rituals
- Train and certify baristas
- Maintain equipment
- Control modifiers and margins
- Launch seasonal beverages
- Review and continuously improve the entire programme

This guide complements the **Menu & Palestinian Culinary Identity** and **Food & Beverage Operations (Focus Area 6)**. It governs beverage operations—not hygiene, suppliers, stocktake, or POS reconciliation.

---

# Coffee, Tea & Beverage Program Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Focus |
|------:|-------------------|
| **1** | Program Identity |
| **2** | Beverage Architecture |
| **3** | Sourcing & Water |
| **4** | Build Standards |
| **5** | The Ritual |
| **6** | Training & Calibration |
| **7** | Equipment Care |
| **8** | Modifiers & Margin |
| **9** | Seasonal Drinks |
| **10** | Review & Renewal |

---

# Stage 1 — Program Identity

## Step 1 — Put Qahwa and Tea at the Heart

The beverage programme begins with identity—not equipment.

Rather than allowing espresso to define the guest experience, Palestine House deliberately places qahwa and traditional tea service at the centre of hospitality.

Develop a **Beverage Program Identity Note** that complements the Culinary Identity Statement.

---

## The Identity Note Should Define

- The meaning of qahwa and tea service
- The guest experience surrounding the ritual
- The place of espresso and international beverages
- When the ritual occurs during the guest journey
- What the beverage programme will never become

---

## Decision Point

**If the espresso machine stopped working tomorrow, would the House still deliver its cultural hospitality in full?**

### Yes

Approve the Beverage Program Identity.

Append it to the Culinary Identity Statement.

Share it with HQ.

### No

Rework the identity until traditional hospitality—not equipment—defines the experience.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Beverage Program Identity approved
- [ ] Qahwa and tea defined as the cultural centre
- [ ] "Never List" completed
- [ ] Identity shared with HQ

---

# Stage 2 — Beverage Architecture

## Step 2 — Build the Beverage Structure Before Individual Drinks

Successful beverage programmes begin with structure.

Define:

- Beverage categories
- Service order
- Drink sizes
- Category limits
- Signature drinks
- Price bands

Qahwa and tea should always appear first.

---

## Beverage Four-Test Screen

Every beverage must satisfy all four tests.

### Cultural Authenticity

A genuine and verifiable cultural story where appropriate.

---

### Supply Stability

Reliable sourcing from approved suppliers.

---

### Financial Performance

Achieves required category margin.

---

### Operational Consistency

Every certified barista can prepare the drink consistently during busy service.

---

## Decision Point

**Does every proposed beverage pass all four tests while maintaining qahwa and tea as the first category?**

### Yes

Proceed to sourcing and water specification.

### No

Revise or remove the drink before launch.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Beverage Architecture approved
- [ ] Categories and sizes confirmed
- [ ] Signature drinks identified
- [ ] Four-Test assessment completed
- [ ] Modifier list fully defined

---

# Stage 3 — Sourcing & Water

## Step 3 — Specify Every Ingredient, Including Water

Water is the primary ingredient in both coffee and tea.

Specify and monitor:

- Water hardness
- TDS
- Chlorine
- Filtration
- Filter replacement schedule

---

## Sourcing Register

Record for every beverage ingredient:

- Role
- Origin
- Roast or grade
- Supplier
- Freshness requirements
- Backup supplier
- Storage standards

Prioritise Palestinian and regional ingredients wherever practical.

---

## Sourcing Rules

- Nothing enters service without specification.
- Every ingredient has an approved supplier.
- Every supplier has a backup.
- Freshness standards are monitored.
- Regional sourcing is reviewed annually.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Sourcing Register completed
- [ ] Water specification documented
- [ ] Filtration installed
- [ ] Equipment commissioned
- [ ] Dietary records completed

---

# Stage 4 — Build Standards

## Step 4 — Standardise Every Beverage

Every beverage should have its own Build Card linked directly to the approved master recipe.

---

## Build Card Includes

- Dose
- Yield
- Extraction time
- Water temperature
- Milk temperature
- Texture
- Glassware
- Garnish
- Build sequence

Version control should always match the approved master recipe.

---

## Beverage Consistency Test

Two certified baristas independently prepare each drink using only the Build Card.

If outcomes differ:

- Revise the Build Card, or
- Retrain staff

Repeat after:

- Bean changes
- Major recipe revisions
- New barista certification

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Build Cards completed
- [ ] Version control aligned
- [ ] Consistency testing passed
- [ ] No beverages prepared from memory

---

# Stage 5 — The Ritual

## Step 5 — Document the Hospitality Ritual

Hospitality should be intentional—not dependent on individual habits.

Document the complete qahwa and tea service ritual.

---

## Ritual Standard

Include:

- Preparation sequence
- Serveware
- Pouring etiquette
- Refill etiquette
- English host script
- Arabic host script
- Service pace
- Story delivery

Every cultural element should be verified through the approved story development process.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the ritual documented clearly enough for a newly certified barista to deliver it exactly the same way next year?**

### Yes

Approve the Ritual Standard.

Begin ritual certification.

### No

Continue refining until the ritual no longer depends on individual memory.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Ritual Standard approved
- [ ] Cultural verification completed
- [ ] Ritual certification established
- [ ] Change control implemented

---

# Stage 6 — Training & Calibration

## Step 6 — Certify the Barista and Calibrate Every Morning

Training and calibration are separate but equally important disciplines.

---

## Certification Covers

- Espresso
- Milk texturing
- Qahwa
- Tea
- Cold beverages
- Hospitality ritual
- Story delivery

No team member serves independently before certification.

---

## Daily Calibration

Complete before service:

- Grinder dial-in
- Taste verification
- Bean adjustments
- Weather adjustments

Record all calibration activity.

---

## Decision Point

**Has every certified barista completed today's calibration before serving guests?**

### Yes

Begin service.

### No

Pause espresso service until calibration is complete.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Barista certifications completed
- [ ] Daily calibration recorded
- [ ] Dial-in log maintained
- [ ] Independent calibration competency confirmed

---

# Stage 7 — Equipment Care

## Step 7 — Maintain Equipment Before It Fails

Preventive maintenance protects quality and reduces operational disruption.

---

## Daily Care

Complete:

- Backflush
- Steam wand cleaning
- Hopper cleaning
- Drip tray cleaning
- Qahwa pot care

---

## Weekly & Monthly Care

Include:

- Burr cleaning
- Gasket inspection
- Descaling
- Water testing
- Filter replacement

Document every activity.

---

## Decision Point

**Could the House support a warranty claim using today's maintenance records?**

### Yes

Continue the maintenance schedule.

### No

Correct documentation immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Maintenance Log complete
- [ ] Faults recorded
- [ ] Unsafe equipment removed from service
- [ ] Water standards maintained
- [ ] Downtime procedures documented

---

# Stage 8 — Modifiers & Margin

## Step 8 — Protect Profit Through Modifier Control

Margins are often lost through uncontrolled beverage modifiers.

Maintain a complete modifier table covering:

- Alternative milks
- Extra espresso shots
- Syrups
- Size upgrades
- Add-ons

Every modifier should be:

- Costed
- Approved
- Reflected in the POS

---

## Modifier Rules

- No unapproved modifiers.
- Alternative milk pricing is deliberate.
- Cross-contact controls are maintained.
- Monthly POS verification completed.
- Re-cost after significant supplier price movements.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Modifier table approved
- [ ] POS aligned
- [ ] Quarterly costing updated
- [ ] Margin protection maintained

---

# Stage 9 — Seasonal Drinks

## Step 9 — Launch Seasonal Drinks with Full Discipline

Temporary drinks require the same governance as permanent menu items.

Every seasonal beverage should have:

- Approved concept
- Verified story
- Build Card
- Costing
- Training
- Rotation plan

Retire seasonal beverages formally.

---

## Seasonal Rules

- No undocumented drinks.
- Baristas trained before launch.
- New beans calibrated.
- Close-out review completed.
- Retirement documented.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Seasonal Launch Brief completed
- [ ] Sourcing confirmed
- [ ] Staff trained
- [ ] Close-out documented

---

# Stage 10 — Review & Renewal

## Step 10 — Review the Beverage Programme

Quarterly review brings together evidence from every operational log.

---

## Quarterly Review

Evaluate:

- Beverage sales
- Margins
- Modifier performance
- Waste
- Calibration
- Equipment reliability
- Water quality
- Training
- Ritual consistency

Review all evidence together.

---

## Annual Review

Refresh:

- Supplier performance
- Sourcing strategy
- Regional ingredients
- Programme identity

Feed findings into:

- Menu Review
- Food & Beverage Review
- Annual Identity Review

---

## Decision Point

**Does today's qahwa provide the same quality and cultural experience delivered when the programme first launched?**

### Yes

Document the evidence.

Approve the next quarterly action plan.

### No

Conduct a full programme calibration review.

Audit:

- Build Cards
- Ritual
- Water
- Equipment
- Training

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Quarterly Beverage Review completed
- [ ] Improvement actions assigned
- [ ] Equipment, water and training trends analysed
- [ ] Annual sourcing review completed
- [ ] Findings incorporated into wider operational reviews

---

