# Stage 1 — Catering Identity

## Step 1 — Decide What the House Will Cater

Before accepting enquiries, define the catering identity.

Specify:

- Services offered
- Cultural purpose of each format
- Monthly capacity
- Events the House will never cater

The catering offer should reinforce the mission of Palestine House rather than become generic commercial catering.

---

## The Identity Note Should Define

- Delivered catering
- Private hire
- Culinary programming
- Guest chef collaborations
- Monthly event capacity
- "Never Cater" list

---

## Decision Point

**Is the monthly catering capacity defined as a specific number, and is the team prepared to decline work beyond that limit?**

### Yes

Approve the Catering Identity.

Append it to the Culinary Identity Statement.

Proceed to package development.

### No

Define the operational capacity before accepting enquiries.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Catering Identity approved
- [ ] Service formats defined
- [ ] Monthly capacity established
- [ ] "Never Cater" list completed
- [ ] Identity shared with HQ

---

# Stage 2 — Package Architecture

## Step 2 — Build Packages Before Receiving Enquiries

Standard packages create consistency, accurate pricing and operational control.

Develop package structures covering:

- Formats
- Headcount ranges
- Inclusions
- Staffing
- Logistics
- Pricing
- Margin targets

Every package should use approved menu items only.

---

## Pricing Rules

Establish before accepting enquiries:

- Minimum order values
- Staffing calculations
- Travel and setup costs
- Delivery charges
- Equipment charges
- Deposit percentage
- Cancellation terms

House Manager approval is required for any non-standard quotation.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Catering Package Sheet approved
- [ ] Costs verified
- [ ] Margin targets confirmed
- [ ] Pricing rules documented
- [ ] Operational boundaries defined

---

# Stage 3 — Enquiry & Qualification

## Step 3 — Qualify Every Enquiry

Not every enquiry should become a booking.

Evaluate each request against four criteria.

---

## Qualification Criteria

### Identity

Does the event align with Palestine House values?

---

### Capacity

Can it be delivered without affecting café operations?

---

### Capability

Are staffing, equipment and facilities available?

---

### Financial Viability

Does the work meet approved package or pricing standards?

---

Record every declined enquiry.

Patterns should inform future planning.

---

## Decision Point

**Would Palestine House willingly host this event if there were no financial payment attached?**

### Yes

Proceed to proposal preparation.

### No

Decline the enquiry and record the reason.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Qualification completed
- [ ] Decisions documented
- [ ] Declines recorded
- [ ] Only suitable enquiries proceed

---

# Stage 4 — Proposal & Quote

## Step 4 — Build Every Quote from Approved Packages

Every proposal should originate from the approved pricing system.

Include:

- Menu
- Story references
- Dietary information
- Staffing
- Service style
- Pricing

Clearly identify everything that is **not included**.

---

## Quote Discipline

State explicitly:

- Exclusions
- Client responsibilities
- Deadlines
- Bespoke costs

Never estimate bespoke work without completing costing first.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Proposal completed
- [ ] Pricing verified
- [ ] Exclusions documented
- [ ] Client deadlines confirmed

---

# Stage 5 — Confirmation & Deposit

## Step 5 — Confirm Before Production Begins

No production should begin until:

- Agreement is signed
- Deposit is received

Record:

- Final menu
- Headcount
- Venue
- Timings
- Staffing
- Deposit
- Cancellation terms
- Client contact

Register the event within the Event Production system.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the signed agreement been received and the deposit paid?**

### Yes

Reserve the date.

Begin production planning.

### No

Do not purchase ingredients, reserve staff or begin preparation.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Booking confirmed
- [ ] Deposit received
- [ ] Event registered
- [ ] Operational ownership assigned

---

# Stage 6 — Production Planning

## Step 6 — Plan Backwards from Service

Production planning begins with the service time and works backwards.

Develop a complete production schedule covering:

- Quantities
- Preparation
- Staffing
- Equipment
- Transport
- Setup
- Service sequence

Assign ownership for every activity.

---

## Planning Rules

- Use confirmed guest numbers.
- Assign named owners.
- Test scaled recipes.
- Confirm transport and holding requirements.

Volume cooking should never be improvised.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Production Plan completed
- [ ] Quantities confirmed
- [ ] Responsibilities assigned
- [ ] Transport verified
- [ ] Operational risks reviewed

---

# Stage 7 — Safety & Allergens

## Step 7 — Maintain the Information Chain

Allergen and dietary information must remain accurate throughout the entire event.

Issue a written declaration covering:

- Allergens
- Dietary status
- Halal status
- Cross-contact risks
- Storage guidance
- House contact details

Map guest dietary requirements before the event.

---

## Decision Point

**Has the client acknowledged the allergen declaration, and have all medical dietary requirements been assigned to specific dishes and controls?**

### Yes

Proceed to service.

### No

Pause service until documentation is complete.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Allergen Declaration issued
- [ ] Client acknowledgement received
- [ ] Dietary controls assigned
- [ ] Information aligned with menu records

---

# Stage 8 — Culinary Programming

## Step 8 — Create Programmes That Teach

Culinary workshops and supper clubs are educational experiences—not simply catered events.

Each programme should define:

- Cultural objective
- Learning outcome
- Featured dishes
- Participation format
- Capacity
- Pricing
- Staffing

Guest chef collaborations require written agreements before promotion.

---

## Collaboration Rules

- Verify every cultural claim.
- Brief facilitators on approved messaging.
- Document guest chef dishes.
- Agree ownership and future use of recipes.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Programme Brief approved
- [ ] Cultural objective defined
- [ ] Collaboration agreement signed
- [ ] Documentation completed
- [ ] Attribution confirmed

---

# Stage 9 — Delivery & Reconciliation

## Step 9 — Measure Actual Performance

Assign one operational decision-maker throughout service.

Record:

- Deviations
- Dietary service
- Operational issues

Complete reconciliation within **five working days**.

---

## Reconciliation Includes

- Actual ingredient costs
- Staffing costs
- Equipment costs
- Transport costs
- Guest numbers
- Waste
- Invoice status
- True margin

Record discounts and goodwill separately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Event Reconciliation completed
- [ ] Actual margins calculated
- [ ] Client feedback requested
- [ ] Final invoice issued

---

# Stage 10 — Review & Renewal

## Step 10 — Improve the Catering Offer

Quarterly reviews should evaluate the entire catering programme.

---

## Quarterly Review

Review:

- Events delivered
- Revenue
- Actual margins
- Conversion rates
- Declined enquiries
- Capacity utilisation
- Complaints
- Programming attendance
- Collaboration outcomes

Refresh package pricing using actual reconciliation data rather than estimated costs.

Retire packages that no longer deliver cultural or financial value.

---

## Decision Point

**Do actual event margins consistently match quoted margins?**

### Yes

Confirm the pricing model.

Implement the next quarterly action plan.

### No

Correct the costing model before preparing the next quotation.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Quarterly Review completed
- [ ] Actions assigned
- [ ] Costing updated
- [ ] Capacity trends analysed
- [ ] Findings shared with Menu, Event Production and Food & Beverage reviews

---

