## Programming Model & Pillars Overview

---

## Purpose

The Programming Model & Pillars element defines how Palestine House designs, structures, and delivers its cultural and community programming in a consistent and disciplined manner.

Its purpose is to ensure that all programming:

* Aligns with the Palestine House mission
* Reflects clear thematic priorities
* Contributes to a cohesive overall experience
* Supports audience engagement
* Represents Palestinian culture with consistency and quality
* Creates long-term value rather than isolated activity

This element establishes a structured framework for:

* The types of programmes delivered
* The themes they address
* How programmes are balanced
* How audiences are considered
* How programming evolves over time
* How proposed activities are reviewed and approved

The Programming Model prevents random, reactive, or purely opportunistic programming.

Every programme must connect to defined pillars and contribute to the wider Palestine House experience.

Without this structure, programming may become:

* Inconsistent
* Fragmented
* Misaligned with the mission
* Unbalanced across audiences or themes
* Dependent on short-term opportunities
* Difficult to evaluate or scale

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Programming Model & Pillars element is used continuously when planning, scheduling, delivering, and reviewing all Palestine House programming.

### During Programme Design

Programme ideas are assessed against the defined pillars to determine whether they:

* Align with the mission
* Fit the approved thematic structure
* Serve a clear audience
* Contribute to strategic objectives
* Meet cultural and quality standards
* Complement the wider programme calendar

Ideas that do not align with the approved pillars must not proceed without review and approval.

### During Calendar Planning

The model is used to ensure that programming is balanced across:

* Approved themes
* Audience segments
* Programme formats
* Levels of participation
* Cultural priorities
* Time periods

This prevents the calendar from becoming overly concentrated in one theme, format, or audience group.

### During Programme Delivery

The model guides how programmes are delivered, ensuring consistency in:

* Tone
* Quality
* Cultural positioning
* Guest experience
* Brand alignment
* Programme purpose

### During Performance Review

Completed programmes are assessed against:

* Audience engagement
* Relevance
* Quality
* Alignment with the pillars
* Contribution to programme objectives
* Operational performance
* Learning for future programming

This element applies to all programming, whether:

* Developed internally
* Delivered by Palestine House teams
* Co-created with ASWĀTNA
* Led by external partners
* Hosted through collaborations

---

## Who Is Responsible

Programming Model & Pillars is defined at the GLOBAL level and implemented at the LOCAL level through structured collaboration.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for:

* Defining the core programming pillars
* Ensuring alignment with the Palestine House mission
* Establishing programme standards
* Providing programming strategy and guidance
* Maintaining consistency across locations
* Reviewing major programming changes
* Approving additions or deviations from the core pillars
* Monitoring network-wide programming performance

GLOBAL protects the integrity of the Programming Model and ensures that all locations operate within a shared cultural and strategic framework.

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

LOCAL leadership, specifically the General Manager or Head of Operations, is responsible for:

* Implementing the Programming Model
* Ensuring that programme ideas align with approved pillars
* Maintaining a balanced programme calendar
* Confirming required reviews and approvals
* Monitoring quality and delivery
* Coordinating internal teams and external partners
* Reviewing programme performance
* Escalating programmes that fall outside the approved framework

LOCAL leadership must ensure that programming decisions are based on the model rather than personal preference or short-term opportunity.

### Programming Team Responsibilities

Programming teams are responsible for:

* Developing programme concepts
* Mapping each programme to an approved pillar
* Identifying intended audiences and outcomes
* Planning programme delivery
* Maintaining required quality standards
* Coordinating with delivery partners
* Reviewing performance after completion
* Applying learning to future programming

### ASWĀTNA Responsibilities

ASWĀTNA, as a preferred programming partner, plays a key role in:

* Co-creating programmes
* Supporting cultural quality
* Contributing programming expertise
* Delivering approved activities
* Ensuring alignment with the programming pillars
* Supporting consistent cultural representation

### Accountability

Accountability is maintained through:

* Programme planning reviews
* Calendar reviews
* Approval processes
* Delivery monitoring
* Performance evaluation
* Reporting
* Corrective action where required

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Programming Model & Pillars element enables Palestine House to deliver a coherent, strategic, and high-quality programme experience.

### Clear Programming Identity

Audiences experience a consistent identity across different programmes.

Each activity feels connected to the wider purpose of Palestine House rather than operating as an isolated event.

This reduces confusion and strengthens audience understanding of what Palestine House represents.

### More Strategic Programming

Every programme contributes to defined objectives, such as:

* Cultural representation
* Audience engagement
* Community participation
* Member value
* Learning
* Long-term relationship building

Programming decisions become more intentional and less reactive.

### Balanced Programme Delivery

The model supports a more balanced calendar across:

* Themes
* Audiences
* Formats
* Participation levels
* Cultural priorities

This helps Palestine House serve a wider community while maintaining a clear identity.

### More Effective Partnerships

Partner relationships improve because expectations are clearly defined.

Partners understand:

* The approved themes
* Required quality standards
* Cultural positioning
* Intended outcomes
* Approval requirements

### Greater Operational Efficiency

Teams have a consistent framework for:

* Evaluating ideas
* Rejecting misaligned proposals
* Building the calendar
* Assigning resources
* Reviewing programme performance

This reduces unnecessary debate and improves planning speed.

### Stronger Long-Term Performance

Over time, the model supports:

* Higher audience retention
* Stronger community connection
* Improved reputation
* More consistent programme quality
* Better partner confidence
* Greater member engagement
* Scalable programming across locations

Palestine House can expand its programming without losing consistency, identity, or quality.

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Programming Model & Pillars element includes:

* Definition of programming pillars
* Thematic structure
* Programme alignment criteria
* Programme planning processes
* Calendar balance
* Audience consideration
* Quality expectations
* Review and approval requirements
* Programme evaluation
* Performance learning

It defines what programming should be delivered and how the overall programme should be structured.

However, it does not replace:

* Detailed event planning
* Programme briefs
* Content development
* Speaker or facilitator management
* Production plans
* Budgets
* Marketing plans
* Operational SOPs
* Safety procedures
* Event checklists

Unrestricted programming outside the approved pillars is not permitted.

This includes programmes that:

* Do not align with the mission
* Sit outside the approved thematic framework
* Create brand or reputational risk
* Conflict with cultural standards
* Are pursued only because an opportunity is available
* Have not completed the required review

The effectiveness of this element depends on:

* Disciplined planning
* Clear programme ownership
* Consistent review
* Accurate performance evaluation
* Strong partner coordination
* Adherence to quality standards
* Timely escalation

It cannot compensate for:

* Weak content
* Poor programme design
* Inadequate delivery
* Low-quality facilitation
* Weak audience understanding
* Operational failures
* Failure to act on performance findings

Any proposed programme that:

* Falls outside the approved pillars
* Introduces a new thematic direction
* Creates brand or reputational risk
* May conflict with governance standards
* Requires a major change to the Programming Model

must be reviewed and approved through GLOBAL channels before proceeding.

This ensures alignment, consistency, and control across the Palestine House network.
$md$, $md$# SIMPLE GUIDE

## Programming Model & Pillars

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook | 10 Steps*

---

## PURPOSE OF THIS GUIDE

Programming is the heartbeat of Palestine House.

This guide explains how to design, curate, deliver, evaluate, and renew cultural programming, including:

* Understanding the five programming pillars
* Building the annual programming calendar
* Collaborating with Aswatna
* Sourcing and assessing artists
* Applying the non-political positioning standard
* Securing written HQ approval
* Planning and delivering events
* Capturing event data and feedback
* Conducting quarterly reviews
* Renewing the annual programme

Great programming is not accidental. It is built through a clear philosophy, disciplined planning, strong collaboration, and continuous learning.

---

## WHO THIS GUIDE IS FOR

This guide is for:

* The House Manager
* Programme teams
* Programme Coordinators
* Event leads
* Local Partner board members with programme oversight
* Staff responsible for artist relationships, event delivery, approval submissions, or programme reporting

Complete the steps in sequence when developing a new annual programme.

For an existing programme, use this guide to review pillar balance, approval compliance, delivery quality, financial performance, and community impact.

---

## PROGRAMMING MODEL & PILLARS LIFECYCLE

| Stage | Focus                     |
| ----: | ------------------------- |
| **1** | Philosophy & Pillars      |
| **2** | Annual Planning           |
| **3** | Aswatna Collaboration     |
| **4** | Artist & Content Sourcing |
| **5** | Approval & Scheduling     |
| **6** | Delivery & Operations     |
| **7** | Review & Renewal          |

---

# 1. Understand and Commit to the Palestine House Programming Philosophy

**Lifecycle Stage:** Philosophy & Pillars
**Timing:** Before programming begins and before the calendar is drafted

Palestine House does not simply run events.

It curates a purposeful cultural experience rooted in:

* Palestinian identity
* Creativity
* Heritage
* Community
* Belonging

Before any programme is designed, every person involved in programming decisions must understand and be able to explain the five programming pillars.

These pillars ensure that every:

* Event
* Exhibition
* Workshop
* Performance
* Screening
* Community experience

reflects why Palestine House exists.

## The Five Programming Pillars

### 1. Heritage & Memory

*Cultural Foundation*

Celebrate, preserve, and share Palestinian heritage, including:

* Language
* Oral history
* Craft
* Food
* Music
* Collective cultural identity across generations

### 2. Contemporary Creativity

*Living Culture*

Showcase and support Palestinian:

* Artists
* Writers
* Musicians
* Filmmakers
* Makers

The purpose is to amplify current creative voices and support sustainable creative careers.

### 3. Education & Exchange

*Dialogue & Understanding*

Deliver programmes that create:

* Genuine learning
* Cross-cultural dialogue
* Deeper understanding of Palestinian history
* Greater understanding of Palestinian culture and experience

### 4. Community Activation

*Belonging & Connection*

Create events and experiences that:

* Bring the Palestinian community together
* Build belonging
* Strengthen relationships
* Connect diaspora communities with each other
* Connect diaspora communities with home

### 5. Commercial Sustainability

*Revenue & Mission*

Deliver ticketed events, premium workshops, and cultural experiences that generate revenue and support the mission without compromising the culture.

Every programme must connect clearly to at least one pillar.

If a proposed programme cannot be traced to any pillar, it should not be included in the calendar.

> **NOTE:** Aswatna is Palestine House's preferred cultural programming partner. Its relationships with Palestinian artists, cultural institutions, and creative networks make it the primary source of curatorial expertise. Engage Aswatna early as a co-designer, not merely as a supplier.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* House Manager and programme team can name and explain all five programming pillars from memory.
* Every team member involved in programming decisions has read the Culture Charter and understands the programming framework.
* Aswatna partnership contact established.
* Initial collaboration discussion with Aswatna completed.
* Non-political positioning clause understood and applied to programming decisions.

---

# 2. Design the Annual Programming Calendar

**Lifecycle Stage:** Annual Planning
**Timing:** 90 days before the new programming year begins

A Palestine House programming calendar is not a rolling list of incoming proposals.

It must be deliberately designed to:

* Balance the five pillars
* Reflect the local cultural context
* Create a coherent narrative across the year
* Respond to the community
* Remain financially viable

The annual calendar should be drafted 90 days before the new programming year begins.

It must be finalised with written HQ approval before it is:

* Published
* Announced
* Committed to artists
* Committed to venues

## Annual Calendar Development Process

### Step 2a: Define the Programming Rhythm

Determine the annual frequency and mix of:

* Exhibitions
* Performances
* Workshops
* Film screenings
* Community dinners
* External partnership events
* Recurring formats

Set a realistic delivery cadence.

A typical cadence is 2–4 major events per month, supported by regular recurring formats.

### Step 2b: Map Calendar Anchor Points

Identify important dates and cycles that the programme should respond to, including:

* Palestinian cultural anniversaries
* Diaspora community milestones
* Local arts seasons
* Funder reporting cycles
* Seasonal demand patterns
* Relevant civic dates

### Step 2c: Balance the Five Pillars

Review the calendar against all five programming pillars.

Ensure that:

* No pillar is absent for more than two consecutive months.
* At least one programme from every pillar appears each quarter.
* Commercial Sustainability programmes are distributed throughout the year rather than clustered together.

### Step 2d: Build a Provisional Event Budget

Assign each proposed event:

* Estimated costs
* Projected revenue
* Lead organiser
* Programming pillar
* Provisional date
* Intended audience

Identify whether the event is:

* Mission-led and subsidised
* Free
* Revenue-generating

Confirm that the total programme remains within the approved annual budget.

## DECISION POINT

**Is the draft calendar balanced across all five pillars and financially viable within the approved budget?**

**YES →** Proceed to Step 3 and bring the draft calendar to the first Aswatna co-design session.

**NO →** Revise the calendar. Redistribute events to address pillar gaps and resolve all budget overruns before engaging Aswatna.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Draft 12-month calendar showing for every event:

  * Event type
  * Estimated date
  * Programming pillar
  * Lead organiser
  * Budget estimate
* Pillar balance analysis showing at least one event from each pillar per quarter.
* Provisional programming budget agreed with the Finance lead.
* Key cultural and community dates identified and mapped to programming opportunities.

---

# 3. Co-Design the Programme with Aswatna

**Lifecycle Stage:** Aswatna Collaboration
**Timing:** During annual planning and for every major programme cycle

Aswatna is not a booking agent or standard supplier.

It is Palestine House's preferred cultural programming partner.

The relationship is based on co-design and co-curation.

## What Each Partner Brings

| Partner           | Contribution                                                                                              |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Aswatna**       | Curatorial expertise, artist networks, cultural knowledge, programme quality, and creative relationships. |
| **Local Partner** | Community knowledge, venue, operational delivery, audience understanding, and local execution.            |

The strongest Palestine House programmes are created when both sides work together from the planning stage.

The Local Partner should not simply give Aswatna a list of empty programme slots to fill.

## Aswatna Co-Design Process

### Step 3a: Share the Draft Calendar

Share the draft annual calendar with Aswatna at least 60 days before the programming year begins.

Request input on:

* Annual themes
* Format mix
* Pillar balance
* Cultural narrative
* Communities and artists to centre

Do not request artist names only.

### Step 3b: Hold an Annual Programming Strategy Session

Hold at least one annual strategy session with Aswatna before the calendar is finalised.

Discuss:

* Cultural themes
* Priority artists
* Communities to centre
* Programme formats
* The annual narrative arc
* Audience needs
* Emerging opportunities

### Step 3c: Hold Quarterly Co-Design Sessions

For every quarter, meet with Aswatna to:

* Confirm artist selections
* Agree curatorial concepts
* Review production requirements
* Confirm technical requirements
* Discuss co-promotion
* Agree shared-revenue arrangements where applicable

### Step 3d: Document All Agreements

Every Aswatna collaboration agreement must be recorded in writing.

The agreement must include:

* Artist names
* Event dates
* Fees
* Technical requirements
* Revenue share, where applicable
* Responsibilities of each party

Do not rely on verbal agreements.

## DECISION POINT

**Is Aswatna available and engaged as the co-design partner for the programming cycle?**

**YES →** Proceed with Aswatna as the co-design partner and document every agreement in writing.

**NO →** Notify HQ. The Local Partner may not appoint a substitute programming partner without written HQ approval.

> **NOTE:** Any involvement of a cultural organisation other than Aswatna as a programming partner requires HQ approval before an agreement is made. This is a non-negotiable governance requirement.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Annual programming strategy session held with Aswatna.
* Strategy session notes documented.
* Quarterly co-design sessions scheduled.
* Written collaboration agreement in place for every programme cycle.
* HQ notified of any event where Aswatna is not the primary programming partner.

---

# 4. Source, Assess, and Confirm Artists and Cultural Contributors

**Lifecycle Stage:** Artist & Content Sourcing
**Timing:** 6–12 weeks before each event

Every artist, speaker, facilitator, and performer appearing under the Palestine House name represents the organisation to the community.

Artist selection is therefore both:

* A curatorial decision
* A reputational decision

The process must be consistent, documented, and include a non-political positioning assessment before any commitment is made.

## Artist Assessment Criteria

### Curatorial Fit

Assess whether:

* The work connects authentically to the relevant programming pillar.
* The contributor can explain the cultural value of their work.
* The programme contributes meaningfully to Palestinian heritage or contemporary creative life.

### Community Connection

Assess whether the artist or contributor is:

* Known to the community
* Respected by the intended audience
* Meaningful to the cultural context

Take advice from:

* Aswatna
* Local community advisers
* Relevant cultural stakeholders

### Non-Political Positioning Check

Assess whether the artist or contributor has:

* Known political affiliations
* Campaign associations
* Public statements
* Organisational roles

that may conflict with Palestine House's non-partisan positioning.

This check is required for every artist and speaker, not only those where a concern has already been identified.

### Practical and Commercial Fit

Confirm:

* Fee
* Technical rider
* Production requirements
* Availability
* Budget fit
* Delivery feasibility

Do not make a commitment until all practical requirements are confirmed.

## DECISION POINT

**Does the artist pass the non-political positioning assessment without concerns?**

**YES →** Proceed with the booking and document the assessment outcome in the Artist Booking Record.

**NO →** Do not book the artist. Where there is genuine uncertainty about whether the affiliation creates a breach, escalate to HQ for a written ruling before making any commitment.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Artist long-list compiled for every event with Aswatna input.
* Non-political positioning assessment completed and documented for every shortlisted artist.
* Preferred artist confirmed through a signed agreement covering:

  * Fee
  * Dates
  * Technical rider
  * Programme description
* Backup artist identified for high-risk or complex programmes.

---

# 5. Apply the Non-Political Positioning Filter and Obtain HQ Approval

**Lifecycle Stage:** Approval & Scheduling
**Timing:** 6–8 weeks before each event

The non-political positioning assessment is not limited to artist selection.

It applies to every element of the programme, including:

* Programme concept
* Event framing
* Marketing language
* Panel composition
* Partner organisations
* Speakers
* Facilitators
* Public announcements

Every element must be assessed before HQ approval is requested.

## Programme Approval Process

### Step 5a: Complete the Programme Submission Form

Complete a Programme Submission Form for every event or programme cycle.

Include:

* Event title
* Event concept
* Programming pillar or pillars
* Artist or facilitator details
* Partner organisations
* Proposed marketing framing
* Ticket pricing
* Non-political positioning assessment outcome

### Step 5b: Complete the House Manager Review

Before submission to HQ, the House Manager must review the full proposal for alignment with:

* Palestine House values
* Brand standards
* Non-political positioning
* Budget
* Operational feasibility

### Step 5c: Submit to HQ

Submit the completed Programme Submission Form to HQ at least 6 weeks before the event date.

HQ will:

* Approve the programme
* Request amendments
* Flag concerns

within 10 business days.

### Step 5d: Wait for Written Approval

Do not commit:

* Venue spend
* Marketing spend
* Artist fees
* Public announcements
* External partnerships

until written HQ approval is received.

Verbal approval is not sufficient.

## DECISION POINT

**Has HQ issued written approval for the programme?**

**YES →** Proceed to operational delivery planning in Step 6. Retain the written approval on file.

**NO →** Do not proceed. Where HQ requests changes, revise and resubmit. Where HQ declines the proposal, do not attempt to deliver the same programme through another route. Discuss alternatives with HQ.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Completed Programme Submission Form for every event in the cycle.
* Written HQ approval on file before any public commitment.
* Artist agreements signed only after written HQ approval.
* Event dates, venue bookings, and marketing activity cleared to proceed.

---

# 6. Build the Operational Delivery Plan

**Lifecycle Stage:** Delivery & Operations
**Timing:** From 4 weeks before each event through event day

HQ approval permits the programme to move into delivery.

Every Palestine House event, regardless of size, requires:

* A written run-of-show
* Confirmed technical requirements
* A staffing and volunteer plan
* A guest communication plan
* Operational contingencies

Programming quality depends on operational discipline as much as curatorial excellence.

## Event Delivery Plan Components

### Run-of-Show

Create a timed schedule covering:

* Doors open
* Guest arrival
* Welcome
* Programme segments
* Breaks
* Closing
* Guest exit
* Post-event activity

Every team member must receive a copy before event day.

### Technical Setup

Confirm:

* Audio-visual requirements
* Lighting
* Sound
* Staging
* Production requirements

Complete these confirmations with the technical team at least 2 weeks before the event.

Schedule and complete the sound check or technical rehearsal before doors open.

### Staffing Plan

Assign named staff members and volunteers to:

* Welcome desk
* Programme hosting
* Technical support
* Bar or café, where applicable
* Social media coverage
* Close-down

All event-day volunteers must complete the non-political positioning briefing before beginning their duties.

### Guest Communication

Send:

* Ticket confirmation after registration
* Event-day information 48 hours before the event
* Directions
* Accessibility information
* What-to-expect guidance
* Post-event thank-you message and survey within 24 hours of closing

### Contingency Plan

Identify likely risks, including:

* Artist cancellation
* Technical failure
* Low attendance
* Security incident

Document a response for every identified risk.

The House Manager must remain reachable throughout the event.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Written run-of-show distributed to staff and Aswatna collaborators at least 48 hours before the event.
* Technical check completed and signed off before doors open.
* All event-day staff and volunteers briefed.
* Non-political positioning briefing log completed.
* Contingency plan agreed.
* House Manager contact confirmed for event day.

---

# 7. Deliver the Programme and Guest Experience

**Lifecycle Stage:** Delivery & Operations
**Timing:** Event day

The moment the doors open, the reputation of Palestine House is being strengthened or damaged by the guest experience.

The run-of-show is the operational structure, but programme delivery also requires:

* Human judgement
* Awareness
* Presence
* Responsiveness
* Leadership

The House Manager or designated programme lead is responsible for the experience from arrival to close.

## Event-Day Requirements

### Pre-Event Briefing

All staff and volunteers must arrive at least 90 minutes before doors open.

Hold the team briefing 60 minutes before doors open.

The briefing must cover:

* Run-of-show
* Role confirmation
* Non-political positioning reminder
* Safeguarding lead
* Emergency procedures
* Last-minute changes

### Welcome and Arrival

The welcome team must be:

* Warm
* Informed
* Prepared
* Brand-consistent

Where a guest is pre-registered, welcome them by name where practical.

Accessibility needs must be accommodated without drawing unnecessary attention.

### Programme Delivery

Host introductions must not contain:

* Political statements
* Political endorsements
* Signals of political affiliation

Artist introductions must focus on the work and its cultural relevance rather than political positioning.

Monitor the room throughout the programme.

If a participant or performer makes a politically charged statement, the host has authority to redirect the discussion.

### Live Social Media

Any person managing live social media must have completed the non-political positioning briefing.

All captions and stories must be reviewed by the programme lead before posting during the event.

### Close and Exit

The programme must close on time.

Guests should be:

* Thanked warmly
* Invited to complete the feedback survey
* Provided with a QR code or feedback card

The team must remain for a 15-minute debrief before leaving.

## DECISION POINT

**Does any participant, performer, or speaker make a politically affiliated statement or take a politically affiliated action during the event?**

**YES →** The programme host must redirect the conversation immediately. Notify the House Manager and notify HQ within 4 hours.

**NO →** Continue the programme as planned. Record any near-miss situations for discussion during the post-event debrief.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Event delivered on schedule and to Palestine House standards.
* Team debrief completed within 30 minutes of event close.
* Verbal review completed covering:

  * What worked
  * What should improve
* Guest feedback survey issued within 24 hours.
* All conduct, safety, or operational incidents documented and reported within 24 hours.

---

# 8. Capture Data, Feedback, and Financial Outcomes

**Lifecycle Stage:** Review & Renewal
**Timing:** Within 5 working days of each event

A Palestine House event is not complete when the final guest leaves.

It is complete when:

* Data has been captured
* Feedback has been reviewed
* Financial performance has been recorded
* Learning has been documented
* Follow-up actions have been scheduled

Without this discipline, the same mistakes repeat and programme quality improves more slowly.

## Post-Event Data Requirements

### Attendance

Record:

* Total ticket capacity
* Actual attendance
* Member attendance
* Public attendance
* Complimentary attendance
* Partner attendance
* Waitlist numbers
* Attendance compared with similar previous events

### Revenue and Cost

Record:

* Actual ticket revenue
* Projected ticket revenue
* Bar or café revenue, where applicable
* Actual event cost
* Budgeted event cost
* Net contribution to House operations

### Guest Feedback

Record:

* Survey response rate
* Overall experience score
* Programme quality score
* Venue and hospitality score
* Likelihood to return
* Likelihood to recommend
* At least 3 representative qualitative comments

### Social Media Reach

Record:

* Impressions
* Reach
* Engagement
* Notable shares
* Press coverage
* Community response

### CRM Additions

Record:

* New members generated
* New registrations
* New mailing-list contacts
* Follow-up activities
* Assigned follow-up owners

### Programming Debrief Note

Prepare a one-page internal note covering:

* What worked
* What should improve
* Artist feedback
* Aswatna feedback
* Conduct issues
* Operational issues
* Recommendations for similar future programmes

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Attendance, revenue, and cost actuals entered into the programming tracker within 5 working days.
* Guest feedback results compiled and reviewed.
* One-page programming debrief note completed and filed.
* CRM updated with new contacts.
* Follow-up actions scheduled.
* HQ monthly programming report updated with event data.

---

# 9. Conduct the Quarterly Programming Review

**Lifecycle Stage:** Review & Renewal
**Timing:** Quarterly

The annual programming calendar is a plan, not an unchangeable contract.

The quarterly review ensures that the team can:

* Identify pillar imbalance early
* Respond to community feedback
* Adjust to financial performance
* Update the calendar
* Provide HQ with visibility before decisions become irreversible

## Quarterly Review Meeting

**Required duration:** 3–4 hours
**Participants:** House Manager, programme team, and Aswatna

## Quarterly Review Agenda

### Performance Against Calendar

Review:

* Programmes delivered as planned
* Programmes postponed
* Programmes cancelled
* Programmes significantly changed
* Cumulative attendance against target
* Cumulative revenue against target

### Pillar Balance

Assess:

* Whether all five pillars remain represented
* Whether any pillar is under-represented
* Which changes are required in the next quarter

### Community Response

Review:

* Strongest-performing themes
* Strongest-performing formats
* Guest feedback
* Low-attendance formats
* Community requests
* Programmes that should be expanded, adapted, or stopped

### Financial Health

Assess:

* Programming budget position
* Revenue performance
* Cost overruns
* Recovery actions required in the next quarter

### Next Quarter Preview

Review the proposed next-quarter programme with Aswatna.

Confirm:

* Artist commitments
* Programme concepts
* HQ submissions required
* Submission deadlines
* Budget implications

## DECISION POINT

**Has the annual programme delivered across all five pillars, and is the annual financial target on track at the quarterly review?**

**YES →** Confirm the next quarter with Aswatna and proceed with HQ submissions for the next programme cycle.

**NO →** Identify the specific pillar gaps or financial underperformance. Redesign the next quarter before confirming it with Aswatna and notify HQ of any significant calendar changes.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Quarterly programming review completed with the House Manager and Aswatna.
* Pillar balance assessment completed.
* Programme gaps identified.
* Next-quarter calendar adjusted where required.
* Financial performance update prepared and shared with the board and HQ.
* Next-quarter programme confirmed.
* HQ approval submission timeline agreed with Aswatna.

---

# 10. Conduct the Annual Programming Review and Calendar Renewal

**Lifecycle Stage:** Review & Renewal
**Timing:** Annually, 90 days before the new programming year

At the end of every programming year, complete a full retrospective review and design the next annual calendar.

The review must determine whether the programme is:

* Serving the community
* Delivering cultural impact
* Supporting Palestinian artists and cultural contributors
* Building audience engagement
* Supporting financial sustainability

## Annual Review Areas

### Full-Year KPI Assessment

Review:

* Total events delivered against plan
* Total attendance against target
* Average guest satisfaction
* Revenue against annual budget
* New members generated through programming
* Social media reach
* Press coverage

### Pillar Performance

Assess:

* Which pillars generated the strongest response
* Which pillars are most important to the community
* Which pillars are most difficult to monetise
* Whether the balance should change in the following year

### Aswatna Relationship Review

Assess:

* Whether the co-design model worked effectively
* What should change
* New curatorial themes
* New artist relationships
* Opportunities for stronger collaboration

### Community Feedback Synthesis

Identify:

* What the community consistently requested
* Which programmes attendance supported
* Which programmes attendance rejected
* Formats to retire
* Formats to repeat
* New formats to pilot

### New-Year Calendar Design

Draft the next annual calendar using the year's learning.

Submit it to HQ with the annual programming report at least 90 days before the new programming year begins.

> **NOTE:** The annual programming report is the principal programming accountability document between the Local Partner and HQ. It must include full-year KPI performance, pillar balance, financial results, the Aswatna collaboration assessment, and the draft new-year calendar.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Annual programming report submitted to HQ, including:

  * KPI results
  * Pillar analysis
  * Financial performance
* Aswatna relationship review completed.
* New-year co-design approach agreed.
* Draft annual programming calendar submitted to HQ at least 90 days before the new programming year.
* All year-end artist agreements closed.
* Final artist payments processed.
* Community feedback synthesis shared with the House Manager and board.
* New programming year launched with:

  * HQ-approved calendar
  * Aswatna collaboration agreement

---

# THE PROGRAMMING STANDARD

> Every programme is a statement of who Palestine House is. The five pillars are not a menu. They are the framework that ensures every event, artist, and experience delivers something meaningful and lasting for the Palestinian community and for those who believe in Palestinian culture.

The non-political positioning clause is not a restriction on cultural programming. It is the condition that allows Palestine House to remain a space for everyone.

Co-design with Aswatna. Obtain written HQ approval. Deliver with discipline. Learn and improve.

*Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.*

