## Aswātna Studio Collaboration Overview

---

## Purpose

The Aswātna Studio Collaboration element defines the structured partnership between Palestine House and Aswātna as a preferred programming and content partner.

Its purpose is to ensure that cultural programming, storytelling, and creative output are delivered with:

* Cultural depth
* Professional quality
* Narrative consistency
* Strong creative direction
* Alignment with the Palestine House mission

This collaboration is not ad hoc. It is a defined operating relationship that integrates Aswātna into the Palestine House Programming Model as a strategic contributor.

The collaboration supports:

* Programme design
* Artistic curation
* Storytelling
* Content creation
* Creative production
* Programme documentation
* Narrative development

By establishing a structured partnership, Palestine House reduces variability in creative delivery and strengthens the cultural experience across all locations.

Without a defined collaboration model:

* Roles may overlap
* Creative quality may vary
* Timelines may become unclear
* Programming may lack narrative coherence
* Content production may become inconsistent
* Accountability may weaken

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Aswātna Studio Collaboration element is used across programming activities involving:

* Content creation
* Artistic production
* Cultural storytelling
* Creative direction
* Programme documentation
* Audience-facing media

### During Programme Design

Aswātna may contribute to:

* Concept development
* Creative direction
* Programme curation
* Narrative framing
* Artistic planning
* Content strategy

Its contribution must be integrated into the planning process from the beginning rather than introduced after the programme has already been designed.

### During Programme Delivery

Aswātna may support execution through:

* Production
* Facilitation
* Artistic participation
* Creative coordination
* Content delivery
* Technical or production support

Its role must be clearly defined before delivery begins.

### During Post-Programme Activities

Following a programme, Aswātna may support:

* Content capture
* Photography or video documentation
* Editing
* Archiving
* Story development
* Content distribution
* Repackaging programme material for wider audiences

This helps extend the value and reach of programming beyond the physical event.

### Across LOCAL and GLOBAL Programming

The collaboration may be applied to:

* LOCAL programmes
* Network-wide initiatives
* GLOBAL campaigns
* Cross-location cultural projects
* Shared content initiatives

It must be included in planning cycles and must not be treated as an external add-on.

---

## Who Is Responsible

The Aswātna Studio Collaboration is a **SHARED** responsibility between GLOBAL, LOCAL, and ASWĀTNA.

Clear roles, communication, and decision rights are required to prevent duplication and ensure efficient execution.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for:

* Defining the partnership framework
* Setting collaboration standards
* Approving the strategic direction
* Ensuring alignment with the Palestine House mission
* Ensuring alignment with programming pillars
* Protecting brand consistency
* Confirming governance requirements
* Approving major changes to the collaboration model
* Monitoring network-wide quality and consistency

GLOBAL ensures that the collaboration remains strategically aligned and consistent across locations.

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

LOCAL leadership, specifically the General Manager or Head of Operations, is responsible for:

* Integrating Aswātna into local programming plans
* Identifying where collaboration is required
* Coordinating planning and execution
* Confirming operational requirements
* Aligning timelines
* Clarifying roles and deliverables
* Monitoring delivery quality
* Escalating issues where required

LOCAL leadership must ensure that Aswātna's involvement supports the programme rather than creating unclear ownership or operational dependency.

### ASWĀTNA Responsibilities

ASWĀTNA is responsible for delivering agreed creative and production contributions, including:

* Content creation
* Artistic direction
* Cultural curation
* Narrative development
* Production support
* Creative facilitation
* Programme documentation

ASWĀTNA must deliver its work in accordance with:

* Agreed briefs
* Approved timelines
* Palestine House brand standards
* Programming pillars
* Governance requirements
* Quality expectations

### Shared Responsibilities

GLOBAL, LOCAL, and ASWĀTNA share responsibility for:

* Clear communication
* Defined roles
* Agreed timelines
* Approved creative briefs
* Quality review
* Issue escalation
* Post-programme evaluation

Accountability must be established before each collaborative activity begins.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Aswātna Studio Collaboration element enables Palestine House to deliver high-quality, culturally grounded programming with strong narrative consistency.

### Stronger Cultural Quality

Programmes benefit from:

* Deeper cultural insight
* Stronger artistic direction
* More coherent storytelling
* Better curation
* Consistent creative standards

### More Engaging Programming

Programmes become:

* More professionally designed
* More immersive
* More relevant to audiences
* Better aligned with the Programming Model
* More consistent across locations

### Extended Programme Reach

Content created through the collaboration allows programming to continue beyond the event itself.

This may support:

* Digital distribution
* Social media
* Archives
* Promotional content
* Educational material
* Future programme development

### Stronger Audience and Partner Experience

Members, guests, and partners experience:

* Greater cultural depth
* Better creative execution
* Clearer programme identity
* Stronger narrative coherence
* More consistent quality

### Differentiated Programming Standards

Over time, the collaboration supports a recognisable and distinctive programming standard across all Palestine House locations.

This strengthens:

* Brand identity
* Audience trust
* Partner confidence
* Cultural credibility
* Network consistency

### Scalable Creative Delivery

A defined collaboration model makes it easier to expand programming while maintaining:

* Quality
* Narrative consistency
* Cultural alignment
* Production standards
* Operational control

---

## Scope and Limitations

The scope of the Aswātna Studio Collaboration element includes collaborative activities relating to:

* Programme design
* Concept development
* Artistic curation
* Content creation
* Creative production
* Programme delivery
* Cultural storytelling
* Documentation
* Content distribution

It defines how the partnership operates but does not replace the internal responsibilities of Palestine House programming teams.

LOCAL teams must remain capable of:

* Planning programmes
* Managing delivery
* Coordinating partners
* Applying programming standards
* Delivering activities independently when required

The collaboration must not create full dependency on Aswātna for all programming or content.

The effectiveness of the partnership depends on:

* Clear communication
* Defined responsibilities
* Coordinated timelines
* Strong planning
* Approved briefs
* Operational discipline
* Timely feedback
* Consistent quality review

The collaboration cannot compensate for:

* Weak programme planning
* Unclear ownership
* Late decision-making
* Incomplete briefs
* Poor coordination
* Inadequate operational preparation
* Failure to manage timelines

Any programme involving Aswātna must remain aligned with:

* GLOBAL standards
* Palestine House programming pillars
* Brand requirements
* Governance processes
* Quality expectations

Any deviation that affects:

* Brand integrity
* Governance
* Cultural positioning
* Programme quality
* Operational control
* Network consistency

must be reviewed and approved through GLOBAL channels before proceeding.
$md$, $md$# SIMPLE GUIDE

## Aswātna Studio Collaboration

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook | 10 Steps*

---

## PURPOSE OF THIS GUIDE

Aswātna Studio is the creative production and content development arm of the Aswātna relationship.

It enables Palestine House locations to commission, co-produce, and release original Palestinian cultural content, including:

* Recorded music
* Films
* Podcasts
* Digital exhibitions
* Live studio sessions
* Educational content
* Heritage archives
* Campaign content

This guide explains how to activate and manage the Aswātna Studio collaboration, from establishing the partnership and developing creative briefs to managing production milestones, approving content, integrating outputs into programming and platforms, and measuring impact.

The purpose of the collaboration is to extend the Palestine House mission beyond the physical venue and reach Palestinian communities and cultural audiences through professionally produced original content.

---

## WHO THIS GUIDE IS FOR

This guide is for:

* The House Manager
* Programme leads
* Content and communications teams
* Staff responsible for creative production
* Local Partner board members with programme or budget oversight
* Anyone managing Aswātna Studio briefs, approvals, rights, release, or impact reporting

Complete the steps in sequence for every new collaboration.

For an existing collaboration, use this guide to review partnership terms, production governance, consent records, HQ approval, platform use, and impact.

---

## ASWĀTNA STUDIO COLLABORATION LIFECYCLE

| Stage | Focus                  |
| ----: | ---------------------- |
| **1** | Relationship Setup     |
| **2** | Annual Agreement       |
| **3** | Brief Development      |
| **4** | Creative Production    |
| **5** | Review & Approval      |
| **6** | Integration & Delivery |
| **7** | Impact & Renewal       |

---

# 1. Understand the Aswātna Studio Relationship and What It Enables

**Lifecycle Stage:** Relationship Setup
**Timing:** Before any studio collaboration begins

Aswātna Studio is not simply a recording facility to book.

It is a creative partner to activate.

The relationship works in both directions:

| Partner             | Contribution                                                                                         |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Aswātna Studio**  | Artist networks, curatorial expertise, creative development, and production capability.              |
| **Palestine House** | Community reach, venue identity, programming context, audience understanding, and release platforms. |

Together, the partners produce original Palestinian cultural content that neither could create alone.

Before submitting a request or commission, the House Manager and programme lead must understand the available collaboration modes.

## The Five Aswātna Studio Collaboration Modes

### 1. Live Studio Sessions

*Performance & Recording*

Aswātna facilitates a live recording session at, or in association with, Palestine House.

This may involve:

* Connecting Palestinian artists with a production environment
* Recording live performances
* Producing original content for Palestine House digital platforms
* Connecting the recording to a live programme or event

### 2. Original Commission

*Content Creation*

Palestine House commissions an original creative work through Aswātna Studio.

Examples include:

* A new piece of music
* A short film
* A podcast series
* A digital exhibition

Aswātna leads the creative production process.

Palestine House provides:

* The brief
* Community context
* Programming context
* The release platform

### 3. Archive & Preservation

*Heritage Documentation*

Aswātna Studio works with Palestine House to document, archive, and digitise Palestinian cultural heritage held within the local community.

This may include:

* Oral histories
* Traditional music
* Historical photographs
* Craft documentation
* Community archives

The resulting archive becomes a permanent Palestine House asset, subject to the agreed intellectual property and consent terms.

### 4. Educational Content

*Learning & Engagement*

Palestine House and Aswātna Studio co-produce educational content, including:

* Workshop recordings
* Language lessons
* Cultural explainer films
* Artist interviews
* Educational series

The purpose is to extend physical programming beyond the venue through Palestine House digital channels.

### 5. Campaign Content

*Awareness & Fundraising*

Aswātna Studio produces purpose-built content for:

* Awareness campaigns
* Membership drives
* Fundraising initiatives
* Strategic communications

The content must meet a specific communication objective and a professional production standard.

The selected collaboration mode determines:

* The creative brief
* Timeline
* Budget
* Production requirements
* Intellectual property arrangements
* Release strategy

Confirm the collaboration mode with Aswātna before developing the brief.

> **NOTE:** Every Aswātna Studio output must comply with the Palestine House non-political positioning clause. No output may promote, imply affiliation with, or raise funds for any political party, movement, or electoral campaign. This applies to the content, release channels, and associated marketing.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* House Manager and programme lead can describe all five collaboration modes.
* Relevant collaboration mode identified for the planned initiative.
* Non-political positioning clause understood in relation to:

  * Studio content
  * Digital release
  * Platform distribution
  * Promotional activity
* Initial discussion held with the Aswātna Studio contact.
* Aswātna interest and availability confirmed.
* Selected collaboration mode documented and shared with Aswātna.

---

# 2. Agree the Annual Studio Collaboration Framework with Aswātna

**Lifecycle Stage:** Annual Agreement
**Timing:** At the start of each programming year

Studio collaboration works best when planned rather than managed reactively.

At the beginning of each programming year, Palestine House should agree an annual framework with Aswātna covering:

* Planned collaborations
* Collaboration modes
* Overall scope
* Budget
* Intellectual property
* Release platforms
* Approval responsibilities

An annual framework prevents:

* Last-minute production pressure
* Budget overruns
* Unclear responsibilities
* Intellectual property disputes
* Release delays
* Approval confusion

## Required Annual Framework Areas

### Collaboration Scope

Confirm:

* How many collaborations are planned
* Which collaboration modes will be used
* Which quarter each collaboration will take place
* Whether outputs connect to specific events or campaigns

### Budget Envelope

Confirm:

* Total annual studio collaboration budget
* Artist fees
* Studio costs
* Post-production costs
* Aswātna facilitation fees
* Palestine House platform costs
* Which party is responsible for each cost

### Intellectual Property Ownership

Confirm who owns and may use the content.

The default position is:

* Palestine House holds a licence to use outputs on its platforms and within its programming.
* Aswātna and contributing artists retain the underlying rights.

Any departure from this default must be agreed in writing before production begins.

### Release and Platform Agreements

Confirm:

* Palestine House platforms where the content will appear
* Whether the content will also appear on Aswātna platforms
* Any exclusivity period
* Distribution responsibilities
* Upload responsibilities
* Promotion responsibilities

### Approval Process

Confirm:

* Who reviews outputs
* Who approves outputs
* Review turnaround times
* Amendment procedures
* What happens where Palestine House requests changes after production

## DECISION POINT

**Has the annual studio collaboration framework been agreed in writing with Aswātna before production begins?**

**YES →** Proceed. File the signed framework with the annual programming agreement and reference it in every individual studio brief.

**NO →** Do not commission studio work. A verbal understanding is insufficient. Intellectual property, budget, approval, and release terms must be documented before production begins.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Written annual studio collaboration framework signed by Palestine House and Aswātna.
* Budget envelope approved by the Palestine House board.
* Intellectual property ownership and licence terms confirmed in writing.
* Release and platform arrangements documented, including:

  * Digital channels
  * Exclusivity windows
  * Distribution responsibilities
  * Approval responsibilities

---

# 3. Develop a Clear Creative Brief for Every Studio Collaboration

**Lifecycle Stage:** Brief Development
**Timing:** 8–12 weeks before planned production

Aswātna Studio cannot produce what Palestine House cannot clearly describe.

A vague brief is likely to produce generic or misaligned content.

A strong brief must be:

* Precise
* Researched
* Rooted in community insight
* Connected to programming
* Clear about the intended impact
* Specific about creative and operational boundaries

The brief is the foundation of every studio collaboration and is primarily the responsibility of Palestine House.

## Required Studio Brief Sections

### 1. Collaboration Mode and Objective

State:

* Which collaboration mode applies
* The specific purpose of the content
* What the audience should feel, know, or do after engaging with it

### 2. Community and Cultural Context

Define:

* The primary audience
* What the audience already knows
* What the audience may already feel about the subject
* Cultural sensitivities
* Representation priorities
* What the local Palestinian community needs the content to communicate

### 3. Content Parameters

Define:

* Approximate length
* Format
* Language or languages
* Tone
* Style
* Required brand elements
* Palestine House name placement
* Required funder credits
* Non-political positioning requirements
* Content that is explicitly outside scope

### 4. Programming or Campaign Connection

Confirm whether the output is connected to:

* A live event
* An exhibition
* A programme cycle
* A campaign
* A membership initiative
* A fundraising initiative

Document any fixed deadlines created by that connection.

### 5. Artists or Contributors

Confirm whether:

* Palestine House has identified specific contributors, or
* Artist selection is delegated to Aswātna

Either approach is acceptable, but the responsibility must be clear.

### 6. Success Criteria

Define how success will be assessed.

Measures may include:

* Views
* Listens
* Downloads
* Community response
* Programme use
* Event attendance
* Press coverage
* Membership sign-ups
* Campaign milestones

Success criteria must be defined before production begins.

## DECISION POINT

**Has the creative brief been reviewed and agreed by both the House Manager and the Aswātna Studio lead before production begins?**

**YES →** Proceed to HQ notification and production planning.

**NO →** Revise the brief until both parties agree. Do not begin production under an unapproved or verbally agreed brief. Disputes before production are easier to resolve than disputes during or after production.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Written creative brief covering all six required sections.
* Brief reviewed and signed by:

  * House Manager
  * Aswātna Studio lead
* HQ notified of the proposed collaboration.
* Brief shared with HQ for information.
* Production timeline agreed with Aswātna.
* Key milestones, review dates, and fixed deadlines documented.
* Project budget confirmed within the annual framework.

> **NOTE:** HQ approval is required before public release, not before production, unless HQ has stated otherwise for the specific collaboration.

---

# 4. Manage the Production Process as an Active Collaboration

**Lifecycle Stage:** Creative Production
**Timing:** From brief approval through final content delivery

Once production begins, Palestine House must remain an active collaborator.

It must not act as a passive client waiting for a finished output.

Aswātna Studio leads the creative process.

Palestine House remains responsible for:

* Community access
* Consent
* Logistical support
* Milestone feedback
* Brand stewardship
* Non-political positioning review
* Community representation review

The quality of the final output depends on the quality of the collaboration during production.

## Palestine House Responsibilities During Production

### Community Access and Facilitation

Where production requires access to:

* Community members
* Oral history participants
* Heritage objects
* Archives
* Event footage
* Private spaces

Palestine House must facilitate access and secure all required permissions before recording or documentation begins.

### Progress Review at Milestones

Attend every agreed milestone review.

Review:

* Rough cuts
* Draft recordings
* Preliminary edits
* Script drafts
* Visual treatments
* Proposed credits

Feedback must be specific and actionable.

Use the brief as the reference point.

For example:

> This does not meet the brief requirement for [specific requirement].

Avoid vague feedback such as:

> I am not sure about this.

### Non-Political Positioning Monitoring

Review every milestone for potential non-political positioning concerns.

This includes:

* Artist statements
* Visual imagery
* Text overlays
* Lyrics
* Musical associations
* Contributor affiliations
* Marketing framing

Flag concerns immediately.

Do not wait until the final review.

### Brand and Language Review

Confirm that:

* Palestine House naming is correct.
* Logo use follows HQ standards.
* Brand language is accurate.
* Co-branding is approved.
* Aswātna and partner attribution is correct.

Where a new brand treatment is proposed, refer it to HQ before agreeing.

### Community Sensitivity Review

Before finalisation, share the content with at least one trusted community representative who was not involved in production.

Their review should assess:

* Accuracy
* Dignity
* Cultural sensitivity
* Representation of Palestinian identity
* Representation of community experience

This external community perspective is an essential quality-control step.

## DECISION POINT

**Does the content accurately and respectfully represent Palestinian identity, community, and culture at the community sensitivity review stage?**

**YES →** Proceed to final editing and the HQ approval request.

**NO →** Return the content to Aswātna with specific community feedback. If the concern is significant, escalate to HQ before requesting further changes.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Signed participant consents and permissions secured before recording.
* Consent forms filed securely.
* All milestone reviews completed on schedule.
* Written feedback documented and shared with Aswātna.
* Non-political positioning check completed at every milestone.
* All identified concerns resolved before progression.
* Community sensitivity review completed.
* Community feedback integrated into the final output.
* Final content delivered by Aswātna Studio in the agreed format.

---

# 5. Review Final Content and Obtain HQ Approval Before Release

**Lifecycle Stage:** Review & Approval
**Timing:** Before any public release or platform upload

No studio output produced under the Palestine House name may be released publicly without written HQ approval.

This applies regardless of:

* Who produced it
* How much it cost
* How well it has been received internally
* Whether it has already been scheduled
* Whether a partner expects immediate release

HQ approval covers:

* Non-political positioning
* Brand standards
* Intellectual property terms
* Strategic alignment
* Community representation
* Public positioning

This is a non-negotiable release gate.

## Final Review and Approval Process

### Step 5a: House Manager Final Review

The House Manager must review the full final output.

They must not review only selected sections.

Complete the Palestine House Studio Content Review Checklist covering:

* Non-political positioning
* Brand compliance
* Intellectual property
* Consent
* Community representation
* Factual accuracy
* Credits
* Platform readiness

The House Manager must sign off that the content is ready for HQ review.

### Step 5b: Submit the Full Package to HQ

Submit:

* Final content file
* Approved creative brief
* Production milestone notes
* Community sensitivity review outcome
* House Manager sign-off
* Intellectual property documentation
* Consent confirmation

Request written approval before release.

### Step 5c: Respond to HQ Feedback

HQ may:

* Approve the content
* Request amendments
* Decline the content

If amendments are requested:

1. Work with Aswātna to complete them.
2. Resubmit the revised content.
3. Obtain confirmation on the amended final version.

Do not release a changed version without renewed approval.

### Step 5d: File the Written Approval

The written HQ approval email or document is the release authorisation.

File it with:

* The final content
* Creative brief
* Production records
* Consent records
* Intellectual property documents

If the content is later modified, even slightly, the modified version requires new HQ approval.

## DECISION POINT

**Has HQ issued written approval for the exact final version of the studio content before any release action?**

**YES →** Proceed to release planning and platform integration.

**NO →** Do not upload, screen, publish, distribute, or release the content. Written approval must be received first.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* House Manager final review checklist completed and filed.
* Full production package submitted to HQ.
* Written HQ approval received and filed.
* Approval reference recorded in the studio collaboration log.
* All HQ-requested changes completed.
* Amended version re-approved before release.

---

# 6. Integrate Studio Content into Palestine House Programming and Platforms

**Lifecycle Stage:** Integration & Delivery
**Timing:** From HQ approval through public launch

A studio output that remains unused in a file store is a missed opportunity.

Every studio collaboration should extend the Palestine House mission into the digital environment and reach people who may never visit the physical venue.

Effective integration requires:

* A planned release
* Platform-ready files
* Correct attribution
* Programming alignment
* Promotion
* Accessibility
* Rights compliance

## Content Integration Steps

### Platform Preparation

Before release, confirm that the output is correctly formatted for every destination platform.

Requirements may include:

* Video resolution
* Audio bitrate
* Aspect ratio
* Captions
* Subtitles
* Accessibility standards
* Metadata
* Thumbnails
* Descriptions

Aswātna Studio should provide platform-ready files.

Palestine House must confirm compliance before uploading.

### Credit and Attribution

Every output must include the correct credits.

The standard credit line is:

> Created in collaboration with Aswātna Studio for Palestine House [City].

Where external artists or contributors are involved, confirm their credits with Aswātna before release.

Do not attribute the full creative work to Palestine House alone.

Doing so misrepresents the collaboration and may damage the Aswātna relationship.

### Release Timing and Programme Integration

Where possible, connect the digital release to live programming.

Examples include:

* Releasing a studio session before an artist's live performance
* Releasing a documentary alongside an exhibition
* Releasing educational content after a workshop
* Releasing campaign content during a membership or fundraising initiative

Strategic integration increases the impact of both the digital and physical programme.

### Promotion Plan

Develop a promotion plan covering:

* Social media
* Community newsletter
* Press release where appropriate
* Partner platform sharing
* Artist channels
* Aswātna channels

The House Manager must approve all promotional content before publication.

All promotional language must pass the non-political positioning filter.

### Internal Programming Use

Some content may be used within Palestine House through:

* Event screenings
* Installations
* Member distribution
* Exhibitions
* Educational sessions
* Large-format display

Confirm with Aswātna whether the agreed licence covers each intended use.

Additional approval or rights may be required for:

* Public projection
* Public screening
* Large-format display
* Paid distribution
* Third-party licensing

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Content uploaded to all agreed platforms in the correct format.
* House Manager confirms each upload before promotion begins.
* Credits and attribution confirmed with Aswātna.
* Attribution applied consistently across all platforms and materials.
* Release coordinated with the programming calendar where applicable.
* Promotion plan implemented.
* Social media, newsletter, press, and partner activity delivered as agreed.
* Promotional content reviewed for non-political positioning compliance before publication.

---

# 7. Manage Ongoing Consents, Rights, and Content Governance

**Lifecycle Stage:** Integration & Delivery
**Timing:** Ongoing from release through archiving

Governance responsibilities continue after release.

Palestine House must actively manage:

* Participant consent
* Intellectual property
* Usage rights
* Attribution
* Community concerns
* Platform changes
* Content records

This is an ongoing operational responsibility.

## Ongoing Content Governance Responsibilities

### Consent Record Maintenance

Every identifiable person who:

* Appears
* Speaks
* Performs
* Is named
* Is interviewed

must have a signed consent form on file.

Consent records must be:

* Stored securely
* Backed up
* Linked to the relevant content record
* Retained while the content remains in distribution

If a participant withdraws consent:

1. Remove the content from Palestine House platforms promptly.
2. Notify Aswātna.
3. Confirm whether further distribution must stop.
4. Record the action taken.

### Usage Monitoring

Periodically confirm that the content is being used only within the agreed terms.

If the content appears:

* On an unauthorised third-party platform
* Without proper credit
* In a context outside the licence
* In a politically affiliated context

notify Aswātna and seek advice on the correct response.

### Community Response Management

Monitor community reactions.

Positive responses may support future communications and impact reporting.

Critical responses, particularly concerns from Palestinian community members about representation, require a genuine response.

Do not dismiss or deflect serious concerns through social media.

Where a significant representation concern is raised:

1. Temporarily remove the content.
2. Consult HQ and Aswātna.
3. Respond privately and directly to the person raising the concern.
4. Reinstate or revise the content only after HQ guidance.

### Platform Updates

Digital platforms change their:

* Terms
* Algorithms
* Accessibility requirements
* Technical specifications
* Metadata rules

Review hosted content periodically.

Update:

* Captions
* Descriptions
* Metadata
* Accessibility features
* Format compatibility

where required.

## DECISION POINT

**Has a community member raised a significant concern about how they or their community is represented in a studio output?**

**YES →** Temporarily remove the content. Do not argue publicly. Consult HQ and Aswātna, respond privately to the community member, and restore or revise the content only after HQ guidance.

**NO →** Continue normal monitoring and record minor feedback for the next quarterly collaboration review.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Studio content log current and complete.
* Every output records:

  * Release date
  * Platform
  * Consent reference
  * HQ approval reference
  * Intellectual property reference
* Consent forms securely stored and backed up.
* Community response monitoring active.
* Positive and critical feedback logged.
* Withdrawn consent or usage concerns addressed promptly.
* HQ notified where required.

---

# 8. Measure and Report the Impact of Every Studio Collaboration

**Lifecycle Stage:** Impact & Renewal
**Timing:** Within 30 days of release and again after 90 days

Impact measurement for studio content differs from event attendance measurement.

It occurs:

* Over time
* Across several platforms
* Through direct and indirect outcomes
* Through quantitative and qualitative evidence

Measurement is not optional.

HQ and the Palestine House board require evidence that studio investment is producing:

* Cultural impact
* Community value
* Artist value
* Programme value
* Mission alignment

View counts alone are insufficient.

## Studio Impact Metrics

### Reach and Distribution

Record:

* Views
* Listens
* Downloads
* 30-day reach
* 90-day reach
* Geographic distribution where available
* Local diaspora reach
* Platform-by-platform performance

### Engagement Quality

Record:

* Comments
* Shares
* Saves
* Community discussion
* Press coverage
* Arts media coverage
* Community reposts

These measures often indicate deeper engagement than passive views.

### Programming Impact

Assess whether the output generated:

* Event attendance
* Membership sign-ups
* Mailing-list registrations
* Partnership enquiries
* Campaign responses
* Programme engagement

These downstream outcomes may be more valuable than reach alone.

### Artist Impact

Document whether the collaboration led to:

* A career milestone
* Press coverage
* A new performance opportunity
* A commissioning opportunity
* Wider recognition
* Community connection

Palestine House should be able to explain what the collaboration achieved for the artist as well as for the organisation.

### Mission Alignment Assessment

Assess whether the output made Palestine House:

* More visible
* More trusted
* More valued
* More connected to the Palestinian community
* More effective in sharing Palestinian culture

This is qualitative but remains the most important assessment.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* 30-day reach and engagement metrics recorded.
* 90-day reach and engagement metrics recorded.
* Programming impact documented where attributable.
* Membership impact documented where attributable.
* Artist impact story completed.
* Artist impact record shared with Aswātna.
* One-page Studio Impact Report prepared.
* Impact report shared with:

  * Aswātna Studio
  * HQ

---

# 9. Conduct the Quarterly Aswātna Studio Collaboration Review

**Lifecycle Stage:** Impact & Renewal
**Timing:** Quarterly

The Aswātna Studio relationship must be actively maintained.

It must not be assumed to remain healthy without review.

A quarterly review provides a structured opportunity to:

* Review delivery
* Review impact
* Address frustrations
* Protect the co-creative model
* Identify new opportunities
* Review compliance
* Confirm the next-quarter pipeline

The collaboration must not drift into a transactional production-for-hire relationship.

## Quarterly Studio Review Agenda

### Outputs Delivered Against Plan

Review:

* Completed collaborations
* Work in progress
* Delayed outputs
* Cancelled outputs
* Scope changes
* Progress against the annual framework

### Impact Review

Review each completed output's impact report.

Assess:

* Community reach
* Engagement quality
* Strongest-performing formats
* Weakest-performing formats
* Programme impact
* Artist impact

### Relationship Health

Discuss whether:

* The relationship remains genuinely co-creative.
* Aswātna is responding to the community brief.
* Palestine House is providing clear briefs.
* Feedback is timely.
* Deadlines are being respected.
* Production decisions are transparent.

Name frustrations early.

Issues are easier to resolve quarterly than after they have accumulated.

### New Opportunities

Review ideas emerging from:

* Programme delivery
* Community feedback
* Aswātna artist networks
* New campaigns
* New platforms
* Heritage documentation needs

Confirm which opportunities:

* Fit the annual framework
* Require a budget amendment
* Require HQ notification
* Require a new brief

### Non-Political Positioning Compliance

Review:

* Production-stage concerns
* Post-release concerns
* Near misses
* Community concerns
* Pending HQ decisions

Confirm that all issues were resolved appropriately.

## DECISION POINT

**Does the quarterly review identify a significant relationship, compliance, or delivery concern requiring HQ involvement?**

**YES →** Notify HQ promptly with a concise description of the concern and the proposed resolution. Do not resolve a significant issue without HQ input.

**NO →** Document the review and agreed actions. Include a summary in the standard quarterly operations report to HQ.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Quarterly studio collaboration review held with Aswātna.
* Review notes documented.
* Impact reports reviewed.
* Key learning recorded.
* Relationship health assessment completed.
* Concerns identified and resolution actions agreed.
* Next-quarter studio pipeline confirmed.
* New briefs and scope changes flagged to HQ where required.

---

# 10. Conduct the Annual Studio Relationship Review and Renew the Framework

**Lifecycle Stage:** Impact & Renewal
**Timing:** Annually, 60 days before the next framework is agreed

The Aswātna Studio collaboration framework must be reviewed and renewed each year before the next programme begins.

This is a strategic review, not simply an administrative renewal.

The review determines whether:

* The collaboration is achieving its purpose.
* The terms remain fair and workable.
* The investment is generating meaningful cultural impact.
* The relationship remains genuinely collaborative.
* Rights, consent, and approval processes remain effective.

## Annual Review Areas

### Full-Year Impact Assessment

Review:

* Outputs delivered against plan
* Total reach
* Total engagement
* Most impactful collaboration
* Least successful collaboration
* Lessons from both
* Total investment against budget

### Collaboration Quality

Assess:

* Whether Aswātna remained genuinely co-creative
* Whether Palestine House provided clear briefs
* Whether feedback was timely
* Whether deadlines were respected
* Whether intellectual property terms were honoured
* Whether credits were applied correctly

Identify what worked and what must improve on both sides.

### Community Impact

Assess:

* Which outputs reached the Palestinian community
* Which outputs generated genuine engagement
* Which outputs were produced but not meaningfully consumed
* Which content types should continue
* Which content types should change

Community impact, not production quality alone, is the primary measure of success.

### Framework Terms

Review whether the following remain appropriate:

* Intellectual property ownership
* Licence terms
* Budget allocation
* Approval requirements
* Release responsibilities
* Platform distribution
* Exclusivity arrangements

Update the framework where the scale or ambition of the collaboration has changed.

### New-Year Framework Design

Define:

* Number of collaborations
* Collaboration modes
* Quarterly timing
* Budget
* Priority themes
* Platform strategy
* Approval process
* Distribution responsibilities

Draft the new framework with Aswātna.

Submit it to HQ and the board before the new year begins.

> **NOTE:** The annual studio collaboration report submitted to HQ must include all outputs, cumulative reach and impact, the relationship health assessment, and the proposed new-year framework. HQ uses this report to assess the health of the wider Aswātna network relationship.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Annual studio collaboration report completed.
* Report includes:

  * All outputs
  * Impact data
  * Financial performance
  * Community impact
  * Artist impact
  * Relationship assessment
* Report shared with the Palestine House board.
* Report submitted to HQ.
* New-year framework drafted with Aswātna.
* New-year framework approved by HQ and the board before new production begins.
* Year-end rights, consent, and intellectual property records filed and backed up.
* Aswātna Studio relationship assessed as healthy, or a documented improvement plan agreed.
* Aswātna co-creator credits and artist acknowledgements finalised for year-end communications.

