## Event Production SOPs Overview

---

## Purpose

The Event Production SOPs element defines the standardised procedures required to plan, execute, and close all events within Palestine House.

Its purpose is to ensure that every event is delivered with:

* Consistency
* Control
* Quality
* Clear accountability
* Operational discipline

These SOPs establish repeatable procedures for:

* Event preparation
* Team coordination
* Venue setup
* Live execution
* Issue management
* Event close-down
* Post-event review

They reduce variability by ensuring that event delivery does not depend on individual experience, personal judgement, or improvisation.

Instead, every event follows a disciplined operating system that:

* Protects the guest experience
* Supports partners
* Clarifies responsibilities
* Reduces operational risk
* Enables consistent delivery across locations

Without defined Event Production SOPs:

* Roles may become unclear
* Important tasks may be missed
* Guest experience may vary
* Operational risks may increase
* Event quality may depend too heavily on individual staff members

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Event Production SOPs element is used across the full event lifecycle, from initial planning through final evaluation.

### During Pre-Event Planning

The SOPs guide:

* Event objectives
* Timelines
* Roles and responsibilities
* Approval requirements
* Logistics
* Guest requirements
* Technical needs
* Partner coordination
* Risk identification
* Escalation planning

This stage ensures that all requirements are defined before setup begins.

### During Event Setup

The SOPs guide preparation of:

* Event spaces
* Furniture and layouts
* Signage
* Technical equipment
* Guest registration areas
* Accessibility provisions
* Back-of-house areas
* Staff positions
* Safety controls

They also confirm that the team is briefed and ready before guests arrive.

### During Live Execution

The SOPs govern:

* Guest arrival and welcome
* Registration
* Programme delivery
* Staff coordination
* Speaker, artist, or facilitator support
* Technical operations
* Timing
* Issue handling
* Escalation
* Guest safety and experience

Live event decisions must follow defined processes rather than improvised responses wherever possible.

### During Event Close-Down

The SOPs guide:

* Guest departure
* Equipment shutdown
* Venue reset
* Supplier close-out
* Asset checks
* Incident recording
* Final operational checks

### During Post-Event Review

The SOPs are used for:

* Team debrief
* Performance assessment
* Guest feedback review
* Incident review
* Partner feedback
* Documentation
* Corrective action
* Learning for future events

These procedures apply to every event, whether:

* Led internally
* Delivered with ASWĀTNA
* Partner-driven
* Member-focused
* Public-facing
* Small-scale
* Large or complex

They must be integrated into normal operational workflows rather than treated as optional event support.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Event Production SOPs are defined at the GLOBAL level and implemented at the LOCAL level.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for:

* Establishing standard event procedures
* Defining operational non-negotiables
* Setting minimum quality requirements
* Defining approval and escalation thresholds
* Maintaining consistency across all Houses
* Reviewing event performance data
* Incorporating operational learning
* Updating SOPs when required
* Approving significant deviations from standard procedures

GLOBAL ensures that all locations follow a common event production framework.

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

LOCAL leadership, specifically the General Manager or Head of Operations, is responsible for:

* Implementing the approved SOPs
* Ensuring team adherence
* Assigning event ownership
* Confirming readiness before each event
* Monitoring event execution
* Managing escalations
* Ensuring documentation is complete
* Conducting post-event reviews
* Implementing corrective actions

LOCAL leadership must ensure that no event proceeds when critical requirements, controls, or approvals are incomplete.

### Event Lead Responsibilities

The designated Event Lead is responsible for:

* Owning the event plan
* Coordinating timelines
* Assigning tasks
* Confirming team readiness
* Managing suppliers and partners
* Leading event briefings
* Monitoring live delivery
* Recording issues
* Leading the post-event debrief

Every event must have one clearly named Event Lead.

### Operational Team Responsibilities

Operational teams are responsible for:

* Following assigned procedures
* Completing checklists
* Attending event briefings
* Coordinating with other functions
* Reporting issues promptly
* Following escalation paths
* Maintaining guest and safety standards
* Completing required event records

### ASWĀTNA Responsibilities

When involved in an event, ASWĀTNA is responsible for:

* Aligning creative and production activities with approved SOPs
* Following agreed roles and timelines
* Coordinating with the Event Lead
* Meeting technical and quality requirements
* Reporting production risks or changes
* Participating in post-event review where required

### Accountability

Accountability is maintained through:

* Event plans
* Assigned owners
* Operational checklists
* Readiness reviews
* Incident reports
* Post-event debriefs
* Performance reporting
* Corrective action tracking

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, Event Production SOPs ensure that Palestine House events are delivered with high consistency, efficiency, and quality.

### Clearer Team Execution

Teams understand:

* What must be completed
* Who owns each task
* When tasks are due
* What standards apply
* When issues must be escalated

This reduces confusion and improves coordination.

### Consistent Guest Experience

Guests receive a predictable and reliable experience regardless of:

* Event format
* Event size
* Event team
* Delivery partner
* Location

This supports trust in the Palestine House brand.

### Reduced Operational Risk

Structured procedures reduce the likelihood of:

* Missed tasks
* Technical failures
* Safety gaps
* Poor guest handling
* Role confusion
* Delayed escalation
* Incomplete close-down

### More Efficient Delivery

Repeatable procedures improve:

* Planning speed
* Team preparation
* Resource coordination
* Supplier management
* Event readiness
* Post-event learning

### Stronger Partner Confidence

Partners gain confidence because events are:

* Professionally planned
* Clearly managed
* Consistently delivered
* Properly reviewed
* Supported by defined systems

### Scalable Event Production

Over time, the SOPs enable Palestine House to increase the number, variety, and complexity of events without losing:

* Quality
* Control
* Consistency
* Safety
* Brand standards

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Event Production SOPs element includes procedures relating to:

* Event initiation
* Planning
* Approvals
* Scheduling
* Team assignment
* Partner coordination
* Risk assessment
* Venue setup
* Technical setup
* Guest management
* Programme execution
* Issue handling
* Event close-down
* Post-event review
* Documentation

It defines how events should be delivered operationally.

However, it does not replace:

* Event-specific plans
* Creative direction
* Programme content
* Artistic development
* Detailed production briefs
* Event budgets
* Marketing plans
* Legal agreements
* Specialist safety advice
* Technical specifications
* Regulatory permissions

Deviation from approved procedures is not permitted without appropriate review and approval.

Uncontrolled deviation may create:

* Safety risk
* Guest experience failure
* Brand inconsistency
* Operational confusion
* Compliance exposure
* Partner dissatisfaction

The effectiveness of the SOPs depends on:

* Team discipline
* Appropriate training
* Accurate planning
* Resource availability
* Checklist completion
* Timely communication
* Leadership oversight
* Consistent follow-through

The SOPs cannot compensate for:

* Poor planning
* Inadequate staffing
* Missing resources
* Weak leadership
* Incomplete briefings
* Ignored checklists
* Failure to escalate
* Lack of technical preparation

Any event involving:

* High operational risk
* Unusual complexity
* Significant public visibility
* Major brand exposure
* Non-standard production requirements
* Departure from approved procedures

must be escalated to GLOBAL for review and approval before proceeding.

This ensures alignment, control, and consistent event quality across the Palestine House network.
$md$, $md$# SIMPLE GUIDE

## Event Production SOPs

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook | 10 Steps*

---

## PURPOSE OF THIS GUIDE

Event Production SOPs form the operational backbone of Palestine House programming.

They transform a strong event idea into a dignified, professional, and brand-consistent experience every time, regardless of format or scale.

Without a disciplined production process:

* Artists feel unsupported.
* Guests feel confused.
* Staff become overwhelmed.
* Operational risks increase.
* The Palestine House brand suffers.

With a clear system, every Palestine House event, from an intimate salon to a large-scale performance, runs with the calm confidence of a team that understands exactly what must happen and when.

This guide covers the complete seven-stage Event Lifecycle:

1. Intake & Approval
2. Brief & Budget
3. Production Planning
4. Marketing & Ticketing
5. Final Readiness
6. Live Delivery
7. Debrief & Reset

It explains:

* Who owns each stage
* Which requirements are non-negotiable
* Where decision points apply
* Which outputs must be completed
* Which templates must be used

Follow every stage in sequence. Do not skip steps.

---

## WHO THIS GUIDE IS FOR

This guide is for:

* House Directors
* House Managers
* Programming Leads
* Event Producers
* Operations Leads
* Front-of-House Leads
* Technical Leads
* Volunteer Leads
* Staff responsible for marketing, ticketing, artist support, or event reporting

Use this guide for every Palestine House event, regardless of size, audience, format, or funding model.

---

## EVENT PRODUCTION SOPs LIFECYCLE

| Stage | Focus                 |
| ----: | --------------------- |
| **1** | Intake & Approval     |
| **2** | Brief & Budget        |
| **3** | Production Planning   |
| **4** | Marketing & Ticketing |
| **5** | Final Readiness       |
| **6** | Live Delivery         |
| **7** | Debrief & Reset       |

---

# 1. Receive the Event Idea and Open the Intake Process

**Lifecycle Stage:** Intake & Approval
**Timing:** Before an event enters the calendar

Every Palestine House event begins through the same formal intake process, regardless of whether the idea is proposed by:

* A community member
* An artist
* A partner organisation
* A staff member
* The programming team

No resource may be committed, date promised, artist confirmed, or public communication issued before the event passes through intake.

This discipline separates a professionally curated cultural space from an ad hoc events venue.

**Stage Owner:** Programming Lead
**Approver:** House Director

## Intake Process

### 1. Receive the Idea and Create a Draft Calendar Entry

Create a provisional calendar entry.

Do not confirm the event publicly.

Mark the entry as **Draft** until the House Director approves it.

Record:

* Who proposed the event
* Date received
* Core concept in two sentences
* Preferred date
* Alternative date

### 2. Complete the Event Intake Form

Use **Template 1 — Event Intake Form, Section 12.11**.

Every field must be completed.

The form must include:

* Proposed title
* Programming pillar
* Event format
* Target audience
* Expected attendance
* One-sentence event promise
* Risk flags
* Preferred date
* Alternative date

### 3. Confirm the Programming Pillar

Every event must align with one of the five Palestine House programming pillars:

* Arts & Culture
* Food & Heritage
* Ideas & Dialogue
* Community
* Marketplace

If the event does not clearly align with a pillar, it must be redesigned before proceeding.

### 4. Complete the Curation Checklist

Use the curation checklist in Section 10 of the Playbook.

Confirm that the event meets Palestine House standards for:

* Cultural integrity
* Community relevance
* Brand alignment
* Programme quality
* Mission fit

### 5. Conduct the Risk Scan

Assess whether:

* The topic is sensitive.
* The guest is high-profile.
* A large crowd is expected.
* The content may imply a political position.
* The proposed partner creates reputational concerns.
* Security or safeguarding risks exist.

Any identified concern must be flagged for House Director review before approval.

## DECISION POINT

**Does the event pass the curation checklist and risk scan without unresolved concerns?**

**YES →** Present the completed Event Intake Form to the House Director for an approval decision.

**NO →** Do not submit the event for approval. Return it to the proposer with specific feedback. If the concern creates significant brand or political risk, notify HQ before declining, as HQ may need to advise on how the decision is communicated.

## Approval Outcomes

| Outcome        | Required Action                                                                       |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **APPROVE →**  | Confirm the tentative date, appoint a named Event Producer, and proceed to Stage 2.   |
| **REDESIGN →** | Give the proposer specific feedback and return the concept to intake after revision.  |
| **DECLINE →**  | Communicate the decision respectfully, record the reason, and take no further action. |

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Event Intake Form completed and filed.
* All fields completed.
* House Director's approval recorded.
* Programming pillar confirmed.
* Curation checklist completed.
* Risk scan completed.
* All identified risks resolved or escalated.
* Approval outcome documented.
* Tentative date confirmed where approved.
* Named Event Producer appointed and informed.

---

# 2. Write the One-Page Event Brief

**Lifecycle Stage:** Brief & Budget
**Timing:** Immediately after intake approval and at least 6 weeks before the event

The Event Brief is the operational blueprint for the event.

It is the single source of truth shared with:

* The artist or speaker
* Technical staff
* Front-of-house staff
* Marketing staff
* Volunteers
* Operations staff

A vague brief creates a vague event.

A precise brief, grounded in community insight and a clear creative vision, creates a memorable Palestine House experience.

## Required Event Brief Sections

### Event Identity

Include:

* Event title
* Programming pillar
* Format
* Duration

Approved formats include:

* Salon
* Culture Night
* Learning Circle
* Workshop
* Performance
* Film
* Other approved format

### Audience and Promise

Define:

* Who the event is for
* What the audience should feel
* What they should learn
* What they should experience

Express the event promise in one clear sentence.

If this cannot be written clearly, the brief is not ready.

### Run-Time Flow

Provide the initial event sequence:

> Welcome → Main Content → Q&A or Interaction → Close

This becomes the foundation of the final Run of Show during Stage 5.

### Speaker or Artist Details

Include:

* Names
* Roles
* Requirements
* Preferences
* Sensitivities
* Technical rider, where applicable

### Experience Notes

Define:

* The welcome ritual
* The event's story moment
* How the Palestine House mission becomes tangible
* How the member benefit is delivered

### Risks and Sensitivities

Document all:

* Content risks
* Audience concerns
* Delivery concerns
* Safeguarding issues
* Political positioning sensitivities
* Operational risks

### Success Metrics

Define before the event:

* Attendance as a percentage of capacity
* Member conversions
* Revenue target
* Post-event satisfaction score

> **NOTE:** Use **Template 2 — One-Page Event Brief, Section 12.11**. Do not create a custom format. Consistency improves briefing, staff training, handover, and quality review.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* One-Page Event Brief completed.
* All required sections included.
* Event promise stated clearly.
* Initial run-time flow documented.
* Artist and speaker requirements confirmed.
* Risks and sensitivities recorded.
* Success metrics established.
* House Director sign-off obtained.

---

# 3. Build and Approve the Mini Budget

**Lifecycle Stage:** Brief & Budget
**Timing:** Before confirming the event

No Palestine House event may be confirmed without an approved budget.

This includes:

* Locking the event date
* Signing an artist agreement
* Publishing marketing
* Confirming technical suppliers
* Committing external expenditure

A budget that has not been reviewed and signed by the House Director is not approved.

## Required Mini Budget Sections

### Revenue

Include only realistic or confirmed revenue:

* Ticket income based on expected attendance
* Confirmed sponsorship
* Food and beverage uplift
* Other confirmed revenue

Do not include aspirational or unconfirmed figures.

### Costs

Include:

* Artist fees
* Technical and AV costs
* Front-of-house staffing
* Duty Manager costs
* Security, where required
* Marketing costs
* Printed materials
* Event materials
* Contingency of at least 10% of total costs

### Break-Even Attendance

State the minimum number of ticket sales required to cover all event costs.

This figure is the financial floor below which the event cannot proceed without an approved subsidy or redesign.

## DECISION POINT

**Has the House Director reviewed and signed the Mini Budget, and does the event remain financially viable at realistic attendance?**

**YES →** File the signed Mini Budget with the Event Brief and proceed to Stage 3.

**NO →** Do not proceed. Redesign the event to bring costs and revenue into alignment, or postpone it until acceptable budget conditions are in place.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Mini Budget completed.
* Revenue documented.
* Costs documented.
* Contingency of at least 10% included.
* Break-even attendance calculated and stated.
* House Director approval recorded.
* Event Brief and Mini Budget filed together.
* Event date confirmed.
* Event Producer briefed.
* Artist or speaker notified only after approval.

---

# 4. Build the Full Operational Plan

**Lifecycle Stage:** Production Planning
**Timing:** 4–6 weeks before the event

Production planning converts the approved brief into a deliverable operational plan.

The Event Producer leads this stage in collaboration with:

* Operations Lead
* Front-of-House Lead
* Technical Lead
* Volunteer Lead, where applicable

The goal is to ensure that every person knows:

* What they must do
* When they must do it
* Where it must happen
* Which equipment is required
* Who holds decision authority

## Production Planning Checklist

### Confirm the Technical Rider

Review all artist or speaker requirements, including:

* Microphones
* Monitors
* Projection
* Internet
* Lighting
* Staging
* Power
* Playback
* Accessibility equipment

Identify and resolve gaps between the rider and venue capability before event day.

### Design the Seating Plan

Account for:

* Total capacity
* Wheelchair positions
* Clear access aisles
* Member priority seating
* VIP or artist guest seating
* Emergency routes
* Technical positions

Create a documented layout.

Do not leave the seating arrangement to event day.

### Confirm Stage, Screen, and AV Setup

Confirm:

* Stage or presentation position
* Screen placement
* Projection path
* Speaker positions
* Control desk
* Load-in route
* Load-in time

### Set the Event-Day Timeline

Build the timeline backwards from the event start time.

The standard structure is:

| Timing    | Requirement                            |
| --------- | -------------------------------------- |
| **T-180** | Setup begins                           |
| **T-120** | Full technical check                   |
| **T-90**  | Soundcheck or rehearsal                |
| **T-60**  | Front-of-house briefing and door setup |
| **T-30**  | Doors open                             |
| **T-0**   | Event begins                           |
| **T+End** | Close and reset begin                  |

Adjust the timing to the event format, but protect rehearsal and soundcheck time.

### Build the Staffing Plan

Assign a named person to every role:

* Duty Manager
* Event Producer
* Front-of-House Lead
* Technical Lead
* Volunteer Lead, where volunteers are used
* Artist liaison
* Content capture lead
* Reset Owner

A named Duty Manager is mandatory for every event.

### Plan Door and Queue Flow

Confirm:

* Queue location
* Member priority process
* Entry route
* Ticket scanning process
* Manual backup process
* Late-entry procedure
* Escalation process

### Prepare Artist and Speaker Hospitality

Confirm:

* Backstage or green-room space
* Water
* Seating
* Quiet preparation area
* Run of Show
* Arrival time
* Required location at each stage
* Payment
* Contract paperwork
* Departure process

Payment and paperwork must not be left until event day.

### Confirm Content Capture

Where photography or filming is planned, confirm:

* Artist permission
* Speaker permission
* Guest consent requirements
* Capture boundaries
* Intended use
* Named photographer or videographer
* Storage and rights arrangements

## DECISION POINT

**Has a named Duty Manager been confirmed for the event?**

**YES →** Proceed. Ensure the Duty Manager receives the complete staffing and production plan.

**NO →** Do not proceed to Marketing & Ticketing or Final Readiness. A Duty Manager is mandatory. If no Duty Manager can be secured, postpone the event.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Technical rider reviewed.
* AV requirements confirmed.
* Technical gaps resolved.
* Seating plan completed.
* Capacity and accessibility confirmed.
* Member priority section defined.
* Event-day timeline documented.
* Staffing plan completed.
* Duty Manager confirmed in writing.
* Door and queue process agreed.
* Ticket scanning backup confirmed.
* Artist hospitality plan prepared.
* Payment and paperwork resolved.
* Content capture permissions confirmed.

---

# 5. Set Up Ticketing and Publish the Event

**Lifecycle Stage:** Marketing & Ticketing
**Timing:** At least 10–14 days before the event

An event that cannot be easily found, understood, and booked will not fill.

Marketing and ticketing are core operational functions.

Before publication, the team must be able to explain the event compellingly in one sentence.

Weak framing leads to weak attendance.

## Ticketing Standards

### Publication Timing

Ticketing must open at least 10–14 days before the event.

High-demand or high-profile events should open 3–4 weeks in advance.

### Ticketing Information

The ticketing page must clearly state:

* Doors-open time
* Event start time
* Late-entry policy
* Refund policy
* Accessibility information
* Member benefit
* Venue information
* Age restriction, where applicable

Ambiguity creates conflict at the door.

### Member Benefit

The applicable member benefit must be visible during booking.

This may include:

* Priority seating
* Early access
* Discount
* Reserved allocation

Members must be able to understand and use the benefit at the booking stage.

## Minimum Marketing Pack

### Event Description

Write one strong event description in the Palestine House tone.

It must be:

* Culturally informed
* Warm
* Specific
* Original

Do not copy the artist's press release without adaptation.

### Three to Five Key Hooks

Explain why the audience should attend.

Hooks are reasons, not general descriptions.

### One to Three Visuals

Use:

* Event artwork
* Artist photograph
* Speaker photograph
* Programme graphic

All visuals must be approved by the House Manager before publication.

### Artist or Speaker Biography

Write for a general audience.

Prioritise:

* Context
* Story
* Cultural relevance
* Contribution

Do not present only a professional CV.

### “Why This Matters” Statement

Include one sentence connecting the event to:

* Palestinian culture
* Community
* Heritage
* Creativity
* The Palestine House mission

## DECISION POINT

**Can the event be explained compellingly in one sentence, and are all ticketing terms clear and visuals approved?**

**YES →** Publish the event. Notify members first, then open booking to the general public.

**NO →** Do not publish. Rewrite the description, clarify all terms, and obtain visual approval before going live.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Ticketing link live at least 10–14 days before the event.
* All terms clearly stated.
* Member benefit visible.
* Member communication sent before public launch.
* Marketing pack published across active channels.
* Promotional copy approved.
* Visuals approved by the House Manager.

---

# 6. Complete the 72-Hour and 24-Hour Readiness Checks

**Lifecycle Stage:** Final Readiness
**Timing:** 72 hours and 24 hours before the event

Final Readiness is a mandatory two-stage quality gate.

It is not a casual check-in.

It is the final opportunity to resolve gaps before they become event-day crises.

Both checklists must be:

* Completed
* Signed
* Dated
* Filed

## 72-Hour Readiness Check

**Owner:** Event Producer
**Participants:** Operations, Front of House, Technical Lead, and optionally the House Director

Confirm:

* [ ] Artist or speaker confirmed in writing.
* [ ] Artist or speaker briefed on the Run of Show, venue, and schedule.
* [ ] Draft Run of Show completed.
* [ ] Draft Run of Show distributed to all team members.
* [ ] All technical and AV requirements confirmed.
* [ ] No unresolved technical items.
* [ ] Staffing plan complete.
* [ ] Every role assigned to a named individual.
* [ ] Seating and room layout confirmed.
* [ ] Member priority section marked.
* [ ] Ticketing link live.
* [ ] Sales tracking against break-even attendance reviewed.
* [ ] Member communications sent.
* [ ] Public communications sent.
* [ ] Risk mini-plan prepared where required.

A risk mini-plan is required for:

* Large crowds
* Sensitive subjects
* High-profile guests
* Security concerns

## DECISION POINT

**Are three or more items on the 72-hour checklist incomplete or unresolved?**

**YES →** Escalate immediately to the House Director. Assess whether the event can responsibly proceed or whether postponement is required.

**NO →** Sign and file the 72-hour checklist, then proceed to the 24-hour check.

## 24-Hour Final Check

**Owner:** Event Producer

Confirm:

* [ ] Final Run of Show distributed.
* [ ] No further Run of Show changes planned.
* [ ] Queue signage prepared.
* [ ] Doors-open time displayed.
* [ ] Event title signage prepared.
* [ ] Accessibility routing signage prepared.
* [ ] VIP and guest list prepared.
* [ ] Printed guest list available as backup.
* [ ] Ticket scanning system tested.
* [ ] Manual ticket list printed.
* [ ] Content capture permissions confirmed in writing.
* [ ] Named Reset Owner confirmed.
* [ ] Post-event reset responsibilities assigned.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Signed and dated 72-Hour Readiness Checklist filed.
* Exceptions documented.
* Signed and dated 24-Hour Final Check filed.
* Final Run of Show distributed.
* All remaining gaps resolved or escalated before event day.

---

# 7. Execute the Event to Palestine House Standard

**Lifecycle Stage:** Live Delivery
**Timing:** Event day, from setup through final guest exit

Event day makes every previous planning decision visible.

The Duty Manager holds overall command.

The Event Producer manages programme flow.

Every other team member performs their assigned function.

The required standard is:

* Calm
* Professional
* Guest-centred
* Consistent
* Safe
* Brand-aligned

Consistency is created by completing Stages 1–5 correctly, not through event-day improvisation.

## Pre-Show Timeline

### T-180 Minutes: Setup Begins

The Operations team prepares:

* Seating
* Stage
* Signage
* Technical positions
* Guest routes
* Accessibility areas

Follow the approved plan.

Do not improvise without authorisation.

### T-120 Minutes: Full Technical Check

The Technical Lead tests:

* Sound
* Projection
* Lighting
* Microphones
* Playback
* Internet
* Presentation files

Resolve failures before soundcheck.

### T-90 Minutes: Soundcheck and Rehearsal

This is protected time.

Do not allow setup delays to remove or shorten it.

Artists and speakers must receive a calm and focused preparation environment.

### T-60 Minutes: Front-of-House Briefing

**Required duration:** 5 minutes
**Template:** Template 7 — FOH Briefing Script, Section 12.11

Cover:

* Event purpose in one sentence
* Member handling
* Queue process
* Ticket scanning
* Late-entry policy
* Escalation route
* Duty Manager contact
* One specific hospitality focus

This briefing is mandatory.

### T-30 Minutes: Doors Open

Apply **Template 8 — Door Policy Guide**.

Confirm:

* Member priority applied
* Ticketing system active
* Backup list ready
* Duty Manager in position

## Event-Day Standards

### Late Entry

| Format              | Late-Entry Rule                          |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| **Talks**           | Allow quiet entry during natural breaks. |
| **Film Screenings** | Limited late entry only.                 |
| **Performances**    | Allow entry between segments only.       |

Do not negotiate policy at the door.

If a situation escalates, involve the Duty Manager.

### Artist and Speaker Hospitality

One named person must manage the artist or speaker experience from arrival to departure.

They must confirm:

* Run of Show
* Audience profile
* Q&A plan
* Holding area
* Stage entry
* Departure process

Payment and paperwork must already be complete.

### Stop-the-Event Authority

The Duty Manager may pause or stop the event immediately where:

* Crowd safety is compromised.
* Harassment or threats occur.
* Fire or building safety issues arise.
* Technical failures create unsafe conditions.

The Duty Manager does not require group approval before acting.

Safety takes priority over programme continuity.

## DECISION POINT

**Has a safety, harassment, or crowd-management incident occurred?**

**YES →** The Duty Manager must stop the event immediately. Contact emergency services where required. Record the incident using Template 11 — Incident Log Add-On and notify the House Director before leaving the venue.

**NO →** Continue delivery while the Duty Manager and Front-of-House Lead maintain active situational awareness.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* FOH Briefing Script completed and signed.
* Run of Show executed.
* Deviations recorded.
* Door policy applied consistently.
* Member priority delivered.
* Late-entry rules followed.
* Artist or speaker hosted professionally.
* Incidents recorded immediately.
* House Director notified on the same night where required.

---

# 8. Run the Five-Minute Debrief

**Lifecycle Stage:** Debrief & Reset
**Timing:** Same night or the following morning

The debrief is mandatory.

It is the discipline that allows the organisation to improve rather than repeat the same problems.

The Event Producer owns the process.

Use **Template 10 — Five-Minute Debrief, Section 12.11**.

## Debrief Questions

### What Worked Best?

Identify one specific element that should be repeated or made standard.

### What Was the Biggest Friction Point?

Identify the most significant:

* Problem
* Delay
* Confusion
* Failure
* Near miss

Focus on one priority issue.

### Were There Incidents or Near Misses?

Where applicable, confirm:

* Incident Log completed
* House Director notified
* Immediate actions taken

### What Should Change Next Time?

Identify one practical change.

Do not record only a general aspiration.

### What Is the Improvement Action?

Assign:

* A named owner
* A deadline
* The required change

Actions without deadlines are unlikely to be completed.

> **NOTE:** Record the debrief in the Issues and Learning Log. Do not leave it only in a team chat, email, or individual notes. If it is not in the log, it does not exist as organisational learning. HQ may inspect this record during quarterly quality audits.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Five-Minute Debrief completed.
* Debrief completed the same night or by the following morning.
* One improvement action identified.
* Named owner assigned.
* Deadline recorded.
* Debrief entered into the Issues and Learning Log.
* Incident Log completed where required.

---

# 9. Complete the Physical Reset

**Lifecycle Stage:** Debrief & Reset
**Timing:** Before the final team member leaves

The reset protects the Palestine House brand.

A venue left disordered after an event weakens the next guest's experience and signals that standards are conditional.

They are not.

A named Reset Owner must be assigned before the event.

The Reset Owner must be one person, not “everyone”.

Use **Template 9 — Post-Event Reset Checklist, Section 12.11**.

## Post-Event Reset Checklist

* [ ] Remove all rubbish and glassware.
* [ ] Wipe tables and surfaces.
* [ ] Restore seating to the standard daily layout.
* [ ] Confirm the layout against the approved diagram.
* [ ] Clean bathrooms to the minimum operating standard.
* [ ] Secure microphones.
* [ ] Store cables safely.
* [ ] Store all AV equipment correctly.
* [ ] Update the “Today” display for the following day.
* [ ] Check for venue damage.
* [ ] Check for equipment damage.
* [ ] Photograph all damage.
* [ ] Record all maintenance issues.

## DECISION POINT

**Has damage to the venue or equipment been identified during the reset?**

**YES →** Photograph and log it immediately. Notify the House Director before leaving the venue. Do not wait until the following business day.

**NO →** Complete, sign, and file the reset checklist before the Reset Owner leaves.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Post-Event Reset Checklist completed.
* Checklist signed by the Reset Owner.
* Space restored to the standard layout.
* Damage photographed.
* Maintenance issues logged.
* House Director notified before staff leave where required.

---

# 10. Apply the Special Format SOP

**Lifecycle Stage:** Special Formats & Ongoing Standards
**Timing:** Applied during Stages 3, 5, and 6

Palestine House uses five core event formats.

Each format has additional requirements that sit on top of the standard seven-stage Event Lifecycle.

These are not optional additions.

They protect:

* Guest experience
* Artist relationships
* Safety
* Programme quality
* Brand consistency

Identify the event format during Stage 1 and apply the relevant requirements from Stage 3 onwards.

## Format-Specific Requirements

| Format                         | Additional SOP Requirements                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Talks & Panels**             | Moderator brief completed before event day; Q&A rules explained at the opening; questions must be respectful and concise; no speeches from the audience; timekeeper assigned; microphone routing plan prepared. |
| **Film Screenings**            | Projection and sound tested before doors open; film licensing confirmed before marketing is published; no entry after the first 5 minutes; content warning issued where applicable.                             |
| **Live Music & Performance**   | Soundcheck protected; setup delays must not reduce rehearsal time; artist arrival, green room, stage entrance, and exit route confirmed; audience movement rules included in the FOH briefing.                  |
| **Workshops**                  | Materials checklist completed; all supplies in the venue before event day; registration and no-show policy communicated; facilitator support assigned; room setup confirmed.                                    |
| **Salons & Community Circles** | Seating arranged in a circle or in the round; discussion ground rules explained at the opening; House Director or senior Programming Lead present or available throughout.                                      |

---

