# SECTION 1: COMMON MISTAKES

## Frequent Event Production Setup and Execution Mistakes

*These issues typically arise during Lifecycle Stages 1–4.*

---

## 01. Running an Event Without a Signed Run-Sheet and HQ-Approved Programme Outline

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

The event concept is agreed verbally with Aswatna or a LOCAL partner.

The team is enthusiastic and logistics begin before:

* A written run-sheet has been produced.
* The programme outline has been submitted to HQ.
* HQ approval has been received.

Activities may already include:

* Ordering catering
* Booking AV
* Sending invitations
* Confirming vendors

During the event, the team improvises:

* Sequencing
* Timing
* Transitions
* Responsibilities

If a speaker overruns, AV fails, or a guest interaction creates reputational risk, there is no documented plan to follow.

### Why It Matters

A run-sheet is not an administrative formality.

It is the document that gives every:

* Team member
* Vendor
* Volunteer
* Aswatna representative

the same understanding of:

* What happens
* When it happens
* In what order
* Who is responsible

Without a run-sheet, sequencing depends on the experience and memory of whoever is leading the room. This is a fragile system that often fails under pressure.

HQ programme approval is the governance check confirming that the event complies with:

* Non-political positioning
* Brand standards
* Quality requirements

Running an event without either document leaves Palestine House without:

* A governance record
* A shared operational plan
* A reliable response structure when something goes wrong

### What to Do

Treat the run-sheet and HQ-approved programme outline as prerequisites.

Apply the following rule:

> No invitation may be sent, no vendor may be contracted, and no booking may be confirmed before the run-sheet is drafted and the programme outline is submitted to HQ.

Use the HQ Programme Review SOP as the formal approval gate.

Level B review is required for all flagship events.

Provide the final run-sheet to:

* Every team member
* Each vendor lead
* The Aswatna point of contact

at least 5 business days before the event.

### Escalate If

An event proceeds without:

* A signed run-sheet
* HQ programme approval

and a reputational, safety, or brand issue occurs during or after the event.

Notify HQ immediately with a complete incident note.

Do not attempt to manage the consequences without HQ involvement.

---

## 02. Booking Speakers or Performers Without Completing Vetting and the Non-Political Positioning Compliance Check

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

A well-known Palestinian artist or public figure is recommended by:

* A community member
* Aswatna
* A trusted partner

The team and community are enthusiastic.

The booking is confirmed and promoted publicly before anyone reviews the person's:

* Public profile
* Social media history
* Political affiliations
* Public statements
* Previous event history

A concern later emerges when a community member, journalist, or funder identifies a political statement or association after the booking is already public.

### Why It Matters

The non-political positioning standard applies to everyone appearing on a Palestine House platform, not only to the formal content of the event.

A speaker or performer publicly associated with:

* A political faction
* A boycott campaign
* A disputed political position
* A politically affiliated organisation

brings that association into Palestine House as soon as their name is connected with the event.

This requirement is not intended to restrict Palestinian expression.

It protects Palestine House as a space that Palestinian community members can enter regardless of their political views.

Confirming and promoting a booking before vetting is complete means reputational exposure already exists by the time the concern is identified.

### What to Do

Apply the speaker and artist vetting checklist to every booking before confirmation.

The checklist must cover:

* Public political affiliations
* Recent social media content
* Previous event history
* Known associations with funders or organisations that may create an ethics conflict
* Relevant public statements

For high-profile or internationally known individuals, complete the **HQ Level C Sensitive Review** covering:

* Programmes
* Brand
* Risk
* Executive review

Do not publicly promote a Level B or Level C booking until the required HQ review is complete.

### Escalate If

A speaker or performer has been publicly confirmed and a political affiliation, ethical concern, or reputational risk is identified afterwards.

Notify HQ before issuing any public communication.

Do not:

* Cancel the booking publicly
* Amend the announcement
* Publish an explanation
* Respond to enquiries

without HQ guidance.

---

## 03. Assigning Volunteers or Junior Staff to Safety and Crowd Management Roles Without Proper Briefing

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

The event is well attended and the team is stretched.

A volunteer who arrived shortly before the event is assigned to manage:

* The entrance
* The queue
* Guest flow

A junior employee without first-aid or emergency-protocol training is designated as the incident contact.

No one has been properly briefed on:

* Capacity limits
* Emergency exits
* The assembly point
* Medical procedures
* The escalation process

The team assumes that the event will run smoothly because previous events have.

### Why It Matters

Crowd management and event safety responsibilities cannot be assigned solely according to availability.

They must be assigned to someone briefed on:

* The specific venue
* The event's legal capacity
* Emergency exits
* Medical procedures
* Evacuation protocol
* Crowd distress response

Palestine House events may include:

* Older community members
* Families with young children
* People who have experienced trauma
* Guests with accessibility needs

A safety failure is not only a legal liability.

It is a fundamental breach of the dignity and duty of care Palestine House owes its community.

### What to Do

Assign a named **Safety Lead** for every event.

The Safety Lead must appear on the run-sheet and receive a pre-event briefing covering:

* Venue capacity and enforcement
* Emergency exits
* Assembly point
* First-aid kit location
* Nearest medical facility
* Incident escalation sequence
* Emergency contact numbers
* Calm crowd communication
* Evacuation responsibilities

Do not assign this responsibility to a first-time volunteer on the day of the event.

### Escalate If

A safety incident occurs involving:

* Injury
* Medical emergency
* Overcrowding
* Guest distress

and the Safety Lead role was either:

* Unfilled
* Assigned to someone who had not been briefed

Notify HQ within 1 hour using the Incident Escalation SOP.

Do not issue a public statement without HQ approval.

---

## 04. Treating the Post-Event Debrief and Reporting Obligation as Optional or Informal

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

The event ends late and the team is tired.

The general feeling is that the event went well.

The post-event debrief is:

* Skipped
* Replaced by informal WhatsApp messages
* Delayed until the detail is forgotten

The report to HQ may be:

* Late
* Incomplete
* Not submitted

Guest feedback forms may have been distributed but are not collected or analysed.

The next event is planned without documenting lessons from the previous one.

### Why It Matters

Post-event reporting is the mechanism through which Palestine House learns:

* What worked
* What failed
* Which risks are emerging
* Which problems are recurring

HQ uses event data to identify:

* Programming quality trends
* Safety incidents
* Community feedback patterns
* Repeated operational failures

Informal communication produces no shared learning record.

This means the same errors may be repeated:

* At the same House
* At another House
* Across different events

HQ also cannot fulfil its quality assurance role for events that are not properly reported.

### What to Do

Include the post-event debrief and report as mandatory final actions within the run-sheet.

The debrief must take place for 30 minutes:

* On the same day, or
* The following morning

It must cover:

* Attendance against capacity
* Guest feedback highlights
* Operational issues
* Corrective actions
* Safety observations
* AV and production notes
* Financial results against budget

Submit the standard HQ event report within 5 business days.

Collect guest feedback and include a summary in the report.

### Escalate If

The debrief identifies:

* A safety incident
* A non-political positioning concern
* A guest complaint that was not resolved during the event

Report the matter through the Incident Escalation SOP.

Do not rely on the standard post-event report for these issues.

---

# SECTION 2: RISKS

## Structural Risks in Event Production

*These risks are most active during Lifecycle Stages 2–6.*

---

## 05. Vendor and Supplier Contracts Without Clear Scope, Cancellation Terms, and Intellectual Property Clauses

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

A supplier is booked through email or WhatsApp with the price agreed verbally.

The supplier may be:

* A caterer
* An AV company
* A photographer
* A videographer
* Another event contractor

Problems later arise:

* Quality is below expectation.
* The supplier invoices beyond the agreed amount.
* A cancellation results in a demand for full payment.
* A photographer claims exclusive ownership of event images.
* The agreed scope cannot be proved.

There is no signed contract to consult.

### Why It Matters

Vendor disputes involving:

* Cancellation fees
* Content rights
* Scope changes
* Payment terms

are common and preventable.

They usually arise because important terms were assumed rather than documented.

Image and content rights are particularly important because event media may later be used in:

* Grant applications
* Promotional campaigns
* HQ toolkit materials
* Media coverage
* Digital platforms

If those rights are not clearly granted, every later use may create legal exposure.

### What to Do

Require a signed scope agreement for every vendor engagement above the locally defined minimum threshold.

The agreement must specify:

* Exact scope of service
* Delivery date
* Delivery format
* Payment schedule
* Payment amount
* Cancellation terms for both parties
* Intellectual property and image rights
* Ownership of produced content
* Restrictions on the supplier's use of Palestine House branding or event content

For photography and video, the default clause should grant Palestine House unlimited rights to use the material for:

* Non-commercial purposes
* Promotional purposes

Where Aswatna contributed to production, the agreed Aswatna credit must be included.

Contracts above the high-value threshold must be reviewed by a legally competent person before signature.

### Escalate If

A vendor dispute concerning:

* Image rights
* Cancellation fees
* Scope
* Payment

cannot be resolved through direct negotiation.

Notify HQ before:

* Paying more than the agreed amount
* Sending formal legal correspondence
* Threatening legal action
* Making a public statement

---

## 06. Underestimating Venue Capacity and Overcrowding at High-Demand Events

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

An event sells out and attracts strong community interest.

Additional people arrive, including:

* Walk-in guests
* Guests of registered attendees
* Volunteers not included in the count
* Community members expecting informal entry

The venue reaches or exceeds its legal capacity.

Further warning signs may include:

* An overwhelmed Safety Lead
* Partially obstructed exits
* Congested entrances
* Informal crowd management
* Reluctance to turn community members away

### Why It Matters

Venue capacity is a legal requirement, not a guideline.

Exceeding permitted occupancy can result in:

* Suspension of the venue's operating permit
* Personal liability for organisers
* Regulatory enforcement
* Serious crowd-safety consequences

A safety failure would also damage the trust the community places in Palestine House and may affect future venue permits.

### What to Do

Record the maximum permitted occupancy as the first item in every event run-sheet.

The ticketing and registration system must enforce the limit.

There must be no walk-in exceptions once capacity is reached.

Brief the Safety Lead on:

* How to count attendees
* How to control entry
* How to enforce the limit
* How to decline entry respectfully

Use a waiting list for high-demand events.

Where possible, provide an Aswatna-branded alternative, such as:

* A livestream
* An overflow session
* A future repeat event

### Escalate If

A capacity breach occurs during an event and the Safety Lead cannot resolve it immediately.

Notify HQ during the event, not afterwards.

Document the circumstances in the post-event incident report.

---

## 07. AV and Technical Failures Caused by Insufficient Testing and Backup Planning

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

The AV system is set up shortly before the event.

The team relies on the fact that the equipment worked previously, but does not test the actual event sequence.

The rehearsal does not cover:

* The speaker's presentation
* Backing tracks
* Microphone handovers
* Video playback
* Lighting cues
* Segment transitions

During the event:

* Microphones create feedback.
* The presentation clicker fails.
* Content does not play.
* Transitions create long silences.
* A speaker or performer cannot be heard.

### Why It Matters

Technical failure at the start of an event can define the entire Guest Journey.

It is particularly damaging because it suggests operational immaturity.

Palestinian community events often carry significant cultural and emotional importance.

Guests expect an execution standard that reflects the seriousness of the mission.

Failures that could have been identified during a short rehearsal are especially avoidable.

### What to Do

Conduct a complete technical rehearsal for every event using AV.

The rehearsal must begin at least 90 minutes before doors open.

Test the programme in its actual sequence, including:

* Every microphone
* Each speaker and performer
* Presentation transitions
* Video playback
* Sound cues
* Lighting cues
* Segment handovers

The AV Lead must prepare a one-page technical run-sheet aligned with the main event run-sheet.

Share it with speakers and performers in advance.

Every event must have backup arrangements for at least:

### Microphone Failure

* Backup microphone location
* Activation process
* Named person responsible

### Presentation Failure

* Backup file on USB
* Secondary device
* Printed speaker notes

### Escalate If

A technical failure causes:

* A speaker to become inaudible
* A performance to end early
* A significant programme interruption
* A delay of more than 20 minutes

Document:

* The failure
* Its cause
* Which backup procedures existed
* Which backup procedures were used
* Corrective action

Include this in the post-event report.

---

# SECTION 3: RED FLAGS

## Warning Signs of Deeper Production Integrity or Governance Failures

*These issues require investigation at any lifecycle stage.*

---

## 08. A Pattern of Events Consistently Exceeding Budget Without Pre-Approval or Post-Event Reconciliation

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What It Looks Like

Individual event budgets are approved by the House Director and submitted to HQ.

Across multiple events, actual spending exceeds the approved budget by 20–40%.

Reasons may include:

* Last-minute vendor changes
* Unexpected guest numbers
* Unplanned AV costs
* Poorly scoped supplier work

The overspend is noted but:

* No formal reconciliation occurs.
* No budget amendment was approved.
* No corrective action is taken.

### Why It Matters

Repeated overspending is a financial governance signal, not simply an event-management problem.

It may indicate that:

* Budgets are poorly constructed.
* Approval is not operating as a genuine gate.
* Production controls are weak.
* Scope is changing without authorisation.

A sustained pattern damages credibility with:

* Funders
* HQ
* The board
* The community

It also consumes financial runway that was allocated elsewhere.

### What to Do

Complete a formal budget reconciliation within 5 business days of every event.

The reconciliation must show:

* Approved budget
* Actual expenditure
* Variance
* Explanation for every variance above 10%

If two consecutive events exceed budget by more than 20% without a pre-approved amendment, conduct a production-planning review with HQ involvement.

Corrective action may include:

* Earlier vendor quotations
* Stronger scope definition
* Clearer approval limits
* Contingency budget lines
* Tighter purchasing controls

### Escalate If

Budget overruns continue after the House Director has been informed and the pattern remains unresolved across two quarterly cycles.

Notify HQ with the supporting financial data.

Do not wait for a wider financial sustainability crisis.

---

## 09. Event Content Collectively Drifts Towards Political Framing Across the Programming Calendar

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What It Looks Like

Each event individually passes the non-political positioning review and receives HQ approval.

However, when the calendar is assessed as a whole, it demonstrates a consistent political direction.

This may include:

* Repeated positive references to one political movement
* Consistent amplification of certain perspectives
* Consistent exclusion of other perspectives
* A recurring pattern of politically associated speakers or performers

No single event may represent an obvious breach, but the portfolio creates an identifiable association.

### Why It Matters

The non-political positioning standard applies to the body of programming produced under the Palestine House name.

A political pattern built through multiple individually approved events is more difficult to detect and correct than a single obvious breach.

It may become visible to:

* Funders
* Journalists
* Community members with different political views
* External partners

Once the pattern is established publicly, correcting it can be difficult.

### What to Do

Conduct a portfolio-level political positioning review every quarter.

Assign the review to someone with sufficient independence from programme development.

Ask:

> If a journalist or funder reviewed the last three months of events as one body of programming, what political association might they identify?

Document the outcome even where no concern is found.

Review:

* Speaker mix
* Performer mix
* Event framing
* Recurring themes
* Perspectives included
* Perspectives repeatedly absent

### Escalate If

The portfolio review identifies a consistent political direction, even where individual events were HQ-approved.

Notify HQ before making programming changes.

HQ will assess the programme portfolio and advise on the response.

---

# SECTION 4: COMPLIANCE ISSUES

## Specific Compliance Requirements for Event Production

*These requirements are active primarily during Lifecycle Stages 3–6.*

---

## 10. Hosting a Public Event Without Confirming All Required Permits and Insurance

**Category:** COMPLIANCE ISSUE

### What It Looks Like

The event date approaches and:

* The venue is booked.
* Speakers are confirmed.
* Invitations are distributed.
* Promotion is live.

The team assumes that the venue's general licence covers the event.

It has not confirmed:

* Whether a specific public-event permit is required
* Whether expected attendance triggers additional requirements
* Whether Palestine House insurance covers the event format
* Whether catering or alcohol requires additional approval

A local authority representative may attend and identify the gap.

The event may be interrupted or closed.

### Why It Matters

Permit and insurance requirements protect:

* Attendees
* The venue
* The production team
* The organisation

Requirements vary according to:

* City
* Venue type
* Event format
* Audience size
* Food service
* Alcohol service

An unpermitted event may result in:

* Regulatory action
* Damage to the venue relationship
* Review of the operating licence
* Restrictions on future events

An insurance gap may create direct personal and organisational liability if an incident occurs.

### What to Do

Use a permits and insurance checklist as a mandatory pre-event gate.

Complete it before public promotion begins.

The checklist must confirm:

* Venue operating licence validity
* Permission for the specific event type
* LOCAL authority permit requirements
* Event liability insurance coverage
* Food-hygiene certification where catering is provided

Assign the check to a named team member.

Require written confirmation rather than assumption.

### Escalate If

An event proceeds without confirmed permits or insurance and:

* An incident occurs
* An inspection takes place
* An authority raises a query

Notify HQ immediately using the Incident Escalation SOP.

Do not submit a formal response to the authority without HQ Legal involvement.

---

# SECTION 5: ESCALATION TRIGGERS

## Situations Requiring Immediate HQ Involvement

> **NOTE:** Every escalation trigger below is non-negotiable. Do not attempt to manage these situations locally without notifying HQ.

---

## E1. A Safety or Medical Incident Occurs During an Event

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What It Looks Like

During an event:

* A guest collapses.
* A medical emergency occurs.
* Crowd distress develops.
* A fire alarm is activated.
* The venue is evacuated.
* A physical altercation takes place.

The LOCAL team addresses the immediate situation but does not notify HQ because it appears to have been resolved before the event ends.

### Why It Matters

Every safety or medical incident is a mandatory HQ notification, regardless of the LOCAL resolution.

HQ is responsible for safety governance across the network and cannot fulfil that responsibility without being informed.

An incident that appears resolved may later result in:

* A guest complaint
* A regulatory investigation
* An insurance claim
* Media attention
* Legal action

Without early notification, HQ cannot provide:

* Governance support
* Legal guidance
* Insurance coordination
* Consistent communications management

### What to Do

Activate the Incident Escalation SOP immediately.

The Safety Lead must:

1. Address the immediate safety or medical need.
2. Contact emergency services where required.
3. Notify HQ through the emergency escalation line during the event.
4. Complete the Incident Note within 24 hours.
5. Include a formal incident review in the post-event debrief.
6. Preserve all relevant evidence and records.

Do not issue:

* A public statement
* A social media update
* A press response

without HQ approval.

### Escalate If

Any of the following occurs:

* Safety incident
* Medical emergency
* Emergency evacuation
* Crowd distress
* Physical altercation

Notify HQ within 1 hour.

Where the incident involves:

* A minor
* Law enforcement
* A formal complaint

notify HQ immediately during the event.

---

## E2. Post-Event Discovery That an Approved Speaker or Performer Breached Non-Political Positioning

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What It Looks Like

After the event, a concern is raised by:

* A community member
* A journalist
* A social media user

The concern relates to a statement made by a speaker or performer that included:

* Explicit political framing
* A partisan statement
* Reference to a political faction
* Endorsement of a boycott campaign beyond Palestine House's non-political positioning

The statement was:

* Not in the approved speaker brief
* Not identified during the event
* Potentially recorded or shared publicly

### Why It Matters

A live non-political positioning breach that is later circulated publicly is one of the most serious event compliance failures.

The content has already been connected with the Palestine House brand before:

* A live audience
* Social media audiences
* Media
* Funders

The fact that it was not pre-approved does not reduce the reputational exposure.

The response must be:

* Immediate
* Factual
* Coordinated
* Led by HQ

### What to Do

Do not issue any public response before notifying HQ.

This includes:

* Social media posts
* Press statements
* Replies to community messages
* Direct messages
* Public corrections

Document:

* The exact statement
* The context
* Who identified it
* Available recordings
* Screenshots
* Links
* Audience feedback
* Media or community response

Preserve all recordings for HQ review.

Notify HQ within 4 hours of discovering the concern.

### Escalate If

Any person identifies a live speaker or performer statement after the event that may breach non-political positioning and was not addressed during the event.

Notify HQ within 4 hours.

Do not communicate publicly without HQ guidance.

---

# THE PRODUCTION STANDARD

Event production is one of the most visible expressions of the Palestine House brand.

Every event demonstrates the organisation's commitment to:

* Quality
* Dignity
* Safety
* Community
* Operational discipline

The following are mandatory protections:

* HQ-approved programme outline
* Signed run-sheet
* Speaker and performer vetting
* Non-political positioning review
* Signed vendor agreements
* Permit and insurance confirmation
* Named and briefed Safety Lead
* Full technical rehearsal
* Post-event debrief
* HQ reporting
* Incident escalation protocol

These protections exist because Palestine House events carry the organisation's name into a community that has trusted it to operate properly.

Every protection is also a protection for the Palestinian community whose cultural life is being hosted and celebrated.

Do not shortcut the process in the excitement of a busy programme.

***Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.***

---

