## Guest Journey & Member Journey Overview

---

## Purpose

The Guest Journey & Member Journey element defines the end-to-end experience of individuals interacting with Palestine House, from first awareness to long-term engagement and retention.

Its purpose is to ensure that every touchpoint is intentional, structured, and aligned with the brand, transforming casual visitors into active participants and long-term members.

This element provides a clear operational framework for how people enter, experience, and continue engaging with the House, ensuring consistency across all stages. It also enables the organization to manage transitions between stages, such as:

* Guest to member
* Initial participation to deeper involvement
* First visit to long-term engagement

By structuring these transitions, Palestine House can reduce drop-off and maximize engagement.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

This element is used across all operational phases and touchpoints, including:

* Marketing
* Front-of-house operations
* Programming
* Post-event engagement
* Membership management

During the awareness and acquisition stage, it is applied across communication channels, outreach activities, and event promotion to attract the right audience.

During the on-site experience, it guides how guests are:

* Welcomed
* Oriented
* Supported
* Engaged

In the post-visit phase, it informs:

* Follow-up communication
* Feedback collection
* Membership conversion
* Continued engagement

For existing members, it defines how engagement is sustained through programming, communication, and service delivery.

This element is active continuously and must be integrated into daily operations, event planning, and performance tracking.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Responsibility for the Guest Journey & Member Journey is shared across LOCAL operations, with structured oversight from GLOBAL.

### LOCAL Responsibilities

The General Manager or Head of Operations is accountable for ensuring that the journey is:

* Clearly defined
* Properly implemented
* Consistently delivered
* Continuously improved

Front-of-house teams are responsible for delivering the on-site experience, including:

* Welcoming guests
* Providing guidance
* Supporting orientation
* Managing guest interactions

Marketing and community teams are responsible for:

* Pre-visit engagement
* Audience communication
* Event promotion
* Post-visit follow-up
* Membership conversion communication

Programming teams contribute by designing experiences that align with the different stages of the guest and member journey.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for:

* Defining the core journey standards
* Ensuring consistency across locations
* Protecting the overall experience model
* Approving major changes that affect the guest or member experience

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, this element produces clear and measurable improvements in engagement, conversion, and retention.

Guests experience a seamless and intentional journey, reducing confusion and increasing satisfaction.

Conversion from guest to member improves through:

* Structured follow-up
* Clear value communication
* Consistent engagement opportunities
* Well-managed transitions

Members engage more frequently and more deeply, resulting in:

* Higher retention
* Stronger community connection
* Increased participation
* Greater long-term involvement

Operational efficiency also improves because teams have clear guidance on how to manage interactions and transitions at each stage.

Over time, Palestine House builds a consistent reputation as a well-structured and welcoming environment where individuals feel guided, supported, and encouraged throughout their engagement.

---

## Scope and Limitations

The scope of this element includes all stages of interaction, from initial awareness to long-term member engagement.

It covers both physical and digital touchpoints, including:

* Marketing and outreach
* Event registration
* Arrival and welcome
* On-site participation
* Post-visit follow-up
* Membership conversion
* Ongoing member communication
* Retention and re-engagement

This element defines the overall structure of the journey but does not replace the detailed tools required for execution, including:

* Standard Operating Procedures
* CRM workflows
* Communication templates
* Staff training materials
* Tracking systems
* Performance dashboards

It also does not allow unstructured or inconsistent local adaptation. All local variations must remain aligned with GLOBAL standards.

The effectiveness of this element depends on:

* Disciplined execution
* Accurate data tracking
* Staff training
* Cross-team coordination
* Consistency in delivery

Key limitations include dependency on data quality, staff capability, and the consistent execution of each touchpoint.

Any change that affects the guest or member experience must be reviewed and approved to ensure alignment with the overall brand and operational model.
$md$, $md$# SIMPLE GUIDE

## Guest Journey & Member Journey

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook*

---

## PURPOSE OF THIS GUIDE

This guide explains how to design, deliver, and continuously improve the Guest Journey and Member Journey at your Palestine House location.

These are not two separate programmes. They are two stages of the same relationship:

> Every guest is a potential member. Every member was once a guest.

Work through each step in sequence:

* **Steps 1–4:** Guest Journey — how someone discovers, visits, and experiences Palestine House
* **Steps 5–8:** Member Journey — how a guest becomes a member and how that relationship is sustained
* **Steps 9–10:** Tracking, reporting, and continuous improvement across both journeys

---

## WHO THIS GUIDE IS FOR

This guide is for the House Manager and any staff member responsible for:

* Guest welcome
* Membership
* Communications
* Events
* CRM management

The Guest Journey and Member Journey are not the responsibility of one department. They are the responsibility of everyone at Palestine House.

### Responsibility Split

* **HQ** provides the framework and digital platform.
* **LOCAL operators** are responsible for delivery at the venue level.

---

# LIFECYCLE STAGES

## Guest Journey & Member Journey

Palestine House operates two interlocking journeys.

The **Guest Journey** begins when someone first hears about Palestine House. It continues through their first and subsequent visits and ends when they either:

* Do not return, or
* Convert to membership

The **Member Journey** begins when someone joins. It continues through:

* Onboarding
* Active membership
* Renewal
* Advocacy

---

# GUEST JOURNEY

## Four Stages

### 1. DISCOVER

#### Awareness & First Contact

**Who:** Potential visitor

**What happens:**
The individual hears about Palestine House through social media, word of mouth, a community event, or a partner organisation. They may visit the website or follow a social media channel.

**Goal:**
*Curiosity — they want to know more.*

---

### 2. ARRIVE

#### First Visit

**Who:** First-time guest

**What happens:**
The guest arrives at the venue for the first time, receives a warm welcome, explores the space, and attends a programme or event. They encounter the Palestine House brand, mission, and Guest Promise in person.

**Goal:**
*A positive first impression that makes them want to return.*

---

### 3. EXPERIENCE

#### Ongoing Visits

**Who:** Returning guest

**What happens:**
The guest attends multiple events or visits over time, builds familiarity with the space and team, and begins to identify as a community member rather than an occasional visitor.

**Goal:**
*Belonging — a sense that Palestine House is their space.*

---

### 4. CONVERT

#### Membership Invitation

**Who:** Conversion-ready guest

**What happens:**
The guest is invited to join as a member, understands the membership offer, and decides whether to join.

**Goal:**
*A committed relationship — from occasional visitor to invested member.*

---

# MEMBER JOURNEY

## Four Stages

### 5. JOIN

#### Membership Onboarding

**Who:** New member

**What happens:**
The individual completes the membership process, receives confirmation and a welcome pack, is introduced to their benefits, and is recorded in the CRM.

**Goal:**
*A member who understands what they have joined and feels welcomed.*

---

### 6. ENGAGE

#### Active Membership

**Who:** Active member

**What happens:**
The member attends events and programmes, uses member benefits, receives regular communications, and participates in the Palestine House community.

**Goal:**
*A member who participates, contributes, and feels a sense of ownership.*

---

### 7. RENEW

#### Renewal & Retention

**Who:** Renewing or lapsing member

**What happens:**
The member is approached before their membership expires. They either renew or lapse. Lapsed members receive a respectful re-engagement communication.

**Goal:**
*A high renewal rate — members renew because they feel the value.*

---

### 8. ADVOCATE

#### Community Champion

**Who:** Long-standing member

**What happens:**
The member refers friends and community members, participates in member-led activities, and becomes part of the community identity of the location.

**Goal:**
*Organic growth through community advocacy — members recruit members.*

---

# 1. Map Your Current Guest and Member Journeys

Before improving the Guest and Member Journey, assess where your location currently stands.

This assessment must be completed:

* Before opening, or
* Within the first month of using this guide if the House is already operating

## Guest Journey Assessment

Ask:

* How do most guests first hear about Palestine House?
* What happens during the first 60 seconds of a guest's first visit?
* Is there a process for following up after a visit?
* Are guests actively invited to become members, or is membership entirely self-service?

## Member Journey Assessment

Ask:

* What does a new member receive within the first 48 hours?
* How and how often do we communicate with members?
* When does the renewal process begin relative to the expiry date?
* Can we identify members at risk of lapsing before they lapse?

> **NOTE:** You do not need to have answers to every question before proceeding. The purpose is to identify gaps where no process currently exists. The remaining steps in this guide address those gaps.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Written list of all current journey touchpoints
* Clear identification of where processes exist and where gaps remain
* House Manager can describe the current first-visit experience in three sentences
* House Manager can describe the current membership onboarding process in three sentences

---

# 2. Set Up the Discovery and Awareness Stage

The Guest Journey begins before a person enters Palestine House. It starts when they first become aware that Palestine House exists.

The purpose of this stage is to ensure that awareness is:

* Positive
* Accurate
* Consistent with the brand
* Connected to a clear next step

## Discovery Touchpoints

### Website

Ensure that:

* Event listings are current
* Membership is clearly explained
* The Guest Promise is visible
* The website functions correctly on mobile
* Booking or RSVP options are easy to find

### Social Media

Publish regular content that communicates:

* The mission
* Current programming
* The atmosphere of Palestine House
* Opportunities to attend or participate

All content must use approved brand assets and HQ-approved language.

### Community Referrals

Partners, community organisations, and existing members must be briefed on how to describe Palestine House.

They should direct interested individuals to:

* The website
* Event listings
* Booking pages
* Venue visit information

### Local Press and Media

Where relevant, press coverage must use accurate, mission-aligned language.

HQ must approve significant:

* Press statements
* Media interviews
* Public announcements

### Google and Maps Listings

Ensure that all listings contain accurate:

* Address details
* Opening hours
* Contact information
* Website links
* Venue information

## DECISION POINT

**Does every discovery touchpoint lead a potential visitor to a clear next step, such as booking an event, visiting the venue, or finding out more?**

**YES →** Continue. Discovery is working and visitors have a clear path to their first visit.

**NO →** Identify the channel without a clear next step and add a booking link, event listing, or visit invitation before proceeding.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Website event listings are current and include booking or RSVP functionality
* Social media content is published regularly using approved brand assets
* Palestine House is correctly listed on Google Maps
* At least three active community referral routes are briefed and operating

---

# 3. Deliver the First Visit Experience

The first visit is one of the most important moments in the Guest Journey.

Everything the guest experiences during their first 30 minutes contributes to their lasting impression. The Guest Promise must be felt, not merely displayed.

## First Visit SOP

The following must happen every time, without exception.

### Within 30 Seconds of Arrival

A staff member acknowledges the guest:

* By name, when they have booked
* With a warm greeting, when they are a walk-in

No guest should remain standing unacknowledged at the entrance.

### Introduction to the Space

The guest receives a brief orientation covering:

* The main spaces
* What is happening that day
* Where to find membership information
* Where to find upcoming events
* Where to ask for assistance

### Guest Promise Delivery

The first interaction must reflect:

* Dignity
* Inclusion
* Excellence

Staff must:

* Greet respectfully
* Answer questions fully
* Avoid rushing the guest
* Offer support where appropriate

### CRM Capture

When the guest is new, their name and contact information should be recorded with their consent through:

* Event registration
* A sign-in process
* A digital form
* A direct membership invitation

### Mission Presence

The mission statement and Guest Promise must be visible within the space.

The guest should encounter the purpose of Palestine House naturally through the environment.

> **NOTE:** The first visit is where the Guest Promise either becomes real or becomes empty. If a guest is not acknowledged within 30 seconds, printed materials will not recover the first impression.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* First Visit SOP is written, trained, and on file
* All guest-facing staff can explain the 30-second arrival protocol
* CRM capture is built into the first-visit process
* Guest Promise delivery is reviewed through at least one mystery visit or staff observation each month

---

# 4. Build the Returning Guest Experience

A guest who visits once is valuable. A guest who returns repeatedly is a potential future member.

The returning guest stage is where occasional interest develops into community belonging.

## How to Build a Strong Returning Guest Experience

### Recognise Returning Guests

Where CRM data allows:

* Greet returning guests by name
* Refer to their previous visit
* Acknowledge their interests
* Make them feel recognised rather than anonymous

### Maintain Programme Quality and Variety

The programme must provide a meaningful reason to return.

Work with Aswatna to maintain:

* Cultural quality
* Consistency
* Variety
* Relevance

### Complete Post-Visit Follow-Up

After every event, send attendees a follow-up communication that includes:

* A thank-you
* A highlight from the event
* Information about upcoming activities
* A clear next step

This closes the visit loop and encourages a return.

### Maintain the Environment

The physical space must maintain its standard on every visit.

This includes:

* Cleanliness
* Comfort
* Accessibility
* Brand consistency
* Operational readiness

Standards must not be limited to launch day or major events.

### Introduce Membership

By the guest's second or third visit, they should understand that membership is available.

This should feel like an invitation to deepen their relationship with Palestine House, not a sales pitch.

## DECISION POINT

**Has the guest visited at least twice and received a post-visit follow-up after each visit?**

**YES →** Continue. The guest is in the returning guest stage and is being managed correctly.

**NO →** Review the gap. Determine whether the follow-up process failed or whether the guest did not return after their first visit.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Post-visit follow-up is sent within 48 hours of every attended event
* CRM records include visit history for guests who provided contact details
* Returning guests can be identified in the CRM
* Membership is introduced naturally by the second or third visit

---

# 5. Convert Guests to Members

The conversion from guest to member is the transition point between the Guest Journey and the Member Journey.

It must be actively and deliberately managed rather than left to chance.

## Manage the Conversion Moment

### Make the Membership Offer Clear

Every guest who has visited twice or more should understand:

* What membership costs
* What membership includes
* What benefits are available
* How to join

The offer should be communicated through:

* Venue materials
* Post-visit communications
* Direct conversations
* The website

### Make the Invitation Personal

A direct invitation from a staff member or House Manager is more effective than passive signage.

For example:

> "We would love you to become a member."

### Keep the Process Simple

A guest who chooses to join should be able to complete the process in under five minutes through:

* The website
* An in-venue process
* A phone-assisted process

Friction in the joining process is a major cause of conversion failure.

### Use the CRM

Record:

* The date of the membership invitation
* Whether the guest joined or declined
* The reason for declining, where known
* The appropriate follow-up date

This prevents repeated and inappropriate invitations.

## DECISION POINT

**Can a guest complete the membership process in under five minutes through at least two channels, including online and in-venue?**

**YES →** Continue. The conversion process is accessible and ready.

**NO →** Identify and resolve the friction point before inviting guests to join.

Possible friction points include:

* A difficult form
* Payment problems
* No in-venue option
* No digital option
* Unclear membership information

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Membership offer is clearly displayed at reception
* Membership is included in post-visit communication
* Online and in-venue joining processes are tested and functional
* CRM records all membership invitations, outcomes, and follow-up dates
* Guest-to-member conversion rate is tracked monthly

---

# 6. Onboard New Members

The first 48 hours of membership are as important as the first visit.

A member who receives no communication after joining may question their decision. A member who receives a structured and personal welcome is more likely to engage and renew.

## New Member Onboarding Requirements

The following must be completed within 48 hours of joining.

### Welcome Message

Send a personal welcome from the House Manager that:

* Uses the member's name
* Thanks them for joining
* Acknowledges their decision
* Welcomes them into the community

This should not be limited to a generic automated email.

### Membership Confirmation

Clearly confirm:

* Membership tier
* Start date
* Expiry date
* Full list of benefits
* How to access those benefits

### Immediate Next Steps

Provide at least two specific invitations, such as:

* An upcoming event
* A member activity
* A benefit to use
* A community opportunity

Give the new member an immediate reason to engage.

### CRM Record

Create or update the CRM record with:

* Membership tier
* Start date
* Expiry date
* Contact details
* Interests or preferences, where known

### Introduction to the Community

Where possible, introduce the new member to:

* A staff member
* An existing member
* A relevant community contact

This is particularly important for members who joined digitally and have not yet visited.

> **NOTE:** Onboarding is not administration. It is the first chapter of the Member Journey. The quality of onboarding is a strong indicator of future renewal.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Personal welcome message sent within 24 hours
* Membership confirmation includes tier, start date, expiry date, and benefits
* At least two specific engagement invitations are sent within the first week
* Complete CRM record is created within 48 hours

---

# 7. Deliver and Sustain the Active Member Experience

A member who joined three months ago and has not heard from Palestine House since is a member in name only.

Active membership requires regular, meaningful engagement throughout the full period between joining and renewal.

## What Sustained Member Engagement Looks Like

### Regular Member Communications

Members receive at least one dedicated communication every month.

This must not be only a general newsletter. It should acknowledge their membership and include:

* Member-specific information
* Relevant opportunities
* Member benefits
* Priority invitations

### Member Events and Programming

At least one activity each month should be:

* Member-priority, or
* Member-exclusive

Members must have a meaningful reason to participate that non-members do not receive.

### Benefits Delivery

Every benefit promised at the point of joining must be delivered.

Confirm that:

* Discount codes work
* Priority access is honoured
* Member recognition is consistent
* Benefits are clearly communicated
* Staff understand how each benefit operates

### CRM Monitoring

Review the CRM monthly to identify members who have not:

* Attended an event, or
* Opened or responded to a communication

for 60 days.

These individuals are considered at risk of lapsing and require proactive re-engagement.

### Feedback and Listening

Ask members for feedback at least once per quarter through:

* Informal conversations
* Short surveys
* Member meetings
* Member events

Acknowledge their feedback and act on it where possible.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Member-specific communication sent at least monthly
* At least one member-priority or member-exclusive activity held each month
* All membership benefits are operational and consistently delivered
* Monthly CRM review identifies at-risk members
* Member feedback is collected at least quarterly

---

# 8. Manage Renewal and Prevent Lapsing

Renewal is the moment of truth in the Member Journey.

A member who renews has felt the value of membership. A member who lapses may be communicating, often silently, that the value was not felt.

Renewal should feel like a natural continuation of the relationship, not a sales transaction.

## Renewal Timeline

The renewal process must begin six weeks before expiry.

### Six Weeks Before Expiry

Send a warm renewal reminder that:

* Uses the member's name
* Refers to their membership year
* Mentions events attended, benefits used, or involvement where possible
* Includes a clear renewal link or process

### Three Weeks Before Expiry

When the member has not renewed, send a personal follow-up from the House Manager.

This must not be only an automated reminder.

Express a genuine desire for the member to continue.

### On the Expiry Date

When the member has still not renewed:

* Send a final renewal opportunity
* Offer a brief period to renew at the same rate
* Explain when access to benefits will end

After the stated period, the membership lapses.

### Within 30 Days After Lapse

Send one respectful re-engagement communication that:

* Acknowledges the lapse
* Expresses that the member is missed
* Invites them to return as a guest
* Explains that they can rejoin at any time

Do not continue pursuing them after this communication.

One follow-up is respectful. Repeated contact becomes pressure.

## DECISION POINT

**Is the renewal sequence configured in the CRM for six weeks, three weeks, and the expiry date, with a personal House Manager message at the three-week stage?**

**YES →** Continue. The renewal process is active.

**NO →** Configure the automated reminders and schedule the three-week personal message as a manual House Manager task.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Renewal reminders are active at six weeks, three weeks, and expiry
* Personal House Manager message is sent at the three-week stage
* Lapse rate is tracked monthly
* Annual lapse target remains below 20%
* Post-lapse re-engagement is sent within 30 days

---

# 9. Track Journey Performance and Use Data

The Guest Journey and Member Journey can only be improved when they are measured.

Palestine House uses the CRM system to track performance across both journeys. The House Manager is responsible for reviewing the data monthly and using it to improve weak points.

## Guest Journey Metrics

### First Visit Count

How many new guests visited for the first time this month?

### Return Visit Rate

Of the guests who visited last month, how many returned this month?

### Conversion Rate

Of the guests who visited at least twice, how many became members within three months of their first visit?

## Member Journey Metrics

### New Members

How many new members joined this month?

### Active Member Engagement

How many members attended at least one event or used at least one benefit this month?

### At-Risk Members

How many members have had no recorded engagement for 60 days?

### Renewal Rate

Of all memberships that expired this month, what percentage renewed?

### Advocacy Indicator

How many new guests were referred by an existing member?

> **NOTE:** If the CRM cannot track every metric, begin with the two most important measures for the current stage, typically new member count and renewal rate. Track them manually until the CRM is configured. Do not let perfect be the enemy of useful.

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Monthly journey report completed
* At-risk member list produced at the beginning of each month
* Conversion-rate trend tracked over at least three months
* Journey data reviewed with the full team each month

---

# 10. Report Journey Performance to HQ and Continuously Improve

HQ monitors Guest Journey and Member Journey performance across the Palestine House network.

The monthly report must include a dedicated **Guest & Member Journey** section, not only financial or event-delivery KPIs.

HQ uses this information to:

* Identify best practices
* Support locations experiencing difficulty
* Compare performance across the network
* Track the overall health of Palestine House

## Monthly HQ Reporting Requirements

Include:

* New guest count
* Primary discovery channel driving first visits
* New member count
* Primary membership conversion channel or moment
* Active member engagement rate
* Renewal rate
* Number of at-risk members
* Re-engagement actions completed
* One specific improvement made to either journey
* Outcome of that improvement

## Continuous Improvement Actions

Collecting data is not enough. The team must respond to what the data shows.

### When Conversion Is Below 15%

Review:

* The clarity of the membership offer
* How and when invitations are made
* Whether the joining process is easy
* Whether guests understand the value

### When Renewal Is Below 80%

Review:

* The active member experience
* Whether benefits are being delivered
* Whether members are receiving enough value
* Whether communications are relevant and consistent

### When At-Risk Membership Is Increasing

Review:

* Communication frequency
* Communication relevance
* Member-event participation
* Re-engagement processes

### When Advocacy Referrals Are Low

Create a simple member referral mechanism, such as:

* A bring-a-friend invitation
* A member referral benefit
* A community guest pass
* A member-hosted introduction event

## EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Monthly HQ report includes all required Guest and Member Journey metrics
* At least one documented journey improvement is implemented each month
* Journey performance is reviewed during the monthly team meeting
* Full annual journey review is submitted to HQ at year end

---

# QUICK REFERENCE

## Guest Journey vs Member Journey

| Aspect                | Guest Journey                                                              | Member Journey                                                  |
| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Who**               | First-time or returning visitor with no formal commitment                  | Committed community member who has formally joined              |
| **Goal**              | Build enough connection that the guest wants to return and eventually join | Deliver enough value that the member renews and refers others   |
| **Key Tool**          | CRM guest record, First Visit SOP, post-visit follow-up                    | CRM member record, renewal sequence, member communications      |
| **Critical Moment**   | First 30 seconds of arrival and the post-visit follow-up                   | First 48 hours after joining and the three-week renewal message |
| **Failure Mode**      | Guest visits once, receives no follow-up, and never returns                | Member joins, receives no meaningful engagement, and lapses     |
| **Success Indicator** | Return visit rate above 40% and conversion rate above 15%                  | Renewal rate above 80% and increasing advocacy referrals        |
| **Responsible Team**  | All guest-facing staff, led by the House Manager                           | House Manager and membership communications lead                |
| **HQ Reporting**      | New guest count, return rate, and conversion rate                          | New members, active engagement, and renewal rate                |

---

# A FINAL WORD

Every person who discovers Palestine House is beginning a journey.

Your responsibility is to make every stage of that journey feel:

* Intentional
* Dignified
* Welcoming
* Valuable
* Worth continuing

The Guest Journey and Member Journey are not administrative processes. They are the human heart of Palestine House operations.

> Design the journey for the person, not for the system. Build the systems to serve the person.

Measure what matters: not only how many members appear on a spreadsheet, but how many genuinely feel part of something.

**Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.**
