## Purpose

This section is your risk shield. It identifies the mistakes that most often derail membership programmes, the red flags that signal deeper problems, the compliance exposures that can create legal risk, and the situations that require immediate escalation to your House Director or HQ.

Read this document:

- Before launching your membership programme
- At the end of every month
- During operational reviews
- Whenever membership issues arise

> **Important**
>
> Every item below is drawn from the **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook — Section 13**.
>
> Any item marked as a **Level 1 Standard** is **non-negotiable**. Failure to comply is considered a governance breach.

---

# Risk Categories

- **Common Mistakes**
- **Risks**
- **Red Flags**
- **Compliance Issues**
- **Escalation Triggers**

---

# Common Mistakes

These are the operational errors partners make most frequently. Left uncorrected, they gradually reduce member trust, lower renewal rates, and weaken the overall membership experience.

---

## 01. Launching Membership Without a Written Tier Structure or Pricing Document

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

The House begins accepting members informally without:

- Defined membership tiers
- Written pricing
- Documented benefits for each tier

As a result, staff provide inconsistent information and members join with different expectations.

### Why it matters

Without a documented membership structure:

- Staff cannot apply benefits consistently.
- Priority access becomes difficult to enforce.
- HQ reporting becomes unreliable.
- Refund disputes become more likely.

### What to do

Before accepting the first paid member:

- Complete the Membership Tier One-Pager.
- Obtain House Director approval.
- Receive HQ acknowledgement.
- Store a copy:
  - In the SOP Binder
  - At the Front Desk

### Escalate if

A member disputes their tier benefits and there is no written record of what was promised.

**Escalate immediately to the House Director.**

> Do **not** issue refunds without House Director approval.

---

## 02. Using a Manual Spreadsheet as the Permanent Membership Management System

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

The House begins with a spreadsheet while planning to implement a CRM later.

Over time:

- The spreadsheet becomes permanent.
- Ownership becomes unclear.
- Records become inconsistent.
- Data begins disappearing.

### Why it matters

A spreadsheet cannot reliably:

- Automate renewal reminders
- Track consent status
- Produce HQ membership dashboards
- Prevent renewal failures
- Support compliance obligations

Missing consent records also create potential data protection breaches.

### What to do

Before opening membership:

- Configure the CRM with all required fields.
- If a spreadsheet is temporarily required:
  - Limit use to the first **30 days**
  - Assign a named owner
  - Set a fixed migration deadline

> Never use a spreadsheet as the primary membership system beyond Day 30.

### Escalate if

Multiple renewal reminders fail during the same month.

Actions:

- Escalate to the Operations Lead.
- Manually review all upcoming renewals.
- Send renewal communications manually until the system is repaired.

---

## 03. Offering Membership as a Discount Card Instead of a Belonging Experience

**Category:** Common Mistake

### What it looks like

Staff focus their membership conversations on:

- Discounts
- Savings
- Reduced prices

Instead of:

- Community
- Belonging
- Exclusive access

### Why it matters

Discount-driven memberships create:

- Price-sensitive members
- Lower loyalty
- Poor long-term renewals

Palestine House membership is defined globally as an **access and belonging product**, not a discount programme.

### What to do

- Retrain staff using the 30-second membership script.
- Remove discount-first messaging from all materials.
- Lead with:
  - Access
  - Early information
  - Exclusive member experiences

Local perks (such as complimentary tea) should always be presented as **additional benefits**, never the headline offer.

### Escalate if

Renewal rates fall below **50%** during the first three months.

Escalate to the House Director and review all membership communications.

---

# Risks

These operational conditions will eventually damage membership revenue, member trust, and Palestine House's reputation if left unresolved.

---

## 04. No Member-Only Moment Scheduled

**Category:** Risk  
**Severity:** High

### What it looks like

Members are paying for membership, but no exclusive member event has been scheduled.

### Why it matters

The member-only moment is a mandatory global benefit.

Failure to provide it:

- Breaches member expectations
- Reduces renewals
- Increases refund pressure
- Damages reputation

### What to do

Before launching membership:

- Schedule at least one member-only event.
- Ensure communications are sent at least **10 days** beforehand.

Examples include:

- Curator walkthrough
- Small salon
- Private gathering

### Escalate if

Two consecutive months pass without a member-only event.

> **Level 1 Breach**
>
> Escalate immediately to the House Director and notify HQ.

---

## 05. Renewal Sequence Not Activated Before First Expiry

**Category:** Risk  
**Severity:** High

### What it looks like

Members reach expiry before:

- T-30
- T-14
- T-3

renewal communications are activated.

### Why it matters

This results in:

- Direct revenue loss
- Forgotten members
- Lower renewal rates
- Poor HQ KPI performance

### What to do

Before the first membership expires:

- Activate the renewal workflow.
- Test using a dummy expiry date.
- Assign a renewal owner.
- Review renewals weekly.

### Escalate if

More than **20%** of members lapse without receiving renewal communications.

Actions:

- Escalate to Operations Lead and House Director.
- Contact all affected members within **48 hours**.

---

## 06. Local Add-Ons Creating Inequality or Confusion

**Category:** Risk  
**Severity:** Medium

### What it looks like

Local membership benefits unintentionally create unequal treatment between members.

Examples include:

- Identity-based benefits
- Political affiliations
- Unequal priority access

### Why it matters

Such benefits may:

- Contradict Palestine House's non-political standard
- Create inconsistency
- Cause disputes at the door

### What to do

Evaluate every proposed local benefit against three questions:

1. Does it create inequality between members?
2. Does staff need to make political or identity-based judgments?
3. Does it associate Palestine House with organisations inconsistent with its values?

If **Yes** to any question:

> Do not implement without HQ approval.

### Escalate if

A member complains another member received benefits unavailable within the same tier.

Suspend the add-on and seek HQ review.

---

# Red Flags

These are early warning signs of serious operational problems.

Immediate action is required.

---

## 07. Staff Are Improvising Membership Conversations

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

FOH staff provide inconsistent explanations of membership benefits.

### Why it matters

This causes:

- Member confusion
- Refund disputes
- Loss of trust
- Poor operational consistency

### What to do

Immediately:

- Pause new membership sign-ups.
- Retrain all FOH staff.
- Display the 30-second cue card.
- Introduce weekly spot checks.

### Escalate if

A guest formally complains they were promised benefits that do not exist.

Escalate to the House Director.

Record the incident in the **Issues & Learning Log**.

---

## 08. Members Are Arguing About Priority Access

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Staff cannot consistently enforce priority access rules during events.

### Why it matters

Door disputes indicate:

- Missing policy
- Poor communication
- Inadequate staff training

### What to do

- Document the Priority Access Policy.
- Brief all FOH staff.
- Keep printed copies at the entrance.
- Escalate disputes only to the Duty Manager.

### Escalate if

More than **two** priority access disputes occur in one month.

Escalate to the House Director.

---

## 09. Membership Revenue Is Covering Operational Shortfalls

**Category:** Red Flag

### What it looks like

Membership income is funding operational expenses before member obligations have been delivered.

### Why it matters

This creates:

- Governance risk
- Refund exposure
- Reputational damage
- Financial sustainability concerns

### What to do

- Report immediately to the House Director.
- Review the Finance Model.
- Ring-fence membership income separately from operating funds.

### Escalate if

Membership income has been used operationally **and** the next member-only event cannot be delivered.

> **Level 1 Governance Concern**

Notify both the House Director and HQ.

---

# Compliance Issues

These are mandatory operational requirements.

Failure to comply may create legal exposure.

---

## 10. Sending Communications Without Recorded Consent

**Category:** Compliance Issue  
**Level:** Level 1

### What it looks like

Members receive:

- Emails
- WhatsApp messages
- Newsletters

without documented consent.

### Why it matters

This may breach:

- GDPR
- Local privacy legislation
- Palestine House data standards

### What to do

Immediately:

- Audit all member records.
- Flag missing consent.
- Stop communications until consent is recorded.
- Update the sign-up workflow.

Seek legal advice where required.

### Escalate if

A member formally complains they never consented to communications.

Escalate immediately.

---

## 11. Modifying Global Membership Benefits Without HQ Approval

**Category:** Compliance Issue  
**Level:** Level 1

### What it looks like

The House removes or weakens mandatory global membership benefits without HQ approval.

### Why it matters

This breaches:

- Global membership standards
- Brand consistency
- The License Agreement

### What to do

Submit a formal HQ change request before modifying any mandatory benefit.

### Escalate if

Mandatory benefits have been removed for more than two months without HQ notification.

---

## 12. Membership Associated With Political Organisations

**Category:** Compliance Issue  
**Level:** Level 1

### What it looks like

A Corporate or Partner Membership is granted to a politically affiliated organisation.

### Why it matters

This breaches Palestine House's non-political operating standard.

### What to do

Review every Corporate or Partner Membership against:

- Ethics standards
- Funding compliance
- Non-political positioning

Obtain HQ approval before acceptance.

### Escalate if

A politically affiliated organisation has already been accepted.

Suspend the membership immediately and notify HQ within **24 hours**.

---

# Escalation Triggers

Stop and escalate immediately whenever any of the following conditions occur.

---

## 13. Renewal Rate Falls Below 50%

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

Less than **50%** of eligible members renew during a calendar month.

### Why it matters

This indicates serious issues with:

- Membership value
- Communications
- Trust
- Benefit delivery

### What to do

Immediately:

- Analyse renewal data.
- Identify affected tiers.
- Contact all lapsed members within **7 days**.
- Review communications and benefits.

### Escalate if

Escalate immediately to the House Director.

Notify HQ within **30 days** if unresolved.

---

## 14. A Member Threatens Legal Action

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

A member threatens legal action relating to:

- Membership benefits
- Refunds
- Data protection
- Communications

### Why it matters

Legal threats require formal handling.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Stop informal discussions.
- Do not admit liability.
- Document every interaction.
- Seek legal advice.

### Escalate if

Escalate to the House Director within **1 hour**.

HQ must be notified within **24 hours**.

---

## 15. Membership Data Is Lost or Breached

**Category:** Escalation Trigger

### What it looks like

Membership information is:

- Lost
- Deleted
- Shared without authorisation
- Accessed by unauthorised persons

### Why it matters

This may trigger mandatory legal reporting requirements, including GDPR notification obligations.

### What to do

Immediately:

- Contain the breach.
- Identify affected records.
- Determine who accessed the data.
- Seek legal advice within one hour.

### Escalate if

Escalate immediately to the House Director.

HQ must be notified within **24 hours**.

Where applicable, notify the relevant data protection authority within **72 hours**.

---

# How to Use This Section

Review all **15 risk items**:

- Before launch
- At the end of every month

Operational requirements:

- Assign a named owner for each risk category.
- Review risks during weekly team huddles.
- Record active risks in the **Issues & Learning Log**.
- Follow every escalation timeframe exactly as specified.

Use this document together with:

- **Membership Operational Checklist (Section 13)**
- **Membership Simple Guide (Section 13)**

---

## Final Reminder

Small operational mistakes compound over time.

Consistent governance, disciplined processes, and timely escalation protect:

- Members
- Staff
- Palestine House
- The wider network

