## Retail / Shop Operations Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Retail / Shop Operations** element defines how Palestine House manages its retail space as a controlled, experience-driven extension of its cultural mission.

Its purpose is to ensure that all products, displays, customer interactions, and transactions are aligned with the identity of Palestine House while remaining operationally disciplined and commercially sustainable.

The retail function is not treated as a standalone store but as an integrated component of the overall guest journey, using carefully curated products to reinforce Palestinian culture, storytelling, and heritage.

This element establishes a structured approach to:

- Product curation
- Merchandising
- Pricing
- Sales operations
- Inventory management
- Customer service
- Retail governance

It ensures consistency in product quality, presentation, pricing, and service while reducing operational risks such as:

- Inventory loss
- Pricing inconsistencies
- Poor merchandising
- Unapproved product offerings
- Operational inefficiencies

Ultimately, this element ensures that the retail space enhances both the guest experience and the long-term sustainability of Palestine House.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Retail / Shop Operations element is applied continuously across all retail-related activities within Palestine House.

### Retail Activities

This element supports:

- Daily retail operations
- Gift shop sales
- Event-based retail activations
- Pop-up retail experiences
- Partner product showcases
- Exhibition merchandise
- Membership merchandise
- Cultural product sales

### Product Planning

During planning, this element governs:

- Product selection
- Product curation
- Supplier evaluation
- Product sourcing
- Pricing preparation
- Inventory planning

Every product should align with the cultural identity, quality standards, and commercial objectives of Palestine House.

### Merchandising and Setup

Before retail operations begin, this element governs:

- Shop layout
- Product displays
- Visual merchandising
- Stock placement
- Pricing presentation
- Store readiness

A consistent and well-organized retail environment enhances both operational efficiency and the guest experience.

### Live Operations

During daily operations, the element governs:

- Sales transactions
- Customer interactions
- Product presentation
- Point-of-sale procedures
- Inventory availability
- Service quality
- Retail supervision

Every interaction should reflect the hospitality and professionalism expected of Palestine House.

### Inventory Management

Ongoing retail operations include:

- Stock monitoring
- Inventory replenishment
- Product rotation
- Inventory reconciliation
- Sales reporting
- Performance monitoring

Retail Operations should be integrated into both daily operational routines and event planning to ensure products remain available and appropriately presented.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Retail / Shop Operations are executed by **LOCAL** teams while operating within standards established by **GLOBAL**.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL is responsible for establishing and maintaining the retail framework across the Palestine House network.

Responsibilities include:

- Product guidelines
- Pricing principles
- Brand standards
- Retail policies
- Merchandising standards
- Network-wide consistency

GLOBAL also reviews and approves:

- Strategic product partnerships
- High-risk retail initiatives
- New product categories
- Significant merchandising changes
- Retail governance requirements

### LOCAL Leadership Responsibilities

The **General Manager** or **Head of Operations** is responsible for ensuring Retail Operations:

- Meet GLOBAL standards
- Support operational objectives
- Comply with approved policies
- Maintain commercial discipline
- Deliver a high-quality guest experience

### Retail Team Responsibilities

Designated retail staff are responsible for daily execution, including:

- Sales operations
- Customer service
- Product merchandising
- Inventory management
- Stock replenishment
- Transaction handling
- Retail reporting
- Store presentation

### Partner Responsibilities

Partners supplying products for retail sale must:

- Comply with approved product guidelines
- Meet quality requirements
- Follow branding standards
- Adhere to operational policies

All partner products remain subject to review, approval, and ongoing monitoring.

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Retail / Shop Operations element delivers clear and measurable operational outcomes.

### Guest Experience Outcomes

Guests experience:

- High-quality products
- Professional customer service
- Consistent pricing
- Well-presented retail spaces
- Meaningful cultural merchandise

The retail environment becomes a natural extension of the Palestine House experience.

### Retail Outcomes

Structured Retail Operations result in:

- Efficient daily operations
- Reliable sales processes
- Consistent merchandising
- Improved inventory accuracy
- Better product availability

### Financial Outcomes

Effective retail management contributes to:

- Sustainable revenue generation
- Better inventory control
- Reduced stock loss
- Improved sales performance
- Greater financial discipline

### Brand Outcomes

Retail should reinforce:

- Palestinian cultural identity
- Brand consistency
- Curated storytelling
- Product quality
- Organizational credibility

Every product should contribute positively to the Palestine House mission and guest journey.

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, Retail Operations help Palestine House:

- Strengthen financial sustainability
- Build customer trust
- Improve partner relationships
- Expand retail performance
- Deliver a consistent cultural retail experience across the network

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Retail / Shop Operations element governs the planning, operation, and management of all retail activities across Palestine House.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Product sourcing
- Product curation
- Merchandising
- Pricing
- Sales operations
- Customer service
- Inventory management
- Stock control
- Retail reporting
- Partner product integration

It establishes how retail operations should be managed to deliver a consistent, high-quality, and commercially sustainable experience.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Financial management systems
- Payment infrastructure
- Commercial contracts
- Legal agreements
- Accounting systems
- Specialist retail technologies

These supporting systems must be managed separately where required.

### Operational Boundaries

Informal sales activities and unapproved products are not permitted.

All Retail Operations must comply with approved:

- Product guidelines
- Pricing standards
- Brand requirements
- Retail policies
- Governance procedures

Effective Retail Operations depend upon:

- Accurate inventory management
- Disciplined operational execution
- Consistent merchandising
- Reliable customer service
- Compliance with pricing and brand standards

### Risk and Escalation

Retail / Shop Operations cannot compensate for:

- Poor product selection
- Weak customer demand
- Inaccurate inventory records
- Inconsistent pricing
- Failure to follow operational procedures

Any retail activity that introduces:

- Financial risk
- Legal risk
- Brand risk
- Reputational risk
- Significant operational risk

must be escalated through the appropriate **GLOBAL** governance channels for review and approval before implementation.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Retail / Shop Operations

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Framework:** 10-Step Retail & Shop Operations Lifecycle

---

# What This Guide Covers

The Palestine House Shop is not simply a retail space.

It is a cultural destination where Palestinian creativity, craftsmanship, heritage, and storytelling become tangible experiences for guests and the wider community.

Every product placed on the shelf is a curatorial decision.

Every purchase supports the broader Palestine House mission of presenting Palestinian culture with:

- Authenticity
- Quality
- Dignity
- Cultural depth

Alongside its cultural purpose, the shop is also one of the House's most reliable revenue streams.

When managed with discipline, it provides sustainable income while strengthening relationships with Palestinian artisans, publishers, musicians, and cultural producers.

This guide explains the complete retail operations lifecycle—from defining the shop concept and curating products through procurement, merchandising, point-of-sale management, daily operations, financial reconciliation, and long-term strategic development.

---

# Retail & Shop Operations Lifecycle

| Stage | Operational Area |
|------:|------------------|
| **1** | Concept & Setup |
| **2** | Product Curation |
| **3** | Supplier & Procurement |
| **4** | Stock & Merchandising |
| **5** | Sales & Transactions |
| **6** | Daily Operations |
| **7** | Review & Refresh |

---

# Stage 1 — Concept & Setup

> **Timing:** Before the shop opens

## Step 1 — Define the Shop Concept and Product Philosophy

The Palestine House Shop should never resemble:

- A generic gift shop
- A souvenir store
- A general online marketplace

Instead, it should reflect the same principles that define Palestine House itself:

- Cultural authenticity
- Artisan quality
- Community connection
- Non-political positioning
- Curated storytelling

Every product should be intentionally selected and presented with cultural context.

---

## The Six Product Categories

| Category | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| **1. Palestinian Artisan Goods** | Handmade ceramics, embroidery, jewellery, textiles, glassware and traditional crafts |
| **2. Cultural Publications** | Books, poetry, essays, catalogues, children's books and academic works |
| **3. Music & Sound** | Palestinian music, recordings, instruments and audio culture |
| **4. Prints & Visual Art** | Artist editions, exhibition prints, photography and posters |
| **5. Palestine House Branded Merchandise** *(HQ Approval Required)* | Apparel, tote bags, notebooks, pins and branded cultural items |
| **6. Palestinian Food & Cultural Gifts** | Olive oil, za'atar, sweets, spices and other cultural food products |

---

## Product Curation Standard

Every product should answer one question:

> **Does this product represent Palestinian culture with the quality, dignity and authenticity expected of Palestine House?**

Products that fail this standard should not be stocked.

---

> **Important**
>
> No product may include imagery, language or associations that imply political party affiliation or endorsement.
>
> Any product creating non-political compliance concerns must be referred to HQ before being introduced.

---

## Expected Output — Shop Concept Defined

- [ ] Shop concept statement written
- [ ] House Manager approval obtained
- [ ] Six product categories established
- [ ] Initial product range identified
- [ ] HQ approval process understood for branded merchandise
- [ ] Non-political compliance embedded into product selection

---

# Stage 1–2 — Concept & Product Curation

> **Timing:** Before launch

## Step 2 — Design the Shop Layout and Merchandising Standards

The presentation of products is as important as the products themselves.

Well-designed merchandising encourages guests to:

- Slow down
- Explore
- Learn
- Purchase

The shop should communicate the same curatorial quality as the rest of Palestine House.

---

## Layout Principles

### Customer Journey

Guide visitors naturally from:

1. Discovery
2. Exploration
3. Purchase

Place the highest-impact products near the entrance.

Position smaller impulse purchases near the till.

---

### Product Zones

Create dedicated areas for each of the six product categories.

Complementary products may be grouped to tell a cultural story.

Example:

- Music
- Books
- Prints

presented together around a shared theme.

---

### Storytelling

Every artisan product should include:

- Maker name
- Place of origin
- Cultural tradition
- Brief story

Guests should leave feeling educated—not simply sold to.

---

### Presentation Standards

Display products using:

- Appropriate lighting
- Clean shelving
- High-quality presentation
- Consistent pricing

Palestine House is never presented as a discount retailer.

---

## Merchandising Standards

Ensure:

- Every product has a visible price
- Displays are refreshed every 4–6 weeks
- Shop displays align with programming
- Empty shelves are avoided
- Out-of-stock areas are consolidated or labelled appropriately

---

## Decision Point

**Does the shop layout reflect Palestine House brand standards and cultural quality?**

### Yes

Proceed with sourcing.

### No

Revise the layout before stock arrives.

---

## Expected Output — Shop Layout Established

- [ ] Floor plan completed
- [ ] Category zones defined
- [ ] Display standards documented
- [ ] Lighting standards established
- [ ] Story card template created
- [ ] Every product priced

---

# Stage 2 — Product Curation

> **Timing:** Before launch and during seasonal reviews

## Step 3 — Curate the Product Range

Product curation is the editorial heart of the shop.

It determines which Palestinian voices, makers and traditions are represented.

---

## Build the Initial Collection

Begin with community knowledge.

Identify:

- Local artisans
- Palestinian makers
- Community recommendations
- Diaspora producers

---

## Work with HQ and Aswatna

Request introductions to:

- Artisan collectives
- Publishers
- Musicians
- Cultural producers

Leverage existing network relationships whenever possible.

---

## Three-Question Product Test

Every product should satisfy all three questions:

1. Does it represent Palestinian culture honestly?
2. Is the quality appropriate?
3. Does it support Palestine House programming and community identity?

If the answer is **No** to any question, the product should not be stocked.

---

## Pricing Diversity

Offer products across multiple price ranges.

Provide:

- Entry-level purchases
- Mid-range products
- Premium artisan pieces

The shop should remain accessible to the wider community.

---

## Consignment

Where appropriate, use consignment agreements for:

- High-value artisan work
- Limited editions
- Unique cultural objects

Typical Palestine House commission:

**25–40%**

---

## Products Requiring Special Care

Review carefully:

- Religious products
- Politically sensitive products
- Symbolic imagery

Seek HQ guidance whenever uncertainty exists.

---

## Decision Point

**Does the product require cultural, religious or political interpretation?**

### Yes

Document the rationale and provide contextual interpretation.

### No

Proceed with normal curation.

Politically sensitive products require written HQ approval before inclusion.

---

## Expected Output — Product Range Curated

- [ ] Minimum 10–15 products per category identified
- [ ] Three-question test completed
- [ ] Product pricing balanced
- [ ] Consignment agreements prepared
- [ ] HQ informed of any non-political compliance concerns

---

# Stage 3 — Supplier & Procurement

> **Timing:** Before first purchasing

## Step 4 — Source and Vet Suppliers

Retail suppliers often include:

- Individual artisans
- Publishers
- Musicians
- Cultural organisations
- Importers
- Diaspora makers

These relationships require careful documentation.

---

## Supplier Categories

### Palestinian Artisans

Document:

- Product range
- Pricing
- Quantities
- Lead times
- Payment terms
- Consignment arrangements

Never rely solely on verbal agreements.

---

### Publishers & Cultural Organisations

Confirm:

- Supply arrangements
- Distribution rights
- Intellectual property permissions

---

### Importers & Distributors

Complete supplier ethics reviews.

Verify:

- Legal registration
- Authentic sourcing
- Appropriate attribution

---

### Local Diaspora Makers

Where available, prioritise local Palestinian makers.

These relationships strengthen both community engagement and shop identity.

---

## Supplier Documentation

Maintain written agreements covering:

- Products supplied
- Pricing
- Commission
- Delivery
- Payment terms
- Unsold stock procedures

---

## Attribution

Every artisan product should include:

- Maker name
- Provenance
- Cultural background

Confirm attribution preferences directly with suppliers.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the supplier producing Palestine House branded merchandise?**

### Yes

Obtain HQ written brand approval before production.

### No

Complete standard supplier vetting.

---

## Expected Output — Suppliers Approved

- [ ] Suppliers identified
- [ ] Agreements documented
- [ ] Ethics checks completed
- [ ] Attribution agreed
- [ ] HQ approval obtained for branded merchandise

---

# Stage 5 — Sales & Transactions

> **Timing:** Before opening

## Step 5 — Configure the Point of Sale System

The POS system manages:

- Transactions
- Inventory
- Pricing
- Reporting
- Financial reconciliation

It must be fully configured before opening.

---

## POS Requirements

Every product entry should include:

- Product name
- Category
- Supplier
- Price
- Tax status
- Product code

Products with multiple sizes require separate entries.

---

## Payment Methods

Support:

- Cash
- Credit cards
- Debit cards
- Digital payments

Cash-only trading is not acceptable.

---

## Member Discounts

Configure Palestine House membership discounts directly within the POS.

Any discretionary discount requires House Manager approval.

---

## Reporting

The POS should generate:

- Daily sales
- Weekly summaries
- Monthly revenue
- Product performance
- Slow-moving stock reports

---

## Pricing Framework

### Wholesale Products

Typical markup:

**2.0–2.5× cost price**

Review local market conditions before finalising prices.

---

### Consignment

Selling prices should be agreed with artisans.

Commission should match written agreements.

---

### Branded Merchandise

Pricing should be established in consultation with HQ.

---

## Decision Point

**Has every product been entered into the POS before being displayed?**

### Yes

Proceed to opening.

### No

Complete POS setup first.

---

## Expected Output — POS Ready

- [ ] Product catalogue completed
- [ ] Payment methods tested
- [ ] Member discounts configured
- [ ] Reporting operational
- [ ] Pricing approved

---

# Stage 4 — Stock & Merchandising

> **Timing:** Launch and ongoing

## Step 6 — Manage Shop Inventory

Retail inventory requires careful handling.

Many products are:

- Fragile
- Unique
- High-value
- Consignment-based

Every item should be individually managed.

---

## Receiving Process

Upon delivery:

- Verify against purchase order
- Inspect every item
- Record defects
- Photograph damaged products
- Notify suppliers

---

## Product Labelling

Every product should be:

- Tagged
- Priced
- Labelled
- Attributed

before reaching the shop floor.

---

## Inventory Recording

Record:

- Product
- Category
- Supplier
- Quantity
- Cost
- Selling price
- Date received

---

## Storage

Back stock should be:

- Organised
- Secure
- Labelled
- Separate from café and facilities inventory

---

## Daily Replenishment

Every trading day:

- Inspect displays
- Replenish shelves
- Trigger reorders when thresholds are reached

Never wait until shelves become empty.

---

## Decision Point

**Are reorder thresholds activated before displays become empty?**

### Yes

Continue.

### No

Review inventory thresholds immediately.

---

## Expected Output — Shop Stock Managed

- [ ] Deliveries inspected
- [ ] Products tagged
- [ ] Inventory updated
- [ ] Daily replenishment completed
- [ ] Reorders triggered proactively

---

# Stage 6 — Daily Operations

> **Timing:** Every trading day

## Step 7 — Operate the Shop

Daily operations follow three phases:

1. Open
2. Trade
3. Close

Consistency creates reliability.

---

## Opening Routine

Before opening:

- Inspect displays
- Replenish products
- Confirm pricing
- Open POS
- Verify payment terminals
- Review daily operational notes

---

## Customer Service Standards

Provide:

- Warm greetings
- Helpful assistance
- Product knowledge
- Cultural context

Avoid pressure selling.

Staff should understand:

- Artisan stories
- Cultural traditions
- Product origins

---

## Closing Routine

At close:

- Run sales reports
- Count cash
- Reconcile transactions
- Identify low-stock products
- Prepare replenishment

---

## Decision Point

**Does cash reconcile with POS records?**

### Yes

Complete closing procedures.

### No

Record the discrepancy.

Notify the Finance Lead.

Investigate recurring discrepancies.

---

## Expected Output — Daily Operations Running

- [ ] Opening routine completed
- [ ] Closing routine completed
- [ ] Daily sales report generated
- [ ] Cash reconciled
- [ ] Stock replenishment planned
- [ ] Staff knowledgeable across all product categories

---

# Stage 5–6 — Monthly Operations

> **Timing:** Monthly

## Step 8 — Process Consignment Payments

Prompt settlement protects artisan relationships.

---

## Monthly Settlement Process

1. Generate consignment sales report.
2. Calculate commissions.
3. Prepare settlement statements.
4. Process payments.
5. Complete reconciliation.

Settlement statements should be issued within the first **10 working days** of the following month.

Standard payment timing:

**30 days from month-end**

---

## Financial Reconciliation

Review:

- Total revenue
- Wholesale revenue
- Consignment revenue
- Branded merchandise
- Gross margin

---

> **Important**
>
> Consignment payments are financial obligations.
>
> Delayed payments damage trust with Palestinian artisans.

---

## Expected Output — Monthly Reconciliation Complete

- [ ] Sales reports generated
- [ ] Settlement statements issued
- [ ] Payments processed
- [ ] Revenue reconciled
- [ ] Gross margin reviewed

---

# Stage 4–7 — Quarterly Reviews

> **Timing:** Quarterly

## Step 9 — Review Products and Refresh the Range

The shop should evolve alongside:

- Programming
- Seasons
- Community interests

---

## Quarterly Review

Review:

- Sales performance
- Slow-moving products
- Display effectiveness
- New suppliers
- New artisans
- Branded merchandise

Products unsold for **60 days** should be reviewed.

---

## Seasonal Refreshes

Key retail periods include:

- Ramadan
- Eid
- Nakba Day
- Year-end gift season

Plan seasonal purchasing at least **six weeks** ahead.

---

## Decision Point

**Has a consignment product remained unsold for over 90 days?**

### Yes

Discuss with the artisan.

Consider:

- Returning stock
- Repricing
- Display changes

### No

Continue monitoring.

---

## Expected Output — Quarterly Review Complete

- [ ] Sales reviewed
- [ ] Slow-moving products actioned
- [ ] Consignment reviewed
- [ ] Displays aligned with programming
- [ ] New suppliers identified
- [ ] Branded merchandise reviewed

---

# Stage 7 — Annual Review

> **Timing:** Annually

## Step 10 — Conduct the Annual Shop Review

The annual review evaluates the shop as:

- A cultural platform
- A revenue stream
- A community asset

---

## Annual Review Framework

Review:

### Financial Performance

- Revenue
- Margin
- Growth
- Contribution to House finances

---

### Cultural Performance

Evaluate:

- Quality
- Diversity
- Authenticity
- Representation

---

### Supplier Relationships

Assess:

- Payment performance
- Relationship health
- New opportunities

---

### Community Impact

Measure:

- Artisan income
- Community feedback
- Product requests
- Cultural engagement

---

### Strategic Direction

Identify two or three major priorities for the coming year.

---

## Expected Output — Annual Review Complete

- [ ] Financial performance reviewed
- [ ] Curatorial mission assessed
- [ ] Supplier relationships evaluated
- [ ] Community impact documented
- [ ] Annual shop plan approved
- [ ] Report shared with the Board and HQ

---

# The Shop Standard

Every product in the Palestine House Shop represents a decision about:

- Whose stories are shared
- Which traditions are preserved
- Which makers are supported
- How Palestinian culture is experienced

Selling a product is more than completing a transaction.

It is introducing someone to Palestinian creativity, craftsmanship and cultural heritage through careful curation.

Manage the shop with the same professionalism, care and discipline applied to every other Palestine House operation.

