## Launching a New House Overview

---

## Purpose

The **Launching a New House** element defines the structured process required to plan, build, and activate a new Palestine House location in full alignment with **GLOBAL** standards.

Its purpose is to ensure that every new House is not simply opened, but opened correctly—with operational readiness, brand consistency, governance, and organizational discipline established from day one.

This element establishes a phased framework for:

- Strategic planning
- Site readiness
- Operational setup
- Team preparation
- System implementation
- Controlled activation
- Launch governance

It prevents rushed or fragmented launches by ensuring that every critical component is completed, verified, and approved before opening.

Each Palestine House should reflect a consistent standard of quality, identity, and operational excellence across the network rather than becoming a locally improvised variation.

Ultimately, this element provides the foundation for scalable expansion while protecting the integrity of the Palestine House brand.

---

## When and Where It Is Used

The Launching a New House element is applied throughout the complete lifecycle of establishing a new Palestine House location.

### Launch Lifecycle

This element governs every stage of the launch process, including:

- Partner selection
- Site confirmation
- Planning
- Facility preparation
- Operational setup
- Team development
- Public opening
- Post-launch stabilization

Every new Palestine House must complete the full launch lifecycle before commencing normal operations.

### Foundation Phase

During the **Foundation** phase, this element governs:

- Vision alignment
- Partner evaluation
- Site assessment
- Governance setup
- Operating model confirmation
- Strategic planning

This phase establishes the strategic and organizational foundations required for a successful launch.

### Engine Phase

During the **Engine** phase, the element supports:

- System implementation
- Technology setup
- Operational process development
- Infrastructure preparation
- Documentation
- Resource planning

The objective is to build the operational systems required for long-term success.

### Readiness Phase

During the **Readiness** phase, this element governs:

- Staff recruitment
- Staff training
- Operational testing
- Facility readiness
- Programming preparation
- Compliance verification

Readiness activities ensure the House can operate safely, consistently, and confidently from opening day.

### Launch Phase

The final phase includes:

- Soft launch activities
- Operational testing
- Guest experience validation
- Final readiness assessments
- Official public opening

Launch activities should only proceed after all readiness requirements have been successfully completed.

### Mandatory Application

This framework is mandatory for every new Palestine House.

No location should bypass, modify, or omit any required launch phase without formal **GLOBAL** approval.

---

## Who Is Responsible

Launching a New House is a **shared responsibility** between **GLOBAL**, **LOCAL**, and **ASWĀTNA**, with clearly defined ownership and accountability.

### GLOBAL Responsibilities

GLOBAL owns and governs the overall launch framework.

Responsibilities include:

- Launch methodology
- Brand standards
- Governance requirements
- Operational standards
- Launch approvals
- Readiness assessments
- Quality assurance

GLOBAL also:

- Approves key strategic decisions
- Reviews launch progress
- Verifies readiness gates
- Protects network consistency
- Grants final launch approval

### LOCAL Responsibilities

LOCAL partners are responsible for executing the approved launch plan within their location.

Responsibilities include:

- Site preparation
- Local compliance
- Operational implementation
- Staff recruitment
- Team management
- Facility readiness
- Local operational coordination

LOCAL teams execute the launch while adhering to approved GLOBAL standards.

### ASWĀTNA Responsibilities

Where applicable, **ASWĀTNA** supports:

- Programming readiness
- Cultural programming
- Community activation
- Creative coordination
- Cultural integration

ASWĀTNA ensures the House is culturally prepared to deliver its intended mission from the outset.

### Launch Approval

Final authorization to open a new Palestine House remains the responsibility of **GLOBAL**.

No House may commence public operations until:

- All readiness gates have been completed
- Required standards have been met
- Formal GLOBAL approval has been granted

---

## Expected Outcomes

When implemented correctly, the Launching a New House element delivers clear and measurable organizational outcomes.

### Operational Outcomes

Every new Palestine House opens with:

- Fully operational systems
- Trained staff
- Active operational processes
- Ready facilities
- Operational discipline
- Consistent execution

Operations begin with confidence rather than improvisation.

### Guest Experience Outcomes

Guests experience:

- Consistent service standards
- Professional operations
- High-quality programming
- Safe and welcoming facilities
- A recognizable Palestine House experience

The opening reflects the expected quality of the global network.

### Organizational Outcomes

Structured launches result in:

- Reduced operational risk
- Better coordination
- Faster operational stabilization
- Improved governance
- Stronger organizational consistency

### Partnership Outcomes

The launch framework strengthens:

- Partner confidence
- Shared accountability
- Clear expectations
- Structured collaboration
- Long-term operational relationships

### Long-Term Outcomes

Over time, this element enables Palestine House to:

- Scale consistently
- Expand responsibly
- Protect brand integrity
- Maintain operational excellence
- Launch new Houses without compromising quality, governance, or organizational control

---

## Scope and Limitations

The Launching a New House element governs the complete process of planning, preparing, approving, and activating new Palestine House locations.

### Scope

This element includes:

- Launch planning
- Partner onboarding
- Site readiness
- Operational setup
- Technology implementation
- Team recruitment
- Staff training
- Programming readiness
- Governance approvals
- Launch execution
- Post-launch stabilization

It establishes the required standards, approval gates, and implementation process for every new Palestine House.

### Limitations

This element **does not** replace:

- Local implementation plans
- Construction management
- Architectural design
- Legal incorporation
- Regulatory approvals
- Jurisdiction-specific compliance requirements

These activities must be managed separately while remaining aligned with the approved launch framework.

### Operational Boundaries

The following are not permitted:

- Shortcuts in the launch process
- Early public openings
- Partial operational readiness
- Bypassing approval gates
- Opening without formal authorization

All launch activities must comply with approved:

- Launch standards
- Operational requirements
- Governance procedures
- Brand guidelines
- Readiness criteria

Effective launch execution depends upon:

- Capable local partners
- Disciplined project management
- Timely delivery
- Full adherence to GLOBAL standards
- Successful completion of every readiness gate

### Risk and Escalation

The Launching a New House element cannot compensate for:

- Weak local partnerships
- Poor project execution
- Inadequate preparation
- Missed milestones
- Failure to meet operational standards

Any:

- Launch delay
- Readiness gap
- Significant project risk
- Operational deviation
- Governance concern
- Failure to meet approval criteria

must be escalated immediately through the appropriate **GLOBAL** governance channels for review, corrective action, and formal approval before proceeding.$md$, $md$# Palestine House

# Launching a New House

> **Palestine House Local Operations Playbook**  
> **Guide:** Launching a New House • 8 Steps

---

# What This Guide Covers

Opening a Palestine House is one of the most complex and rewarding undertakings a local partner can pursue.

Launching successfully requires:

- Selecting the right partner
- Finding the right venue
- Establishing the legal entity
- Building the team
- Designing the cultural programme
- Raising sustainable funding
- Developing community partnerships
- Delivering an exceptional guest experience

This guide follows the **Palestine House 120-Day Launch Playbook**, structured around three Go/No-Go Gates that ensure every location meets the required operational standard before opening.

> **Principle**
>
> **Standards over speed.**
>
> This is not a guide to opening quickly—it is a guide to opening correctly.

---

# Launch Lifecycle

| Stage | Timeline |
|------:|----------|
| **1** | Partner Selection |
| **2** | Foundation (Days 1–30) |
| **3** | Engine Build (Days 31–60) |
| **4** | Operations Build (Days 61–90) |
| **5** | Launch Readiness (Days 91–108) |
| **6** | Soft Opening |
| **7** | Grand Opening |
| **8** | Stabilisation & Ongoing Operations |

---

# Stage 1 — Partner Selection

## Step 1 — Select, Vet & Contract the Right Local Partner

The quality of the local partner determines the success of every subsequent stage.

Everything else depends upon this decision:

- Venue
- Team
- Programme
- Fundraising
- Community trust
- Operational discipline

HQ does not compromise on partner selection.

Neither should any future Palestine House location.

---

## The Five Launch Phases (120-Day Structure)

| Phase | Timeline | Gate | Primary Outcome |
|--------|----------|------|-----------------|
| **Foundation** | Days 1–30 | **Gate 1** | Governance established |
| **Engine Build** | Days 31–60 | **Gate 2** | Organisation legally operational |
| **Operations Build** | Days 61–90 | Preparation | Systems and people operational |
| **Launch Readiness** | Days 91–108 | **Gate 3** | Ready to host guests safely |
| **Launch** | Days 109–120 | — | Grand Opening |

---

## Partner Selection Criteria

Evaluate every prospective partner against:

- Mission and values alignment
- Community reputation
- Operational leadership
- Financial capacity
- Governance capability
- Accountability to HQ
- Cultural credibility
- Long-term sustainability
- Funding integrity
- Commitment to Palestine House standards

---

## Decision Point

**Has the partner passed the full vetting process and have all agreements been signed?**

### Yes

The **120-Day Launch Clock** officially begins.

Proceed to Foundation Phase.

### No

Do not begin the launch.

The legal relationship must always precede operational activity.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Partner assessment completed
- [ ] Due diligence completed
- [ ] Licence Agreement signed
- [ ] Term Sheet agreed
- [ ] Day 1 formally confirmed

---

# Stage 2 — Foundation (Days 1–30)

## Step 2 — Governance, Strategy & Venue Search

The Foundation Phase establishes the conditions that everything else depends upon.

Poor foundations create problems throughout the remainder of the launch.

---

## Governance

Establish the launch team immediately.

Required roles:

- House Manager
- Finance Lead
- Operations Lead
- Programming Lead

---

## Weekly Launch Rhythm

Beginning on Day 1:

Conduct weekly launch meetings covering:

- Progress
- Risks
- Gate readiness
- Decisions
- Actions
- HQ updates

---

## City & Neighbourhood Strategy

Before selecting a venue, define:

- Target neighbourhood
- Audience access
- Venue specifications
- Accessibility requirements
- Maximum rent
- Strategic fit

Never choose a venue before completing the location strategy.

---

## Venue Search

Begin in **Week 1**.

Aim to shortlist **2–3 suitable venues** before Day 20.

---

## Venue Due Diligence

Assess:

- Ownership
- Legal status
- Planning permissions
- Structural condition
- Accessibility
- Lease conditions

---

## Decision Point

**By Day 30 have governance, strategy, budget and venue shortlisting been completed?**

### Yes

Gate 1 is passed.

Proceed to Engine Build.

### No

Remain in Foundation.

Resolve outstanding issues before continuing.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Governance team appointed
- [ ] Weekly meetings established
- [ ] City strategy completed
- [ ] Venue shortlist created
- [ ] Budget approved
- [ ] Gate 1 completed

---

# Stage 3 — Engine Build (Days 31–60)

## Step 3 — Legal Entity, Brand Licence & Fit-Out

By Day 60 the organisation should become legally and operationally real.

---

## Lease Negotiation

Every lease must receive independent legal review.

Review:

- Rent
- Service charges
- Alterations
- Break clauses
- Guarantees
- Permitted use

Negotiate wherever possible:

- Fit-out allowance
- Rent-free period
- Break clause

---

## Legal Entity

Before signing the lease:

- Register the organisation
- File governing documents
- Confirm mission alignment

---

## Banking

Open organisational bank accounts.

Maintain:

- Two signatories
- Financial controls
- No personal accounts

---

## Brand Licence

Activate the HQ Brand Licence before:

- Signage
- Marketing
- Social media
- Press releases
- Public announcements

---

## Decision Point

**By Day 60 have the lease, legal entity, banking, brand licence and fit-out all been completed?**

### Yes

Gate 2 is passed.

Proceed to Operations Build.

### No

Identify the blocking issue and notify HQ immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Lease executed
- [ ] Legal entity registered
- [ ] Bank account operational
- [ ] Brand Licence activated
- [ ] Fit-out commenced
- [ ] Gate 2 completed

---

# Stage 4 — Operations Build (Days 61–90)

## Step 4 — Build the Operating Organisation

The venue may not yet be open—but by Day 90 the organisation itself should be operational.

---

## Digital Infrastructure

Deploy:

- Website
- CRM
- Ticketing
- Email platform
- Point-of-Sale

Systems should be live and tested before Day 90.

---

## Membership

Launch:

- Pre-opening memberships
- Email subscriptions
- Community database

Begin building the launch audience immediately.

---

## Recruitment

Complete hiring before the Soft Opening.

Typical roles include:

- Café Manager
- Programme Coordinator
- Communications Lead
- Retail Lead
- Operations Coordinator

---

## Cultural Programme

Develop the launch programme with **Aswatna**.

Complete:

- Programme planning
- Artist booking
- Marketing information

before Day 90.

---

## Decision Point

**By Day 90 are systems, staff, programme and community partnerships operational?**

### Yes

Proceed toward Gate 3.

### No

Escalate significant delays immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Digital systems operational
- [ ] Membership launched
- [ ] Staff recruited
- [ ] Programme confirmed
- [ ] Community partnerships established
- [ ] Finance systems operational

---

# Stage 5 — Launch Readiness (Days 91–108)

## Step 5 — Partnerships, Marketing & SOPs

The final phase ensures the House is genuinely ready to welcome guests.

Evidence—not intention—is required.

---

## Partnerships & Fundraising

Establish relationships with:

- Palestinian organisations
- Universities
- Cultural institutions
- Community groups
- Media
- Sponsors

---

## Marketing Campaign

Launch the four-stage campaign:

1. Anticipation
2. Announcement
3. Build-Up
4. Opening Week

---

## SOPs

Document procedures for:

- Guest experience
- Café
- Shop
- Events
- Ticketing
- Safeguarding
- Emergencies
- Media enquiries

Train all staff before the Soft Opening.

---

## Decision Point

**By Day 108 can the House safely welcome guests?**

### Yes

Gate 3 is passed.

Confirm Grand Opening.

### No

Delay the opening.

Quality always takes priority over schedule.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Permits obtained
- [ ] Staff trained
- [ ] Systems tested
- [ ] Marketing active
- [ ] Partnerships established
- [ ] Six months of financial runway confirmed
- [ ] Gate 3 completed

---

# Stage 6 — Soft Opening

## Step 6 — Test Every Aspect of Operations

The Soft Opening is an operational quality test.

It is not a celebration.

---

## Invite

Include:

- Early members
- Community leaders
- Cultural partners
- HQ representative

Seek honest feedback.

---

## Run Full Operations

Operate exactly as during normal business.

Include:

- Café
- Shop
- Ticketing
- Front-of-House
- Cultural programme

---

## Snagging Process

Record every issue.

Each item should include:

- Number
- Description
- Owner
- Deadline
- Completion status

Prioritise:

1. Safety
2. Guest experience
3. Cosmetic issues

---

## Decision Point

**Have all Critical and High priority issues been resolved?**

### Yes

Proceed to the Grand Opening.

### No

Resolve issues before opening publicly.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Soft Opening completed
- [ ] Operations fully tested
- [ ] Snagging log completed
- [ ] Critical issues resolved
- [ ] Guest feedback collected
- [ ] Gate 3 readiness confirmed

---

# Stage 7 — Grand Opening

## Step 7 — Execute Launch Week

Launch Week is the execution of everything prepared over the previous 120 days.

---

## Community Opening

Host a dedicated event for:

- Palestinian community
- Families
- Local supporters

Create a welcoming and culturally meaningful experience.

---

## Grand Opening

Deliver a flagship programme with **Aswatna** including:

- Music
- Visual arts
- Spoken word
- Café
- Shop
- Community gathering

---

## Media

Coordinate:

- Press
- Interviews
- Photography
- Community coverage

Assign one media lead.

Escalate politically sensitive enquiries to HQ.

---

## Documentation

Track throughout Launch Week:

- Attendance
- Memberships
- Revenue
- Media
- Feedback

Submit a launch report to HQ within **five working days**.

---

## Decision Point

**Is Launch Week fully confirmed and operationally ready?**

### Yes

Execute with discipline.

### No

Resolve outstanding issues immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Community opening delivered
- [ ] Grand Opening completed
- [ ] Attendance recorded
- [ ] Membership growth tracked
- [ ] Media documented
- [ ] HQ report submitted

---

# Stage 8 — Stabilisation

## Step 8 — Transition to Ongoing Operations

Launching is the beginning—not the end.

The first month determines long-term sustainability.

---

## Post-Launch Review

Complete within two weeks.

Review:

- Launch performance
- Guest feedback
- Financial performance
- Lessons learned
- Process improvements

---

## Monthly HQ Reporting

Begin from Month One.

Include:

- Attendance
- Membership
- Programme
- Finance
- Community issues
- Priorities

---

## Maintain Programme Quality

Ensure the next three months of programming are confirmed before Launch Week concludes.

Avoid any post-launch programme gap.

---

## Community Engagement

Maintain relationships through:

- Community outreach
- Monthly programmes
- Membership conversion
- Feedback collection

---

## Decision Point

**By the end of Month One has the organisation transitioned into stable operations?**

### Yes

The launch is complete.

Continue operating under the Local Operations Playbook.

### No

Escalate significant operational issues to HQ immediately.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] Post-launch review completed
- [ ] Monthly HQ reporting established
- [ ] Three months of programming confirmed
- [ ] Operational gaps addressed
- [ ] Community engagement plan active
- [ ] Financial performance monitored

---

# Launch Standard

Launching a Palestine House is not simply opening a venue.

It is founding a cultural institution that will serve, represent and strengthen the Palestinian community for years to come.

The:

- 120-Day Launch Framework
- Three Go/No-Go Gates
- Structured operational discipline

exist because experience has demonstrated that rushing the process creates long-term organisational risk.

Every gate exists to protect:

- The community
- The partner
- The team
- The Palestine House brand
- The future sustainability of the organisation

Never skip a step because of pressure or impatience.

Open when you are ready—not before.

Then continue operating with the same discipline that made the launch successful.

