## Mission, Values & Guest Promise Overview

---

## Purpose

The Mission, Values & Guest Promise element defines the core foundation of how Palestine House operates, makes decisions, and delivers its experience. Its purpose is to translate the abstract idea of a cultural embassy into clear, actionable standards that guide behavior across every level of the organization. The mission defines why the House exists, the values define how it behaves, and the guest promise defines what every visitor should consistently experience. This element ensures that all operations, programming, partnerships, and service delivery remain aligned with a single, coherent identity. Without this foundation, execution becomes fragmented, decisions become inconsistent, and the brand loses clarity and trust.

---

## When and where it is used

This element is used continuously across all aspects of Palestine House operations, from strategic planning to daily execution. During launch, it is used to align the local team, inform design decisions, and shape the overall experience model. In ongoing operations, it is applied in staff training, programming decisions, partnership selection, and guest interaction standards. It is also used as a decision filter when evaluating opportunities, resolving conflicts, or responding to unexpected situations. At the front-of-house level, it directly shapes how guests are welcomed, guided, and engaged. At the leadership level, it is used to ensure that all actions remain consistent with the mission and values, particularly when trade-offs or high-pressure decisions are required.

---

## Who is responsible

Ownership of the Mission, Values & Guest Promise is shared across GLOBAL and LOCAL, with clear accountability. GLOBAL is responsible for defining, maintaining, and protecting the mission, values, and core guest promise, ensuring consistency across the entire network. GLOBAL also ensures that these elements are clearly documented, communicated, and embedded into all systems and standards. LOCAL leadership, specifically the General Manager or Head of Operations, is responsible for translating these principles into daily behavior, training staff, and ensuring that the guest experience consistently reflects the defined promise. All staff, including front-of-house, programming, and support teams, are responsible for applying these principles in their roles. This is not a passive document; it is an active operational standard that must be visible in every interaction and decision.

---

## Expected outcomes

When implemented correctly, this element produces consistent and measurable outcomes across the House. Guests experience a clear and coherent identity, where every visit feels aligned, intentional, and welcoming. Staff operate with clarity and confidence, understanding how to act and make decisions without constant supervision. Programming and partnerships reflect the same cultural positioning, reinforcing trust and credibility with the community. The House builds a strong reputation as a reliable, respectful, and well-run cultural space. Over time, this consistency drives repeat visits, stronger membership engagement, and deeper community trust. It also reduces operational friction, as teams are guided by shared principles rather than reacting inconsistently to situations.

---

## Scope and limitations

The scope of this element includes the definition and application of mission, values, and the guest promise across all operational areas, including service delivery, programming, partnerships, and internal culture. It acts as a guiding framework for behavior and decision-making but does not replace detailed SOPs, training modules, or operational checklists, which are required to implement it effectively. It also does not allow subjective interpretation that contradicts established standards; all local adaptations must remain aligned with GLOBAL definitions. This element is not optional or flexible at its core level. Any deviation that risks brand consistency, cultural positioning, or guest experience must be escalated and corrected. While local expression is encouraged in tone and execution, it must always operate within the defined mission, values, and guest promise to ensure that every Palestine House delivers a consistent and trusted experience.
$md$, $md$# SIMPLE GUIDE

## Mission, Values & Guest Promise

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook*

---

## PURPOSE OF THIS GUIDE

This guide explains how to understand, embed, and live the Palestine House Mission, Values, and Guest Promise in your location. These are not aspirational statements — they are operational standards. Every staff member, every interaction, and every decision at Palestine House must reflect them.

Work through each step in order before your location opens to the public. Return to this guide whenever you onboard new staff, review your operations, or feel the values need to be re-anchored in your team.

---

## WHO THIS GUIDE IS FOR

This guide is for the House Manager and all staff at every Palestine House location. It is also for the Local Partner organisation as a whole. The Mission, Values, and Guest Promise apply to the House as an institution — not only to individual staff members.

Every new staff member must review this guide before their first guest-facing shift. House Managers are responsible for ensuring this happens.

---

## 1. Understand the Palestine House Mission

Palestine House exists to celebrate Palestinian culture, creativity, and identity — and to share it with the world. Every location in the network carries this purpose. The mission is not determined by the local partner. It is set by Palestine Cultural Embassy (HQ) and applies uniformly across all locations.

### THE PALESTINE HOUSE MISSION

> To create a global network of Palestinian cultural spaces where communities gather, creators thrive, and Palestinian identity is celebrated with dignity.

As a local operator, your role is to serve this mission in your city. You are not creating something new — you are delivering Palestine House in your context. Everything you do operationally must be assessed against this question:

> *"Does this decision serve the Palestinian community with dignity and advance the mission of Palestine House?"*

### DECISION POINT: Does every operational decision you make pass the mission test?

**YES ->** Continue. The decision is aligned with the Palestine House mission.

**NO  ->** Stop. Do not proceed. Reassess the decision or escalate to HQ before acting.

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* House Manager can state the mission accurately without referring to notes
* All staff members have read the mission statement during onboarding
* The mission is displayed visibly in the staff area of the venue

---

## 2. Learn the Core Values

Palestine House operates according to five core values. These are not slogans. They are the standards by which every team member, every programme, and every guest interaction is measured. Know them. Discuss them. Apply them daily.

### Dignity

Every person who enters Palestine House — guest, community member, artist, partner, or staff member — is treated with full respect and without judgement.

*In practice: Greet every guest by name when known. Speak respectfully in all interactions. Resolve complaints without condescension.*

### Authenticity

Palestine House celebrates Palestinian culture as it is — rich, diverse, complex, and living. We do not flatten or simplify it for external consumption.

*In practice: Programme cultural events that reflect the full breadth of Palestinian expression. Do not reduce Palestinian identity to a single narrative.*

### Excellence

Every aspect of Palestine House — the venue, the programming, the service, the communications — must meet a high standard. Passion alone is not enough. Discipline delivers.

*In practice: Use the checklists. Follow the SOPs. Do not cut corners. Review quality consistently and correct failures quickly.*

### Inclusion

Palestine House is a space for the Palestinian community and for everyone who wants to engage with Palestinian culture respectfully. No guest is turned away on the basis of who they are.

*In practice: Ensure the space is physically accessible. Train staff on inclusive guest service. Ensure programming reflects diverse voices within Palestinian culture.*

### Non-Political Positioning

Palestine House is a cultural institution, not a political organisation. It does not endorse political parties, movements, or candidates. It does not host speakers or events that function as political advocacy without HQ approval.

*In practice: Review all programming and partnerships through the non-political lens before confirming. When in doubt, escalate to HQ.*

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* All five core values are known by name to every staff member
* Values are displayed in the staff area and referenced in team meetings
* House Manager can give a practical example of each value in daily operations

---

## 3. Understand and Commit to the Guest Promise

The Guest Promise is what Palestine House commits to every person who walks through the door. It is not marketing language — it is an operational contract with your guests. Every staff member must know it, and every operational system must support it.

### 1. You will always be welcomed.

Every guest receives a warm, genuine acknowledgement within 30 seconds of entering the venue. No guest is left standing unacknowledged. Greet by name when known.

### 2. You will experience Palestinian culture at its best.

Programming, exhibitions, and food must meet the quality standard. Aswatna is the preferred cultural programming partner. HQ approves all major programming decisions.

### 3. Your dignity will always be respected.

No guest is treated differently based on their background, appearance, beliefs, or community affiliation. Complaint resolution is handled with care and discretion. Staff speak respectfully at all times.

### 4. You will find a space that is safe, clean, and well-run.

The venue is maintained to a high standard at all times. Safety checks are completed daily. Accessibility needs are met. Cleanliness is non-negotiable.

### 5. Your concerns will always be heard.

Any complaint is acknowledged within 2 minutes in person, or within 24 hours in writing. Complaints are resolved or escalated — never ignored. Guests are followed up within 48 hours of any escalated complaint.

> *NOTE: The Guest Promise is a standard, not an aspiration. If your operations cannot currently deliver on any of these five promises, that gap must be identified and resolved before opening to the public.*

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* All five Guest Promise commitments are known by all guest-facing staff
* Each promise has a corresponding operational system in place (greeting protocol, quality check, complaint process, safety log, follow-up procedure)
* Guest Promise is displayed in a public-facing area of the venue

---

## 4. Train Your Team on Mission, Values and the Guest Promise

Before your location opens, every staff member must complete Mission & Values Orientation. This is not optional and it is not a one-time activity. It is the foundation of every other training at Palestine House.

The Mission & Values Orientation must cover:

* The Palestine House mission statement — what it means and why it matters
* All five core values — with a practical example for each
* The Guest Promise — all five commitments and how each is delivered operationally
* The non-political positioning principle — what it means in practice and what to do if it is challenged
* The escalation path — what staff should do when a situation feels like it conflicts with the values

The House Manager leads the first orientation session. New staff joining after opening must complete orientation within their first three working days, before any guest-facing shift.

### DECISION POINT: Has every staff member completed Mission & Values Orientation before their first guest-facing shift?

**YES ->** Continue. Your team is values-trained and cleared to serve guests.

**NO  ->** Stop. No untrained staff member may work a guest-facing shift. Schedule orientation immediately.

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Signed training register showing all staff have completed Mission & Values Orientation
* Orientation session notes or materials on file
* House Manager has confirmed each team member understands the non-political positioning principle

---

## 5. Embed the Values in the Physical Space

The mission and values must be visible and felt in the venue itself — not just in training sessions. A guest who has never read your handbook should be able to sense the values of Palestine House from the moment they enter.

Required physical embeddings:

* Mission statement displayed in a prominent public area — framed or printed to brand standard
* Guest Promise displayed at reception or near the main entrance
* Staff area: values posted visibly where staff gather for briefings
* All signage, printed materials, and digital screens use the approved Palestine House brand guidelines
* The space is clean, welcoming, and accessible — physical environment reflects the dignity value

All physical brand materials must be approved by HQ before installation. Do not create your own versions of the mission statement or values without HQ review.

### DECISION POINT: Has HQ approved all physical brand materials displaying the mission, values, or Guest Promise?

**YES ->** Materials are approved. Proceed with installation.

**NO  ->** Materials have not been approved. Submit for HQ review before displaying. Do not install unapproved materials.

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Mission statement displayed in public area — HQ-approved version
* Guest Promise visible at reception or entrance
* Values posted in staff area
* All brand materials carry HQ approval confirmation

---

## 6. Apply the Values in Daily Operations

Values are not a policy document — they are a daily practice. The House Manager must actively connect operational decisions to the values in every team meeting, briefing, and debrief.

Practical ways to embed values in daily operations:

* Start every shift briefing with one values-based question: "Which value are we focusing on today and why?"
* When reviewing a guest complaint, name which value was not delivered and discuss how to restore it
* When approving programming, ask explicitly: "Does this reflect Authenticity and our non-political positioning?"
* When hiring, assess candidates against the values — not just technical skills
* When a vendor, partner, or supplier interaction feels wrong, name which value is being compromised

Every week, the House Manager should identify at least one instance where a value was delivered well, and one where it was not — and discuss both in the team.

> *NOTE: Values are most tested under pressure — during a busy event, a difficult complaint, or a resource shortage. This is when the discipline must hold. Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.*

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Values referenced by name in at least one team meeting per week
* House Manager can give a recent example of a values-based operational decision
* Staff are able to name the relevant value when discussing a guest service situation

---

## 7. Handle Situations That Challenge the Values

There will be moments when a guest, a partner, a vendor, or an internal situation appears to conflict with the Palestine House values. These situations require a clear, confident response — not improvisation.

Common challenging situations and how to respond:

| Situation                                                                        | Values at Risk            | Required Response                                                                                                                    |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| A guest or speaker attempts to use the venue for political advocacy              | Non-Political Positioning | Politely decline or redirect. Escalate to HQ before the event if possible. Do not allow the session to proceed without HQ clearance. |
| A guest is rude or dismissive to a staff member                                  | Dignity                   | Support the staff member. The House Manager addresses the guest calmly. Dignity applies to staff as much as guests.                  |
| A partner proposes programming that feels culturally inauthentic or stereotyping | Authenticity              | Raise the concern with the partner. Escalate to HQ and Aswatna for review before confirming the programme.                           |
| A guest with accessibility needs arrives and the venue is not fully prepared     | Inclusion, Dignity        | Accommodate immediately and creatively. Log the gap. Implement the fix within 48 hours. Report to HQ.                                |
| A staff member cuts corners on a safety or service standard under event pressure | Excellence                | Stop the shortcut. The standard applies regardless of pressure. The House Manager addresses this immediately — not after the event.  |

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* All staff know what to do when they observe a values conflict
* House Manager has reviewed the challenge scenarios with the team
* Escalation path to HQ is known and accessible for political content or brand conflicts

---

## 8. Communicate the Mission to Community and Partners

The Palestine House mission and values must be communicated externally — to guests, community partners, sponsors, media, and the wider public. This communication must be consistent with HQ-approved language.

How to communicate the mission externally:

* Use the approved mission statement — word for word — in all printed and digital materials
* Include the Guest Promise on your website, at reception, and in member welcome communications
* When introducing Palestine House to a new partner, begin with the mission and values — not the commercial offer
* In media or press interviews, lead with the cultural mission. Do not comment on political topics without HQ direction
* In sponsorship conversations, make clear that Palestine House is non-political and that all partnerships must be consistent with the values

Any external communication that references the Palestine House mission, values, or Guest Promise must use HQ-approved language. Do not paraphrase or rewrite the mission for different audiences.

### DECISION POINT: Does this external communication use HQ-approved language for the mission and values?

**YES ->** Proceed. The language is approved and consistent.

**NO  ->** Do not publish. Submit the communication to HQ for review and approval before releasing.

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Mission statement and Guest Promise are live on the local website — in HQ-approved language
* Partner introductory materials reference the mission and values
* Media and sponsorship communications have been reviewed against the non-political positioning standard

---

## 9. Report on Values Alignment to HQ

HQ monitors values alignment across the network. As a local operator, you are required to report on how the Mission, Values, and Guest Promise are being embedded and lived in your location — not just on commercial or operational KPIs.

What to include in your monthly HQ report under Mission & Values:

* One example of a decision or action that reflects each core value from the past month
* Any situation where a value was challenged and how it was handled
* Any programming or partnership decision that required a non-political positioning assessment
* Guest feedback or complaints that relate to the Guest Promise, and how they were resolved
* Any staff values concerns — conduct, training gaps, or cultural misalignments

If a values breach occurs — a decision was made that contradicts the mission, a political situation was handled incorrectly, or the Guest Promise was systematically not delivered — this must be reported to HQ as a priority flag, not included as a footnote in the monthly report.

> *NOTE: Values reporting is not a compliance exercise. It is how HQ understands whether Palestine House is being delivered with integrity across the network. Take it seriously.*

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Monthly HQ report includes a Mission & Values section with specific examples
* Any values breach or political situation has been escalated to HQ separately and promptly
* Guest Promise delivery is referenced in the customer service section of the monthly report

---

## 10. Conduct a Quarterly Values Review

Every quarter, the House Manager leads a structured Values Review with the full team. This is not a training repeat — it is a live reflection on how well the values are being practiced and where the gaps are.

The Quarterly Values Review agenda:

* Opening: State one thing the team did well in living the values this quarter
* Review each value: For each of the five values, identify one success and one gap from the past three months
* Guest Promise audit: Review the past quarter's complaint log and guest feedback — which promises were most challenged?
* Non-political positioning check: Were there any situations this quarter that tested our non-political positioning? How were they handled?
* Action plan: Identify the top two values-based improvements for the next quarter. Name the owner and the target date.
* Close: Re-read the mission statement as a team

The House Manager must submit a brief Values Review Summary to HQ within 7 days of the quarterly session. The summary does not need to be long — one paragraph per value, plus the action plan.

### EXPECTED OUTPUT

* Quarterly Values Review completed on schedule — within the first 2 weeks of each quarter
* Values Review Summary submitted to HQ within 7 days
* Action plan from the review is live — owner named and target date set
* All staff have participated in the session or been briefed on the outcomes

---

## QUICK REFERENCE: MISSION, VALUES & GUEST PROMISE

| Step | Action                                              | Who                       | When                                    |
| ---- | --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| 1    | Understand the Palestine House Mission              | House Manager + all staff | Before opening — then ongoing           |
| 2    | Learn the five Core Values                          | House Manager + all staff | Before opening — then ongoing           |
| 3    | Know and commit to the Guest Promise                | House Manager + all staff | Before opening — then ongoing           |
| 4    | Complete Mission & Values Orientation for all staff | House Manager             | Before first guest-facing shift         |
| 5    | Embed values in the physical space                  | House Manager             | Before opening — HQ approval required   |
| 6    | Apply values in daily operations                    | House Manager + all staff | Every day                               |
| 7    | Handle values challenges correctly                  | House Manager + all staff | As situations arise                     |
| 8    | Communicate mission to community and partners       | House Manager             | Ongoing — HQ-approved language only     |
| 9    | Report values alignment to HQ                       | House Manager             | Monthly                                 |
| 10   | Conduct Quarterly Values Review                     | House Manager + team      | Every quarter — summary to HQ in 7 days |

---

## A FINAL WORD

Palestine House is more than a venue. It is a statement of culture, identity, and possibility. Everything you do as a local operator — how you greet a guest, how you resolve a complaint, how you choose a programme, how you treat a staff member — carries the weight of that statement.

The mission, values, and Guest Promise are your operating system. They do not constrain what you can do. They define what Palestine House is.

**Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.**
$md$, $md$# WHAT TO WATCH OUT FOR

## Mission, Values & Guest Promise

*Palestine House Local Operations Playbook   |   15 Risk Cards*

---

## Risk Card Categories

* COMMON MISTAKE
* RISK
* RED FLAG
* COMPLIANCE ISSUE
* ESCALATION TRIGGER

> *Each card identifies a specific risk or failure mode in Mission, Values & Guest Promise operations. The "Escalate if" field defines when the situation must go to HQ. When in doubt, escalate. Palestine House would rather receive an unnecessary escalation than learn about a values failure through a third party.*

---

# SECTION 1: COMMON MISTAKES — Operational Errors That Weaken the Values Framework

---

## 01. Treating the Mission as a Wall Display, Not an Operating Standard

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What it looks like

The mission statement is printed and framed at reception, the values poster is in the staff room, and the Guest Promise card sits at the front desk — but none of them are ever referenced in team meetings, briefings, or decisions. Staff can point to where the values are posted but cannot say what they mean in their daily role.

### Why it matters

When mission and values exist only as decor, they stop functioning as standards. The venue looks aligned on the surface but operates without a guiding framework underneath. Guests notice the gap — even when they cannot name it — in the quality of interactions, the consistency of service, and the feel of the space.

### What to do

Reference the values by name in every shift briefing. When making any programming, partnership, or service decision, state which value it serves or tests. The House Manager must model this behaviour every day — values-referencing must be a habit, not an event.

### Escalate if

A guest or partner comments that "something feels off" about the space, or that the experience does not match how Palestine House presents itself publicly. Or HQ visits and finds no evidence that the values are being actively discussed in operations.

---

## 02. Paraphrasing or Rewriting the Mission Statement for Local Context

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What it looks like

The House Manager or Local Partner produces their own version of the Palestine House mission — shorter, simpler, or "more relevant" for the local community. The rewritten version appears on the local website, in partner materials, and in event programmes.

### Why it matters

The Palestine House mission statement is set by HQ and is uniform across all locations. It is not a template for local adaptation — it is a fixed standard. A locally rewritten mission creates brand inconsistency, dilutes the global network identity, and may misrepresent the purpose of Palestine House to local audiences, funders, and partners.

### What to do

Use the HQ-approved mission statement word for word in all materials. If you believe the local context requires a supplementary statement, submit a proposal to HQ for review. Do not publish any version of the mission statement that has not been confirmed by HQ.

### Escalate if

HQ discovers a locally rewritten version of the mission statement in any published material — digital, print, or display. This requires immediate correction and may trigger a brand compliance review.

---

## 03. Skipping Mission & Values Orientation for Staff Who "Already Get It"

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What it looks like

A new team member joins with a strong Palestinian background or cultural knowledge. The House Manager assumes that because they care about the cause, they do not need the formal orientation. The training register is left incomplete.

### Why it matters

Mission & Values Orientation is not a test of cultural knowledge or personal commitment — it is a structured onboarding into Palestine House operating standards. Staff who skip it may be deeply passionate but still not know the Guest Promise, the non-political positioning rules, or the escalation path. These are operational requirements, not cultural credentials.

### What to do

Every staff member completes the orientation before their first guest-facing shift — without exception. Cultural knowledge and personal connection to Palestine are assets, but they do not substitute for the standardised onboarding. Document the completed orientation in the training register with a date and signature.

### Escalate if

An untrained staff member handles a politically sensitive guest interaction or Guest Promise situation and makes a decision based on personal judgement rather than Palestine House protocol.

---

## 04. Applying Values Selectively to Guests and Not to Staff

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What it looks like

The House Manager holds the Dignity and Inclusion values to a high standard when dealing with guests but does not apply them internally. Staff members are spoken to dismissively, junior team members are excluded from decisions that affect them, or a values breach by a staff member is treated as a minor operational issue.

### Why it matters

Values that apply only externally are not values — they are marketing. A team that does not experience Dignity, Inclusion, and Authenticity in how they are managed will not deliver them consistently to guests. Cultural inconsistency between how staff are treated and how guests are expected to be treated erodes team trust and undermines the entire values framework.

### What to do

Apply the five values explicitly to internal team management. In the Quarterly Values Review, include a reflection on how the values are being lived internally — not just in guest interactions. Address internal values breaches with the same seriousness as external ones.

### Escalate if

A staff member raises a concern that the values are not being applied to how the team is managed. Or HQ observes a significant gap between how the House presents its values externally and how the team describes working there.

---

# SECTION 2: RISKS — Structural Risks to Mission Integrity Over Time

---

## 05. Commercial Pressure Causing Values Compromises

**Category:** RISK

### What it looks like

The House is under financial pressure. A sponsorship is offered by an organisation whose values do not fully align with Palestine House. A venue hire is accepted from a group whose purpose is unclear. A programming decision is made primarily because it generates revenue rather than because it serves the mission.

### Why it matters

Commercial sustainability is a real and legitimate concern. But Palestine House locations that compromise their values for short-term revenue create long-term reputational damage that is far more costly. The mission is the brand. If the brand is compromised, the value of the whole network is reduced — not just the local House.

### What to do

Every commercial decision must pass the mission test before it passes the financial test. If a revenue opportunity requires a values compromise, escalate to HQ rather than accepting it unilaterally. HQ may have options or context that are not visible at the local level.

### Escalate if

A sponsorship or venue hire arrangement is agreed that a reasonable observer would consider inconsistent with Palestinian cultural values, the non-political positioning principle, or the Dignity and Inclusion standards.

---

## 06. Staff Burnout Causing Values Drift Over Time

**Category:** RISK

### What it looks like

In the first months after opening, mission and values are frequently referenced and genuinely felt. Over time — as operational pressures increase, staff become tired, and the novelty of launch fades — the values start to be mentioned less often. The Quarterly Values Review is treated as a compliance exercise rather than a genuine reflection.

### Why it matters

Values drift is one of the most common long-term risks in mission-driven organisations. It does not happen dramatically — it happens gradually, through small compromises, dropped routines, and declining energy for the harder conversations. By the time it becomes visible, it has usually been accumulating for months.

### What to do

Build structural habits that do not depend on energy or enthusiasm — mandatory values reference in shift briefings, the Quarterly Values Review as a fixed calendar event, HQ monthly reporting with genuine examples. The House Manager must treat values maintenance as an operational discipline, not an optional extra.

### Escalate if

Three consecutive monthly HQ reports contain no specific values examples — only generic statements. Or a Quarterly Values Review is cancelled or postponed without rescheduling within the same quarter.

---

## 07. Guest Promise Commitments Made Without Supporting Systems

**Category:** RISK

### What it looks like

The Guest Promise is displayed at reception. Staff have memorised it. But the systems that deliver it are not in place — there is no complaint acknowledgement protocol, no follow-up process, no accessibility provision, and no quality check on programming. The promise exists as a statement, not as an operational reality.

### Why it matters

A Guest Promise that cannot be delivered is worse than no promise at all. It sets an expectation that the House then systematically fails to meet. Guests who feel the gap between the promise and the reality are more likely to be disappointed than guests who had no prior expectation.

### What to do

Before displaying the Guest Promise, complete the operational checklist for each commitment. Confirm: a greeting protocol exists and is followed, a complaint process is documented and trained, accessibility is assessed and provided for, quality checks are scheduled, and follow-up is tracked. Only display the promise when the systems are live.

### Escalate if

Guest feedback or complaint data shows that one or more Guest Promise commitments is being systematically not met. Or a mystery visit or HQ audit reveals a gap between the promise and operational reality.

---

# SECTION 3: RED FLAGS — Situations That Require Immediate Action

---

## 08. A Speaker, Programme, or Partnership Functions as Political Advocacy

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What it looks like

An event is booked or a partnership is confirmed that, on examination, functions as political advocacy — promoting a political party, movement, candidate, or position — even if it is framed as cultural or educational. The content may feel aligned with Palestinian values but is in practice political in nature.

### Why it matters

Palestine House's non-political positioning is the most critical and most vulnerable of its operating principles. A single political event or endorsement — even one that feels morally justified — can permanently damage the network's credibility with funders, government bodies, media, and community partners who rely on Palestine House remaining a cultural institution. The damage extends beyond the local House to the entire network.

### What to do

Do not approve any programme, speaker, or partnership that you are uncertain about. The test is not whether the content feels right — it is whether a reasonable outside observer would characterise it as political advocacy. When in doubt, escalate to HQ before confirming. Do not rely on your own assessment for borderline cases.

### Escalate if

Any programme, speaker, event, or partnership that could be characterised as political advocacy by a neutral observer — regardless of intent. HQ must review and approve before the event proceeds. If the event has already been announced, HQ must be notified immediately.

---

## 09. The Mission Statement Appears in External Materials Without HQ Approval

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What it looks like

A locally produced version of the mission statement, values, or Guest Promise — different in wording, emphasis, or structure from the HQ-approved version — is published on the local website, in a grant application, in a press release, or in a partner prospectus.

### Why it matters

Unauthorised mission language creates brand inconsistency across the Palestine House network and may misrepresent the purpose and scope of the institution to external audiences. In grant or funding contexts, an unapproved mission statement could create legal or contractual complications for HQ.

### What to do

Audit all current external materials immediately. Remove any version of the mission, values, or Guest Promise that is not the exact HQ-approved text. Replace with the approved versions. Submit a correction to HQ noting what was published, where, and for how long.

### Escalate if

The unapproved version has appeared in a funding application, a government submission, or a media outlet. HQ must be notified immediately so they can assess whether a formal correction is needed.

---

## 10. A Guest or Partner Publicly Challenges Palestine House Values

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What it looks like

A guest, former partner, or community member makes a public statement — on social media, to media, or in a community forum — alleging that Palestine House is not living its values: that guests were treated without dignity, that the space is exclusionary, that programming was inauthentic, or that political content was hosted.

### Why it matters

Public challenges to values alignment are high-stakes events that combine reputational risk (how the network is perceived) with compliance risk (was there an actual values breach?) and relationship risk (how community trust is affected). Left unaddressed, they compound quickly.

### What to do

Do not respond publicly without HQ direction. Take a screenshot and preserve the full record of the public statement. Conduct an internal review immediately to assess whether the allegation has any basis in fact. Escalate to HQ within 2 hours with your assessment and the full record.

### Escalate if

The statement is shared widely, cited in media, or corroborated by other community members. In this case, HQ takes the lead on all communications and community engagement. The local House manager does not make any public statement without explicit HQ approval.

---

# SECTION 4: COMPLIANCE ISSUES — Breaches of Palestine House Operating Standards

---

## 11. Mission & Values Orientation Not Completed Before Guest-Facing Shifts

**Category:** COMPLIANCE ISSUE

### What it looks like

A staff member has worked guest-facing shifts without completing the Mission & Values Orientation. The training register is either not up to date or shows the orientation was completed after the staff member had already been working with guests.

### Why it matters

Mission & Values Orientation is a mandatory pre-condition for any guest-facing role at Palestine House. A staff member who has not completed it may misrepresent the values, fail to recognise a non-political positioning situation, or handle a Guest Promise breach without knowing the correct protocol. This is an operational compliance failure, not merely a training administration gap.

### What to do

Remove the staff member from guest-facing shifts immediately until the orientation is completed. Update the training register accurately — do not backdate entries. Report the gap to HQ in the next monthly report. Implement a check that prevents shift scheduling for staff who have not completed the orientation.

### Escalate if

A guest-facing staff member who has not completed orientation is involved in a values-sensitive situation — a political content complaint, a safeguarding concern, or a Guest Promise breach. The resulting error must be reported to HQ immediately along with the training register gap.

---

## 12. Quarterly Values Review Not Completed on Schedule

**Category:** COMPLIANCE ISSUE

### What it looks like

The Quarterly Values Review has been missed, postponed past the end of the quarter, or conducted without all staff and without producing an action plan. The summary was not submitted to HQ within 7 days of the session.

### Why it matters

The Quarterly Values Review is a compliance requirement, not an optional best practice. It is the primary mechanism through which HQ monitors values alignment across the network and through which local teams course-correct before drift becomes embedded. A missed review is a reporting compliance breach that undermines the integrity of the values framework.

### What to do

Schedule all four quarterly reviews at the start of the year as fixed, non-negotiable calendar events. If a session must be rescheduled due to genuine emergency, notify HQ before the original date and confirm the new date within 7 days. Never allow two consecutive quarters to pass without a completed review.

### Escalate if

Two consecutive quarters have passed without a completed Quarterly Values Review. HQ escalation is required and a corrective action plan must be submitted within 14 days.

---

# SECTION 5: ESCALATION TRIGGERS — When Local Authority Is Not Sufficient

---

## 13. Any Political Content Situation That Was Not Escalated Before Proceeding

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What it looks like

A programme, partnership, or communication with political content proceeded without HQ review. This may have been because it seemed borderline, because the decision was made quickly under event pressure, or because the House Manager believed they could assess it independently.

### Why it matters

The non-political positioning principle exists precisely for situations that feel borderline — clear cases are easy to manage. Situations that feel complex, nuanced, or politically sensitive are exactly the situations that require HQ involvement. Independent local assessment of political content risk is not permitted under Palestine House operating standards.

### What to do

Immediately after identifying that a political content situation was handled without HQ review, notify HQ with a full account of what happened, what decision was made, and what the current status is. Do not attempt to retroactively manage the situation without HQ direction.

### Escalate if

Any situation in which political content was present in programming, communications, or a partnership, and HQ was not consulted before the decision was finalised. This is a compliance breach regardless of outcome — HQ must be notified even if no harm resulted.

---

## 14. A Guest, Staff Member, or Community Partner Alleges a Values Breach Formally

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What it looks like

A guest, staff member, or community organisation submits a formal written complaint, files a grievance, or makes a public statement specifically alleging that Palestine House violated one or more of its stated values — Dignity, Authenticity, Excellence, Inclusion, or Non-Political Positioning.

### Why it matters

A formal values breach allegation is categorically different from an operational complaint about service quality. It challenges the integrity of the institution, not just the performance of a single interaction. Left at the local level, it may be mishandled in ways that create legal, reputational, or contractual exposure for HQ and the broader network.

### What to do

Do not attempt to investigate or respond to a formal values breach allegation independently. Preserve all written records. Notify HQ on the same day with the full written allegation and a factual summary of the situation. Await HQ direction before any written or public response is made.

### Escalate if

The allegation appears in writing — email, letter, or published statement. It names Palestine House by its full name. It alleges a specific values failure. HQ must lead the response and may involve legal counsel. No communication from the local House is appropriate without explicit HQ approval.

---

## 15. HQ Requests a Values Alignment Audit and the House Cannot Produce Evidence

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What it looks like

HQ requests documentation confirming values alignment — training registers, orientation sign-off records, Quarterly Values Review summaries, values assessment logs for programming and partnerships — and the House Manager cannot produce complete, accurate records.

### Why it matters

The inability to produce values alignment evidence is itself a compliance failure. Palestine House operates a network model that depends on each location demonsteding consistent standards. A location that cannot document its values practice has effectively lost the ability to demonstrate that it is operating as a Palestine House. This has implications for the license agreement and for HQ's ability to report on network-wide standards to funders and governance bodies.

### What to do

Treat documentation as an operational discipline from day one — not something to create retrospectively when requested. Maintain the training register, review summaries, assessment logs, and brand approval records as live operational files. The House Manager is accountable for document completeness at all times.

### Escalate if

HQ requests an audit and the House cannot produce: a current training register, at least one Quarterly Values Review summary, evidence of non-political positioning assessments, and brand approval records for all displayed materials. Incomplete documentation triggers a formal compliance review and may affect the license agreement.

---

# A NOTE FOR HOUSE MANAGERS

The risks in this document are not theoretical. They represent the points at which mission-driven organisations most commonly fail — not through bad intentions, but through accumulated small decisions, skipped habits, and gradual drift. The discipline of Palestine House is in preventing that drift.

*Use this document in onboarding, in Quarterly Values Reviews, and whenever you feel the values need to be re-anchored in your team. The mission of Palestine House is worth protecting. Passion Inspires. Discipline Delivers.*
