# SECTION 1: COMMON MISTAKES

## Operational Errors That Undermine Brand Standards

---

## 01. Treating the Brand Kit as a Starting Point, Not a Fixed Standard

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

The House Manager or a supplier receives the Brand Kit but treats it as a template that may be adapted.

Examples include:

* Adjusting colours slightly to suit the venue
* Choosing a similar but unapproved typeface because the approved font is unavailable locally
* Creating a “cleaner” version of the logo for digital use
* Changing spacing, proportions, or visual treatments
* Reinterpreting approved layouts

### Why It Matters

The Palestine House Brand Kit is not a design brief. It is a fixed specification established by HQ.

Every approved element is intended to create a consistent identity across the global network.

A guest who visits different Palestine House locations should experience the same level of brand quality and consistency. Local interpretations, even when well-intentioned, weaken that consistency.

### What to Do

Treat every Brand Kit element as non-negotiable unless HQ provides written approval for a change.

When an approved asset does not work in a specific context:

1. Document the constraint.
2. Submit the issue to HQ.
3. Request guidance or an approved alternative.
4. Wait for written approval before proceeding.

Provide the complete Brand Kit directly to every supplier.

Before work begins, obtain written confirmation that the supplier has:

* Received the Brand Kit
* Reviewed the applicable standards
* Understood that substitutions and adaptations are not permitted

### Escalate If

A supplier deliverable or installed brand element uses assets, colours, typography, or treatments that are not included in the Brand Kit.

Stop production or installation immediately.

Do not accept or pay for the non-compliant deliverable until it has been corrected to the approved Brand Kit standard.

---

## 02. Treating a Phone Call or WhatsApp Message as Written HQ Approval

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

The House Manager needs to proceed with a new brand element, such as:

* Signage
* A digital campaign
* An event design
* A printed item
* A website update

An HQ contact gives a positive verbal response during a phone call or sends a brief WhatsApp message. The team proceeds on the assumption that formal approval has been granted.

### Why It Matters

Verbal and informal approvals are not sufficient evidence of HQ approval.

The person who provided the response may:

* Change roles
* Misremember the discussion
* Lack the authority to approve
* Have approved only part of the proposal
* Have intended the response as preliminary guidance

During a compliance review or audit, the location must be able to demonstrate that the specific material received formal approval.

Without an approval record, the material is treated as unapproved.

### What to Do

Every HQ brand approval must be confirmed by email.

The email must:

* Identify the specific material
* Explain its intended use
* Include the relevant design or file
* Clearly state that approval has been granted

When verbal or WhatsApp guidance is received, follow it immediately with an email such as:

> Confirming our conversation, you have approved [describe the material] for [specific use]. Please reply to confirm.

Do not proceed until written confirmation is received.

### Escalate If

An HQ audit or compliance review requests approval evidence and the House can provide only:

* A WhatsApp exchange
* A handwritten note
* A verbal account
* A reference to a phone call

The material will be recorded as an unapproved brand use and will require corrective action.

---

## 03. Briefing Suppliers Verbally and Assuming They Understand the Standard

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

A local printer, designer, photographer, AV supplier, or events company is engaged to produce Palestine House materials.

The House Manager:

* Describes the brand verbally
* Mentions the colours during a conversation
* Sends a logo file through WhatsApp
* Assumes the supplier understands the required standard

The final deliverable arrives inconsistent with the Brand Kit.

### Why It Matters

Suppliers work according to their own professional habits, technical processes, and design preferences.

Without a formal written briefing, suppliers may make well-intentioned but non-compliant decisions.

Common outcomes include:

* Incorrect logo treatment
* Unapproved colour substitutions
* Wrong typography
* Inappropriate imagery
* Incorrect sizing or spacing
* Production beginning before review

By the time the error is discovered, the materials may already have been printed, installed, or distributed.

### What to Do

Issue every supplier a formal written brand briefing before work begins.

The briefing must include:

* The complete Brand Kit
* Requirements specific to the deliverable
* Approved files and assets
* Clear examples of permitted and prohibited treatments
* The review and approval process
* A requirement to submit proofs before production
* The consequences of non-compliant delivery

The supplier terms should make clear that:

* Corrections may be required at the supplier's cost
* Production cannot begin before approval
* Payment may be withheld until the work is compliant

Retain written acknowledgement that the supplier received and understood the briefing.

### Escalate If

A supplier deliverable is produced, printed, installed, distributed, or published before the House Manager completes a Brand Kit review.

When the material is already in public use:

* Notify HQ within 24 hours
* Document the extent of use
* Stop further distribution
* Implement a correction plan

---

## 04. Publishing Digital Content Without a Brand Review

**Category:** COMMON MISTAKE

### What It Looks Like

A staff member or volunteer publishes content without a House Manager brand review.

This may include:

* Social media posts
* Website updates
* Email campaigns
* Event listings
* Digital advertisements
* Partner announcements

The content may contain:

* Incorrect colours
* Unapproved typography
* Off-brand imagery
* A modified logo
* Paraphrased mission language
* Inconsistent tone or messaging

### Why It Matters

Digital content is one of the highest-volume and fastest-moving Palestine House brand touchpoints.

It may also be the first interaction that a potential guest, partner, media contact, or funder has with the organisation.

Non-compliant content can spread before the House Manager has an opportunity to correct it. Screenshots and forwarded emails may preserve the error after the original content is removed.

### What to Do

Establish a mandatory pre-publication review for all digital content.

No content may be published without House Manager sign-off against the Brand Kit.

Before granting publishing access, ensure that every staff member or volunteer understands:

* The Brand Kit
* Approved templates
* The review process
* Who may authorise publication
* Which content requires HQ approval

Consider using a one-page brand checklist covering:

* Logo use
* Colour accuracy
* Typography
* Imagery
* Mission and values language
* Co-branding
* Required approvals

### Escalate If

Non-compliant content has been published publicly.

Remove or correct it immediately.

Notify HQ within 24 hours when:

* The content references the Palestine House mission, values, or name
* An unauthorised logo or brand asset was used
* The content was viewed by more than 200 people
* The content involved a partner, sponsor, or political association

---

# SECTION 2: RISKS

## Structural Risks to Brand Integrity Over Time

---

## 05. Brand Drift Through Accumulated Small Adaptations

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

Small brand changes accumulate over several months.

Examples include:

* A slightly different shade of olive because the approved colour was unavailable
* A logo displayed below the approved minimum size
* A different typeface used for social media graphics
* A shortened mission statement on the website
* Locally created templates replacing HQ-approved files
* Inconsistent spacing or image treatments

Each change appears minor and practical, so no formal review takes place.

### Why It Matters

Individual adaptations may appear insignificant.

Collectively, they can create a location that no longer looks, sounds, or feels like Palestine House.

Brand drift is a common long-term risk for network organisations. It develops gradually and may continue for a significant period before becoming obvious.

When several deviations become the local norm, correcting them may require:

* Reprinting materials
* Replacing signage
* Rebuilding templates
* Retraining staff
* Revising supplier relationships

It may also signal to HQ that brand discipline is not being maintained.

### What to Do

Conduct a monthly brand audit.

Compare all current materials directly against the HQ Brand Kit, not against previous local materials.

Review:

* Physical signage
* Interior displays
* Printed materials
* Website content
* Social media templates
* Email communications
* Event materials
* Supplier deliverables
* Co-branded assets

Log every deviation, regardless of size.

Correct each deviation within five working days.

### Escalate If

A quarterly brand compliance summary, HQ visit, or audit reveals several simultaneous deviations.

This indicates systemic non-compliance and requires:

* A formal brand compliance review
* A documented root cause assessment
* A corrective action plan
* Submission to HQ within 14 days

---

## 06. Event Pressure Leading to Unapproved Co-Branding

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

A sponsor confirms support close to an event date and sends its logo for inclusion on:

* Event signage
* Digital promotions
* Printed programmes
* Venue screens
* Social media content

Under time pressure, the team produces the materials without HQ approval.

The sponsor branding may appear at a scale or prominence that exceeds Palestine House branding.

### Why It Matters

Co-branding and third-party brand placement require HQ approval.

Unapproved co-branding may:

* Dilute Palestine House identity
* Create an implied endorsement
* Conflict with non-political positioning
* Give a sponsor inappropriate prominence
* Create a funder dependency perception
* Associate Palestine House with an organisation not assessed by HQ

### What to Do

Build co-branding approval into the sponsorship process rather than the event production process.

HQ approval should already be in place before materials are designed.

Request standard co-branding approval at least 10 working days before production begins.

The approval submission should include:

* Sponsor or partner identity
* Nature of the relationship
* Proposed logo placement
* Relative logo sizes
* Intended materials
* Duration of use
* Final artwork or mock-ups

When a sponsor confirms late, delay production rather than proceeding without approval.

### Escalate If

Co-branded materials are produced, installed, distributed, or published before HQ approval.

Immediately:

1. Remove or cover the co-branding where possible.
2. Stop further production or distribution.
3. Contact HQ.
4. Provide the proposed materials and context.
5. Follow HQ instructions.

When the event is imminent, contact HQ by phone and follow up by email immediately.

---

## 07. Staff Turnover Eroding Brand Knowledge

**Category:** RISK

### What It Looks Like

The House Manager or communications lead who received the original brand training leaves or changes roles.

New staff members receive system access and operational responsibilities without structured brand training.

Over time, no current team member has sufficient knowledge of:

* The Brand Kit
* Approval requirements
* Supplier controls
* Co-branding rules
* Brand deviation reporting

### Why It Matters

Brand standards cannot be protected by files alone.

They depend on people understanding how to apply the standards in real operational situations.

Without structured knowledge transfer, brand management becomes dependent on individual employees.

Each staff transition may increase the risk of:

* Incorrect asset use
* Unapproved publication
* Missing approval records
* Supplier errors
* Brand drift

### What to Do

Make brand training a mandatory onboarding requirement for every role involving:

* Communications
* Design
* Event production
* Procurement
* Website management
* Social media
* Signage
* Supplier management

Maintain a current training register showing:

* Staff member
* Role
* Training date
* Training areas completed
* Trainer
* Refresher date where applicable

Review the register quarterly and whenever responsibilities change.

### Escalate If

An HQ audit or inspection finds that:

* No current team member can demonstrate familiarity with the Brand Kit
* Staff in communications or design roles have not completed brand training
* Brand training records do not exist

This triggers mandatory retraining and a brand compliance remediation plan.

---

# SECTION 3: RED FLAGS

## Situations That Require Immediate Action

---

## 08. The Palestine House Logo Has Been Recreated, Modified, or Redrawn

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What It Looks Like

A modified version of the Palestine House logo is discovered on:

* Signage
* Digital assets
* Printed programmes
* Event materials
* Supplier files
* Merchandise
* Website pages

Modifications may include:

* Colour changes
* Redrawing or recreation
* Distortion
* Stretching
* Simplification
* Added or removed elements
* Unapproved effects
* Incorrect proportions

### Why It Matters

Logo integrity is a foundational element of the Palestine House identity.

Any modification creates an unauthorised version of the brand mark.

Public use of a modified logo may:

* Misrepresent Palestine House
* Weaken visual consistency
* Create intellectual property complications
* Make enforcement of logo ownership more difficult
* Cause third parties to reuse the incorrect version

### What to Do

Remove all instances of the modified logo immediately.

Check:

* Physical signage
* Digital platforms
* Printed materials
* Shared folders
* Supplier files
* Templates
* Partner materials

Identify the source of the modification.

Determine whether:

* The supplier lacked the official asset
* The official file was unavailable
* A staff member altered the logo
* An outdated or unofficial file was circulated

Report to HQ with:

* Examples of the modified logo
* Where it appeared
* How long it was in use
* Who created it
* Corrective action taken

### Escalate If

The modified logo has been used publicly, including:

* Published digital content
* Exterior signage
* Distributed printed materials
* Partner or sponsor materials
* Media-facing materials

Notify HQ immediately.

HQ will determine whether a public correction or clarification is required.

---

## 09. Non-Compliant Brand Materials Have Been in Use for More Than 30 Days Without HQ Notification

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What It Looks Like

A brand deviation is identified, but the House Manager does not report it to HQ.

Examples include:

* Non-compliant signage
* Incorrect digital templates
* Wrong colours or typography
* An unapproved supplier deliverable
* Incorrect mission language

The team intends to correct the issue quietly at a later date, but more than 30 days pass and the material remains in use.

### Why It Matters

HQ depends on timely reporting to monitor brand consistency across the network.

Managing deviations locally without notifying HQ means the location is operating outside the required compliance process.

A deviation that remains unreported for more than 30 days is no longer only an operational error. It becomes a compliance breach.

### What to Do

Report every public-facing brand deviation to HQ within 24 hours of identification.

The notification must include:

* Description of the deviation
* Where it appeared
* How it occurred
* How long it was in use
* Estimated audience exposure
* Immediate action taken
* Correction plan
* Target resolution date

Report the deviation even when it has already been corrected.

### Escalate If

HQ discovers during an audit or inspection that the House Manager knew about a deviation but did not report it.

This triggers:

* A formal review of brand management practices
* Corrective action requirements
* A corrective action plan submitted within seven days

---

## 10. A Sponsor or Partner Brand Appears More Prominently Than Palestine House Branding

**Category:** RED FLAG

### What It Looks Like

A sponsor or partner logo, name, or visual identity appears more prominently than Palestine House branding.

This may occur through:

* Larger logo size
* Superior positioning
* Repeated placement
* Stronger colour dominance
* More prominent venue placement
* Greater frequency in digital content

The issue may appear on:

* Event banners
* Social media posts
* Printed programmes
* Venue walls
* Digital screens
* Partner announcements

### Why It Matters

Palestine House must remain the primary institutional identity in every co-branded environment.

Excessive third-party prominence may:

* Create an implied endorsement
* Reverse the intended institutional hierarchy
* Make the House appear to be a promotional platform
* Dilute independence
* Conflict with non-political positioning
* Mislead guests about ownership or control

### What to Do

Review all active materials containing third-party brands.

Correct any instance where Palestine House branding is not primary.

Develop a standard co-branding template specifying:

* Logo hierarchy
* Permitted size ratios
* Placement
* Clear space
* Frequency
* Approved language

Submit the template to HQ for approval before using it as a standing reference.

### Escalate If

The third party has a political identity or association that was not reviewed before the co-branding arrangement was agreed.

Notify HQ immediately.

This is both:

* A brand compliance issue
* A potential non-political positioning breach

---

# SECTION 4: COMPLIANCE ISSUES

## Breaches of Palestine House Brand Standards

---

## 11. Physical Brand Installations Are in Place Without Written HQ Approval

**Category:** COMPLIANCE ISSUE

### What It Looks Like

Physical brand installations are already in use, but the House Manager cannot produce written HQ approval for each item.

Examples include:

* Exterior signage
* Mission statement displays
* Guest Promise displays
* Interior brand elements
* Wall graphics
* Directional signage
* Permanent venue treatments

Approval may have been:

* Verbal
* Assumed
* Discussed by phone
* Mentioned informally
* Never documented

### Why It Matters

All physical brand installations require written HQ approval before installation.

This is both:

* A Brand Experience Standard
* A license agreement requirement

Without documentary evidence, HQ has no verifiable record of what was reviewed and approved.

During a compliance review, an installation without written approval is treated as unapproved.

### What to Do

Audit all current physical brand installations.

For each item, confirm:

* Description
* Location
* Installation date
* Supplier
* Design file
* Written HQ approval
* Any approved amendments

When written approval is missing:

1. Notify HQ.
2. Request retrospective review.
3. Submit photographs.
4. Provide the original design files.
5. Explain what approval was sought at the time.
6. Follow HQ's corrective instructions.

Do not install any new physical brand element until written approval is on file.

### Escalate If

HQ finds physical installations without written approval during an audit.

This triggers a formal brand compliance review.

The Local Partner must obtain or regularise approval for all affected installations within 30 days.

---

## 12. No Brand Deviation Log Is Maintained

**Category:** COMPLIANCE ISSUE

### What It Looks Like

The House Manager does not maintain a written brand deviation log.

When asked to demonstrate brand monitoring, the location can provide only verbal explanations.

There is no reliable record of:

* Deviations identified
* Dates identified
* Corrective action
* Responsible owner
* Resolution date
* HQ notification

### Why It Matters

The brand deviation log is evidence that the House Manager is actively monitoring compliance between formal HQ audits.

Without it, HQ cannot verify that:

* Deviations are being identified
* Corrective action is being completed
* Repeated failures are being investigated
* Brand standards are being actively managed

The absence of a log results in a higher-risk compliance assessment.

### What to Do

Create and maintain the deviation log from the first day of operations.

Every deviation must include:

| Field                 | Required Information                    |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| **Date Identified**   | Date the deviation was first discovered |
| **Description**       | Clear description of the issue          |
| **Location**          | Where the deviation appeared            |
| **Cause**             | How and why it occurred                 |
| **Immediate Action**  | Steps taken to contain the issue        |
| **Corrective Action** | Permanent correction required           |
| **Owner**             | Named person responsible                |
| **HQ Notification**   | Date and method of notification         |
| **Resolution Date**   | Date the issue was fully resolved       |

Review the log monthly.

Include a summary in the quarterly brand compliance submission to HQ.

### Escalate If

HQ requests the deviation log and none exists.

The location must:

1. Establish the log immediately.
2. Conduct a retrospective audit covering the previous three months.
3. Document all identifiable deviations.
4. Submit a catch-up compliance report to HQ within 14 days.

---

# SECTION 5: ESCALATION TRIGGERS

## When Local Authority Is Not Sufficient

---

## 13. A Non-Compliant Brand Element Has Been Published Publicly and Cannot Be Immediately Withdrawn

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What It Looks Like

A public-facing brand deviation cannot be removed or corrected immediately.

Examples include:

* A widely shared social media post
* Distributed printed materials
* Exterior signage
* Published partner content
* An email campaign already delivered

The deviation may involve:

* An incorrect logo
* Unapproved mission language
* Non-compliant imagery
* A co-branding violation
* Incorrect visual identity

### Why It Matters

Public brand deviations may remain visible after correction through:

* Screenshots
* Shares
* Forwarded emails
* Photographs
* Printed copies
* Media coverage

HQ must assess whether proactive communication is needed with:

* Media
* Funders
* Government contacts
* Partners
* Community stakeholders

### What to Do

Contact HQ immediately by phone.

Follow with an email within one hour that includes:

* Description of the deviation
* Where it appeared
* Publication or installation date
* Estimated audience exposure
* Current status
* Action already taken
* Proposed correction

Do not issue:

* A public correction
* A social media explanation
* An apology
* A clarification notice

without HQ direction.

### Escalate If

The non-compliant material has been seen by:

* Media
* Funders
* Government contacts
* Major partners
* A significant portion of the community audience

HQ will lead all external communication.

The Local House must not make a public statement without explicit written approval.

---

## 14. A Supplier Has Produced and Distributed Non-Compliant Materials Before Review

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What It Looks Like

A printer, designer, photographer, AV supplier, or events company produces and distributes Palestine House materials before brand review is completed.

The materials may already be:

* In guests' hands
* Displayed at the venue
* Published online
* Shared with partners
* Used during an event

The issue may involve:

* An incorrect logo
* Political imagery
* Unapproved mission language
* Wrong colours
* Wrong typography
* Unapproved co-branding

### Why It Matters

Once materials are distributed, the error is no longer contained within the production process.

Every person who receives the material encounters a version of Palestine House that does not meet the approved standard.

Depending on the deviation, the issue may create:

* Reputational risk
* Contractual risk
* Political positioning concerns
* Partner relationship risk
* Community confusion

### What to Do

Recall or withdraw the materials as quickly as possible.

Immediately:

1. Stop further distribution.
2. Remove accessible copies.
3. Do not accept or pay for the non-compliant deliverable.
4. Record the quantity produced.
5. Record the quantity distributed.
6. Identify recipients or distribution locations.
7. Notify HQ within 24 hours.
8. Submit a correction plan.
9. Review why the approval stage was bypassed.

### Escalate If

The materials contain:

* Political imagery or language
* Unapproved partner or sponsor branding
* A significant mission statement deviation
* A modified Palestine House logo
* Content that could create reputational harm

Notify HQ immediately.

HQ may need to communicate proactively with affected parties.

---

## 15. An HQ Brand Audit Reveals Systemic Non-Compliance Across Multiple Touchpoints

**Category:** ESCALATION TRIGGER

### What It Looks Like

An HQ audit identifies multiple brand failures at the same time.

Examples include:

* Non-compliant physical signage
* Outdated digital assets
* Incorrect logo versions
* No deviation log
* Missing training records
* No written approval for installations
* Unapproved supplier materials
* Inconsistent co-branding

### Why It Matters

Systemic non-compliance is different from an isolated error.

It indicates that brand standards are not being actively monitored or enforced as an operational discipline.

Failure at one location can affect the credibility of the wider Palestine House network with:

* Guests
* Funders
* Partners
* Community organisations
* Government contacts
* Media

### What to Do

Do not attempt to correct all findings without coordination.

Wait for HQ's formal audit findings and corrective action requirements.

Then:

1. Review every finding.
2. Assign an owner.
3. Set a completion deadline.
4. Correct each issue systematically.
5. Provide documentary evidence.
6. Maintain a central corrective action tracker.
7. Prepare for a follow-up audit.

HQ may schedule a follow-up review within 60 days.

### Escalate If

The audit finds that:

* Physical installations lack written approval
* No brand deviation log exists
* Brand training records are absent
* Several active materials are non-compliant

This triggers a formal license agreement compliance review.

Depending on the severity, HQ may suspend new materials production until the location establishes a clean compliance record.

---

# A NOTE FOR HOUSE MANAGERS

Brand standards matter most when they are difficult to maintain:

* When a supplier is running late
* When an event deadline is approaching
* When a sponsor needs an immediate answer
* When an approved asset is inconvenient to use
* When a staff member believes a small change will not matter

The discipline of Palestine House is demonstrated by maintaining the standard precisely when it is most inconvenient to do so.

> HQ is your partner in protecting the brand, not merely your auditor.

Use this document to identify and resolve risks before they become public failures.

When something goes wrong, notify HQ early.

