# Purpose of This Guide

This guide explains how to establish, operate, and protect the technology infrastructure that supports every Palestine House location.

Technology is not a back-office function—it is the operational backbone that enables the House to:

- Manage memberships
- Sell tickets
- Process payments
- Communicate with the community
- Produce operational reports
- Report accurately to HQ

A failure in the technology stack is an operational failure.

Use this guide:

- Before opening
- When introducing new technology
- When changing suppliers
- During audits
- When responding to data breaches or technology failures

---

# Who This Guide Is For

This guide is intended for:

- House Manager
- Digital Operations staff
- Membership administrators
- Communications staff
- Finance staff
- Anyone handling guest or member data

Technology standards apply to everyone handling personal or operational data.

HQ defines the approved technology stack.

No location may introduce:

- New platforms
- Payment systems
- Data storage tools
- Brand-facing technology

without written HQ approval.

---

# Step 1 — Understand the Palestine House Technology Stack

The technology stack consists of approved digital platforms used consistently across every Palestine House location.

Standardisation ensures:

- Consistent operations
- Secure data management
- Reliable financial reporting
- Uniform guest experience

---

## Layer 1 — CRM & Membership

Manages:

- Members
- Guests
- Communications
- Community relationships

Every location must use the HQ-approved CRM.

---

## Layer 2 — Ticketing & Booking

Supports:

- Event ticketing
- Venue bookings
- Admissions

Integrates with CRM and financial reporting.

---

## Layer 3 — Payments & Finance

Includes:

- Payment gateway
- Accounting software

All Palestine House income must pass through the approved payment infrastructure.

---

## Layer 4 — Communications

Supports:

- Email marketing
- Newsletters
- Community communications
- Social media management

Approved templates must always be used.

---

## Layer 5 — Reporting & Data

Supports:

- KPI reporting
- Operational dashboards
- HQ reporting

All reports must remain compatible with HQ reporting formats.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] House Manager understands all five technology layers
- [ ] HQ Technology Specification received
- [ ] Technology documentation stored centrally
- [ ] No unapproved technology platforms in use

---

# Step 2 — Set Up the CRM & Membership System

The CRM is the most important operational system.

It manages:

- Member records
- Guest data
- Communications
- Event attendance

Correct configuration before launch is mandatory.

---

## CRM Data Flow

```text
Member Sign-up
        │
        ▼
Record Created
        │
        ▼
Tags Applied
(Member Type • Interests • Event History)
        │
        ▼
Automated Communications
        │
        ▼
HQ Reporting Dashboard
```

---

## CRM Setup Checklist

Before Soft Launch:

- HQ-managed CRM instance configured
- HQ-approved data schema applied
- Membership tiers configured
- Ticketing integration completed
- Payment integration completed
- Welcome automation tested
- Staff permissions configured
- Data exports tested

Apply the principle of **minimum necessary access**.

---

## Decision Point

**Is the CRM operating within the HQ-managed environment using the approved data structure?**

### Yes

Continue.

The CRM is ready for member data.

### No

Do not collect any member information.

Contact HQ for configuration support.

---

## Expected Output

- [ ] CRM live within HQ environment
- [ ] Approved data schema implemented
- [ ] Welcome automation tested
- [ ] Staff access documented

